 'C',
 $q$Under reflux with a strong oxidising agent, the oxidation proceeds past ethanal to ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH. Distil off the product early and you trap ethanal instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2024,2, $q$Isotopes of an element have$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the same number of neutrons but different protons"},{"label":"B","text":"the same number of protons but different neutrons"},{"label":"C","text":"different protons and different neutrons"},{"label":"D","text":"different numbers of electrons only"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Protons define the element; neutrons can vary. Chemical behaviour is nearly identical because it depends on electron arrangement, which stays the same.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2025,1, $q$A bond formed by the transfer of electrons from potassium to chlorine is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"metallic"},{"label":"B","text":"covalent"},{"label":"C","text":"ionic"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen bonding"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A metal from group 1 meets a non-metal from group 7, the electronegativity gap is large, and electrons are transferred outright — the definition of ionic bonding, giving K⁺ and Cl⁻.$q$,
