 $q$Heterozygous parents carry both the dominant and recessive alleles, so the recessive phenotype reappears in one quarter of the offspring.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Write out the gametes","kind":"text","content":"Each Tt parent produces two kinds of gamete: T and t, in equal numbers.","note":"Gametes carry one allele per gene — this is the segregation step behind the ratio."},{"title":"Cross the gametes","kind":"math","content":"T × T = TT,  T × t = Tt,  t × T = Tt,  t × t = tt","note":"Four equally likely outcomes: 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt."},{"title":"Read off the phenotypes","kind":"text","content":"TT and Tt are tall (T is dominant), tt is short — giving 3 tall : 1 short.","note":"Genotype ratio 1:2:1; phenotype ratio 3:1. The two are related but never the same answer."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2021,2, $q$The observable physical characteristics of an organism, produced by its genes and the environment, make up its$q$,
