 $q$Logarithms of positive quantities only — that constraint is half the marks, and it silently rejects one of the quadratic's roots.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Combine the logs","kind":"math","content":"log₁₀[x(x − 3)] = 1, since log a + log b = log(ab)","note":"The sum of two logs is the log of a product — one line, one law."},{"title":"Rewrite in index form","kind":"math","content":"x(x − 3) = 10¹ → x² − 3x − 10 = 0","note":"log₁₀y = 1 means y = 10; then factorise: (x − 5)(x + 2) = 0."},{"title":"Reject the invalid root","kind":"text","content":"x = 5 or x = −2. But x = −2 makes log₁₀(−2) undefined, so only x = 5 is valid.","note":"Check every candidate against the original logs' domains before answering."}]$st$,
 3),
('mathematics','progressions',2016,2, $q$An arithmetic progression has first term 3 and common difference 2. Its 10th term is$q$,
