('mathematics','quadratics',2021,1, $q$Find the values of k for which the equation x² + kx + 9 = 0 has equal roots, explaining the condition used.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"k = 6 only"},{"label":"B","text":"k = ±6"},{"label":"C","text":"k = 3"},{"label":"D","text":"k = ±3"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Equal roots ⇔ discriminant = 0. Since k appears squared, two values survive — forgetting the negative one is the classic miss.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"State the condition for equal roots","kind":"text","content":"A quadratic has two identical real roots exactly when its discriminant b² − 4ac = 0.","note":"Positive discriminant → two roots; zero → one repeated root; negative → none real."},{"title":"Apply it to the equation","kind":"math","content":"k² − 4(1)(9) = 0 → k² − 36 = 0","note":"Here a = 1, b = k, c = 9 — map the symbols carefully."},{"title":"Solve for k","kind":"math","content":"k² = 36 → k = ±6","note":"Both values give the same repeated root structure: x² + 6x + 9 = (x+3)²."}]$st$,
