 $q$Power rule: d/dx(axⁿ) = anxⁿ⁻¹, so 2 × 3x = 6x. The constant multiplier rides along; the exponent descends.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('mathematics','progressions',2022,1, $q$A geometric progression has first term 2 and common ratio 3. Its 4th term is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"18"},{"label":"B","text":"27"},{"label":"C","text":"54"},{"label":"D","text":"162"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Tₙ = arⁿ⁻¹ = 2 × 3³ = 54. The first term takes exponent 0, so the 4th term uses r³, not r⁴ — the geometric twin of the AP trap.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2022,2, $q$In triangle ABC, angle A = 60°, angle B = 30° and side a = 6. Using the sine rule, side b equals$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"√3"},{"label":"B","text":"2√3"},{"label":"C","text":"3√3"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Check the third angle first: C = 90°, so the hypotenuse is indeed a. Sine rule: b = a·sin B/sin A = 6 × (1/2)/(√3/2) = 2√3. Know both routes to b — the sine rule and the 30-60-90 ratio 2√3:6.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('mathematics','geometry',2022,3, $q$The area of a sector of a circle of radius 6 cm with angle 60° at the centre is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2π cm²"},{"label":"B","text":"6π cm²"},{"label":"C","text":"12π cm²"},{"label":"D","text":"36π cm²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sector area = (θ/360) × πr² = (60/360) × 36π = 6π cm². The sector is one-sixth of the circle — a proportion check that catches calculator slips.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2024,1, $q$Rationalising 1/(√2 − 1) gives$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"√2 + 1"},{"label":"B","text":"√2 − 1"},{"label":"C","text":"1 − √2"},{"label":"D","text":"(√2 + 1)/3"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Multiply top and bottom by the conjugate (√2 + 1): the denominator becomes (√2)² − 1² = 1, leaving √2 + 1. Label your conjugate before you multiply — sign errors live there.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2024,2, $q$The gradient of the curve y = x² at the point where x = 3 is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"9"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$dy/dx = 2x, evaluated at x = 3, gives 6. The gradient of the curve differs from the y-coordinate of the point (which is 9) — read which one is asked.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('mathematics','indices-logs',2024,3, $q$Evaluate log₁₀2 + log₁₀5.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"log₁₀7"},{"label":"B","text":"log₁₀10"},{"label":"C","text":"1"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$log 2 + log 5 = log(2 × 5) = log 10 = 1. Sum of logs is the log of the product — and products that make the base of the log collapse to clean answers.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2024,4, $q$The mean of the numbers 4, 6, 9, 11 and 15 is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"9"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mean = sum/count = 45/5 = 9. The mean sits near the middle of an ordered set — anything far outside that band signals an addition slip.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('mathematics','statistics',2025,1, $q$Events A and B are independent with P(A) = ½ and P(B) = ¼. P(A and B) equals$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1/8"},{"label":"B","text":"3/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/6"},{"label":"D","text":"1/2"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$For independent events, probabilities multiply: ½ × ¼ = 1/8. Independence is a condition to be stated, not assumed — without it, the product rule is wrong.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2025,2, $q$A right-angled triangle has legs of length 5 cm and 12 cm. Its hypotenuse is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"13 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"15 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"17 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"169 cm"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$√(25 + 144) = √169 = 13. The 5-12-13 triple joins 3-4-5 and 8-15-17 among the triples junior examiners recycle constantly.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2025,3, $q$The value of cos 60° is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"√3/2"},{"label":"B","text":"√2/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"√3/3"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Special angles: cos 60° = ½ (while sin 60° = √3/2). Remember them as the 30-60-90 sides — adjacent over hypotenuse = 1/2.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('mathematics','calculus',2025,4, $q$∫2x dx equals$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2x + C"},{"label":"B","text":"x² + C"},{"label":"C","text":"2x² + C"},{"label":"D","text":"x³/3 + C"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Reverse the power rule and raise the exponent: ∫2x dx = 2 × x²/2 + C = x² + C. Differentiate your answer to check — you should get back 2x.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);


-- ================================================================== ENGLISH (24)
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('english','lexis-structure',2000,1, $q$Neither the boys nor their teacher ____ present at the meeting.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"are"},{"label":"B","text":"is"},{"label":"C","text":"were"},{"label":"D","text":"have being"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it — teacher (singular). Find the nearer subject first; the plural boys is the decoy.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2000,2, $q$The word most nearly opposite in meaning to generous is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"kind"},{"label":"B","text":"lavish"},{"label":"C","text":"miserly"},{"label":"D","text":"pleasant"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Miserly means unwilling to spend — the direct opposite of generous. Note the register too: miserly fits money-talk, which is where JAMB loves it.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','lexis-structure',2004,1, $q$By this time next week, the students ____ their examinations.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"are writing"},{"label":"B","text":"will have written"},{"label":"C","text":"would write"},{"label":"D","text":"write"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$By + a future time flags the future perfect: an action completed before that point. Will have written is the only form that marks completion before next week.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','oral-forms',2004,2, $q$Which of these words is stressed on its second syllable?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"table"},{"label":"B","text":"woman"},{"label":"C","text":"recite"},{"label":"D","text":"holiday"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$re-CITE carries the stress on the second syllable (a verb pattern shared with de-CIDE, in-VITE). Many noun/verb pairs flip their stress between syllables — record, present, export.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','lexis-structure',2008,1, $q$Rebecca has a flair ____ literature.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"on"},{"label":"B","text":"for"},{"label":"C","text":"of"},{"label":"D","text":"with"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fixed collocation: a flair FOR something. Preposition questions are won by memorised partnerships — flair, thirst, knack, appetite all take for.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2008,2, $q$The word closest in meaning to candid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"deceitful"},{"label":"B","text":"humorous"},{"label":"C","text":"frank"},{"label":"D","text":"aggressive"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Candid = open and straightforward — frank. Option A is the opposite; trait options like humorous and aggressive sound plausible but drift from the core meaning.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','comprehension',2013,1, $q$Read this passage: "When the new teacher arrived, lessons that had once felt endless became short and lively. Even the backbenchers began asking questions." The passage suggests that the new teacher$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"punished the backbenchers until they complied"},{"label":"B","text":"made the lessons more engaging"},{"label":"C","text":"dismissed class early every day"},{"label":"D","text":"taught a difficult subject"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Short, lively lessons plus silent students now volunteering questions both point to engagement. Inference questions reward the simplest conclusion supported twice.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','oral-forms',2013,2, $q$Which pair of words rhymes?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"through / rough"},{"label":"B","text":"blue / new"},{"label":"C","text":"been / nine"},{"label":"D","text":"weight / sight"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$blue and new share the /uː/ sound. Spelling misleads in rhyme questions — trust the sound: through/rough looks rhymed but is /uː/ versus /ʌf/.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','lexis-structure',2016,1, $q$He summed up the lecture "in a nutshell". This means the lecture was given$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"in a confused manner"},{"label":"B","text":"briefly"},{"label":"C","text":"in a hurry"},{"label":"D","text":"with difficulty"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In a nutshell = in a few words, briefly. Idioms must be answered with their set meaning, not the feelings of the sentence around them.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2016,2, $q$The word opposite in meaning to scarce is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"rare"},{"label":"B","text":"abundant"},{"label":"C","text":"costly"},{"label":"D","text":"hidden"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Scarce is about availability, not price: abundant is the clean opposite. Expensive is the trap — scarcity often raises prices, but that is a consequence, not the meaning.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','novel',2019,1, $q$The prescribed novel Sweet Sixteen was written by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Chinua Achebe"},{"label":"B","text":"Bolaji Abdullahi"},{"label":"C","text":"Khadija Abubakar Jalli"},{"label":"D","text":"Chimeka Garricks"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sweet Sixteen — the JAMB compulsory text for 2019–2021 — is by Bolaji Abdullahi. Option C is the author of The Life Changer (2022–2025), a deliberately swapped decoy.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','lexis-structure',2019,2, $q$Each of the girls ____ a novel to read.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"have"},{"label":"B","text":"has"},{"label":"C","text":"were having"},{"label":"D","text":"are having"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each is grammatically singular no matter what follows it. Subject–verb agreement follows form, not the mental image of many girls.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','comprehension',2019,3, $q$Read this passage: "The old bridge swayed whenever a lorry crossed it, yet the villagers preferred it to the long detour through the river valley." The passage implies that the bridge$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"had been recently rebuilt"},{"label":"B","text":"was unsafe despite being the faster route"},{"label":"C","text":"carried no heavy vehicles"},{"label":"D","text":"was slower than the valley road"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The sway signals danger; the preference for it signals convenience. Inference means holding both halves of the sentence at once — B does exactly that.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','novel',2019,4, $q$In Sweet Sixteen, the story is essentially built around$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a boy leaving home for his first job"},{"label":"B","text":"a father preparing his sixteen-year-old daughter, Aliya, for adulthood"},{"label":"C","text":"a sister's sacrifice for her family"},{"label":"D","text":"a village recovering from drought"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The novel's frame is the birthday-eve conversation between Aliya and her father, through which he walks her into the realities of growing up. Everything else in the book hangs off that frame.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','novel',2021,1, $q$The events of Sweet Sixteen reach the reader mainly through$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a courtroom testimony"},{"label":"B","text":"letters Aliya writes to her school principal"},{"label":"C","text":"a conversation between Aliya and her father around her birthday"},{"label":"D","text":"a stranger's diary found in a library"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The narrative is structured as dialogue between father and daughter on the eve of her sixteenth birthday, with flashbacks folded in. Questions about narrative method are as examinable as plot facts.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','oral-forms',2021,2, $q$Which of these words contains the vowel sound /ɑː/ as in "father"?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fate"},{"label":"B","text":"calm"},{"label":"C","text":"led"},{"label":"D","text":"kite"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$calm = /kɑːm/. The adjacent letter l is silent; the vowel is the long /ɑː/. Exam a's mouth shape, not the spelling.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','novel',2022,1, $q$The prescribed novel The Life Changer was written by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Bolaji Abdullahi"},{"label":"B","text":"Wole Soyinka"},{"label":"C","text":"Khadija Abubakar Jalli"},{"label":"D","text":"Chinelo Okparanta"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The Life Changer (the JAMB text from 2022 through 2025) is by Khadija Abubakar Jalli — author questions are free marks if you memorise the pairing with the years of use.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','lexis-structure',2022,2, $q$In the sentence "She answered quickly," the word quickly functions as a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"adjective"},{"label":"B","text":"adverb"},{"label":"C","text":"verb"},{"label":"D","text":"noun"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$It modifies the verb answered — how she answered — which is the definition of an adverb. Ask what the word describes before naming its class.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','novel',2023,1, $q$The Life Changer is narrated by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Salma"},{"label":"B","text":"Ummi"},{"label":"C","text":"Omar"},{"label":"D","text":"a third-person omniscient narrator"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Ummi tells the story to her children at home, so the university experiences reach us through her first-person narration. Option D always looks scholarly — but the book is firmly first-person.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2023,2, $q$The word closest in meaning to resilient is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fragile"},{"label":"B","text":"forgetful"},{"label":"C","text":"tough"},{"label":"D","text":"careless"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Resilient describes something that recovers under stress — tough, tenacious. Fragile is the opposite; the other options are merely adjacent personalities.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','novel',2024,1, $q$In The Life Changer, the experiences Ummi narrates took place mainly$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"at a secondary boarding school"},{"label":"B","text":"at a university"},{"label":"C","text":"in a market town"},{"label":"D","text":"abroad"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Ummi's stories are of her university days — registration stress, hostel life, lecturers — told to her children at home. Frame and content are different questions; this one asks where the told events happened.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','comprehension',2024,2, $q$Read this passage: "The council had promised repairs within a week. Three months later, the potholes had grown into small ponds." The passage suggests that the council$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"had failed to act as promised"},{"label":"B","text":"repaired another street instead"},{"label":"C","text":"was delayed by heavy rainfall"},{"label":"D","text":"had planned the delay"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Nothing was implied about cause — only about outcome. The contrast of one week against three months is the evidence; resist inventing explanations the passage never offers.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','novel',2025,1, $q$Salma's character in The Life Changer is best described as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"humble and quiet"},{"label":"B","text":"proud and outspoken"},{"label":"C","text":"shy and withdrawn"},{"label":"D","text":"unknown to the narrator"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Salma is strikingly beautiful, self-assured to the point of arrogance, and says what she thinks — traits that drive her biggest troubles in the story. Character questions judge the pattern, not one scene.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','lexis-structure',2025,2, $q$A number of candidates ____ absent from the examination hall.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"were"},{"label":"B","text":"was"},{"label":"C","text":"is"},{"label":"D","text":"has been"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$"A number of" takes a plural verb (the people are many and scattered), while "the number of" takes singular. This pair is a JAMB evergreen — learn both sides at once.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);


-- ================================================================== YEAR-GAP FILL (every year 2000-2025 covered; era-faithful)
-- Paper era 2000-2014 (PPT), CBT-250 2015-2016, CBT-180 2017+. qnum 9 never clashes.
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
-- ---- PHYSICS fills
('physics','kinematics',2001,9, $q$A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2 m s⁻² for 8 s. What distance does it cover?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"32 m"},{"label":"B","text":"48 m"},{"label":"C","text":"64 m"},{"label":"D","text":"96 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$s = ½at² = ½ × 2 × 64 = 64 m. Paper-era sets loved the from-rest form because only s = ½at² applies. (reconstructed in the 2001 SSL pattern)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2002,9, $q$A tuning fork produces a note of frequency 512 Hz. If the speed of sound in air is 340 m s⁻¹, the wavelength of the note is closest to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.33 m"},{"label":"B","text":"0.50 m"},{"label":"C","text":"0.66 m"},{"label":"D","text":"1.50 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$λ = v/f = 340/512 ≈ 0.66 m. The v = fλ bridge is one of the most frequent wave items of the paper era. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2004,9, $q$A 0.5 kg metal block at 100 °C is dropped into 0.5 kg of water at 20 °C. Taking c(metal) = 400 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ and c(water) = 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, the final temperature is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"22 °C"},{"label":"B","text":"27 °C"},{"label":"C","text":"33 °C"},{"label":"D","text":"45 °C"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Heat lost = heat gained: 200(100 − θ) = 2100(θ − 20) → 20,000 − 200θ = 2100θ − 42,000 → θ ≈ 27 °C. Water's huge heat capacity keeps the mixture near its own temperature. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','light-optics',2005,9, $q$An object is placed 30 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. The image distance is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"7.5 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"15 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"30 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/10 − 1/30 = 2/30 → v = 15 cm, a real image. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2007,9, $q$A pulley system of velocity ratio 5 raises a 900 N load with an effort of 200 N. Its efficiency is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60%"},{"label":"B","text":"75%"},{"label":"C","text":"80%"},{"label":"D","text":"90%"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$η = MA/VR × 100% = (900/200)/5 × 100% = 90%. The MA/VR chain is the classic machine fingerprint of mid-2000s papers. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2009,9, $q$Resistors of 3 Ω and 6 Ω are connected in parallel across a 12 V battery of negligible internal resistance. The total current drawn is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 A"},{"label":"B","text":"4 A"},{"label":"C","text":"6 A"},{"label":"D","text":"9 A"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$R = (3×6)/(3+6) = 2 Ω; I = V/R = 12/2 = 6 A. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2010,9, $q$A projectile is fired at 20 m s⁻¹ at 60° to the horizontal. Taking g = 10 m s⁻², its maximum height is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 m"},{"label":"B","text":"15 m"},{"label":"C","text":"17.3 m"},{"label":"D","text":"20 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$H = u²sin²θ/2g = (400 × 3/4)/20 = 15 m. (reconstructed; 2010 papers are archived but wording varies between banks)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2012,9, $q$A battery of emf 12 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is connected to a 5.5 Ω resistor. The terminal potential difference is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9 V"},{"label":"B","text":"10 V"},{"label":"C","text":"11 V"},{"label":"D","text":"12 V"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$I = E/(R + r) = 12/6 = 2 A; V = E − Ir = 12 − 1 = 11 V. The E − Ir terminal-voltage fingerprint recurs across eras. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2014,9, $q$The pressure exerted at the base of a liquid column 5 m deep of density 800 kg m⁻³ is (g = 10 m s⁻²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"400 Pa"},{"label":"B","text":"4,000 Pa"},{"label":"C","text":"10,000 Pa"},{"label":"D","text":"40,000 Pa"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$P = ρgh = 800 × 10 × 5 = 40,000 Pa. The last full paper-era year before the CBT-only policy was locked in. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','electricity',2015,9, $q$A transformer has 200 primary turns and 50 secondary turns. With 240 V applied to the primary, the output voltage is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"30 V"},{"label":"B","text":"48 V"},{"label":"C","text":"60 V"},{"label":"D","text":"80 V"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Vs/Vp = Ns/Np → Vs = 240 × 50/200 = 60 V. 2015 was the first year every candidate sat the exam on computer, though the paper still carried 250 questions (English 100 + 3 × 50). (CBT-250 era marker)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2017,9, $q$A conductor of length 0.5 m carrying a current of 4 A lies perpendicular to a magnetic field of 0.2 T. The force on it is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.05 N"},{"label":"B","text":"0.4 N"},{"label":"C","text":"0.8 N"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$F = BIL = 0.2 × 4 × 0.5 = 0.4 N. 2017 was the reform year: the paper dropped to 180 questions (English 60 + 3 × 40) in two hours. (CBT-180 era marker)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2018,9, $q$Light of frequency 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz ejects electrons whose maximum kinetic energy is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Taking h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, the work function of the surface is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"},{"label":"B","text":"2.5 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"},{"label":"C","text":"4.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"},{"label":"D","text":"5.9 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$hf = 6.6×10⁻³⁴ × 7.5×10¹⁴ ≈ 4.95×10⁻¹⁹ J; W = hf − KEmax ≈ 4.0×10⁻¹⁹ J. The photoelectric chain is a modern-physics evergreen. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','dynamics',2020,9, $q$A 2 kg body moving at 5 m s⁻¹ collides with a stationary 3 kg body and they move off together. Their common velocity is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 m s⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"2 m s⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 m s⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"3 m s⁻¹"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Momentum conservation: 2 × 5 = 5v → v = 2 m s⁻¹. The 2020 exam ran in March, ahead of the COVID lockdown, under the 180-question format. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2023,9, $q$A vibrating string of length 50 cm sounds its fundamental note at 200 Hz. With the tension unchanged, a length of 25 cm will produce a frequency of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"100 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"200 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"400 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"800 Hz"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$f ∝ 1/L for the same tension and mass per unit length, so halving the length doubles the frequency to 400 Hz. (reconstructed)$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
-- ---- CHEMISTRY fills
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2001,9, $q$An element X forms an ion X²⁺. Its chloride will most likely have the formula$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"XCl"},{"label":"B","text":"XCl₂"},{"label":"C","text":"XCl₃"},{"label":"D","text":"XCl₄"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A 2+ cation balances two Cl⁻ ions: XCl₂. Charge pairing is a permanent Group-checking skill on the syllabus. (reconstructed)$q$,
