('english','lexis-structure',2005,22, $q$Fill each gap with the most appropriate option: There ___ many reasons why the project must begin now.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"is"},{"label":"B","text":"was"},{"label":"C","text":"are"},{"label":"D","text":"has been"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In 'there is/are' sentences, the verb agrees with the real subject that follows; 'many reasons' is plural.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2005,23, $q$Choose the option nearest in meaning to the word in quotation marks: Hospital work can be very "mundane". $q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"exciting"},{"label":"B","text":"commonplace"},{"label":"C","text":"unusual"},{"label":"D","text":"heavenly"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$'Mundane' means ordinary and lacking excitement, which matches 'commonplace'; 'exciting' and 'unusual' express the opposite.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','comprehension',2005,24, $q$In the sentence 'Her comments on my new dress were quite uncharitable,' the word 'uncharitable' suggests that the comments were$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"generous."},{"label":"B","text":"unkind."},{"label":"C","text":"religious."},{"label":"D","text":"temporary."}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$'Uncharitable' means not charitable in feeling, i.e. cruel or unkind in judging others; 'generous' is the opposite of the required sense.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','lexis-structure',2005,25, $q$Fill each gap with the most appropriate option: ___ I known of your arrival earlier, I would have met you at the airport.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Have"},{"label":"B","text":"Had"},{"label":"C","text":"Having"},{"label":"D","text":"If"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$'Had I known ...' is the inverted third conditional (= 'If I had known'), formed by putting 'had' before the subject and omitting 'if'.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','oral-forms',2005,26, $q$In some two-syllable words the stress shifts according to the part of speech. Which option is stressed on the first syllable when used as a noun and on the second syllable when used as a verb?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"project"},{"label":"B","text":"prospect"},{"label":"C","text":"proceed"},{"label":"D","text":"prudent"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$'Project' is PRO-ject as a noun and pro-JECT as a verb; 'prospect' is a first-syllable noun, 'proceed' only a verb, and 'prudent' only an adjective.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2005,27, $q$Choose the option opposite in meaning to the word in quotation marks: The old soldier was a notorious "spendthrift". $q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"miser"},{"label":"B","text":"prodigal"},{"label":"C","text":"philanthropist"},{"label":"D","text":"gambler"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A 'spendthrift' wastes money freely, so his opposite is a 'miser', who hoards money; 'prodigal' and 'philanthropist' also involve spending.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','lexis-structure',2005,28, $q$Fill each gap with the most appropriate option: Her request for an official car was ___ by the board.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"turned away"},{"label":"B","text":"turned off"},{"label":"C","text":"turned down"},{"label":"D","text":"turned out"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$'Turn down' means to reject a request, application or offer; 'turn away' means to refuse a person entry and 'turn out' means to produce or disprove.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','comprehension',2005,29, $q$Choose the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The two prefects are birds of a feather.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"They hold opposing views."},{"label":"B","text":"They belong to different parties."},{"label":"C","text":"They are alike in character."},{"label":"D","text":"They are fond of birds."}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$'Birds of a feather (flock together)' means that people of the same character or habits associate with one another.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2005,30, $q$Choose the option nearest in meaning to the word in quotation marks: The rebel colonel had to "relinquish" power at last.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"retain"},{"label":"B","text":"seize"},{"label":"C","text":"amass"},{"label":"D","text":"give up"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$'Relinquish' means to give up or surrender something one holds; it is the opposite of 'retain' and 'amass'.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','lexis-structure',2005,31, $q$Choose the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The candidate kept beating about the bush whenever he was asked about his age.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"He avoided the main subject."},{"label":"B","text":"He wandered in the bushes."},{"label":"C","text":"He spoke out plainly."},{"label":"D","text":"He cut the discussion short."}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$'Beat about the bush' means to talk around a subject, avoiding the main point instead of stating it directly.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','oral-forms',2005,32, $q$Choose the option that rhymes with 'hidden'.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"brighten"},{"label":"B","text":"ridden"},{"label":"C","text":"rotten"},{"label":"D","text":"button"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$'Hidden' /ˈhɪdən/ and 'ridden' /ˈrɪdən/ share the stressed short vowel /ɪ/ and the ending; 'brighten' has /aɪ/, 'rotten' short /ɒ/, and 'button' short /ʌ/ before the close of the word.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2005,33, $q$Choose the option opposite in meaning to the word in quotation marks: The panel moved to "terminate" the contract. $q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"commence"},{"label":"B","text":"conclude"},{"label":"C","text":"finish"},{"label":"D","text":"postpone"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$'Terminate' means to bring to an end, so its opposite is 'commence', to start; 'conclude' and 'finish' are synonyms.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','comprehension',2005,34, $q$Choose the option that best explains the information conveyed in the sentence: The suspect clammed up when the detective arrived.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"He became talkative."},{"label":"B","text":"He refused to talk."},{"label":"C","text":"He confessed quickly."},{"label":"D","text":"He called his lawyer."}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$'Clam up' means to stop speaking or refuse to answer questions; it is the opposite of becoming talkative.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','oral-forms',2005,35, $q$Which option contains the same vowel sound as the one in 'sit'?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"seat"},{"label":"B","text":"fit"},{"label":"C","text":"site"},{"label":"D","text":"set"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$'Sit' has the short vowel /ɪ/, which is also in 'fit'; 'seat' has long /iː/, 'site' the diphthong /aɪ/, and 'set' short /e/.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2005,36, $q$Choose the option nearest in meaning to the word in quotation marks: The witness gave a "vague" account of the accident.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"definite"},{"label":"B","text":"precise"},{"label":"C","text":"unclear"},{"label":"D","text":"flattering"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$'Vague' means not clearly expressed, i.e. unclear; 'definite' and 'precise' are opposites.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('english','oral-forms',2005,37, $q$Choose the question that the following sentence, with emphatic stress on the word in capital letters, answers: The doctor examined the PATIENT.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Did the doctor treat the patient?"},{"label":"B","text":"Did the nurse examine the patient?"},{"label":"C","text":"Did the doctor discharge the patient?"},{"label":"D","text":"Did the doctor examine the nurse?"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Emphatic stress on 'patient' contrasts the person examined with another person, the nurse, so the implied question is about the nurse.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('english','antonyms-synonyms',2005,38, $q$Choose the option opposite in meaning to the word in quotation marks: The clerk is always "punctual" at work.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"prompt"},{"label":"B","text":"early"},{"label":"C","text":"regular"},{"label":"D","text":"late"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$'Punctual' means arriving at the exact time, so its opposite is 'late'; 'prompt', 'early' and 'regular' are close to it or unrelated.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);
