 'A',
 $q$The momentum reverses, so the size of the change is 0.2 × (10 + 6) = 3.2 N s. Option C treats the rebound as if in the same direction, erasing the sign flip.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2020,15, $q$A horizontal force of 30 N pushes two blocks of 2 kg and 3 kg, kept in contact, along a frictionless floor. The force the 2 kg block exerts on the 3 kg block is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12 N"},{"label":"B","text":"18 N"},{"label":"C","text":"30 N"},{"label":"D","text":"24 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Whole system first: a = 30/5 = 6 m/s². The front block then needs 3 × 6 = 18 N of push; option A is the rear block's own share.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2020,16, $q$A car of mass 1000 kg rounds an unbanked curve of radius 50 m at 15 m/s. The frictional force providing the centripetal acceleration is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3000 N"},{"label":"B","text":"2000 N"},{"label":"C","text":"500 N"},{"label":"D","text":"4500 N"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$F = mv²/r = 1000 × 225/50 = 4500 N. Slip on v² and the answer shrinks by the square of the error.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2020,17, $q$Two steel balls of radii 1.0 mm and 2.0 mm fall through the same oil, each soon travelling at terminal velocity. The ratio of the larger ball's terminal velocity to the smaller's is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 4"},{"label":"B","text":"2 : 1"},{"label":"C","text":"4 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"8 : 1"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$For slow spheres the terminal speed scales with r²: doubling the radius quadruples it. Option B scales with radius alone and option D with radius cubed.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','work-energy',2020,18, $q$A 0.5 kg mango falls from a branch 10 m above the ground. Just before it lands, its kinetic energy is (g = 10 m/s², ignoring air resistance)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"25 J"},{"label":"B","text":"50 J"},{"label":"C","text":"100 J"},{"label":"D","text":"500 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Every joule of mgh = 0.5 × 10 × 10 = 50 J converts to kinetic energy in free fall. Option C scales as if g were 20 m/s².$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','work-energy',2020,19, $q$A boy of weight 400 N runs up a flight of stairs 4.0 m high in 5 s. His useful power output is$q$,
