 'C',
 $q$Efficiency = MA/VR = 8/10 = 80%. Option A inverts the ratio; no real machine can exceed 100%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2020,21, $q$How much heat is needed to raise 2.0 kg of water from 15°C to 35°C? (Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J/(kg °C))$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8.4 × 10^4 J"},{"label":"B","text":"4.2 × 10^4 J"},{"label":"C","text":"1.68 × 10^4 J"},{"label":"D","text":"1.68 × 10^5 J"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Q = mcΔT = 2 × 4200 × 20 = 168000 J. Option A forgets the 20-degree rise and uses 10 instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2020,22, $q$The vacuum in the double-walled container of a vacuum flask prevents heat flow mainly by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"conduction only"},{"label":"B","text":"convection only"},{"label":"C","text":"conduction and convection"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A vacuum removes the matter that conduction and convection need; radiation is handled separately by the silvered walls. That division of labour is exactly what the flask design is built on.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2020,23, $q$Water has its maximum density at about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0°C"},{"label":"B","text":"100°C"},{"label":"C","text":"40°C"},{"label":"D","text":"4°C"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Water is densest near 4°C: below that, cooling opens up the ice lattice instead of contracting the liquid. That is why lakes freeze from the top downward.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','fluids-heat',2020,24, $q$The frequency of a wave whose period is 0.004 s is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"25 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"250 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"40 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"0.25 Hz"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$f = 1/T = 1/0.004 = 250 Hz. Option A belongs to a period of 0.04 s - one zero slips too many.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','waves-sound',2020,25, $q$Doubling the amplitude of a wave multiplies the energy it carries by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 times"},{"label":"B","text":"4 times"},{"label":"C","text":"8 times"},{"label":"D","text":"16 times"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Wave energy and intensity follow amplitude squared: 2 up becomes 4 up. Option A treats energy as directly proportional to amplitude.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2020,26, $q$Which colour of visible light has the longest wavelength?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"violet"},{"label":"B","text":"green"},{"label":"C","text":"yellow"},{"label":"D","text":"red"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Red stretches longest across the visible band; violet packs in the shortest. Order the spectrum once and every colour question answers itself.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','light-optics',2020,27, $q$A coin resting on the bottom of a water tank appears closer to the surface than it really is because light$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bends away from the normal as it leaves the water"},{"label":"B","text":"bends toward the normal as it leaves the water"},{"label":"C","text":"is absorbed and re-emitted on the way up"},{"label":"D","text":"is polarised by the water surface"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$On exit the ray speeds up and bends away from the normal; the eye back-projects along that final straight line, lifting the image. The depth ratio is exactly the refractive index, n = real/apparent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2020,28, $q$An object 4 cm tall stands 60 cm from a converging lens of focal length 20 cm. The image formed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8 cm, upright"},{"label":"B","text":"2 cm, upright"},{"label":"C","text":"2 cm, inverted"},{"label":"D","text":"4 cm, inverted"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$1/v = 1/20 - 1/60 = 2/60, so v = 30 cm and magnification = -30/60 = -0.5: a 2 cm image, upside down. Option B keeps the size but loses the sign, and option A doubles it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2020,29, $q$Three identical lamps, each of resistance 4.0 ohms, are wired in series across a 12 V supply of negligible internal resistance. The current through each lamp is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.0 A"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"9.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 A"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Series resistance adds: 12 ohms, then I = 12/12 = 1 A. Option C is the all-parallel arrangement (12/(4/3) = 9 A).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2020,30, $q$Two 12-ohm resistors in parallel are connected across a 24 V battery of negligible internal resistance. The current drawn from the battery is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 A"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"8.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 A"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The pair reduce to 12/2 = 6 ohms, so the battery supplies 24/6 = 4 A in total, 2 A in each branch. Option C doubles the total as if the branches were separate circuits.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2020,31, $q$An electric iron of resistance 48 ohms operates on a 240 V supply. The electrical power it consumes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2400 W"},{"label":"B","text":"120 W"},{"label":"C","text":"7200 W"},{"label":"D","text":"1200 W"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Find the current or square first: I = 240/48 = 5 A, then P = IV = 1200 W (or P = V²/R directly). Option B drops one power of ten in the squaring.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Current drawn","kind":"math","content":"I = V/R = 240/48 = 5.0 A","note":"Ohm's law on the element itself."},{"title":"Power delivered","kind":"math","content":"P = IV = 5.0 × 240 = 1200 W","note":"Equivalently P = V²/R = 240²/48."},{"title":"Cross-check by squaring","kind":"math","content":"240² = 57600; 57600/48 = 1200 W","note":"Option B drops a factor of 10 in 57600/48."}]$st$,
 3),
('physics','electricity',2020,32, $q$A cell of e.m.f. 2.0 V drives a current of 0.5 A through a 3.0-ohm resistor. The terminal potential difference of the cell is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 V"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 V"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 V"},{"label":"D","text":"3.0 V"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The terminal p.d. is what the external resistor receives: 0.5 × 3 = 1.5 V. The missing 0.5 V (option C) is the internal drop of the cell.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)('physics','waves-sound',2020,33, $q$A tuning fork of frequency 500 Hz sounds first resonance over a tube when the air column is 15.5 cm long above a well. Taking the end correction as 1.5 cm, the speed of sound implied by the data is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"310 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"272 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"340 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"680 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Correct the column length before any wavelength appears.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Effective air column","kind":"math","content":"L + e = 0.155 + 0.015 = 0.17 m","note":"The antinode sits a little beyond the open end."},{"title":"Wavelength of the resonance","kind":"math","content":"wavelength = 4(L + e) = 4 × 0.17 = 0.68 m","note":"First resonance hosts a quarter wavelength."},{"title":"Speed of sound","kind":"math","content":"v = f × wavelength = 500 × 0.68 = 340 m/s","note":"Option A comes from skipping the end correction."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','magnetism',2020,34, $q$A transformer with 500 turns on its primary is fed 240 V from the mains and delivers 24 V. The number of turns on its secondary coil is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 turns"},{"label":"B","text":"500 turns"},{"label":"C","text":"5000 turns"},{"label":"D","text":"50 turns"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Ns = Np × Vs/Vp = 500 × 24/240 = 50 turns: a 10:1 step-down. Options A and C trip over the decimal in that ratio.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2020,35, $q$A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 5 years. Of an initial 64 g, the mass remaining after 15 years is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 g"},{"label":"B","text":"4 g"},{"label":"C","text":"8 g"},{"label":"D","text":"16 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$15 years is 3 half-lives: 64 g goes to 32, then 16, then 8 g. Count halvings, not years.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2020,36, $q$The minimum frequency of light that can eject electrons from a given metal surface is called the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"work function"},{"label":"B","text":"threshold frequency"},{"label":"C","text":"resonant frequency"},{"label":"D","text":"natural frequency"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Threshold frequency is the gate between no emission and emission; work function is the same idea expressed in energy (hf at threshold). Below it, intensity alone cannot free any electron.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2020,37, $q$A book cover looks blue in white daylight because it$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"absorbs the red and green components of white light"},{"label":"B","text":"absorbs the blue component of white light"},{"label":"C","text":"reflects all colours of light equally"},{"label":"D","text":"emits blue light of its own"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The cover subtracts: red and green are soaked up, blue is reflected to the eye. Pigments work by absorption, the opposite logic from mixing coloured lights.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2020,38, $q$The image formed on the retina of a normal human eye is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"virtual, upright and the same size"},{"label":"B","text":"real, magnified and inverted"},{"label":"C","text":"real, inverted and diminished"},{"label":"D","text":"virtual, diminished and upright"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The eye lens converges light from beyond 2f onto the retina, so the image is real, small and inverted - the brain flips it upright.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
);
-- 2021 (qnum 10-35)
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2021,10, $q$A vernier caliper has a main scale in millimetres and a vernier of 10 divisions spanning 9 mm. Its least count is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"0.01 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.9 mm"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Least count = smallest main division / number of vernier divisions = 1/10 = 0.1 mm. Option C is a micrometer's least count, not a vernier's.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2021,11, $q$The most suitable instrument for measuring the thickness of a single sheet of paper is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a metre rule"},{"label":"B","text":"vernier calipers"},{"label":"C","text":"a micrometer screw gauge"},{"label":"D","text":"a tape measure"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Fractions of a millimetre demand 0.01 mm resolution: only the micrometer supplies it. Vernier (0.1 mm) and rule or tape are orders too coarse.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','kinematics',2021,12, $q$A bus travelling at 24 m/s brakes uniformly to rest in 8 s. The distance it covers while braking is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"48 m"},{"label":"B","text":"192 m"},{"label":"C","text":"60 m"},{"label":"D","text":"96 m"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$s = (u + v)t/2 = 12 × 8 = 96 m using the average speed 12 m/s. Option A halves the distance as if a = u/2 had been assumed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2021,13, $q$A 2 kg trolley moving at 3 m/s couples with a stationary 3 kg trolley. Their common speed afterwards is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.6 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.2 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"3.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Momentum 2 × 3 = 6 kg m/s now rides on 5 kg: v = 1.2 m/s. Option C is the naive mean of the two speeds.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2021,14, $q$A passenger of mass 60 kg stands on a scale in a lift that reads 480 N. The lift is accelerating (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 m/s² upward"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 m/s² upward"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 m/s² downward"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 m/s² downward"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The true weight is 600 N, so the reading fell by 120 N: m(g - a) = 480 gives a = 2 m/s² downward. An upward acceleration would have lifted the reading to 720 N.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2021,15, $q$A rocket ejects burnt gas at a steady speed of 100 m/s. To maintain a thrust of 50 N, the mass of gas it must eject per second is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 kg/s"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 kg/s"},{"label":"C","text":"0.05 kg/s"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 kg/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Thrust = (mass per second) × ejecta speed, so 50/100 = 0.5 kg/s. Option C inverts the ratio by a factor of 10.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2021,16, $q$A car travels round an unbanked horizontal bend of radius 50 m at 15 m/s. The minimum coefficient of friction between tyres and road that will prevent skidding is (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25"},{"label":"B","text":"0.35"},{"label":"C","text":"0.60"},{"label":"D","text":"0.45"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Friction must supply mv²/r: μ mg = mv²/r gives μ = 225/(50 × 10) = 0.45. Option A comes from dividing by 900 instead of 500.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','work-energy',2021,17, $q$A steady force compresses a spring until the compression is 0.20 m and the force then reads 50 N. The work done on the spring during the compression is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 J"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 J"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 J"},{"label":"D","text":"25 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The force builds from 0 to 50 N across the 0.20 m, so the average is 25 N: work = 25 × 0.20 = 5 J (half of F x). Option A treats the peak force as constant throughout.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','work-energy',2021,18, $q$A pump raises 2000 kg of water through a height of 15 m every minute. Its useful power output is (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"300 W"},{"label":"B","text":"500 W"},{"label":"C","text":"3000 W"},{"label":"D","text":"5000 W"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Work each minute = 2000 × 10 × 15 = 300000 J; divide by 60 s to get 5000 W. Option B divides by 600 s.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2021,19, $q$A crane draws 50 kJ of electrical energy while raising a 5000 N load through 8 m. The efficiency of the crane is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"80%"},{"label":"B","text":"100%"},{"label":"C","text":"125%"},{"label":"D","text":"64%"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Output is 5000 × 8 = 40000 J of the 50000 J drawn: 80%. No machine reaches 100%, which is what rules out option B.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2021,20, $q$A block of relative density 2.5 weighs 15 N in air. When fully immersed in a liquid of relative density 0.8, its apparent weight is (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12 N"},{"label":"B","text":"7.5 N"},{"label":"C","text":"10.2 N"},{"label":"D","text":"11 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Compare what equal volumes of solid and liquid weigh, then subtract.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Upthrust as a fraction of weight","kind":"math","content":"upthrust/weight = 0.8/2.5 = 0.32","note":"For the same volume, weights scale with relative densities."},{"title":"Upthrust in newtons","kind":"math","content":"upthrust = 0.32 × 15 = 4.8 N","note":"This is the loss of weight while submerged."},{"title":"Apparent weight","kind":"math","content":"15 - 4.8 = 10.2 N","note":"Option B just halves the weight; the actual loss is 32%."}]$st$,
 3),
('physics','fluids-heat',2021,21, $q$A gas occupies 2.0 dm³ at a pressure of 4.0 × 10^5 Pa. At constant temperature, its volume at a pressure of 8.0 × 10^5 Pa is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"1.5 dm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Boyle's law: doubling the pressure halves the volume, giving 1.0 dm³. Option A runs the relation backwards.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2021,22, $q$A thermometer whose ice point sits at the 2°C mark and whose steam point sits at the 98°C mark reads 74°C in a bath. The true temperature of the bath is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"75°C"},{"label":"B","text":"70°C"},{"label":"C","text":"80°C"},{"label":"D","text":"72°C"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The reading spans (74 - 2)/96 = 0.75 of the distance between the fixed points, so the true temperature is 0.75 × 100 = 75°C. Always scale by the span, then map.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','waves-sound',2021,23, $q$The tension in a stretched wire of fixed length is quadrupled. Its fundamental frequency$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"falls to a quarter"},{"label":"B","text":"is unchanged"},{"label":"C","text":"quadruples"},{"label":"D","text":"doubles"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Frequency goes as the square root of tension, so a 4-fold tension doubles f. Option C forgets the square root and rides the tension directly.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2021,24, $q$A man stands 170 m from a cliff face and claps his hands. About how long after the clap does he hear the echo? (Speed of sound = 340 m/s)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 s"},{"label":"B","text":"0.5 s"},{"label":"C","text":"1.0 s"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The sound must travel 340 m there and back, which takes 1.0 s. Option B is the one-way walking time of the wave only.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2021,25, $q$Sound travels faster in warm air than in cold air mainly because in warm air$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the air becomes less dense"},{"label":"B","text":"air molecules move faster and pass the vibration on sooner"},{"label":"C","text":"the sound source vibrates harder"},{"label":"D","text":"the wavelength of every sound shortens"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sound advances molecule to molecule through collisions; hotter molecules jostle faster, so the disturbance rides along sooner. Density by itself runs the wrong way - lighter air alone would slow it at fixed temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','light-optics',2021,26, $q$An object 24 cm from a converging lens forms a real image 48 cm away on the far side. The focal length of the lens is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"16 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"24 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"32 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"12 cm"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$1/f = 1/u + 1/v = 1/24 + 1/48 = 1/16, so f = 16 cm. Option D is merely half of u; adding the reciprocals, not the distances, sets the focus.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2021,27, $q$A boy walks toward a plane mirror at a steady 1.0 m/s. The speed at which his image approaches him is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The image closes in from the far side at the same 1.0 m/s he advances, so the separation shrinks at 2 × 1.0 m/s. Every planar-mirror separation question carries this factor of 2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2021,28, $q$Two cells, each of e.m.f. 2.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm, are connected in series with a 3.0-ohm resistor. The current in the circuit is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 A"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 A"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Series cells add both their e.m.f.s and their internal resistances before Ohm's law runs.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Add the e.m.f.s","kind":"math","content":"E total = 2.0 + 2.0 = 4.0 V","note":"Internal resistances add the same way: 0.5 + 0.5 = 1.0 ohm."},{"title":"Whole loop resistance","kind":"math","content":"R total = 3.0 + 1.0 = 4.0 ohms","note":"Option D divides the e.m.f. by the external resistor alone."},{"title":"Current","kind":"math","content":"I = 4.0/4.0 = 1.0 A","note":"Check: 1 A × 3.0 = 3.0 V across the resistor, 1 V lost inside."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','electricity',2021,29, $q$The resistivity of copper is 1.7 × 10^-8 ohm metre. A copper wire is 2.0 m long with cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10^-7 m². Its resistance is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.034 ohm"},{"label":"B","text":"3.4 ohms"},{"label":"C","text":"0.34 ohm"},{"label":"D","text":"34 ohms"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$R = resistivity × length / area = (1.7 × 10^-8 × 2)/(1 × 10^-7) = 0.34 ohm. Shifting one power of ten in the area shifts the answer tenfold.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2021,30, $q$Resistors of 4 ohms and 12 ohms are connected in parallel across a 24 V battery of negligible internal resistance. The current through the 4-ohm resistor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 A"},{"label":"B","text":"8.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"12.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 A"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In parallel each branch sees the full 24 V: I = 24/4 = 6 A (and only 2 A through the 12-ohm). Option B quotes the total, not the branch.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2021,31, $q$In Fleming's left-hand rule for the force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field, the first finger points along$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the magnetic field"},{"label":"B","text":"the current"},{"label":"C","text":"the conductor's motion"},{"label":"D","text":"the force itself"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$First finger = field, thumb = force (motion), middle finger = current, for the left hand only. The right hand is reserved for induction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2021,32, $q$A metal of work function 3.5 eV is illuminated by photons each carrying 6.0 eV. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9.5 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"2.5 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"3.5 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 eV"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$K.E.max = 6.0 - 3.5 = 2.5 eV; the surface keeps its share. Option A adds the two energies, which would have the metal donating power to the electron.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)('physics','modern-physics',2021,33, $q$During one half-life, the count rate (activity) of a radioactive source falls to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a quarter of its initial value"},{"label":"B","text":"zero"},{"label":"C","text":"half its initial value"},{"label":"D","text":"about 37% of its initial value"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each half-life halves the surviving nuclei, so the count rate halves too. The 37% figure belongs to the exponential's mean lifetime, sometimes quoted but not asked here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2021,34, $q$A train covers the first 10 km of a trip at 40 km/h and the next 10 km at 60 km/h. Its average speed for the whole trip is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40 km/h"},{"label":"B","text":"50 km/h"},{"label":"C","text":"44 km/h"},{"label":"D","text":"48 km/h"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Times: 0.25 h then 0.167 h, total 20 km in 0.4167 h, giving 48 km/h. Option B is the simple mean of the speeds, wasted because the slow leg costs more time.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','dynamics',2021,35, $q$A 0.5 kg ball strikes a wall head-on at 8.0 m/s and rebounds along its original line at 2.0 m/s. The impulse the wall receives from the ball is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.0 N s"},{"label":"B","text":"3.0 N s"},{"label":"C","text":"4.0 N s"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 N s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The momentum reverses, so the change is 0.5 × (8 + 2) = 5.0 N s. Option B quietly treats the rebound as +2 m/s in the same sense.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
);
-- 2022 (qnum 10-36)
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2022,10, $q$A vernier caliper shows a main-scale reading of 5.7 cm while the 4th vernier division coincides with a main-scale mark. Taking the vernier least count as 0.01 cm, the reading is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.78 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"5.74 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"5.70 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"5.84 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Reading = 5.7 + 4 × 0.01 = 5.74 cm. Option C drops the vernier contribution entirely, which is what the coinciding mark is there to supply.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2022,11, $q$A vernier has 25 divisions equal in length to 24 main-scale millimetres. Its least count is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"0.96 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"0.02 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.04 mm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$One vernier division spans 24/25 mm, so least count = 1 - 24/25 = 0.04 mm. Option B is merely the length of a single division.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','kinematics',2022,12, $q$A ball is kicked from level ground at 10 m/s at 30° for the horizontal (g = 10 m/s²; sin 30° = 0.5, cos 30° ≈ 0.87). Its maximum height of flight is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.31 m"},{"label":"B","text":"2.5 m"},{"label":"C","text":"1.25 m"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The vertical component alone decides the height.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Resolve the launch","kind":"math","content":"uy = 10 × sin 30° = 5.0 m/s","note":"The horizontal 8.7 m/s never enters the height."},{"title":"Apex condition","kind":"math","content":"At the top vy = 0, so H = uy²/2g = 25/20 = 1.25 m","note":"Option B uses g = 10 without squaring properly (25/10)."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','kinematics',2022,13, $q$A body falls from rest. The distance it covers during the 3rd second of its fall is (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"25 m"},{"label":"B","text":"20 m"},{"label":"C","text":"45 m"},{"label":"D","text":"30 m"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$By 2 s the fall is 20 m and by 3 s it is 45 m; the 3rd second alone spans 45 - 20 = 25 m. Option C states the 3-second total, not the increment.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2022,14, $q$A car speeds up from 10 m/s to 30 m/s while covering 200 m. Its acceleration is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 m/s²"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$v² - u² = 2as: 900 - 100 = 400 = 2 × 200 × a, so a = 2.0 m/s². Option A drops the factor 2 and solves for a with v² only.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2022,15, $q$A block slides from rest down a frictionless plane inclined at 30° for the horizontal. Its acceleration is (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"8.7 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"2.5 m/s²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$a = g sin 30° = 5 m/s²; only the along-plane component of g acts. Option C uses cos 30°, which belongs to the normal force's balance.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2022,16, $q$A 20 g bullet moving horizontally at 200 m/s embeds itself in a 1.98 kg block of wood hanging at rest. Their common velocity just after impact is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Momentum 0.020 × 200 = 4.0 kg m/s is shared by the combined 2.00 kg mass: v = 2.0 m/s. Option A keeps the bullet's own speed for the pair.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2022,17, $q$A body of mass 5 kg moving at 4 m/s is acted on by a 10 N force in its direction of motion for 3 s. Its final speed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"6.0 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"8.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"16 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$a = F/m = 2 m/s², so the speed gains 2 × 3 = 6 m/s: v = 10 m/s. Option D belongs to a 6-second push.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2022,18, $q$A car engine develops 20 kW while the car holds a steady 25 m/s on the level. The total resistive force acting on the car is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"500 N"},{"label":"B","text":"1000 N"},{"label":"C","text":"800 N"},{"label":"D","text":"1600 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At steady speed the driving force matches the drag: F = P/v = 20000/25 = 800 N. Option D belongs to a speed of 12.5 m/s in place of 25.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2022,19, $q$A pile driver of weight 2000 N falls freely through 3.0 m onto a stake. The work done on the stake by the driver (all its energy delivered) is (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2000 J"},{"label":"B","text":"600 J"},{"label":"C","text":"6000 J"},{"label":"D","text":"9000 J"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$W = weight × fall distance = 2000 × 3 = 6000 J. Option B divides the height by 10 as if converting units.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2022,20, $q$For a given liquid, the height of capillary rise is inversely proportional to the tube's radius. A 1.0 mm bore tube shows a rise of 3.0 cm; the rise in a 0.5 mm bore tube of the same liquid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.0 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"6.0 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"12 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Halving the radius doubles the rise: 6.0 cm. Option D would belong to a quarter-millimetre bore.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2022,21, $q$A fixed mass of gas is kept at constant volume. Its pressure is 1.0 × 10^5 Pa at 27°C; at 327°C the pressure is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.2 × 10^5 Pa"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 × 10^5 Pa"},{"label":"C","text":"2.6 × 10^5 Pa"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 × 10^5 Pa"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Pressure tracks kelvin temperature: 600 K over 300 K doubles it to 2.0 × 10^5 Pa. Option B runs the ratio the wrong way, as if pressure fell with temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2022,22, $q$A heater supplies 8500 J to a 0.50 kg metal block and its temperature rises by 40°C. The specific heat capacity of the metal is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"425 J/(kg °C)"},{"label":"B","text":"850 J/(kg °C)"},{"label":"C","text":"212.5 J/(kg °C)"},{"label":"D","text":"170 J/(kg °C)"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Both the mass and the temperature rise sit in the denominator, so divide after listing:$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"List the data","kind":"math","content":"Q = 8500 J, m = 0.50 kg, rise = 40°C","note":"The mass and the rise both sit in the denominator."},{"title":"Solve for c","kind":"math","content":"c = Q/(m × rise) = 8500/(0.50 × 40) = 425 J/(kg °C)","note":"Option C halves the mass silently."}]$st$,
 3),
('physics','waves-sound',2022,23, $q$Waves with crests 0.50 m apart travel at 2.0 m/s through a medium. The number of complete waves passing a fixed point in 4 s is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"32"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$f = v/wavelength = 2.0/0.50 = 4 Hz, so 4 s carries 16 full waves. Option A counts only 1 second's worth.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2022,24, $q$Two sources sounding 440 Hz and 444 Hz together are heard for 5 s. The total number of beats heard in that time is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"20"},{"label":"C","text":"25"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The beat rate is the 4 Hz difference; sustained for 5 s that is 20 beats. Option A is the rate, not the count.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2022,25, $q$A stretched string fixed at both ends vibrates in its third harmonic. Counting the fixed ends, the total number of nodes on the string is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"5"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The third harmonic packs 3 loops between the ends, giving 4 nodes altogether. Option B counts only the interior ones and forgets the fixed ends.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2022,26, $q$An object stands 60 cm from a converging lens of focal length 20 cm. The image distance is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"30 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"60 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"15 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"45 cm"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$1/v = 1/20 - 1/60 = 2/60, so v = 30 cm; the real image ends up half-size and inverted. Option B puts the image where the object is - work in reciprocals, not distances.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2022,27, $q$Blue light is added to a patch of light that is already yellow (red + green). The resulting patch appears$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"green"},{"label":"B","text":"magenta"},{"label":"C","text":"white"},{"label":"D","text":"orange"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Yellow already carries red and green, so blue completes the trio of additive primaries and the patch turns white. Complementary pairs always add to white, never to a third colour.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2022,28, $q$A capacitor of capacitance 2 × 10^-4 F is charged to 100 V. The energy it stores is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 J"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 J"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 J"},{"label":"D","text":"20 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$E = CV²/2 = (2 × 10^-4 × 10^4)/2 = 1.0 J. Option D shows what forgetting the factor 1/2 costs, and option C halves the already-halved value.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2022,29, $q$When a 9.0 V battery drives a current of 1.5 A through a coil, the resistance of the coil is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.0 ohms"},{"label":"B","text":"13.5 ohms"},{"label":"C","text":"0.17 ohm"},{"label":"D","text":"10.5 ohms"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$R = V/I = 9.0/1.5 = 6.0 ohms. Option B multiplies instead of divides; option C inverts the ratio upside down.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','electricity',2022,30, $q$A circuit on a 240 V mains is protected by a 5.0 A fuse. The maximum power the circuit may safely draw is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"48 W"},{"label":"B","text":"200 W"},{"label":"C","text":"2400 W"},{"label":"D","text":"1200 W"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Work from the fuse limit.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Fuse sets the current ceiling","kind":"math","content":"I max = 5.0 A before the fuse blows","note":"The rating reads as an ampere limit, not a watt limit."},{"title":"Power at that current","kind":"math","content":"P = IV = 5.0 × 240 = 1200 W","note":"Option A divides the other way; option C doubles the share."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','magnetism',2022,31, $q$A transformer steps 12 V up to 120 V. If its secondary coil has 1000 turns, the number of turns on its primary is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 turns"},{"label":"B","text":"100 turns"},{"label":"C","text":"10000 turns"},{"label":"D","text":"50 turns"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Np = Ns × Vp/Vs = 1000 × 12/120 = 100 turns: a 1:10 step-up. Option C runs the ratio as if the secondary drove the primary.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2022,32, $q$For light above the threshold frequency, increasing the light's intensity increases$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the number of electrons emitted per second"},{"label":"B","text":"the maximum kinetic energy of each emitted electron"},{"label":"C","text":"the threshold frequency of the metal"},{"label":"D","text":"the work function of the surface"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Intensity sets the photon arrival rate, so the emission rate - the current - climbs. Each photon still carries the same energy, so K.E.max and the metal's fixed properties stay put.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)('physics','modern-physics',2022,33, $q$How much energy does the complete conversion of 1.0 g of matter into radiation release? (Take c = 3.0 × 10^8 m/s)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3 × 10^13 J"},{"label":"B","text":"9 × 10^16 J"},{"label":"C","text":"9 × 10^13 J"},{"label":"D","text":"3 × 10^10 J"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Convert grams to kilograms first: E = mc² = 0.001 × 9 × 10^16 = 9 × 10^13 J. Option B runs the arithmetic using grams as kilograms - the 10^-3 mass step is the whole point.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','modern-physics',2022,34, $q$The mass number (nucleon number) of an atom equals$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the number of protons only"},{"label":"B","text":"the number of neutrons only"},{"label":"C","text":"the sum of the protons and neutrons"},{"label":"D","text":"the number of orbital electrons"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A counts the nucleons: Z protons plus N neutrons. It drops in alpha decay but survives beta decay untouched.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('physics','modern-physics',2022,35, $q$A machine of velocity ratio 5 is 80% efficient. Its mechanical advantage is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.8"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0"},{"label":"C","text":"6.25"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Efficiency = MA/VR, so MA = 0.8 × 5 = 4.0. Option C inverts the relation and exceeds the velocity ratio, which friction can never permit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2022,36, $q$A train starting from rest accelerates uniformly and covers 400 m in 20 s. Its velocity at the end of the 20 s is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"80 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"40 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"20 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"16 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$s = (u + v)t/2 with u = 0 gives 400 = 10v, so v = 40 m/s. Option A mistakes the 40 m/s average for the final speed and doubles it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

















insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2000,10, $q$The separation of crude petroleum into petrol, kerosene and diesel fractions is done by fractional distillation because the components of crude oil differ in their$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"boiling points"},{"label":"B","text":"densities"},{"label":"C","text":"solubilities in water"},{"label":"D","text":"melting points"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Fractional distillation separates a liquid mixture by gradual vaporization and condensation at successively higher temperatures. It is therefore the difference in boiling points that drives the separation.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2000,11, $q$How many neutrons are present in an atom of the isotope ²³Na of sodium (atomic number 11)?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"23"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"11"},{"label":"D","text":"10"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Number of neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 23 − 11 = 10. The proton count (11) fixes the element; the rest of the mass is neutrons.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2000,12, $q$The mass of 0.25 mol of sodium hydroxide (NaOH = 40 g mol⁻¹) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 g"},{"label":"B","text":"10 g"},{"label":"C","text":"20 g"},{"label":"D","text":"2.5 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mass relates to moles through m = n × M. Here m = 0.25 × 40 = 10 g, which is a quarter of the molar mass.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Relate mass to moles","kind":"math","content":"n = m/M, so m = n × M","note":"rearrange the mole equation"},{"title":"Multiply","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.25 × 40 = 10 g","note":"M(NaOH) = 40 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Check","kind":"text","content":"A quarter of 40 g is 10 g","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2000,13, $q$An aqueous solution of sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) is basic because the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium ion reacts with water to give hydrogen ions"},{"label":"B","text":"trioxocarbonate(IV) ion is a spectator ion in solution"},{"label":"C","text":"solution is saturated with the salt"},{"label":"D","text":"trioxocarbonate(IV) ion hydrolyses, producing hydroxide ions"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The CO₃²⁻ ion accepts a proton from water: CO₃²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻, so the hydroxide ion concentration rises. Hydrolysis of the anion of a weak acid always makes the salt solution basic.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2000,14, $q$The oxidation number of sulphur in tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H₂SO₄, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+4"},{"label":"B","text":"+6"},{"label":"C","text":"+2"},{"label":"D","text":"−2"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Applying the zero-sum rule: 2(+1) + x + 4(−2) = 0, so x = +6. The (VI) in the systematic name also advertises the +6 state of sulphur.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2000,15, $q$The atomic radii of the elements decrease from sodium to chlorine across the third period because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the nuclear charge increases while electrons enter the same outermost shell"},{"label":"B","text":"extra electron shells are successively being added"},{"label":"C","text":"the metallic character of the elements increases"},{"label":"D","text":"screening of the nucleus by inner electrons increases sharply"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Across a period, protons are added to the nucleus while electrons go into the same outer shell. The rising effective nuclear charge pulls that shell inward, shrinking the atom.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2000,16, $q$Which statement about a catalyst is correct?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"It increases the yield of products at equilibrium"},{"label":"B","text":"It lowers the activation energy of the reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"It shifts the position of equilibrium to the right"},{"label":"D","text":"It is permanently used up during the reaction"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A catalyst provides an alternative reaction path of lower activation energy. It changes rate only — not yield, not the equilibrium position — and it is recovered unchanged at the end.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2000,17, $q$In the electrolytic extraction of aluminium from purified alumina, cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) is added mainly to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"act as a reducing agent for the aluminium ions"},{"label":"B","text":"prevent the burning away of the carbon anodes"},{"label":"C","text":"lower the melting point and improve the conductivity of the electrolyte"},{"label":"D","text":"remove iron impurities as red mud"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Pure alumina melts at about 2050 °C, but dissolved in molten cryolite the electrolyte operates near 950 °C. This saves enormous energy and keeps the melt conducting.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2000,18, $q$Ethanol warmed with excess concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid at about 140 °C gives mainly$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethene"},{"label":"B","text":"ethoxyethane"},{"label":"C","text":"ethyl hydrogen tetraoxosulphate(VI)"},{"label":"D","text":"ethane"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$At about 140 °C two ethanol molecules condense, losing water to give the ether C₂H₅OC₂H₅. At about 170 °C the same reagent dehydrates ethanol further to ethene instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2000,19, $q$A white solid X turns directly to vapour when warmed and recondenses as the same solid on a cold surface, leaving sodium chloride behind. The suitable separation technique is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"crystallization"},{"label":"B","text":"simple distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"decantation"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A solid that changes from solid to vapour and back without melting can be separated from a non-volatile one by sublimation, e.g. ammonium chloride from sodium chloride.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2000,20, $q$An atom has the electron configuration 2, 8, 8, 2. The element is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"magnesium"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium"},{"label":"C","text":"strontium"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The configuration totals 20 electrons, so Z = 20: calcium. Note that 2, 8, 2 is magnesium and 2, 8, 8, 1 is potassium.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2000,21, $q$A compound has the empirical formula CH₂O and a relative molecular mass of 180. Its molecular formula is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"C₆H₁₂O₆"},{"label":"B","text":"C₃H₆O₃"},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₄O₂"},{"label":"D","text":"C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n = 180 ÷ 30 = 6, so the molecular formula is the empirical formula multiplied by 6: C₆H₁₂O₆. Glucose illustrates exactly this result.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2000,22, $q$The colour of phenolphthalein in sodium hydroxide solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"yellow"},{"label":"B","text":"colourless"},{"label":"C","text":"red"},{"label":"D","text":"pink"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Phenolphthalein is colourless below about pH 8.3 and pink in alkaline solution. Yellow in alkali would point instead to methyl orange.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2000,23, $q$Which of these compounds is covalent even though it contains a metal and a non-metal?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"aluminium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium oxide"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The small, highly charged Al³⁺ polarizes the chloride electron cloud so strongly that the bonding becomes essentially covalent. The other pairs of ions form ordinary ionic lattices.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2000,24, $q$In a galvanic cell consisting of a copper rod in copper(II) sulphate solution joined by a salt bridge to a zinc rod in zinc sulphate solution, electrons flow through the external circuit from$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc to copper"},{"label":"B","text":"copper to zinc"},{"label":"C","text":"the salt bridge to the copper electrode"},{"label":"D","text":"copper to the salt bridge"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Zinc is the more reactive metal, so it is the negative electrode being oxidized: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. Electrons therefore travel the wire from zinc to copper, where Cu²⁺ is reduced to Cu.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2000,25, $q$How many structural isomers does butane, C₄H₁₀, have?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Only two: n-butane and 2-methylpropane. Three would be the answer for pentane, C₅H₁₂, not for butane.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2000,26, $q$Iron rusts only in the presence of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide alone"},{"label":"B","text":"oil and grease"},{"label":"C","text":"water only"},{"label":"D","text":"oxygen and moisture"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Rusting is the formation of hydrated iron(III) oxide, which needs both air (oxygen) and water. Electrolytes such as salt water only speed the process up; they cannot cause it alone.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2000,27, $q$Which mass of oxygen gas, O₂, contains the same number of molecules as 2.0 g of hydrogen gas, H₂?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"16.0 g"},{"label":"B","text":"32.0 g"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"8.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Equal numbers of molecules mean equal numbers of moles. The hydrogen sample is exactly 1 mol, so 1 mol of oxygen, i.e. 32 g, contains the same number of molecules.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles of hydrogen","kind":"math","content":"n(H₂) = 2.0 ÷ 2.0 = 1.0 mol","note":"M(H₂) = 2 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Same molecules, same moles","kind":"text","content":"Equal molecule counts require equal moles: n(O₂) = 1.0 mol","note":"Avogadro principle"},{"title":"Mass of oxygen","kind":"math","content":"m(O₂) = 1.0 × 32 = 32 g","note":"M(O₂) = 32 g mol⁻¹"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2000,28, $q$For the equilibrium N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH is negative. Increasing the pressure on this system at constant temperature shifts the equilibrium position to the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"right, giving more ammonia"},{"label":"B","text":"left, giving more nitrogen and hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"no direction; the position is unchanged"},{"label":"D","text":"right only if the temperature is also lowered"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Four moles of gas on the left face only two on the right. Raising the pressure pushes the equilibrium toward the side with fewer gas molecules, which is the ammonia side.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2000,29, $q$Which of the following salts will give an acidic solution when dissolved in water?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium ethanoate"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium trioxocarbonate(IV)"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$NH₄Cl comes from a weak base and a strong acid, so the NH₄⁺ ion hydrolyses to release H₃O⁺. Salts of strong bases with weak acids (ethanoate, trioxocarbonate(IV)) are basic, and NaCl is neutral.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2000,30, $q$The number of electrons in the M shell of an atom of iron (Z = 26) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"14"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Iron is 2, 8, 14, 2; the M (third) shell holds 3s²3p⁶3d⁶ = 14 electrons. Eight belongs to the L shell and two is just the valence count.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2000,31, $q$When carbon(IV) oxide is passed into lime water, the liquid first turns milky and the milkiness later clears because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the lime water becomes concentrated on standing"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) is removed by the heat of the reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"the reaction reverses completely to calcium hydroxide and water"},{"label":"D","text":"insoluble calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) forms first, then excess CO₂ converts it to soluble calcium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃↓ makes the suspension milky. With excess carbon(IV) oxide the carbonate dissolves: CaCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca(HCO₃)₂, which is soluble, so the milkiness clears.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2000,32, $q$How many isomeric alkanols are represented by the formula C₄H₁₀O?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"7"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The four alkanols are butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol, 2-methylpropan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol. Seven would count the three ethers with the same formula as well.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2000,33, $q$The quantity of electricity delivered by a current of 4 A flowing for 25 minutes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"100 C"},{"label":"B","text":"1500 C"},{"label":"C","text":"3600 C"},{"label":"D","text":"6000 C"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Charge is current multiplied by time in seconds. One ampere for one second is one coulomb, so the time must first be converted to seconds before multiplying.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Convert the time","kind":"math","content":"t = 25 min × 60 s = 1500 s","note":"charge uses time in seconds"},{"title":"Compute the charge","kind":"math","content":"Q = I × t = 4 × 1500 = 6000 C","note":"I = 4 A"},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"6000 C corresponds to about 0.062 mol of electrons","note":"6000 ÷ 96500 ≈ 0.062"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2000,34, $q$The boiling point of hydrogen fluoride is abnormally high compared with the other hydrogen halides because HF molecules$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"are the lightest of the series"},{"label":"B","text":"are non-polar"},{"label":"C","text":"are held together by hydrogen bonds in addition to dipole forces"},{"label":"D","text":"contain the strongest covalent bonds of the series"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The small, highly electronegative fluorine atom forms strong intermolecular H-bonds (F···H–F) that must be overcome on boiling. Covalent bonds inside the molecule are not broken during vaporization.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2000,35, $q$Engine oil mixed with water forms two separate layers. The best technique for separating them is to use$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a separating funnel"},{"label":"B","text":"filter paper"},{"label":"C","text":"a fractionating column"},{"label":"D","text":"a magnet"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Immiscible liquids of different densities form two layers. Opening the tap of a separating funnel lets the denser bottom layer run off first.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2000,36, $q$Which gas cannot be dried over concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonia"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Concentrated H₂SO₄ is an acidic drying agent, and ammonia is basic: the two react to form ammonium salts. Ammonia is dried with a basic agent such as soda-lime instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2000,37, $q$The number of molecules in 0.20 mol of nitrogen gas is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.02×10²³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.204×10²³"},{"label":"C","text":"6.02×10²²"},{"label":"D","text":"3.01×10²²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$N = n × Nₐ = 0.20 × 6.02×10²³ = 1.204×10²³ molecules. The Avogadro constant applies equally to atoms, molecules or ions of any substance.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2001,10, $q$Potassium trioxonitrate(V) and sodium chloride have markedly different solubilities in water. Which technique best separates a solid mixture of the two salts?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fractional crystallization"},{"label":"B","text":"simple distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"decantation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Dissolve the mixture in a minimum of hot water and cool slowly: the less soluble salt crystallizes first. Fractional crystallization exploits exactly such solubility differences.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2001,11, $q$Isotopes of an element have identical$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mass numbers"},{"label":"B","text":"numbers of protons"},{"label":"C","text":"numbers of neutrons"},{"label":"D","text":"numbers of protons and neutrons combined"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Isotopes share the same atomic number (proton count) but differ in neutron number and hence in mass number. Identical chemistry but different nuclei is the essence of isotopy.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2001,12, $q$The percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium trioxonitrate(V), NH₄NO₃, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"17.5%"},{"label":"B","text":"28.0%"},{"label":"C","text":"35.0%"},{"label":"D","text":"46.7%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The two nitrogens contribute 2 × 14 = 28 to a molar mass of 14 + 4 + 14 + 48 = 80. Hence (28/80) × 100 = 35.0%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2001,13, $q$The conjugate base of the hydrogen tetraoxosulphate(VI) ion, HSO₄⁻, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"H₂SO₄"},{"label":"B","text":"SO₄²⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"OH⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"H₃O⁺"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A conjugate base is what remains after an acid has lost one proton. Removing H⁺ from HSO₄⁻ leaves the tetraoxosulphate(VI) ion, SO₄²⁻.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2001,14, $q$Which statement concerning a chlorine atom and a chloride ion, Cl⁻, is correct?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Cl⁻ is smaller than Cl because it has lost a shell"},{"label":"B","text":"the two have exactly the same radius"},{"label":"C","text":"Cl⁻ is larger than Cl"},{"label":"D","text":"their sizes cannot be compared"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Gaining an electron adds repulsion within the same third shell while the nuclear charge is unchanged. The extra repulsion swells the anion beyond the neutral atom.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2001,15, $q$In the reaction MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺, the reducing agent is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Fe²⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"Fe³⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"MnO₄⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"H⁺"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Fe²⁺ loses an electron to become Fe³⁺, so it is the species being oxidized and hence the reducing agent. Meanwhile Mn goes from +7 in MnO₄⁻ to +2, so MnO₄⁻ is the oxidizing agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2001,16, $q$Marble chips, CaCO₃, react with dilute hydrochloric acid. Which change will NOT increase the rate of this reaction?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"raising the temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"increasing the concentration of the acid"},{"label":"C","text":"using larger marble chips"},{"label":"D","text":"grinding the marble to a fine powder"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Larger chips expose a smaller surface area, so fewer solid particles are available for collision. Temperature, concentration and powdered surface all raise the effective collision rate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2001,17, $q$Zinc oxide dissolves both in dilute hydrochloric acid and in aqueous sodium hydroxide. Zinc oxide is therefore classified as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a basic oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"an acidic oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"a neutral oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"an amphoteric oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Reacting with both acids and alkalis marks an oxide as amphoteric. Aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃, and lead(II) oxide behave in the same double-sided way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2001,18, $q$The enzymes (zymase) in yeast ferment glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆, to give mainly$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanoic acid and water"},{"label":"B","text":"methanol and carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanol and carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"glycerol and hydrogen"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Fermentation follows C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂ under anaerobic conditions. The zymase complex in yeast catalyzes this conversion to ethanol, not to ethanoic acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2001,19, $q$In a paper chromatography experiment, the Rf value of a dye is 0.35 and the solvent front moves 10.0 cm up the paper. The distance moved by the dye is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.35 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"3.5 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"35.0 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"10.4 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Rf = distance moved by the dye ÷ distance moved by the solvent front. Hence d = 0.35 × 10.0 = 3.5 cm.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2001,20, $q$The relative atomic mass of calcium is 40. The mass of one calcium atom is approximately$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.4×10²⁴ g"},{"label":"B","text":"6.6×10²³ g"},{"label":"C","text":"6.6×10⁻²³ g"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0×10⁻²² g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$m = M ÷ Nₐ = 40 ÷ (6.02×10²³) ≈ 6.6×10⁻²³ g. One mole of atoms weighs 40 g, so a single atom is that mass spread over 6.02×10²³ particles.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2001,21, $q$The mass of 2.24 dm³ of carbon(IV) oxide gas at STP is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.44 g"},{"label":"B","text":"4.4 g"},{"label":"C","text":"44 g"},{"label":"D","text":"8.8 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$At STP one mole of gas occupies 22.4 dm³, so the sample is 2.24/22.4 = 0.10 mol. Mass = 0.10 × 44 g mol⁻¹ = 4.4 g.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles from volume","kind":"math","content":"n = 2.24 ÷ 22.4 = 0.10 mol","note":"molar volume at STP = 22.4 dm³"},{"title":"Mass from moles","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.10 × 44 = 4.4 g","note":"M(CO₂) = 44 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"The sample weighs 4.4 g","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2001,22, $q$25.0 cm³ of 0.100 M hydrochloric acid required 20.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution for complete neutralization. The concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.125 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.080 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.100 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.250 M"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$HCl and NaOH react 1:1, so CaVa/CbVb = 1 and Cb = (0.100 × 25.0)/20.0 = 0.125 mol/dm³. Note that a smaller volume of base must be more concentrated.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Set the ratio","kind":"text","content":"For HCl + NaOH (1:1) at the end point, CaVa = CbVb","note":"equal mole bridges on both sides"},{"title":"Solve for Cb","kind":"math","content":"Cb = (0.100 × 25.0) ÷ 20.0 = 0.125 M","note":"Va = 25.0 cm³, Vb = 20.0 cm³"},{"title":"Sanity check","kind":"text","content":"Less base was needed, so the base must be more concentrated than the acid","note":"0.125 > 0.100 ✓"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2001,23, $q$Which element has the highest electronegativity?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"fluorine"},{"label":"C","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Electronegativity increases across a period and up a group. Fluorine, at the extreme top right of the periodic table, heads the list.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2001,24, $q$In potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII), KMnO₄, the oxidation number of manganese is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+2"},{"label":"B","text":"+4"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+6"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Potassium is +1 and each oxygen is −2, so +1 + x − 8 = 0 gives x = +7. The (VII) in the name confirms the manganese state.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2001,25, $q$For the equilibrium N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), the correct expression for the equilibrium constant Kc is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"[N₂][H₂]³ ÷ [NH₃]²"},{"label":"B","text":"2[NH₃] ÷ (3[N₂][H₂])"},{"label":"C","text":"[NH₃]² ÷ [N₂][H₂]"},{"label":"D","text":"[NH₃]² ÷ ([N₂][H₂]³)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Kc always places products over reactants, each concentration raised to its stoichiometric coefficient. For this reaction that is [NH₃]²/([N₂][H₂]³).$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2001,26, $q$When washing soda, Na₂CO₃, is added to permanently hard water containing calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI), the calcium ions are removed as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"calcium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium hydroxide"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"insoluble calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Na₂CO₃ reacts with Ca²⁺ to precipitate CaCO₃, leaving harmless sodium salts in solution. Adding slaked lime works this way for temporary hardness, but the carbonate method clears both types.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2001,27, $q$Warming butan-2-ol with acidified potassium tetraoxodichromate(VI) solution gives$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"butanone"},{"label":"B","text":"butanal"},{"label":"C","text":"butanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"but-2-ene"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Secondary alkanols are oxidized to alkanones: CH₃CHOHCH₂CH₃ → CH₃COCH₂CH₃. Aldehydic products would require a primary alkanol as the starting material.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2001,28, $q$Passing propan-2-ol vapour over heated aluminium oxide gives mainly$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propene"},{"label":"B","text":"propanone"},{"label":"C","text":"propanal"},{"label":"D","text":"propane"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Hot aluminium oxide (or concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid) dehydrates alkanols: water is eliminated to give the alkene CH₃–CH=CH₂. Propanone would require oxidation, not dehydration.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2001,29, $q$Ethanol (boiling point 78 °C) and water are completely miscible. The method that separates them is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"using a separating funnel"},{"label":"C","text":"evaporation"},{"label":"D","text":"decantation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Miscible liquids with sufficiently different boiling points are separated by fractional distillation. A separating funnel only works when the liquids form immiscible layers.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2001,30, $q$The number of unpaired electrons in a nitrogen atom (Z = 7) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Nitrogen is 1s²2s²2p³, and by Hund's rule the three 2p electrons occupy three separate orbitals singly with parallel spins. That leaves three unpaired electrons.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Write the configuration","kind":"text","content":"N: 1s² 2s² 2p³","note":"Z = 7 electrons in total"},{"title":"Apply Hund's rule","kind":"text","content":"The three 2p electrons enter three different 2p orbitals singly, with parallel spins","note":"this minimizes electron repulsion"},{"title":"Count unpaired electrons","kind":"text","content":"Three singly occupied orbitals give 3 unpaired electrons","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2001,31, $q$The pH of a 0.001 mol/dm³ solution of hydrochloric acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$HCl is a strong acid, so it ionizes completely and [H⁺] = 10⁻³ mol/dm³. Hence pH = −log(10⁻³) = 3.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2001,32, $q$Metals are good conductors of electricity in the solid state because their$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ions are free to move through the lattice"},{"label":"B","text":"protons drift under an applied potential difference"},{"label":"C","text":"covalent bonds let shared electron pairs migrate"},{"label":"D","text":"valence electrons are delocalized and mobile through the cation lattice"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Metallic bonding is a lattice of positive cations sitting in a sea of delocalized outer electrons. Only the electrons move when a potential is applied; the ions stay fixed, which is also why metals conduct but remain rigid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2001,33, $q$During the electrolysis of molten sodium chloride, the product formed at the cathode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine gas"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium metal"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen gas"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium hydroxide"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In the melt only Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are present. Na⁺ gains electrons at the cathode (reduction), while Cl⁻ loses electrons at the anode to give Cl₂ — no water means no hydrogen or hydroxide.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2001,34, $q$For an endothermic reaction at equilibrium, increasing the temperature shifts the equilibrium position$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"to the left, toward the reactants"},{"label":"B","text":"to the right, toward the products"},{"label":"C","text":"nowhere; the position is unchanged"},{"label":"D","text":"toward the side with fewer gas molecules"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Heat behaves as if it were a reactant in an endothermic process, so supplying more of it drives the forward direction. This is Le Chatelier's principle applied to temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2001,35, $q$In the blast furnace, the principal reaction that reduces haematite, Fe₂O₃, to iron involves$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(II) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The main reduction step is Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂. Carbon(II) oxide, generated from burning coke in the furnace, is the dominant reducing agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2001,36, $q$When bromoethane is warmed with aqueous sodium hydroxide, the main organic product is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"B","text":"ethene"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanal"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$This is a nucleophilic substitution (hydrolysis): the OH⁻ ion replaces the bromide to give C₂H₅OH, with NaBr formed alongside. Elimination would need hot ethanolic (not aqueous) alkali.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2001,37, $q$Which hydrocarbon rapidly decolorizes bromine water in the dark?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hexane"},{"label":"B","text":"cyclohexane"},{"label":"C","text":"benzene"},{"label":"D","text":"hex-1-ene"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A carbon–carbon double bond adds bromine across itself, destroying the colour at once. Hexane and cyclohexane are saturated, and benzene resists addition reactions under these mild conditions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2001,38, $q$A gas has a vapour density of 22 relative to hydrogen. Its relative molecular mass is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"11"},{"label":"B","text":"44"},{"label":"C","text":"22"},{"label":"D","text":"88"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Relative to hydrogen, vapour density is half the relative molecular mass: M = 2 × VD. Hence M = 2 × 22 = 44, the value for carbon(IV) oxide.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2002,10, $q$Chromatography separates the components of a dye mixture because the components$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"have different densities"},{"label":"B","text":"are adsorbed to different extents on the stationary phase as the solvent moves"},{"label":"C","text":"have different melting points"},{"label":"D","text":"are different colours"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each component partitions between a mobile solvent and a stationary (paper or alumina) phase. Different adsorption strengths mean different travel rates, so the spots separate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2002,11, $q$How many electrons are present in the ⁴⁰₂₀Ca²⁺ ion?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40"},{"label":"B","text":"22"},{"label":"C","text":"20"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Z = 20 gives 20 protons; the 2+ charge means two electrons have been removed, leaving 18. Ionization changes electrons only — the neutron count stays at 40 − 20 = 20.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2002,12, $q$The number of moles in 9.8 g of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H₂SO₄ (M = 98 g mol⁻¹), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9.8 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"0.20 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"0.98 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"0.10 mol"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$n = m/M = 9.8 ÷ 98 = 0.10 mol. Dividing a mass by ten times its molar mass must give one tenth of a mole, not nine moles.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2002,13, $q$An aqueous solution of ammonium chloride$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"turns blue litmus red"},{"label":"B","text":"is neutral to litmus"},{"label":"C","text":"turns red litmus blue"},{"label":"D","text":"has a pH of about 10"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$NH₄Cl is the salt of a weak base with a strong acid, so NH₄⁺ hydrolyses to give H₃O⁺. The solution is acidic, pH below 7, and turns blue litmus red.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2002,14, $q$Which statement explains why neon has a far higher first ionization energy than sodium?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an atom of neon contains more electrons than an atom of sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"ionizing neon would require breaking a covalent bond"},{"label":"C","text":"neon has a complete octet, whereas sodium readily parts with its single outer electron"},{"label":"D","text":"neon has the larger atomic radius of the two"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A closed-shell atom of neon holds its electrons with great effective nuclear pull on a compact shell. Sodium's lone 3s electron, shielded and far from the nucleus, is easily removed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2002,15, $q$In potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI), K₂Cr₂O₇, the oxidation number of chromium is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+3"},{"label":"B","text":"+6"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+2"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The charge balance is 2(+1) + 2x + 7(−2) = 0, giving x = +6. The (VI) in the name signals this chromium state, as it does in the acid itself.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2002,16, $q$The effect of adding a catalyst to a reaction already at equilibrium is that the catalyst$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shifts the position of equilibrium toward the products"},{"label":"B","text":"speeds the forward and reverse reactions equally, so the equilibrium position is unchanged"},{"label":"C","text":"speeds up only the forward reaction"},{"label":"D","text":"changes the value of the equilibrium constant"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A catalyst lowers the activation energy of both directions by the same path change. Equilibrium is reached sooner, but the composition of the mixture and the constant Kc are untouched.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2002,17, $q$A gas that decolorizes acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) solution and turns acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI) solution from orange to green is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen sulphide"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonia"},{"label":"D","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$SO₂ is a reducing gas: it converts Mn(VII) to colourless Mn(II) and Cr(VI) orange to Cr(III) green while being oxidized to the +6 state. Carbon(IV) oxide cannot reduce either reagent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2002,18, $q$Ethanol reacts with ethanoic acid in warm concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid. The organic product formed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethyl ethanoate"},{"label":"B","text":"methyl ethanoate"},{"label":"C","text":"butanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"ethyl propanoate"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Esterification joins the acid part to the alcohol part with loss of water: CH₃COOC₂H₅. The acid supplies the alkanoate portion and the ethanol supplies the alkyl group, hence ethyl ethanoate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2002,19, $q$Rock salt, a mixture of sodium chloride and sand, is to be purified. The first step is to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"add water to dissolve the salt, then filter"},{"label":"B","text":"heat the mixture strongly in a furnace"},{"label":"C","text":"subject the mixture to sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporate the mixture directly to dryness"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Dissolving exploits solubility: NaCl passes into solution while sand stays as an insoluble residue caught by the filter. The salt is then recovered by evaporation or crystallization.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2002,20, $q$Chlorine exists as ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl, and its relative atomic mass is 35.5. The abundance ratio of ³⁵Cl to ³⁷Cl is approximately$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 3"},{"label":"C","text":"3 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"2 : 3"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The average 35.5 lies a quarter of the way from 35 toward 37, so one part in four must be ³⁷Cl. That gives ³⁵Cl : ³⁷Cl = 3 : 1.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Set up the weighted mean","kind":"math","content":"(35a + 37b) ÷ (a + b) = 35.5","note":"a, b are the isotope counts"},{"title":"Solve the fraction","kind":"math","content":"b ÷ (a + b) = 0.25, so a ÷ b = 3","note":"one quarter of the atoms are ³⁷Cl"},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"³⁵Cl : ³⁷Cl = 3 : 1","note":"matches option C"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2002,21, $q$The mass of anhydrous sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) needed to prepare 500 cm³ of a 0.100 M solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10.6 g"},{"label":"B","text":"2.65 g"},{"label":"C","text":"5.30 g"},{"label":"D","text":"53.0 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Moles required = 0.100 × 0.500 = 0.050 mol; mass = 0.050 × 106 g mol⁻¹ = 5.30 g. Dissolve this and make the solution up to the 500 cm³ mark.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles needed","kind":"math","content":"n = C × V = 0.100 × 0.500 dm³ = 0.050 mol","note":"500 cm³ = 0.500 dm³"},{"title":"Convert to mass","kind":"math","content":"m = n × M = 0.050 × 106 = 5.30 g","note":"M(Na₂CO₃) = 106 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"Weigh 5.3 g and make up to the mark","note":"matches option C"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2002,22, $q$Tetraoxophosphate(V) acid, H₃PO₄, is classified as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"monobasic"},{"label":"B","text":"dibasic"},{"label":"C","text":"tribasic"},{"label":"D","text":"tetrabasic"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Basicity counts the replaceable hydrogen atoms per molecule, and H₃PO₄ carries three ionizable O–H hydrogens. It can therefore form three series of salts, ending in Na₃PO₄.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2002,23, $q$The shape of the water molecule is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"linear"},{"label":"B","text":"angular (V-shaped)"},{"label":"C","text":"trigonal planar"},{"label":"D","text":"pyramidal"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Oxygen carries two bond pairs and two lone pairs of electrons. The lone pairs compress the tetrahedral arrangement to a bent shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2002,24, $q$Which of the following reactions is an example of disproportionation?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"},{"label":"B","text":"NH₃ + HCl → NH₄Cl"},{"label":"C","text":"CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂"},{"label":"D","text":"2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In H₂O₂ the oxygen is at −1; on decomposition it rises to 0 in O₂ (oxidized) and falls to −2 in H₂O (reduced) simultaneously. One element playing both roles is the hallmark of disproportionation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2002,25, $q$The synthesis of ammonia, N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, is exothermic. Raising the temperature at constant pressure shifts the equilibrium position$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"to the right, favoring ammonia"},{"label":"B","text":"to the left, favoring nitrogen and hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"nowhere; the position is unchanged"},{"label":"D","text":"to the right only if a catalyst is present"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In an exothermic synthesis heat acts as a product, so added heat drives the reverse direction. The 450 °C used industrially is a compromise between rate and yield.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2002,26, $q$Aluminium is extracted electrolytically rather than by heating its oxide with coke, because aluminium is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"less dense than iron"},{"label":"B","text":"obtained from an abundant ore"},{"label":"C","text":"a good electrical conductor"},{"label":"D","text":"so electropositive that carbon cannot reduce its oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Aluminium's affinity for oxygen makes Al₂O₃ too stable for carbon reduction — indeed aluminium is the agent used to reduce other oxides (thermite). Only electrolysis of the molten cryolite-alumina melt can force the reduction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2002,27, $q$Which reagent distinguishes ethanal from propanone?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium hydroxide solution"},{"label":"B","text":"bromine water"},{"label":"C","text":"phenolphthalein indicator"},{"label":"D","text":"ammoniacal silver trioxonitrate(V) solution (Tollens)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Tollens' reagent gives a silver mirror with aldehydes, which are themselves oxidized to alkanoic acids. Ketones lack the aldehydic hydrogen and give no reaction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2002,28, $q$The structural formula of ethyne may be written as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"H–C≡C–H"},{"label":"B","text":"H₂C=CH₂"},{"label":"C","text":"CH₃–CH₃"},{"label":"D","text":"H₃C–C≡CH"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Ethyne (acetylene) is a linear molecule with one triple covalent bond sharing six electrons between the two carbons. Option D would be propyne, which has an extra methyl group.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','separation',2002,29, $q$Iodine crystals mixed with sodium chloride can be separated by sublimation because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iodine reacts with the salt on heating"},{"label":"B","text":"iodine changes directly to vapour on gentle heating while the salt remains solid"},{"label":"C","text":"iodine has the higher melting point of the two"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium chloride also passes directly to vapour on gentle heating"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sublimation separates a solid that passes straight to vapour from one that does not. The iodine vapour recondenses on a cold surface as pure crystals, leaving NaCl in the dish.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2002,30, $q$The electron configuration of the sodium atom (Z = 11) in s, p notation is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1s²2s²2p⁶3s²"},{"label":"B","text":"1s²2s²2p⁶3p¹"},{"label":"C","text":"1s²2s²2p⁶3s¹"},{"label":"D","text":"1s²2s²2p⁶4s¹"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Eleven electrons fill the orbitals in aufbau order, ending with a lone electron in 3s. The 3s² pattern belongs to magnesium and 4s¹ to potassium.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2002,31, $q$Under identical conditions, which of these gases diffuses fastest?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide (M = 44)"},{"label":"B","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide (M = 64)"},{"label":"C","text":"oxygen (M = 32)"},{"label":"D","text":"methane (M = 16)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Graham's law puts rate ∝ 1/√M, so the lightest gas moves fastest. Methane, at 16 g mol⁻¹, tops the list here — about twice the rate of sulphur(IV) oxide.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2002,32, $q$The most suitable indicator for the titration of sodium hydroxide solution with ethanoic acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methyl orange"},{"label":"B","text":"phenolphthalein"},{"label":"C","text":"litmus"},{"label":"D","text":"cobalt(II) chloride paper"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The end point of a weak acid against a strong base lies in alkaline territory (the salt hydrolyses basic). Phenolphthalein changes between pH 8.3 and 10 and fits; methyl orange would change far too early.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2002,33, $q$Which of the following isoelectronic species (all with 10 electrons) has the smallest radius?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"O²⁻"},{"label":"B","text":"F⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"Na⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"Al³⁺"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$All four hold ten electrons, but nuclear charge climbs from 8+ to 13+. The greatest pull, in Al³⁺, draws the same electron cloud in tightest, giving the smallest radius.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2002,34, $q$The number of faradays of electricity required to deposit one mole of aluminium from molten alumina is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 F"},{"label":"B","text":"2 F"},{"label":"C","text":"3 F"},{"label":"D","text":"0.33 F"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al demands three electrons per ion, so 3 mol of electrons — 3 × 96500 C, or 3 F — are needed per mole of aluminium. One faraday handles a mole of singly charged ions only.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Write the half-equation","kind":"text","content":"Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al","note":"three electrons per aluminium ion"},{"title":"Count moles of electrons","kind":"math","content":"1 mol Al × 3 = 3 mol e⁻","note":"1 F is the charge on 1 mol of electrons"},{"title":"Convert to faradays","kind":"math","content":"3 × 96500 C = 289500 C = 3 F","note":"matches option C"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2002,35, $q$The rate of a reaction increases as temperature rises mainly because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the activation energy of the reaction decreases"},{"label":"B","text":"a greater fraction of molecules acquires energy at or above the activation energy, so effective collisions increase"},{"label":"C","text":"the concentration of the reactants increases"},{"label":"D","text":"the activation energy of the reaction increases"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Higher temperature shifts the kinetic energy distribution upward, so more colliding molecules exceed the activation energy. The activation energy itself is a property of the reaction path, not of temperature — only a catalyst would lower it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2002,36, $q$Which metal is kept stored under kerosene?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"copper"},{"label":"B","text":"aluminium"},{"label":"C","text":"potassium"},{"label":"D","text":"lead"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The reactive alkali metals (lithium, sodium, potassium) attack air and water so readily that kerosene is used to exclude both. Copper, aluminium and lead survive ordinary exposure by passivating.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2002,37, $q$The IUPAC name of the alkanol (CH₃)₃COH is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"butan-1-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"2-methylpropan-2-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"pentan-3-ol"},{"label":"D","text":"2-methylpropan-1-ol"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The longest chain is propane with the OH group and a methyl group both on C-2. Tertiary alkanols of this shape are named 2-methylpropan-2-ol, a butane-class isomer with four carbons in all.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2002,38, $q$The number of acyclic alkene isomers with the molecular formula C₄H₈ is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$They are but-1-ene, but-2-ene and 2-methylpropene. Cycloalkanes with the same formula are excluded by 'acyclic'. $q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2003,10, $q$Pure water is to be obtained from an aqueous solution of potassium trioxonitrate(V). The most appropriate technique is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"simple distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"decantation"},{"label":"C","text":"using a separating funnel"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation to dryness"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The dissolved salt is non-volatile, so on boiling only water vaporizes, condenses and is collected. Evaporation to dryness would give back the salt, not pure water.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2003,11, $q$The particle ³⁵₁₇Cl⁻ contains$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"17 protons, 17 electrons and 18 neutrons"},{"label":"B","text":"17 protons, 18 electrons and 18 neutrons"},{"label":"C","text":"18 protons, 17 electrons and 17 neutrons"},{"label":"D","text":"17 protons, 18 neutrons and 17 electrons"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Z = 17 fixes 17 protons; the negative charge adds one extra electron, making 18; neutrons = 35 − 17 = 18. Option D merely swaps electrons and neutrons.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2003,12, $q$20.0 cm³ of 0.050 M tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid neutralized 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution. The concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.040 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.080 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.100 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.160 M"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is dibasic, so the mole ratio H₂SO₄ : NaOH is 1 : 2 at the end point. Only 0.001 mol of acid reacted, so the base in 25.0 cm³ was twice that amount.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles of acid","kind":"math","content":"n(acid) = 0.050 × 0.0200 dm³ = 1.0×10⁻³ mol","note":"convert 20.0 cm³ to dm³ first"},{"title":"Apply the 1 : 2 ratio","kind":"math","content":"n(NaOH) = 2 × 1.0×10⁻³ = 2.0×10⁻³ mol","note":"dibasic acid needs two hydroxides"},{"title":"Concentration of the base","kind":"math","content":"C = 2.0×10⁻³ ÷ 0.0250 dm³ = 0.080 mol/dm³","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2003,13, $q$Which pair of oxides contains only acidic oxides?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Na₂O and CO₂"},{"label":"B","text":"CaO and SO₃"},{"label":"C","text":"Al₂O₃ and SO₃"},{"label":"D","text":"CO₂ and SO₃"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$CO₂ and SO₃ are the anhydrides of carbonic and tetraoxosulphate(VI) acids, dissolving in water to give acids. Na₂O and CaO are basic, while Al₂O₃ is amphoteric.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2003,14, $q$The reactivity of the alkali metals increases from lithium to caesium down the group because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"each element has one more proton than the element above it"},{"label":"B","text":"their densities increase regularly"},{"label":"C","text":"their first ionization energies decrease down the group"},{"label":"D","text":"they gain electrons more easily down the group"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$As atoms grow larger down the group, the outer electron is farther from the nucleus and better shielded. It is therefore lost more easily, and loss of that electron is what reactivity with water measures.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2003,15, $q$In ammonium trioxonitrate(V), NH₄NO₃, the oxidation numbers of nitrogen in the ammonium and the trioxonitrate(V) ion respectively are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+3 and −5"},{"label":"B","text":"−3 and −5"},{"label":"C","text":"−3 and +5"},{"label":"D","text":"+3 and +5"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In NH₄⁺ the balance is x + 4(+1) = +1, so nitrogen is −3. In NO₃⁻ it is x + 3(−2) = −1, so nitrogen is +5. Both states coexist in the two different ions of one salt.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2003,16, $q$For the equilibrium 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) used in the Contact process, increasing the total pressure of the system will$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shift the equilibrium toward the reactants"},{"label":"B","text":"leave the position of equilibrium unchanged"},{"label":"C","text":"increase sulphur(VI) oxide only at a lower temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"shift the equilibrium to the right, increasing the yield of SO₃"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Three moles of gas on the left face two on the right, so higher pressure drives the equilibrium to the product side. Industrially, only modest pressures are used because the shift is small.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2003,17, $q$Aluminium is preferred to copper for overhead electric cables mainly because aluminium$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"is cheaper"},{"label":"B","text":"has a higher density"},{"label":"C","text":"is much lighter and still conducts electricity well"},{"label":"D","text":"is attracted by a magnet"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Over long spans, cable weight matters more than raw conductivity. Aluminium's low density with adequate conductivity makes it the practical choice; purity also keeps contact resistance low.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2003,18, $q$Which of the following compounds is an alkanoate (ester)?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CH₃COOH"},{"label":"B","text":"CH₃COOC₂H₅"},{"label":"C","text":"CH₃CH₂OH"},{"label":"D","text":"CH₃COCH₃"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Alkanoates carry the —COOC— linkage formed between an alkanoic acid and an alkanol. The acid, alkanol and ketone in the options lack that ester bridge.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2003,19, $q$A mixture of iron filings and powdered sulphur is best separated by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dissolving in water"},{"label":"B","text":"magnetic attraction"},{"label":"C","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"decantation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Iron is strongly ferromagnetic while sulphur is not, so a magnet picks out the filings cleanly. Mixing before use shows they retain their individual properties.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2003,20, $q$The maximum number of electrons that the M shell (n = 3) can hold is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"32"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Shell capacities follow 2n², so for n = 3 the capacity is 2 × 9 = 18 electrons. The value 32 belongs to the N shell (n = 4).$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2003,21, $q$The mass of 11.2 dm³ of methane, CH₄, measured at STP is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 g"},{"label":"B","text":"16.0 g"},{"label":"C","text":"8.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"32.0 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At STP, n = 11.2/22.4 = 0.50 mol, and M(CH₄) = 16 g mol⁻¹. Mass = 0.50 × 16 = 8.0 g — never mistake half a mole for a full one.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2003,22, $q$If a 0.1 M solution of hydrochloric acid is diluted tenfold, the pH of the solution becomes$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"10"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Dilution by ten divides [H⁺] by ten, putting pH up by exactly one unit: from 1 to 2. Each further tenfold dilution would raise the pH another unit toward (but never past) 7.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2003,23, $q$The bond angle in the ammonia molecule is closest to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"90°"},{"label":"B","text":"104.5°"},{"label":"C","text":"107°"},{"label":"D","text":"109.5°"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Nitrogen holds three bond pairs and one lone pair. The lone pair squeezes the tetrahedral angle from 109.5° down to about 107°; in water, two lone pairs compress it further to 104.5°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2003,24, $q$The mass of copper deposited when 0.20 faraday of electricity is passed through copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution is (Cu = 63.5)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.18 g"},{"label":"B","text":"9.60 g"},{"label":"C","text":"12.70 g"},{"label":"D","text":"6.35 g"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so 2 F deposit one mole (63.5 g) of copper: 0.20 F deposits 0.10 mol = 6.35 g. Using one faraday per mole would double the answer wrongly.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2003,25, $q$For a first-order reaction, the half-life is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"proportional to the initial concentration"},{"label":"B","text":"doubled when the initial concentration is doubled"},{"label":"C","text":"independent of the initial concentration"},{"label":"D","text":"halved as the reaction proceeds"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A first-order half-life, t½ = 0.693/k, is set by the rate constant alone. Whatever the starting concentration, half of it is consumed in that fixed time.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2003,26, $q$When a few drops of water are added to anhydrous copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI), its colour changes$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"from white to blue"},{"label":"B","text":"from blue to white"},{"label":"C","text":"from green to blue"},{"label":"D","text":"from white to green"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The anhydrous salt is white; water of crystallization gives the blue hydrate CuSO₄·5H₂O. This colour change is a standard test for the presence of water.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2003,27, $q$Propene reacts with hydrogen bromide. The main product is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2-bromopropane"},{"label":"B","text":"1-bromopropane"},{"label":"C","text":"1,2-dibromopropane"},{"label":"D","text":"propane"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$By Markovnikov's rule the hydrogen adds to the carbon already carrying more hydrogens, leaving Br on the middle carbon: (CH₃)₂CHBr. Dibromination would require bromine, not HBr.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2003,28, $q$Which of the following molecular formulas represents benzene, an arene?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"C₆H₆"},{"label":"B","text":"C₆H₁₂"},{"label":"C","text":"C₆H₁₄"},{"label":"D","text":"C₆H₁₀"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The arene homologous series has the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₋₆, and benzene (n = 6) fits with C₆H₆. The saturated alkane C₆H₁₄ is hexane, not an arene.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','separation',2003,29, $q$Gold is separated from sandy deposits by panning because gold and sand differ greatly in$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"solubility in water"},{"label":"B","text":"magnetism"},{"label":"C","text":"density"},{"label":"D","text":"melting point"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Swirling with water lets the dense gold particles settle and stay behind while the lighter sand washes over the edge. Gravity separation works on exactly this density contrast.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2003,30, $q$The relative atomic mass of magnesium is 24.3 rather than a whole number because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"every magnesium atom is identical"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium atoms differ in proton number"},{"label":"C","text":"the measured value was rounded up"},{"label":"D","text":"magnesium is a naturally occurring mixture of isotopes of different neutron numbers"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Natural magnesium combines ²⁴Mg, ²⁵Mg and ²⁶Mg in fixed proportions, so the weighted average is not a whole number. Proton number is constant (12); only neutrons vary.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2003,31, $q$The total number of atoms in 4.4 g of carbon(IV) oxide, CO₂, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.02×10²²"},{"label":"B","text":"1.204×10²³"},{"label":"C","text":"1.806×10²³"},{"label":"D","text":"6.02×10²³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The sample holds 0.10 mol of molecules, but each CO₂ molecule contains three atoms, giving 0.30 mol of atoms. Multiplying by the Avogadro constant yields 1.806×10²³.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles of molecules","kind":"math","content":"n = 4.4 ÷ 44 = 0.10 mol CO₂","note":"M(CO₂) = 44 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Moles of atoms","kind":"math","content":"0.10 × 3 = 0.30 mol of atoms","note":"each CO₂ has 1 C and 2 O"},{"title":"Number of atoms","kind":"math","content":"0.30 × 6.02×10²³ = 1.806×10²³","note":"matches option C"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2003,32, $q$A solution of ethanoic acid together with sodium ethanoate resists a change in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added, because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the ethanoic acid is completely ionized"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium ethanoate is insoluble in water"},{"label":"C","text":"buffer solutions always maintain the pH at exactly 7"},{"label":"D","text":"each addition is consumed by the buffer pair, keeping the H⁺ concentration almost constant"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Added H⁺ is removed by CH₃COO⁻ forming CH₃COOH, and added OH⁻ is neutralized by the acid itself. The weak acid/weak base equilibrium soaks up small additions and the pH barely moves — a buffer is not necessarily neutral.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2003,33, $q$Which substance has a giant covalent (network) structure?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iodine"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"solid carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"graphite"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In graphite every carbon atom is covalently bonded to three neighbours in flat layers that extend indefinitely. Iodine and solid CO₂ are simple molecular, and NaCl is ionic.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2003,34, $q$During the electrolysis of concentrated sodium chloride solution, the process taking place at the anode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chloride ions are oxidized to chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium ions are reduced to sodium metal"},{"label":"C","text":"water molecules gain electrons to form hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"hydroxide ions are generated at the anode"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The anode is the site of oxidation: 2Cl⁻ − 2e⁻ → Cl₂. Hydrogen and hydroxide are cathode-side events in brine electrolysis.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2003,35, $q$For the equilibrium N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), where nitrogen(IV) oxide is dark brown, decreasing the pressure on the system will$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shift the equilibrium to the left, lightening the colour"},{"label":"B","text":"leave the position of equilibrium unchanged"},{"label":"C","text":"shift the equilibrium left while darkening the colour"},{"label":"D","text":"shift the equilibrium to the right, deepening the colour"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Reducing the pressure favors the side with the greater number of gas molecules — here the two NO₂ on the right. The mixture therefore turns a deeper brown.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2003,36, $q$Hydrogen is described as a clean fuel because when it burns it produces$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide and water"},{"label":"B","text":"no measurable heat"},{"label":"C","text":"only water"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon(II) oxide as well as water"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The simple equation 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O leaves no carbon(IV) oxide or carbon(II) oxide, so no greenhouse or poisoning emissions. That single product is what earns the label 'clean'.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2003,37, $q$When ethanoic acid reacts with sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) solution, the gas evolved is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(II) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Reaction with the hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) ion releases CO₂, which turns lime water milky. This behavior marks a true alkanoic acid and separates it from alkanols, which do not react.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2004,10, $q$A precipitate so fine that it passes through an ordinary filter paper can best be separated from its liquid by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"filtration"},{"label":"B","text":"decantation"},{"label":"C","text":"centrifugation"},{"label":"D","text":"sieving"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Rapid spinning in a centrifuge hurls the fine particles to the bottom, after which the supernatant liquid is poured off. Filtration fails because the particles slip through the pores of the paper.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2004,11, $q$Which pair of nuclides are isotopes of the same element?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"³⁵₁₇Cl and ³⁷₁₇Cl"},{"label":"B","text":"¹⁶₈O and ¹⁶₇N"},{"label":"C","text":"¹²₆C and ¹⁴₇N"},{"label":"D","text":"⁴⁰₂₀Ca and ⁴⁰₁₈Ar"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Isotopes share the same atomic number but differ in mass number. The other three pairs share only the mass number and belong to different elements (isobars).$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2004,12, $q$The volume of hydrogen, measured at STP, liberated when 6.5 g of zinc reacts completely with excess dilute hydrochloric acid is (Zn = 65)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"2.24 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"11.2 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"22.4 dm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ shows a 1 : 1 mole ratio between zinc and hydrogen. The 6.5 g sample is exactly 0.10 mol of zinc, so 0.10 mol of hydrogen is liberated.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles of zinc","kind":"math","content":"n(Zn) = 6.5 ÷ 65 = 0.10 mol","note":"M(Zn) = 65 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Mole bridge through the equation","kind":"text","content":"Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂, so n(H₂) = n(Zn) = 0.10 mol","note":"1 : 1 ratio"},{"title":"Volume at STP","kind":"math","content":"V = 0.10 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2004,13, $q$The buffer system that keeps the pH of blood close to 7.4 is based on the conjugate pair$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"HCl and Cl⁻"},{"label":"B","text":"H₂SO₄ and SO₄²⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"H₂CO₃ and HCO₃⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"CO and CO₂"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A buffer needs a weak acid paired with its conjugate base, and the dissolved carbon(IV) oxide/tetraoxocarbonate system does this job in blood. Strong-acid conjugates like Cl⁻ cannot give back the acid when base is added.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2004,14, $q$Which sequence places three period-3 elements in order of decreasing metallic character?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Al > Mg > Na"},{"label":"B","text":"Mg > Na > Al"},{"label":"C","text":"Al > Na > Mg"},{"label":"D","text":"Na > Mg > Al"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Metallic character falls as the effective nuclear charge rises across a period. Sodium is the most metallic of the three and aluminium the least.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2004,15, $q$The oxidation number of phosphorus in tetraoxophosphate(V) acid, H₃PO₄, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+3"},{"label":"B","text":"+4"},{"label":"C","text":"+5"},{"label":"D","text":"−3"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The charge balance is 3(+1) + x + 4(−2) = 0, giving x = +5. The (V) in the systematic name advertises the same state.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2004,16, $q$Grinding a solid reactant into a fine powder speeds up its reaction because grinding$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lowers the activation energy of the reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"raises the temperature of the reaction mixture"},{"label":"C","text":"increases the concentration of the solid"},{"label":"D","text":"exposes a greater surface area for collisions"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Powdering creates many new surfaces, so more solid particles are available for effective collision each second. Temperature, particle energy and concentration are unchanged by the grinding itself.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2004,17, $q$Magnesium blocks attached to buried steel pipes protect the pipes from rusting because magnesium$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"is coated with grease"},{"label":"B","text":"is unreactive toward water"},{"label":"C","text":"is more electropositive than iron, and corrodes in preference to it"},{"label":"D","text":"is a better electrical insulator than iron"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Being higher in the activity series, magnesium supplies electrons and is itself oxidized first. This sacrificial protection keeps the iron full of electrons, so it cannot rust.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2004,18, $q$How many isomeric alkanols have the molecular formula C₅H₁₁OH (acyclic)?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$They are pentan-1-ol, pentan-2-ol, pentan-3-ol, 2- and 3-methylbutan-1-ol, 2- and 3-methylbutan-2-ol, and 2,2-dimethylpropan-1-ol — eight acyclic (chain) alkanols in all.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2004,19, $q$On a chromatogram of a dye mixture, one spot has Rf 0.2 and another has Rf 0.8. The dye that moved the shorter distance is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the less strongly adsorbed dye"},{"label":"B","text":"the more strongly adsorbed dye"},{"label":"C","text":"the more soluble dye"},{"label":"D","text":"the denser dye"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Strong adsorption on the stationary phase pins a component close to the origin, giving a small Rf value. The Rf 0.8 dye travels freely because it is barely held back.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2004,20, $q$An atom X has the electron configuration 2, 8, 7. The ion most likely to be formed from X is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"X⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"X²⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"X²⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"X⁻"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$With seven outer electrons, gaining one completes the octet far more easily than losing seven. The result is a singly charged negative ion, exactly as chlorine behaves.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2004,21, $q$The volume of oxygen required for the complete combustion of 10 dm³ of propane, C₃H₈, measured at the same temperature and pressure is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"30 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"40 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"50 dm³"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O gives a 1 : 5 gas ratio. At equal temperature and pressure, gas volumes follow mole ratios, so 5 × 10 = 50 dm³ of oxygen are needed.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Balance the combustion","kind":"text","content":"C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O","note":"oxygen ratio is 1 : 5"},{"title":"Apply gas-volume ratios","kind":"math","content":"V(O₂) = 5 × 10 dm³ = 50 dm³","note":"equal T and P ⇒ volumes in mole ratio"},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"Five times the propane volume of oxygen is needed","note":"matches option D"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2004,22, $q$Which salt solution turns red litmus paper blue?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"potassium trioxonitrate(V)"},{"label":"C","text":"potassium ethanoate"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanoic acid"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Potassium ethanoate is the salt of a strong base and a weak acid, so the ethanoate ion hydrolyses to give hydroxide ions. Ammonium chloride hydrolyses the other way, and the nitrate salt is neutral.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2004,23, $q$The triple covalent bond in a nitrogen molecule, N≡N, consists of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"three sigma bonds"},{"label":"B","text":"one sigma bond and one pi bond"},{"label":"C","text":"two sigma bonds and one pi bond"},{"label":"D","text":"one sigma bond and two pi bonds"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$One σ bond forms head-on along the internuclear axis, and the two remaining shared pairs overlap sidewise as π bonds. Six bonding electrons in all hold the nitrogen atoms together.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2004,24, $q$During the electrolysis of dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, the ratio by volume of hydrogen liberated at the cathode to oxygen at the anode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 2"},{"label":"C","text":"2 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"4 : 1"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The half reactions give 2H₂ for every O₂ (overall: 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂). Twice the gas volume collects at the cathode, which is the basis of the classic water-volume demonstration.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2004,25, $q$When the temperature of a reaction system is raised, the rate constant k$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increases"},{"label":"B","text":"decreases"},{"label":"C","text":"is unaffected by temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"fluctuates randomly"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Higher temperature pushes more molecules past the activation energy, so the rate constant grows for every reaction, endothermic or exothermic. Only a change in the reaction path (e.g. a catalyst) alters k at fixed temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2004,26, $q$Chlorine bleaches moist litmus paper but hardly affects dry litmus. The explanation is that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dry chlorine is itself the effective bleaching agent"},{"label":"B","text":"moist chlorine forms HCl and HOCl, and the HOCl is the oxidizing bleach"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine reduces the dye to a colourless compound"},{"label":"D","text":"water simply dissolves the dye"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HOCl, and the small amount of hypochlorous acid oxidizes the dye to a permanent colourless state. No water means no HOCl, and dry chlorine is inert toward litmus.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2004,27, $q$Vegetable oils are hardened into margarine by adding hydrogen across their double bonds. The catalyst normally used is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nickel"},{"label":"B","text":"iron"},{"label":"C","text":"copper"},
 {"label":"D","text":"zinc"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Finely divided nickel catalyzes hydrogenation at moderate temperatures, saturating the C=C bonds and raising the melting point. Iron, copper and zinc do not serve this purpose in fat hardening.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2004,28, $q$A single reagent that distinguishes ethanoic acid from ethanol is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) solution"},{"label":"B","text":"lime water"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium chloride solution"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium bromide solution"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The alkanoic acid liberates CO₂ with brisk effervescence from the hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV), while ethanol gives no reaction. The gas is confirmed by lime-water milkiness.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2004,29, $q$Which of the following contains the greatest number of molecules?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 g of hydrogen (H₂)"},{"label":"B","text":"32.0 g of oxygen (O₂)"},{"label":"C","text":"14.0 g of nitrogen (N₂)"},{"label":"D","text":"44.0 g of carbon(IV) oxide (CO₂)"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Count moles: H₂ = 4/2 = 2 mol; O₂ = 32/32 = 1 mol; N₂ = 14/28 = 0.5 mol; CO₂ = 44/44 = 1 mol. Two moles of hydrogen win, because the same mass of a light gas holds more particles.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2004,30, $q$An element has the electron configuration 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p³. Its atomic number is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"13"},{"label":"B","text":"14"},{"label":"C","text":"15"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Add the electrons: 2 + 2 + 6 + 2 + 3 = 15, so the element is phosphorus. The 3p³ pattern also marks group 15 — one electron short of a full p set in each of three orbitals.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2004,31, $q$The percentage by mass of water of crystallization in copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) pentahydrate, CuSO₄·5H₂O (M = 249.5), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"11.3%"},{"label":"B","text":"36.1%"},{"label":"C","text":"44.4%"},{"label":"D","text":"62.5%"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The five waters carry a mass of 90 g within the 249.5 g formula unit. That is (90/249.5) × 100 ≈ 36.1% — roughly a third, as expected for both salts and hydrates of copper.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Mass of the waters","kind":"math","content":"5 × 18 = 90 g of water per formula unit","note":"each H₂O is 18 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Percentage","kind":"math","content":"(90 ÷ 249.5) × 100 ≈ 36.1%","note":"whole-formula mass = 249.5"},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"About a third of the crystal mass is water","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2004,32, $q$Which of the following is a strong electrolyte in dilute aqueous solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonia solution"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium hydroxide"},{"label":"D","text":"trioxocarbonate(IV) acid"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$NaOH ionizes completely in dilute solution, giving maximal conductivity. The molecular acids and ammonia listed ionize only partially and so are weak electrolytes.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2004,33, $q$Solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity, whereas molten sodium chloride does. The reason is that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"electrons carry the current in the solid crystal"},{"label":"B","text":"the ions are held in fixed positions in the crystal but become mobile when the solid melts"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine is liberated from the solid on heating"},{"label":"D","text":"the ions are fully mobile in the solid state"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Conduction requires mobile charge carriers. In the rigid lattice the Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are locked in position; only on melting or dissolving are they free to drift toward the electrodes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2004,34, $q$Which of the following reactions is a redox reaction?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu"},{"label":"B","text":"HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O"},{"label":"C","text":"AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl + NaNO₃"},{"label":"D","text":"CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Zinc loses electrons to copper(II), so both oxidation and reduction occur and the reaction is a displacement. Neutralization, precipitation and the thermal decomposition listed transfer no electrons at all.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2004,35, $q$For the exothermic reaction 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) at equilibrium, the yield of SO₃ is decreased by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"raising the temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"raising the pressure"},{"label":"C","text":"adding more oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"continuously removing the SO₃ formed"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Heat behaves as a product here, so raising the temperature drives the reverse direction and lowers the yield. Options B–D all favor the forward side — which is exactly why industry runs the converter hot enough only for speed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2004,36, $q$In the blast furnace, limestone is charged with the ore mainly in order to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fuel the furnace"},{"label":"B","text":"reduce the iron(III) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"combine with the sandy impurity, SiO₂, forming a removable slag"},{"label":"D","text":"oxidize carbon(II) oxide to carbon(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$CaCO₃ decomposes to CaO, which reacts with SiO₂ to give liquid calcium silicate, CaSiO₃ — the slag. Reduction itself is done by carbon(II) oxide, and the coke supplies the heat.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2004,37, $q$When an ester is boiled with sodium hydroxide solution, the products are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an alkene and water"},{"label":"B","text":"an alkanol and the sodium salt of the alkanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"a ketone and an alkanol"},{"label":"D","text":"an alkane and carbon(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Alkaline hydrolysis (saponification) splits the ester bond into the parent alkanol and the sodium alkanoate. Acid hydrolysis would give the free alkanoic acid instead of its sodium salt.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2004,38, $q$The condensed structural formula of methyl propanoate is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CH₃COOC₂H₅"},{"label":"B","text":"CH₃COOCH₃"},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₅COOC₂H₅"},{"label":"D","text":"CH₃CH₂COOCH₃"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The propanoate part (CH₃CH₂COO–) carries three carbons and the methyl part creates a total of four. Option B, with two carbons, is the isomer methyl ethanoate — a classic swap.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2005,10, $q$A solution of a green vegetable dye is poured down a chromatography column packed with alumina and washed through with solvent. The component that emerges first from the bottom is the one$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"most strongly adsorbed on the alumina"},{"label":"B","text":"least strongly adsorbed on the alumina"},{"label":"C","text":"that has the greatest molecular mass"},{"label":"D","text":"of the deepest colour"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The travelling solvent carries each component at a speed set by how tightly the alumina holds it. Components held least are washed to the bottom quickly; strong adsorption means a slow, late exit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2005,11, $q$The isotope of hydrogen called tritium, ³₁H, contains how many neutrons?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Neutrons = mass number − proton number = 3 − 1 = 2. Protium has none and deuterium one; the three isotopes of hydrogen are the classic textbook set.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2005,12, $q$The total number of moles of ions present when 0.20 mol of aluminium tetraoxosulphate(VI), Al₂(SO₄)₃, is completely dissolved in water is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.40 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"0.60 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"1.00 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"1.20 mol"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each formula unit yields 2Al³⁺ + 3SO₄²⁻, that is 5 ions. Multiplying by 0.20 mol gives 1.00 mol of ions in all — don't forget the sulphate anions when counting.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2005,13, $q$Which species is a Lewis acid, that is, an electron pair acceptor?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"NH₃"},{"label":"B","text":"Cl⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"H₂O"},{"label":"D","text":"BCl₃"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The boron in BCl₃ carries only six outer electrons and holds an empty orbital, so it readily accepts an electron pair. Ammonia, water and chloride are all pair donors — Lewis bases.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2005,14, $q$Down group 1 from lithium to caesium, the first ionization energy$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"decreases"},{"label":"B","text":"increases"},{"label":"C","text":"remains constant"},{"label":"D","text":"rises and falls irregularly"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Atoms grow larger down the group, so the outer electron is farther from the nucleus and more strongly shielded by inner shells. Less energy is then needed to remove it — which is the same reason the metals become more reactive.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2005,15, $q$The mass of silver deposited on the cathode when a current of 0.50 A flows through silver trioxonitrate(V) solution for 30 minutes is (Ag = 108)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.50 g"},{"label":"B","text":"2.02 g"},{"label":"C","text":"1.01 g"},{"label":"D","text":"4.04 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag needs one electron per silver ion, so moles of silver equal moles of electrons passed: about 9.3 × 10⁻³ mol. Multiplying by 108 g mol⁻¹ gives ≈ 1.0 g.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Time in seconds","kind":"math","content":"t = 30 × 60 = 1800 s","note":"current in amperes × seconds gives coulombs"},{"title":"Charge passed","kind":"math","content":"Q = I × t = 0.50 × 1800 = 900 C","note":"Q/96500 = moles of electrons"},{"title":"Mass deposited","kind":"math","content":"m = (900 ÷ 96500) × 108 ≈ 1.01 g","note":"matches option C"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2005,16, $q$Which substance, added to hydrogen peroxide solution, speeds up its decomposition without itself being consumed?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"manganese(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"water"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"dilute hydrochloric acid"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$MnO₂ provides a surface on which 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂ proceeds rapidly, and it is recovered unchanged. Water, brine and dilute acid simply dilute or sit idle.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2005,17, $q$Adding silver trioxonitrate(V) solution to a solution containing ion Y gives a white precipitate that dissolves in dilute ammonia solution. Ion Y is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bromide ion"},{"label":"B","text":"iodide ion"},{"label":"C","text":"chloride ion"},{"label":"D","text":"tetraoxosulphate(VI) ion"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$AgCl is the white precipitate, easily dissolved by ammonia through complex formation. AgBr is cream and AgI yellow — the three halide colours are the examiner's favourite trio.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2005,18, $q$The direct addition of steam to ethene, over a catalyst, produces$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethane"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanal"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanol"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The H and OH of water add across the C=C bond, placing OH on one carbon and H on the other to give CH₃CH₂OH. Oxidation steps would be needed to reach ethanal or the acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','separation',2005,19, $q$In paper chromatography, the stationary phase is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the solvent rising up the paper"},{"label":"B","text":"the glass tank holding the apparatus"},{"label":"C","text":"the water held between the cellulose fibres of the paper"},{"label":"D","text":"the lid covering the tank"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Cellulose holds a film of water firmly, and it is against this immobilized water that dissolved dyes compete with the rising solvent. The moving solvent is the mobile phase by definition.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2005,20, $q$Which of the following ions does NOT have the same electron configuration as an atom of argon (18 electrons)?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"K⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"Ca²⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"Cl⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"Na⁺"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$K⁺, Ca²⁺ and Cl⁻ each count 18 electrons and mimic argon. Na⁺ has only ten — it copies neon instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2005,21, $q$A gas occupying 5.0 dm³ at a pressure of 1.0 atm is compressed at constant temperature to 2.0 dm³. Its new pressure is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.40 atm"},{"label":"B","text":"the same, 1.0 atm"},{"label":"C","text":"4.00 atm"},{"label":"D","text":"2.50 atm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Boyle's law links pressure and volume inversely at constant temperature: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂. Halving the volume doubles the pressure, so 2.5 atm is exactly right.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"State the law","kind":"math","content":"At constant T: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂","note":"pressure × volume is fixed"},{"title":"Rearrange","kind":"math","content":"P₂ = (1.0 × 5.0) ÷ 2.0 = 2.5 atm","note":"P₂ = P₁V₁/V₂"},{"title":"Check","kind":"text","content":"Smaller volume ⇒ larger pressure","note":"matches option D"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2005,22, $q$Sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV), NaHCO₃, is classified as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a normal salt"},{"label":"B","text":"an acid salt"},{"label":"C","text":"a basic salt"},{"label":"D","text":"a double salt"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) ion still carries one replaceable hydrogen, which is the definition of an acid salt. Normal salts have no such hydrogen, and double salts hold two cations — like alum.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2005,23, $q$Solid carbon(IV) oxide ('dry ice') sublimes at about −78 °C because the molecules in its crystal$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"are tied into a giant covalent network"},{"label":"B","text":"are bound by strong hydrogen bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"are held together only by weak van der Waals forces"},{"label":"D","text":"are joined by ionic bonds"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Dry ice is a simple molecular solid: sublimation separates whole CO₂ molecules without breaking any covalent bond inside them. With so little intermolecular attraction to overcome, the solid goes straight to gas.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2005,24, $q$In the reaction SO₂(g) + 2H₂S(g) → 3S(s) + 2H₂O(l), the oxidizing agent is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen sulphide"},{"label":"C","text":"sulphur"},{"label":"D","text":"water"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sulphur in SO₂ falls from +4 to 0, so SO₂ is reduced and is the oxidizing agent. The sulphur in H₂S rises from −2 to 0, so H₂S is the reducing agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2005,25, $q$For the reaction A + B → products, doubling the concentration of A alone doubles the rate of reaction. The order of reaction with respect to A is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zero"},{"label":"B","text":"second"},{"label":"C","text":"one-half"},{"label":"D","text":"first"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$When a twofold change in [A] produces a twofold change in rate, the rate is proportional to [A] — first order. Zero order would ignore the concentration change entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2005,26, $q$Ion-exchange resins soften hard water mainly by exchanging$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Cl⁻ and SO₄²⁻ for OH⁻"},{"label":"B","text":"Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ for Na⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"Na⁺ for K⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"H⁺ and OH⁻ for H₂O"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The resin holds exchangeable Na⁺, which swaps for the hardness-causing Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ in the water. With those ions gone, the water can form lather with soap readily.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2005,27, $q$Propan-1-ol, warmed with EXCESS acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) solution, is oxidized to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propanal"},{"label":"B","text":"propanone"},{"label":"C","text":"propanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"propane"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A primary alkanol passes through the alkanal stage on the way to the alkanoic acid. With oxidant in excess the oxidation does not stop at propanal; secondary alkanols alone stop at alkanones.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2005,28, $q$Vulcanizing natural rubber with sulphur greatly improves its usefulness mainly because the sulphur$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"acts as a dark pigment protecting the rubber from light"},{"label":"B","text":"forms cross-links between the long rubber polymer chains"},{"label":"C","text":"melts into the rubber and simply softens it"},{"label":"D","text":"reduces the elasticity of the rubber"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sulphur bridges lock adjacent rubber chains together, preventing them from sliding past one another. The product stays elastic yet far stronger — the classic material of vehicle tyres.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2005,29, $q$In the simple distillation of a solution, the function of the Liebig condenser is to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"condense the vapour back to liquid for collection"},{"label":"B","text":"heat the vapour further before collection"},{"label":"C","text":"record the boiling point of the distillate"},{"label":"D","text":"filter solid particles from the distillate"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Cold water flowing round the jacket removes heat from the vapour, which turns back to liquid and drips into the receiver. Distillation is useless without this condensing step.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2005,30, $q$The sharp line spectra emitted by excited atoms show that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the energy levels of the electrons in an atom are quantized, and jumps between them release distinct lines"},{"label":"B","text":"electrons in atoms radiate away their energy continuously"},{"label":"C","text":"light is a wave phenomenon and nothing more"},{"label":"D","text":"the nuclei of the atoms emit the radiation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A glowing gas emits photons only when electrons drop between allowed levels, whose energies are fixed. Discrete jumps therefore give discrete lines — the evidence behind the Bohr model.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2005,31, $q$25.0 cm³ of 0.100 M sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) solution required 20.0 cm³ of hydrochloric acid for exact neutralization. The concentration of the acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.125 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.0625 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.250 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.500 M"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The equation Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂ needs two acid molecules per carbonate. The base in 25.0 cm³ was 2.5×10⁻³ mol, so 5.0×10⁻³ mol of acid sat in 20.0 cm³ — that is 0.25 M.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles of carbonate","kind":"math","content":"n = 0.100 × 0.0250 = 2.5×10⁻³ mol","note":"convert 25.0 cm³ to dm³"},{"title":"Mole bridge through the equation","kind":"text","content":"Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → ... + CO₂, so n(HCl) = 2 × 2.5×10⁻³ = 5.0×10⁻³ mol","note":"acid : salt ratio is 2 : 1"},{"title":"Concentration of acid","kind":"math","content":"C = 5.0×10⁻³ ÷ 0.0200 dm³ = 0.250 M","note":"matches option C"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2005,32, $q$Which of the following pH values indicates a basic solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Values above 7 mark excess hydroxide and so a base; below 7 mark acid. pH 11 is well into alkaline territory, roughly 10 000 times less acidic than neutral water.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2005,33, $q$At room temperature fluorine and chlorine are gases, bromine a liquid and iodine a solid. The reason for these differences is that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the molecules grow larger and more polarizable down the group, strengthening the dispersion forces between them"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen bonding occurs only in solid iodine"},{"label":"C","text":"only chlorine molecules are permanent dipoles"},{"label":"D","text":"fluorine exists as free atoms rather than molecules"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$All four elements are non-polar molecules attracted by London dispersion forces alone. The bigger the molecule, the more polarizable its cloud — so the forces and hence boiling points rise from F₂ to I₂.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2005,34, $q$Which statement about the anode of an electrochemical cell is correct for BOTH a galvanic (Daniell) cell and an electrolytic cell?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"reduction occurs there in both cell types"},{"label":"B","text":"oxidation occurs there in both cell types"},{"label":"C","text":"it carries the positive sign in both cell types"},{"label":"D","text":"it carries the negative sign in both cell types"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The anode is defined by the process — always oxidation. Polarity, however, flips between cell types: positive in electrolysis, negative in a galvanic cell.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2005,35, $q$In the titration of ethanedioic acid against acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII), the reaction is very slow at first but speeds up as it proceeds because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the reaction mixture cools as the reaction goes on"},{"label":"B","text":"the acid concentration rises steadily"},{"label":"C","text":"the Mn²⁺ ion formed acts as an autocatalyst"},{"label":"D","text":"the solution becomes less acidic as the reaction proceeds"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$One product of the reaction, the pale Mn²⁺ ion, catalyzes its own formation — autocatalysis. A drop of the purple reagent sits unreduced until enough Mn²⁺ accumulates, after which decolorization races along.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2005,36, $q$Aluminium window frames resist corrosion in damp air because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the metal is too dense to react with oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"a thin, strongly adherent film of aluminium oxide forms on the surface and protects the metal beneath"},{"label":"C","text":"aluminium melts at a very low temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"the metal conducts heat so well that corrosion cannot begin"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fresh aluminium oxidizes in air almost at once, sealing itself under an impervious Al₂O₃ layer a few nanometres thick. Passivation, not inertness, is why the shiny surface lasts outdoors.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2005,37, $q$Polyethene is manufactured from ethene by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"condensation polymerization"},{"label":"B","text":"fermentation"},{"label":"C","text":"addition polymerization"},{"label":"D","text":"free radical substitution"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Thousands of C=C bonds open up and the units chain together, with no small molecule expelled. If a small molecule such as water were released, the process would be condensation polymerization instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2005,38, $q$The functional group present in all alkanoic (carboxylic) acids is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"—OH"},{"label":"B","text":"—COOH"},{"label":"C","text":"—CHO"},{"label":"D","text":"—CO—"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The carboxylic group, —COOH, couples a carbonyl with a hydroxyl on one carbon; it is what releases H⁺ to water. The —OH of alkanols and the —CHO of alkanals are different families entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2006,10, $q$Common salt is to be recovered from sea water in coastal pans. The most suitable process is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"evaporation"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"decantation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Evaporation driven by sun and wind removes the water and leaves crystalline sodium chloride. Filtration or decantation would only remove suspended solids, not the dissolved salt.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2006,11, $q$Which isotope of hydrogen is radioactive?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"protium (¹H)"},{"label":"B","text":"deuterium (²H)"},{"label":"C","text":"tritium (³H)"},{"label":"D","text":"none of the three"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Tritium, with two neutrons in its nucleus, decays by beta emission with a half-life of about twelve years. Protium and deuterium are both stable.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2006,12, $q$The percentage by mass of oxygen in calcium trioxocarbonate(IV), CaCO₃, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"48.0%"},{"label":"B","text":"40.0%"},{"label":"C","text":"16.0%"},{"label":"D","text":"32.0%"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The three oxygens contribute 3 × 16 = 48 to a formula mass of 40 + 12 + 48 = 100. The percentage is (48/100) × 100 = 48.0%.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Mass of the oxygens","kind":"math","content":"3 × 16 = 48 g per formula unit","note":"each O is 16 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Formula mass","kind":"math","content":"40 + 12 + 48 = 100 g","note":"Ca = 40, C = 12"},{"title":"Percentage","kind":"math","content":"(48 ÷ 100) × 100 = 48.0%","note":"matches option A"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2006,13, $q$Which of the following molecular formulas represents an alkene?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"C₂H₂"},{"label":"B","text":"C₂H₄"},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₆"},{"label":"D","text":"C₃H₈"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Alkenes have the general formula CₙH₂ₙ; with n = 2 that is C₂H₄, ethene. C₂H₂ is an alkyne, and the others are alkanes.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2006,14, $q$Which member of group 16 has the largest atomic radius?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"sulphur"},{"label":"C","text":"selenium"},{"label":"D","text":"tellurium"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Atomic radius grows down a group as extra shells are added, so the element lowest in the group is the largest. Tellurium sits below selenium in group 16.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2006,15, $q$Which of the following is a weak acid?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"B","text":"tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid"},{"label":"C","text":"trioxonitrate(V) acid"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanoic acid"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$HCl, H₂SO₄ and HNO₃ ionize completely in water, giving high H₃O⁺ concentrations. Ethanoic acid ionizes only slightly, which is why its solutions are weakly acidic and conduct poorly.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2006,16, $q$The oxidation number of carbon in sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV), NaHCO₃, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+2"},{"label":"B","text":"−4"},{"label":"C","text":"+4"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The charge balance is (+1) + (+1) + x + 3(−2) = 0, so x = +4. Carbon is in its highest common oxidation state here, just as in CO₂.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2006,17, $q$Which change speeds up the reaction between granulated zinc and dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lowering the temperature of the acid"},{"label":"B","text":"diluting the acid further"},{"label":"C","text":"raising the temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"adding an inhibitor"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A warmer acid supplies faster-moving particles with more energy above the activation energy, so hydrogen is released at a greater rate. Dilution and cooling both slow the reaction, and an inhibitor does too.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2006,18, $q$Because of its very high density, the metal used to shield workers from X-rays is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"aluminium"},{"label":"B","text":"iron"},{"label":"C","text":"lead"},{"label":"D","text":"zinc"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Dense lead absorbs penetrating radiation efficiently in a thin barrier. It is used in X-ray rooms, radiation aprons and around radioactive sources.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','separation',2006,19, $q$Distillation, rather than simple evaporation, is chosen when the material to be collected is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dissolved solid"},{"label":"B","text":"liquid"},{"label":"C","text":"residue left in the flask"},{"label":"D","text":"precipitate"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In distillation, vaporized solvent condenses and is run off as liquid, while evaporation simply lets it escape and keeps the solid behind. Which product you want decides which technique you choose.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2006,20, $q$The electron configuration of the zinc ion, Zn²⁺ (Z = 30), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2, 8, 18"},{"label":"B","text":"2, 8, 18, 2"},{"label":"C","text":"2, 8, 16"},{"label":"D","text":"2, 8, 8, 2"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The neutral atom is 2, 8, 18, 2; removing the two outer electrons gives 2, 8, 18 — a very stable filled-shell arrangement. Option B is the neutral atom, and option D is calcium.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2006,21, $q$The mass of sodium hydroxide needed to prepare 2.0 dm³ of a 0.10 M solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 g"},{"label":"B","text":"8.0 g"},{"label":"C","text":"16.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"40.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Moles needed = concentration × volume = 0.10 × 2.0 = 0.20 mol, and each mole weighs 40 g. The 8.0 g in 2 dm³ gives exactly 0.10 mol/dm³.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles needed","kind":"math","content":"n = C × V = 0.10 × 2.0 = 0.20 mol","note":"M(NaOH) = 40 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Convert to mass","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.20 × 40 = 8.0 g","note":"matches option B"},{"title":"Check","kind":"text","content":"0.20 mol in 2 dm³ is 0.10 mol/dm³","note":"concentration as required"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2006,22, $q$Which gas turns moist red litmus paper blue?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonia"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Ammonia dissolves in the moisture on the paper to give an alkaline solution: NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻. Hydrogen chloride and chlorine affect litmus the opposite way (acidic or bleaching).$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2006,23, $q$The first ionization energy of magnesium is higher than that of sodium because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"magnesium has one more electron shell than sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium atoms are heavier than sodium atoms"},{"label":"C","text":"the greater nuclear charge of magnesium attracts the outer electrons of the same shell more strongly"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium atoms are more numerous in a sample"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Both atoms keep their outer electrons in the third shell, but magnesium's nucleus carries one more proton. Stronger attraction means more energy is needed to remove an electron.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2006,24, $q$In the reaction Cl₂(g) + 2KBr(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + Br₂(aq), chlorine acts as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a dehydrating agent"},{"label":"B","text":"an oxidizing agent"},{"label":"C","text":"a reducing agent"},{"label":"D","text":"a catalyst"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Chlorine falls from oxidation number 0 to −1, taking electrons from the bromide ions. The species reduced is by definition the oxidizing agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2006,25, $q$For an exothermic reaction at equilibrium, which change shifts the position of equilibrium toward the products?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"raising the temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"removing some of the reactant"},{"label":"C","text":"increasing the concentration of a reactant"},{"label":"D","text":"adding an inert gas at constant volume"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Le Chatelier's principle answers composition changes: more reactant drives formation of more product. Heat acts like a product in an exothermic reaction, so raising the temperature pushes the reverse way, and an inert gas cannot shift a closed-volume equilibrium.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2006,26, $q$The main impurity removed from bauxite when it is purified into alumina is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"silicon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"iron(III) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"cryolite"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Hot concentrated sodium hydroxide dissolves the alumina as sodium aluminate while Fe₂O₃ stays behind as insoluble 'red mud'. The aluminate is later re-precipitated and calcined to pure Al₂O₃.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2006,27, $q$Which of these compounds is an aromatic compound?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"benzene"},{"label":"B","text":"cyclohexane"},{"label":"C","text":"hexane"},{"label":"D","text":"hex-1-ene"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Aromatics contain a benzene ring with delocalized π electrons spread over six carbons. Cyclohexane shares the ring shape but is fully saturated — no π system, no aromaticity.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2006,28, $q$Methanol reacts with ethanoic acid in the presence of concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid to give the ester$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethyl ethanoate"},{"label":"B","text":"ethyl methanoate"},{"label":"C","text":"methyl ethanoate"},{"label":"D","text":"methyl propanoate"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The alcohol supplies the alkyl group (methyl) and the acid supplies the alkanoate part (ethanoate). Naming an ester runs alkyl first, then acid part — hence methyl ethanoate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2006,29, $q$An atom has the electron configuration 2, 8, 8, 1. With four shells, it is a period-4 element of group$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"7"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A single electron in the outermost shell places the element in group 1 (the alkali metals); Z = 19 identifies it as potassium. Group number tracks the outer electron count for main-group elements.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2006,30, $q$Heating 8.4 g of sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) until decomposition is complete liberates what volume of carbon(IV) oxide, measured at STP?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.56 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"2.24 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"5.60 dm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The equation 2NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂ gives half as much CO₂ as the salt heated. From 0.10 mol of NaHCO₃, that is 0.05 mol of gas = 1.12 dm³ at STP.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles heated","kind":"math","content":"n(NaHCO₃) = 8.4 ÷ 84 = 0.10 mol","note":"M(NaHCO₃) = 84 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Mole bridge","kind":"math","content":"2NaHCO₃ → ... + CO₂, so n(CO₂) = 0.10 ÷ 2 = 0.05 mol","note":"2 : 1 ratio"},{"title":"Volume at STP","kind":"math","content":"V = 0.05 × 22.4 = 1.12 dm³","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2006,31, $q$Which equation represents the slaking of lime?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂"},{"label":"B","text":"Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O"},{"label":"C","text":"CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂"},{"label":"D","text":"CaO + CO₂ → CaCO₃"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Slaking is the exothermic reaction of quicklime, CaO, with water to give slaked lime, Ca(OH)₂. Option C describes making quicklime; option B is limewater absorbing carbon dioxide.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2006,32, $q$Elements in the same group of the periodic table show similar chemical properties because they possess the same$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"number of neutrons"},{"label":"B","text":"number of shells"},{"label":"C","text":"number of electrons in the outermost shell"},{"label":"D","text":"relative atomic masses"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Chemical reactions involve the valence electrons, and members of a group share the same outer-electron count. Shell number and neutron count vary down a group without affecting the pattern.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2006,33, $q$In the reaction 2Na(s) + Cl₂(g) → 2NaCl(s), the total number of electrons transferred is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each sodium atom gives one electron to a chlorine atom, and two sodium atoms take part. The total transfer is therefore 2 electrons — matching the two Na⁺ ions formed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2006,34, $q$Which of the following is NOT a factor affecting the rate of a chemical reaction?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"concentration of the reactants"},{"label":"C","text":"presence of a catalyst"},{"label":"D","text":"the shape of the reaction vessel"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Temperature, concentration, surface area, pressure (for gases) and catalysts all alter collision frequency or effectiveness. The flask geometry — round-bottomed or conical — has no interest beyond convenience.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2006,35, $q$Which metal reacts least vigorously with cold water?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sodium and potassium skim about on the surface, and calcium reacts visibly. Magnesium, though above hydrogen, is barely attacked by cold water — it needs steam to react briskly — because its hydroxide films over the surface.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2006,36, $q$The monomer used to manufacture poly(vinyl chloride), PVC, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethene"},{"label":"B","text":"chloroethane"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"D","text":"chloroethene"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$PVC is the addition polymer of vinyl chloride (chloroethene), CH₂=CHCl, where each unit keeps its chlorine substituent. Chloroethane is saturated and cannot polymerize.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2006,37, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH₂OH is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propan-1-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"propan-2-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"propanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"propanone"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The hydroxyl group sits on the terminal carbon, giving propan-1-ol, a primary alkanol. Placing OH on the middle carbon would switch to propan-2-ol, the secondary isomer.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2007,10, $q$Coarse sand stirred into water is left to stand until the grains settle. The clear water above is then carefully poured off. The technique used is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"decantation"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"distillation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Decantation simply pours off the standing liquid once the solid has settled, avoiding the need for filter paper. It suits coarse, quick-settling solids.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2007,11, $q$The atomic number of an element is the number of ___ in one atom of that element.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"neutrons"},{"label":"B","text":"protons"},{"label":"C","text":"electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"nucleons"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The atomic number Z counts the protons in the nucleus, and it alone fixes the identity of the element. In a neutral atom the electron count equals Z, but it is not the definition.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2007,12, $q$What mass of carbon(IV) oxide is formed when 200 cm³ of 2.0 M hydrochloric acid is poured onto excess marble chips, CaCO₃?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.4 g"},{"label":"B","text":"8.8 g"},{"label":"C","text":"17.6 g"},{"label":"D","text":"22.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Marble dissolves as CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂, so two acid molecules release one CO₂. From 0.40 mol of acid, only 0.20 mol of gas can form — 8.8 g.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles of acid","kind":"math","content":"n(HCl) = 2.0 × 0.200 dm³ = 0.40 mol","note":"excess CaCO₃ means HCl limits the yield"},{"title":"Mole bridge","kind":"math","content":"2HCl → ... + CO₂, so n(CO₂) = 0.40 ÷ 2 = 0.20 mol","note":"acid : gas ratio 2 : 1"},{"title":"Mass of CO₂","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.20 × 44 = 8.8 g","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2007,13, $q$Which of the following will conduct an electric current when dissolved in water?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sucrose (table sugar)"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"C","text":"trioxonitrate(V) acid"},{"label":"D","text":"benzene"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Trioxonitrate(V) acid ionizes fully in water, supplying mobile ions that carry the current. Sugar, ethanol and benzene are covalent and do not ionize, whatever their solubility.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2007,14, $q$The number of electrons in the outermost shell of every halogen is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Group 17 elements have seven valence electrons — one short of a full octet. That shared deficit is why they so eagerly accept an electron and act as oxidizing agents.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2007,15, $q$During the electrolytic refining of copper, the impure copper is made the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"anode"},{"label":"B","text":"cathode"},{"label":"C","text":"electrolyte"},{"label":"D","text":"anode mud"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The impure anode dissolves as Cu²⁺, and pure copper plates onto the cathode. Worthless metals settle below the anode as 'anode mud', where gold and silver are later recovered.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2007,16, $q$Which factor affects the rate of a gaseous reaction but has almost no effect on a reaction occurring in dilute solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"pressure"},{"label":"C","text":"the presence of a catalyst"},{"label":"D","text":"exposure to light"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Gases are highly compressible, so squeezing them packs more molecules into each dm³ and multiplies collisions. Liquids are already incompressible, so added pressure leaves their concentrations essentially unchanged.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2007,17, $q$Sodium metal reacts enthusiastically with cold water, evolving$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(II) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂; the metal darts about on the surface, hissing, and the alkaline solution turns indicator purple. Oxygen and carbon oxides have no part in this simple displacement.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2007,18, $q$The reaction of methane with chlorine in bright sunlight is an example of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"addition"},{"label":"B","text":"polymerization"},{"label":"C","text":"esterification"},{"label":"D","text":"substitution"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$UV light splits Cl₂ into radicals that swap in for hydrogen step by step: CH₄ → CH₃Cl → CH₂Cl₂ and so on. No molecule is added across a bond, since methane has no unsaturation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2007,19, $q$A hot saturated solution of a salt is cooled slowly instead of being evaporated to dryness whenever pure crystals are wanted, because in crystallization$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"impurities remain dissolved in the mother liquor while the salt crystallizes out cleanly"},{"label":"B","text":"the crystals formed are denser than the solution"},{"label":"C","text":"the salt becomes insoluble at high temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"impurities crystallize first and are discarded"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$As the saturated solution cools, the solute deposits as regular crystals, while the impurities — still far below their own saturation limits — stay in the liquid that is afterwards poured off. Evaporation to dryness traps all impurities inside the solid crust.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2007,20, $q$An atom has the electron configuration 2, 8, 7. In the periodic table, it belongs to group$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"I"},{"label":"B","text":"II"},{"label":"C","text":"VII (17)"},{"label":"D","text":"IV (14)"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Seven electrons in the outermost shell mark a halogen of group 17, and Z = 17 identifies chlorine. Group number equals the count of outer electrons for main-group elements.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2007,21, $q$The number of moles of hydrogen atoms present in 0.50 mol of ammonia, NH₃, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.50 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"1.50 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"2.00 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"3.00 mol"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Every ammonia molecule carries three hydrogen atoms, so the atom count is three times the molecule count. That gives 0.50 × 3 = 1.50 mol of H atoms.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Atoms per molecule","kind":"text","content":"Each NH₃ contains 3 H atoms","note":"count the subscripts"},{"title":"Scale up","kind":"math","content":"0.50 mol NH₃ × 3 = 1.50 mol H atoms","note":"matches option B"},{"title":"Check","kind":"text","content":"Three times as many H atoms as molecules","note":"ratio 3 : 1"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2007,22, $q$Either methyl orange or phenolphthalein may be used as the indicator when strong hydrochloric acid is titrated against strong sodium hydroxide, because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"both indicators are weak bases"},{"label":"B","text":"both change colour long before the equivalence point is reached"},{"label":"C","text":"neither changes colour until well past the equivalence point"},{"label":"D","text":"both change colour within the steep pH jump at the equivalence point"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The titration of a strong acid with a strong base gives an almost vertical pH jump spanning roughly pH 4 to 10. Methyl orange (3.1–4.4) and phenolphthalein (8.3–10.0) both fall inside it, so either gives a sharp end point.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2007,23, $q$The element found in period 3, group 2 of the periodic table is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium"},{"label":"C","text":"aluminium"},{"label":"D","text":"silicon"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Period 3 means the electrons fill up to the third shell, and group 2 means two of them are outer. Z = 12 gives the configuration 2, 8, 2: magnesium.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2007,24, $q$In the reaction Zn(s) + H₂SO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + H₂(g), the oxidizing agent is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc metal"},{"label":"B","text":"dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid"},{"label":"C","text":"zinc tetraoxosulphate(VI) formed"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen gas evolved"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Hydrogen ions fall from +1 to 0, gaining electrons, so the acid is reduced — the oxidizing agent. Zinc climbs from 0 to +2 and is the reducing agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2007,25, $q$According to collision theory, two reactant molecules react only when they$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"collide with at least the activation energy and in the correct orientation"},{"label":"B","text":"come into contact regardless of their energies"},{"label":"C","text":"collide along the walls of the reaction vessel"},{"label":"D","text":"are identical in molecular mass"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Only collisions that both exceed the energy barrier and strike the reactive sites produce a reaction — 'effective collisions'. Every other bump simply bounces the molecules apart, no matter how frequent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2007,26, $q$Which property of the alkali metals increases steadily down the group from lithium to caesium?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"first ionization energy"},{"label":"B","text":"atomic radius"},{"label":"C","text":"melting point"},{"label":"D","text":"electronegativity"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each extra shell pushes the frontier of the atom outward, so radius grows down the group. Ionization energy, electronegativity and melting point all run the other way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2007,27, $q$The general molecular formula of the alkynes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂"},{"label":"B","text":"CₙH₂ₙ"},{"label":"C","text":"CₙH₂ₙ₋₂"},{"label":"D","text":"CₙH₂ₙ₋₆"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The triple bond removes two hydrogens from the alkane pattern CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, leaving CₙH₂ₙ₋₂ — ethyne itself is C₂H₂. CₙH₂ₙ₋₆ belongs to the arenes instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2007,28, $q$Within the alkane series, the boiling point rises steadily as the chain lengthens because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the covalent bonds in a longer chain are intrinsically stronger"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen bonds form between the alkane molecules"},{"label":"C","text":"longer chains vaporize more easily"},{"label":"D","text":"longer molecules have larger surface areas, so the van der Waals attractions between them are stronger"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Boiling breaks intermolecular attractions, not covalent bonds, and for open chains the molecular contact area — and therefore the dispersion forces — grows with chain length. Alkanes contain no O, N or F with which to hydrogen-bond.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2007,29, $q$The nucleon (mass) number of an atom is the sum of the numbers of its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"protons and neutrons"},{"label":"B","text":"protons and electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"neutrons and electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"protons only"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Protons and neutrons are the massive particles of the nucleus, so their counts give the mass number. Electrons are so light (about 1/1836 of a proton) that they are ignored in the sum.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2007,30, $q$The number of moles of oxygen required for the complete combustion of 2.5 mol of ethanol, C₂H₅OH, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.0 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"10.0 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"7.5 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"15.0 mol"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The balanced equation C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O needs three oxygen molecules per ethanol. Multiplying: 2.5 × 3 = 7.5 mol of O₂.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Balance the combustion","kind":"text","content":"C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O","note":"oxygen ratio 1 : 3"},{"title":"Mole bridge","kind":"math","content":"n(O₂) = 2.5 × 3 = 7.5 mol","note":"matches option C"},{"title":"Check the balance","kind":"text","content":"C: 2 = 2, H: 6 = 6, O: 1 + 6 = 7 on the left and 4 + 3 = 7 on the right ✓","note":"equation balances"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2007,31, $q$Which salt remains neutral when its solid is dissolved in water?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"potassium trioxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"aluminium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium trioxonitrate(V)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$KNO₃ comes from a strong base and a strong acid, so neither ion feels any urge to hydrolyse. The carbonate salt hydrolyses basic, while the ammonium and aluminium salts hydrolyse acidic.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2007,32, $q$Chlorine is more electronegative than sulphur, its period-3 neighbour, because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine's greater nuclear charge attracts the shared electrons of the same shell more strongly"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine has more occupied shells than sulphur"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine atoms are heavier than sulphur atoms"},{"label":"D","text":"chlorine is normally a gas"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Across a period the shell stays fixed while protons are added one by one. Chlorine's seventeen protons pull the bonding pair harder than sulphur's sixteen can.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2007,33, $q$The conversion of Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ involves the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"gain of one electron"},{"label":"B","text":"loss of two electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"loss of one electron"},{"label":"D","text":"gain of two electrons"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Oxidation is electron loss, and here the charge rises by exactly one unit, from +2 to +3. One electron leaves per ion — no more, no less.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2007,34, $q$In the industrial synthesis of ammonia, a temperature of about 450 °C is maintained even though a lower temperature would give a higher equilibrium yield of ammonia. The reason is that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"higher temperatures actually give better yields in exothermic syntheses"},{"label":"B","text":"the iron catalyst works only at exactly 450 °C"},{"label":"C","text":"the moderate temperature is a compromise between the speed of reaction and the equilibrium yield"},{"label":"D","text":"pressure has no effect on the yield of ammonia"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Below about 400 °C the reaction crawls, starving the plant of output, while the maximum yield belongs to low temperatures. Industry therefore balances rate against yield and leans on pressure and the catalyst to recover what heat costs.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2007,35, $q$Painting an iron gate prevents rusting chiefly because the paint layer$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"excludes oxygen and moisture from the metal surface"},{"label":"B","text":"strengthens the metal beneath"},{"label":"C","text":"neutralizes the carbon(IV) oxide of the atmosphere"},{"label":"D","text":"dissolves any rust already formed"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Rusting requires both air and water touching the iron, and the paint film keeps one of the pair away. Galvanizing goes further by sacrificing zinc, but simple exclusion is what paint achieves.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2007,36, $q$The union of ethene with bromine to form 1,2-dibromoethane is an example of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"substitution"},{"label":"B","text":"addition"},{"label":"C","text":"polymerization"},{"label":"D","text":"esterification"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Two bromine atoms add across the C=C bond, which collapses to a single bond. Addition needs an unsaturated molecule, exactly why alkenes take bromine water so easily.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2007,37, $q$Alkanoates (esters) are widely used as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"detergents"},{"label":"B","text":"dyes"},{"label":"C","text":"flavouring agents and perfumes"},{"label":"D","text":"antiseptics"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Simple esters are volatile liquids with distinctive fruity smells — ethyl ethanoate recalls pineapple. That volatility and scent suit them to flavourings and perfumery.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','separation',2007,38, $q$Powdered galena (PbS) is concentrated from its worthless rocky gangue by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"froth flotation"},{"label":"B","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"paper chromatography"},{"label":"D","text":"sublimation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Pulverized sulphide ore is agitated with water, oil and air: the metal-sulphide particles are wetted by the oily film and ride the bubbles into the froth, while the water-wetted rock sinks. The froth is skimmed off as the concentrate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2008,10, $q$A pure liquid boils at 180 °C at atmospheric pressure but decomposes above 100 °C. The distillation technique that will recover it unchanged is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"distillation under reduced pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"simple distillation at atmospheric pressure"},{"label":"C","text":"evaporation to dryness"},{"label":"D","text":"sublimation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Lowering the applied pressure makes a liquid boil far below its normal boiling point: well below 100 °C here, before decomposition can occur. Thermolabile liquids must always be distilled this way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2008,11, $q$Which subatomic particle carries no electric charge?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"electron"},{"label":"B","text":"neutron"},{"label":"C","text":"proton"},{"label":"D","text":"alpha particle"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The neutron is neutral, adding mass to the nucleus without affecting its charge. An alpha particle, a bundle of two protons and two neutrons, is overall positive.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2008,12, $q$What mass of silver is deposited at the cathode when 0.20 faraday of electricity is passed through silver trioxonitrate(V) solution? (Ag = 108)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10.8 g"},{"label":"B","text":"21.6 g"},{"label":"C","text":"43.2 g"},{"label":"D","text":"108 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag demands exactly one electron per silver ion, so moles deposited equal faradays passed. From 0.20 mol of electrons we get 0.20 mol of silver = 21.6 g.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Half-equation","kind":"text","content":"Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag","note":"one electron per silver ion"},{"title":"Moles deposited","kind":"math","content":"n(Ag) = n(e⁻) = 0.20 mol","note":"1 F = 1 mol of electrons"},{"title":"Mass","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.20 × 108 = 21.6 g","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2008,13, $q$When ammonia gas meets hydrogen chloride gas, the white fumes formed are of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ammonium hydroxide"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonium trioxonitrate(V)"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonium tetraoxosulphate(VI)"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The two gases combine directly: NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s), the dense white smoke of the classic diffusion rate race. No water is needed, so no ammonium hydroxide is involved.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2008,14, $q$Across the third period from sodium to chlorine, the electronegativity$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"decreases"},{"label":"B","text":"remains constant"},{"label":"C","text":"first decreases, then increases"},{"label":"D","text":"increases"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Protons accumulate in the nucleus while the outer shell stays the same distance away and screening changes little. The terminal element, chlorine, is consequently the strongest electron-puller of the period.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2008,15, $q$In the reaction Fe₂O₃(s) + 3CO(g) → 2Fe(l) + 3CO₂(g), the substance that is oxidized is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Fe₂O₃"},{"label":"B","text":"CO"},{"label":"C","text":"Fe"},{"label":"D","text":"CO₂"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Carbon climbs from +2 in CO to +4 in CO₂, so the carbon(II) oxide is oxidized. It therefore serves as the reducing agent while the iron(III) oxide is reduced.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2008,16, $q$A heterogeneous catalyst is most effective when prepared in a form that is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a few large, smooth pieces"},{"label":"B","text":"coated with wax"},{"label":"C","text":"finely divided, presenting a very large surface area"},{"label":"D","text":"of especially high density"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A solid catalyst does its work on its surface, so grinding or sponging it multiplies the active sites per gram. Big smooth lumps bury almost their entire bulk out of play.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2008,17, $q$The metal used as the reducing agent in thermite welding of railway tracks is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iron"},{"label":"B","text":"aluminium"},{"label":"C","text":"copper"},{"label":"D","text":"zinc"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$2Al + Fe₂O₃ → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe races forward with such heat that molten iron runs into the rail joints. Aluminium's strong grip on oxygen is what powers the reaction.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2008,18, $q$When lumps of sodium metal are dropped into ethanol, the gas evolved is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂: sodium displaces the acidic H of the –OH group. Ethanol reacts far more gently than water does because its –OH hydrogen is less exposed than water's.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2008,19, $q$In a simple distillation set-up, the thermometer bulb is placed level with the side arm of the distilling flask so that it shows the temperature of the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"liquid boiling in the flask"},{"label":"B","text":"vapour passing into the condenser"},{"label":"C","text":"cold water in the jacket"},{"label":"D","text":"atmosphere of the laboratory"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The bulb must sit in the vapour stream itself, just leaving for the condenser — that temperature identifies the fraction being collected. Too low and it reads the boiling liquid; too high and it reads nothing useful.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2008,20, $q$The density of carbon(IV) oxide at STP is approximately (M = 44 g/mol)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.50 g dm⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.25 g dm⁻³"},{"label":"C","text":"1.96 g dm⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"4.40 g dm⁻³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Density equals molar mass divided by the molar gas volume: 44 ÷ 22.4 ≈ 1.96 g/dm³. Option B belongs to the gas of molar mass 28, such as nitrogen.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2008,21, $q$Aluminium hydroxide, Al(OH)₃, dissolves both in dilute hydrochloric acid and in sodium hydroxide solution because it is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"amphoteric"},{"label":"B","text":"a strong alkali"},{"label":"C","text":"an acidic hydroxide"},{"label":"D","text":"a neutral hydroxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$As a base it neutralizes the acid; as an acid it dissolves in excess OH⁻ to form the aluminate complex, Al(OH)₄⁻. This two-way behaviour, shared by Zn(OH)₂, defines the amphoteric class.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2008,22, $q$The electron configuration of the magnesium ion, Mg²⁺, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2, 8, 1"},{"label":"B","text":"2, 7"},{"label":"C","text":"2, 8, 2"},{"label":"D","text":"2, 8"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The neutral atom is 2, 8, 2; the ion has shed its two outer electrons to copy the neon configuration. Option C is the neutral magnesium atom.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2008,23, $q$In the ordinary dry (Leclanché) cell, the negative electrode is made of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc"},{"label":"B","text":"copper"},{"label":"C","text":"graphite"},{"label":"D","text":"lead"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The zinc casing itself is the anode being consumed, while the central carbon rod only collects current. The electrolyte is a paste of ammonium chloride — hence the name 'dry'.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2008,24, $q$The number of molecules present in 22 g of carbon(IV) oxide is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.02×10²³"},{"label":"B","text":"3.01×10²³"},{"label":"C","text":"1.204×10²³"},{"label":"D","text":"6.02×10²²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Half a molar mass means half a mole: n = 22 ÷ 44 = 0.50 mol. The molecule count is 0.50 × 6.02×10²³ = 3.01×10²³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2008,25, $q$As magnesium ribbon dissolves in excess dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, the rate of reaction is followed most directly by recording$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the final volume of hydrogen collected after one hour"},{"label":"B","text":"the mass of acid remaining at the end"},{"label":"C","text":"the colour of the solution"},{"label":"D","text":"the volume of hydrogen evolved per minute"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Rate is the change in a measurable quantity per unit time — here gas volume per minute. A single reading, however early or late, gives only an amount, not a rate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2008,26, $q$Metals can be hammered into sheets (malleability) because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"their particles are joined by directional bonds that forbid any sliding"},{"label":"B","text":"their electrons are bound tightly to individual atoms"},{"label":"C","text":"the ions in them drift freely toward the electrodes"},{"label":"D","text":"their metallic bonding is non-directional, so layers of cations can slip without breaking the bond"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$When one layer of positive cores slides over another, the sea of delocalized electrons simply re-adjusts and keeps the metal intact. Comparable sliding in an ionic crystal brings like charges face to face — and the crystal shatters.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2008,27, $q$Ethanol is warmed with excess acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI) solution, and the volatile product is distilled from the flask as it forms. The distillate is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanal"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"ethyl ethanoate"},{"label":"D","text":"ethene"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Distilling the volatile aldehyde (bp 20 °C) away as it forms keeps it out of reach of the excess oxidant, which would otherwise carry the oxidation on to ethanoic acid. Refluxing the same mixture in the flask is exactly how the acid is made.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2008,28, $q$A sample of 13.0 g of zinc dissolves completely in excess dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid. The mass of zinc tetraoxosulphate(VI), ZnSO₄ (M = 161 g mol⁻¹), formed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"16.1 g"},{"label":"B","text":"32.2 g"},{"label":"C","text":"64.4 g"},{"label":"D","text":"161 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂ pairs zinc and the salt 1 : 1. The 13.0 g is 0.20 mol of zinc, so 0.20 mol × 161 g mol⁻¹ = 32.2 g of salt results.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moles of zinc","kind":"math","content":"n(Zn) = 13.0 ÷ 65 = 0.20 mol","note":"M(Zn) = 65 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Mole bridge","kind":"text","content":"Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂, so n(ZnSO₄) = 0.20 mol","note":"1 : 1 ratio"},{"title":"Mass of salt","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.20 × 161 = 32.2 g","note":"matches option B"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2008,29, $q$The number of electrons in the nitride ion, N³⁻ (Z = 7), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"7"},{"label":"B","text":"13"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The three minus signs mean three electrons were gained: 7 + 3 = 10, the neon configuration. Ionization touches electrons only — the nucleus and the mass number are untouched.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2008,30, $q$Which halogen is the strongest oxidizing agent?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fluorine"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"C","text":"bromine"},{"label":"D","text":"iodine"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Oxidizing power fades down the group as atoms grow larger and their pull on electrons weakens. Fluorine will even snatch electrons from water's own oxygen, a feat chlorine cannot match.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2008,31, $q$Solutions of hydrochloric acid and ethanoic acid of the same molar concentration differ in pH because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"HCl is fully ionized in water while ethanoic acid is only slightly ionized"},{"label":"B","text":"the two acids have different relative molecular masses"},{"label":"C","text":"the ethanoic acid solution is more concentrated than it is labelled"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanoic acid contains no ionizable hydrogen at all"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Equal molarity does not mean equal H₃O⁺: the weak acid holds most of its protons in reserve as undissociated molecules. Strength — not molar mass or concentration — sets the hydrogen ion level, hence the pH.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2008,32, $q$The oxidation number of chlorine in sodium trioxochlorate(V), NaClO₃, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+1"},{"label":"B","text":"+7"},{"label":"C","text":"+5"},{"label":"D","text":"−1"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The balance reads (+1) + x + 3(−2) = 0, giving x = +5, already disclosed in the name. These chlorate(V) ions power match-heads and bleach by redox chemistry.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2008,33, $q$Which of these metals is extracted by heating its oxide with coke in a furnace?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc"},{"label":"B","text":"aluminium"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Carbon can strip oxygen from metal oxides up to about zinc in the activity series. Aluminium, sodium and calcium sit above that line, so only electrolysis (or more powerful reducing agents) frees them.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2008,34, $q$For the equilibrium N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) established inside a hot engine cylinder, changing the total pressure of the mixture$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"causes no shift, because gas moles are equal on the two sides"},{"label":"B","text":"shifts the equilibrium to the right"},{"label":"C","text":"shifts the equilibrium to the left"},{"label":"D","text":"changes the value of the equilibrium constant"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Two moles of gas face two moles of gas, so pressure cannot tilt the balance either way. Temperature is what moves NO formation — hotter cylinders mean more nitric oxide escaping up the exhaust.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2008,35, $q$Which of the following is a saturated hydrocarbon?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"C₂H₄"},{"label":"B","text":"C₂H₂"},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₆"},{"label":"D","text":"C₆H₆"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ethane already holds the maximum hydrogen its two carbons can carry, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, so nothing can be added. Ethene, ethyne and benzene all leave spare bonding capacity open to addition.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','organic',2008,36, $q$The functional group in ethylamine (aminoethane), C₂H₅NH₂, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"—OH"},{"label":"B","text":"—COOH"},{"label":"C","text":"—NH₂"},{"label":"D","text":"—CHO"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The amino group, —NH₂, marks an amine; it grabs H⁺ from acids to form salts such as ethylammonium chloride. Alkanols, acids and alkanals wear different badges entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2008,37, $q$A gas occupies 200 cm³ at 27 °C. Keeping the pressure constant, its volume at 54 °C is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"400 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"200 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"183 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"218 cm³"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Charles' law speaks in kelvin: the temperatures are 300 K and 327 K, not 27 and 54. So V₂ = 200 × 327/300 ≈ 218 cm³ — a modest 9% swelling, not a doubling.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Convert to kelvin","kind":"math","content":"T₁ = 27 + 273 = 300 K, T₂ = 54 + 273 = 327 K","note":"gas laws require absolute temperature"},{"title":"Apply Charles' law","kind":"math","content":"V₂ = V₁ × T₂/T₁ = 200 × 327/300 ≈ 218 cm³","note":"matches option D"},{"title":"Trap","kind":"text","content":"Using °C directly would wrongly suggest 400 cm³","note":"the classic error"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2008,38, $q$CH₃COCH₃ is classified as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an alkanal (aldehyde)"},{"label":"B","text":"an alkanoate (ester)"},{"label":"C","text":"an alkanol"},{"label":"D","text":"an alkanone (ketone)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The carbonyl carbon sits inside the chain with no H attached — the ketone pattern, named propanone. Move that C=O to the chain end and it would be the alkanal propanal.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);

-- Past Graph :: chemistry 2009-2011 (continuation rows, qnum 10+)

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2009,10, $q$The best method for separating a mixture of sodium chloride crystals and iodine crystals is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"filtration"},{"label":"B","text":"decantation"},{"label":"C","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"sublimation"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Iodine sublimes on gentle warming, leaving the non-volatile sodium chloride behind; the iodine vapour is collected on a cold surface. Filtration and decantation need a solvent, and both solids here are soluble in the same liquids only in different ways.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2009,11, $q$An element X has atomic number 11. The electronic configuration of X is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁴ 3s³"},{"label":"B","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s²"},{"label":"C","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁵ 3s²"},{"label":"D","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Eleven electrons fill 1s², 2s² and 2p⁶ (ten electrons) with the last electron entering 3s. Element X is therefore sodium.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2009,12, $q$Which of the following pairs of substances are NOT allotropes?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"rhombic and monoclinic sulphur"},{"label":"B","text":"diamond and graphite"},{"label":"C","text":"oxygen and ozone"},{"label":"D","text":"water and ice"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Allotropes are different structural forms of the same element in the same physical state. Water and ice are the same compound, differing only in physical state.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2009,13, $q$10.0 g of calcium trioxocarbonate(IV), CaCO₃ (M = 100 g mol⁻¹), reacts completely with excess dilute hydrochloric acid. The mass of carbon(IV) oxide evolved is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.2 g"},{"label":"B","text":"4.4 g"},{"label":"C","text":"8.8 g"},{"label":"D","text":"10.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n(CaCO₃) = 10.0 ÷ 100 = 0.10 mol, and the CaCO₃ : CO₂ ratio is 1 : 1 (CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂). Mass of CO₂ = 0.10 × 44 = 4.4 g.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2009,14, $q$The pH of a 0.001 mol dm⁻³ solution of hydrochloric acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$HCl is a strong acid and ionizes completely, so [H⁺] = 1 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³. pH = −log(10⁻³) = 3.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2009,15, $q$The oxidation number of sulphur in tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H₂SO₄, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+2"},{"label":"B","text":"+4"},{"label":"C","text":"+6"},{"label":"D","text":"+8"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Two H atoms contribute +2 and four O atoms contribute −8. For the molecule to be neutral, sulphur must be +6, which the (VI) naming also records.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2009,16, $q$N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹. Increasing the temperature of this system at equilibrium will$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shift the equilibrium to the right"},{"label":"B","text":"shift the equilibrium to the left"},{"label":"C","text":"have no effect on the position of equilibrium"},{"label":"D","text":"increase the concentration of ammonia"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The forward reaction is exothermic, so added heat favours the backward (endothermic) direction. The equilibrium shifts left and the ammonia yield falls, even though the rate rises.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2009,17, $q$Brass is an alloy of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"copper and tin"},{"label":"B","text":"copper and zinc"},{"label":"C","text":"lead and tin"},{"label":"D","text":"iron and carbon"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Brass is copper–zinc; copper–tin is bronze, lead–tin is solder, and iron–carbon is steel. Alloy composition is a standard mending of metals in industry.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2009,18, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH₂OH is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propan-1-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"propan-2-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"propanone"},{"label":"D","text":"propanoic acid"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The three-carbon chain carries the –OH group on the first (terminal) carbon, giving propan-1-ol. The locant 1 marks the position of the hydroxyl group.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2009,19, $q$How many structural isomers has pentane, C₅H₁₂?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$They are n-pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane. Each has a different carbon skeleton, which is chain isomerism.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2009,20, $q$25.0 cm³ of 0.10 M sodium hydroxide solution is neutralized by 25.0 cm³ of 0.05 M tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the acid is dibasic, needing only half the moles of acid for the alkali present"},{"label":"B","text":"the alkali and the acid have equal molarities"},{"label":"C","text":"the acid is a weak acid"},{"label":"D","text":"the alkali is dibasic"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n(NaOH) = 0.025 × 0.10 = 0.0025 mol; H₂SO₄ is dibasic, so n(H₂SO₄) needed = 0.00125 mol, i.e. 25.0 cm³ of 0.05 M acid. The 1 : 2 mole ratio exactly matches the 0.10 M : 0.05 M concentration ratio.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2009,21, $q$2.50 g of a sample of CuSO₄·xH₂O was heated to constant mass, and 1.60 g of anhydrous CuSO₄ (M = 160 g mol⁻¹) remained. The value of x is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"5"},{"label":"D","text":"7"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Water driven off = 2.50 − 1.60 = 0.90 g, which is 0.05 mol. Anhydrous CuSO₄ = 1.60 ÷ 160 = 0.01 mol, so CuSO₄ : H₂O = 0.01 : 0.05 = 1 : 5 and x = 5 (blue vitriol).$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Water of crystallization lost","kind":"math","content":"Mass of water removed = 2.50 g − 1.60 g = 0.90 g. Moles of H₂O = 0.90 ÷ 18 = 0.05 mol.","note":"Heating to constant mass means all the water of crystallization has been expelled."},{"title":"Moles of anhydrous salt","kind":"math","content":"Moles of CuSO₄ = 1.60 g ÷ 160 g mol⁻¹ = 0.01 mol.","note":"Cu = 64, S = 32, O₄ = 64, giving M = 160 g mol⁻¹."},{"title":"Take the mole ratio","kind":"text","content":"CuSO₄ : H₂O = 0.01 : 0.05 = 1 : 5, so the formula is CuSO₄·5H₂O.","note":"This is the familiar blue crystalline salt used in the test for water."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2009,22, $q$A current of 4.0 A is passed through copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution for 1,930 s using suitable electrodes (F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹; Cu = 64). The mass of copper deposited at the cathode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.28 g"},{"label":"B","text":"2.56 g"},{"label":"C","text":"5.12 g"},{"label":"D","text":"8.64 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Q = It = 4.0 × 1,930 = 7,720 C, which is 7,720 ÷ 96,500 = 0.08 faraday of electrons. Since Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, n(Cu) = 0.04 mol and m = 0.04 × 64 = 2.56 g.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge passed","kind":"math","content":"Q = It = 4.0 A × 1,930 s = 7,720 C.","note":"m = ZIt always begins with the charge Q = It."},{"title":"Convert to faradays","kind":"math","content":"n(e⁻) = Q ÷ F = 7,720 ÷ 96,500 = 0.08 mol of electrons.","note":"F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹ is the charge on one mole of electrons."},{"title":"Apply the electrode ratio and mass","kind":"math","content":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so n(Cu) = 0.08 ÷ 2 = 0.04 mol; m = 0.04 × 64 = 2.56 g.","note":"The 2 : 1 electron-to-copper ratio is fixed by the ion charge."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','organic',2009,23, $q$A liquid effervesces with sodium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV) solution, giving a gas that turns limewater milky. The liquid most likely contains the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carboxylic acid group"},{"label":"B","text":"aldehyde group"},{"label":"C","text":"hydroxyl group"},{"label":"D","text":"ester group"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$NaHCO₃ reacts with acids: CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂, and the CO₂ turns limewater milky. Only the –COOH group is acidic enough for this test.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2009,24, $q$The type of salt formed when ALL the replaceable hydrogen ions of an acid are completely replaced by metal ions is called a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"normal salt"},{"label":"B","text":"acid salt"},{"label":"C","text":"basic salt"},{"label":"D","text":"double salt"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Complete replacement of the ionizable hydrogen gives a normal salt, e.g. Na₂SO₄ from H₂SO₄. Partial replacement would give an acid salt, such as NaHSO₄.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2009,25, $q$Chlorine has two isotopes of masses 35 and 37 occurring in the ratio 3 : 1. The relative atomic mass of chlorine is therefore$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"35"},{"label":"B","text":"35.5"},{"label":"C","text":"36.0"},{"label":"D","text":"37"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$R.A.M. = (35 × 3 + 37 × 1) ÷ 4 = 142 ÷ 4 = 35.5. The quoted atomic mass of any element is this weighted average of its isotopes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2009,26, $q$In paper chromatography, the Rf value of a component is defined as the ratio of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the distance moved by the solvent front to the distance moved by the component"},{"label":"B","text":"the distance moved by the component to the distance moved by the solvent front"},{"label":"C","text":"the mass of the component to the mass of the solvent"},{"label":"D","text":"the volume of solvent used to the time of separation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Rf = distance moved by the component ÷ distance moved by the solvent front, and it is always less than 1. It is a constant for a given paper, solvent and temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2009,27, $q$A catalyst increases the rate of a chemical reaction by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"raising the temperature of the reactants"},{"label":"B","text":"providing an alternative path of lower activation energy"},{"label":"C","text":"increasing the concentration of the reactants"},{"label":"D","text":"increasing the number of moles of products"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The catalyst offers a new reaction route with a lower activation energy, so more colliding particles possess the minimum energy needed. It is not consumed in the process.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2009,28, $q$In the industrial manufacture of ammonia by the Haber process, the catalyst used is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"copper"},{"label":"B","text":"vanadium(V) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"nickel"},{"label":"D","text":"finely divided iron"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Finely divided iron (with promoters) is used at about 450 °C and 200 atm. Vanadium(V) oxide belongs to the contact process and nickel to catalytic hydrogenation of oils.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2009,29, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CHClCOOH is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chloropropanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"2-chloropropanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"3-chloropropanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"propanoyl chloride"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The parent chain is propanoic acid and the chlorine is attached to carbon-2, the middle carbon counted from the –COOH end. Hence 2-chloropropanoic acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2009,30, $q$Reduction is correctly defined as the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"gain of electrons with an increase in oxidation number"},{"label":"B","text":"loss of electrons with a decrease in oxidation number"},{"label":"C","text":"gain of electrons with a decrease in oxidation number"},{"label":"D","text":"loss of electrons with an increase in oxidation number"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A reduced species accepts electrons, so its oxidation number falls, e.g. MnO₄⁻ (+7) → Mn²⁺ (+2). Electron loss with an increased oxidation number is oxidation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2009,31, $q$Across period 3 of the periodic table (Na → Ar), the atomic radius progressively decreases because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shielding by inner electrons increases"},{"label":"B","text":"the outer electrons enter a new shell"},{"label":"C","text":"the relative atomic masses decrease"},{"label":"D","text":"the nuclear charge increases while the added electrons enter the same outermost shell"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$From Na to Ar, electrons are added to the same third shell while the nuclear charge rises from +11 to +18. The stronger pull of the nucleus draws the shell in, so the radius shrinks.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2009,32, $q$The number of molecules present in 8.0 g of oxygen gas is (Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.505 × 10²³"},{"label":"B","text":"3.01 × 10²³"},{"label":"C","text":"6.02 × 10²³"},{"label":"D","text":"1.204 × 10²⁴"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n(O₂) = 8.0 ÷ 32 = 0.25 mol, and molecules = 0.25 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.505 × 10²³. Remember the molar mass 32 g mol⁻¹, not 16.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2009,33, $q$Why is an aqueous solution of sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) alkaline?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"The carbonate ion undergoes hydrolysis, generating excess hydroxide ions"},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium ions react with water to form alkali"},{"label":"C","text":"The solution absorbs carbon(IV) oxide from the air"},{"label":"D","text":"The salt is a strong electrolyte"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Na₂CO₃ is the salt of a strong base and a weak acid, so its anion hydrolyses water. CO₃²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻ leaves excess OH⁻ and a pH above 7.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Identify the parent acid and base","kind":"text","content":"Na₂CO₃ comes from NaOH (a strong base) and H₂CO₃ (a weak acid).","note":"Salts of strong bases and weak acids are alkaline in water."},{"title":"Write the hydrolysis","kind":"text","content":"CO₃²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻: the carbonate ion grabs a proton from water.","note":"The alkaline solution turns red litmus blue."},{"title":"Conclude the acidity class","kind":"text","content":"Excess OH⁻ makes pH > 7, so the aqueous salt is alkaline.","note":"Salts of strong acids and strong bases, like NaCl, stay neutral."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2009,34, $q$The dissolved substance mainly responsible for the temporary hardness of water is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"calcium and magnesium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonates(IV)"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium and magnesium tetraoxosulphates(VI)"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"iron(III) oxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Temporary hardness is caused by Ca(HCO₃)₂ and Mg(HCO₃)₂. Boiling decomposes them: Ca(HCO₃)₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O + CO₂, removing the hardness; the sulphates cause permanent hardness.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2009,35, $q$The ester formed when ethanol reacts with ethanoic acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methyl ethanoate"},{"label":"B","text":"ethyl ethanoate"},{"label":"C","text":"ethyl propanoate"},{"label":"D","text":"propyl ethanoate"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The equation is CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O, using concentrated H₂SO₄ as catalyst. The alkyl group of the alcohol (ethyl) is named first, followed by the alkanoate (ethanoate).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2009,36, $q$During the electrolysis of concentrated sodium chloride solution (brine) using inert electrodes, the product liberated at the cathode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium metal"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine gas"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen gas"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In aqueous solution, H⁺ from water is discharged in preference to Na⁺, so H₂ evolves at the cathode. Sodium metal is only obtained by electrolyzing molten NaCl.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2009,37, $q$For the reaction 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH negative. Increasing the total pressure of the system will$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shift the equilibrium to the right, increasing the yield of SO₃"},{"label":"B","text":"shift the equilibrium to the left, decreasing the yield of SO₃"},{"label":"C","text":"have no effect on the position of equilibrium"},{"label":"D","text":"decompose the sulphur(VI) oxide formed"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$There are 3 gaseous molecules on the left and 2 on the right, so higher pressure favours the side with fewer molecules. The equilibrium shifts right towards SO₃.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2009,38, $q$The major ore from which aluminium is extracted is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"haematite"},{"label":"B","text":"cassiterite"},{"label":"C","text":"bauxite"},{"label":"D","text":"galena"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Bauxite is Al₂O₃·2H₂O; haematite is Fe₂O₃, cassiterite is SnO₂ and galena is PbS. Aluminium is obtained from purified bauxite by electrolysis in molten cryolite.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2010,10, $q$A mixture of ethanol (b.p. 78 °C) and water (b.p. 100 °C) can best be separated by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"decantation"},{"label":"C","text":"paper chromatography"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Ethanol boils 22 °C below water, so a fractionating column separates them by boiling point difference. Decantation and chromatography do not apply to completely miscible liquids like these.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2010,11, $q$An atom has 17 protons, 17 electrons and 20 neutrons. Its mass number is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"17"},{"label":"B","text":"20"},{"label":"C","text":"34"},{"label":"D","text":"37"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Mass number = protons + neutrons = 17 + 20 = 37. This species is the chlorine-37 isotope; electrons do not count in the mass number.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2010,12, $q$Which of the following molecules has a pyramidal shape because of a lone pair of electrons on the central atom?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CH₄"},{"label":"B","text":"BF₃"},{"label":"C","text":"CO₂"},{"label":"D","text":"NH₃"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In ammonia, nitrogen carries one lone pair that repels the three N–H bond pairs, giving a pyramidal shape with a bond angle of about 107°. CH₄ is tetrahedral and CO₂ is linear.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2010,13, $q$What volume of hydrogen is liberated at S.T.P. when 6.5 g of zinc (M = 65 g mol⁻¹) dissolves completely in excess dilute hydrochloric acid? (1 mol of gas occupies 22.4 dm³ at S.T.P.)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.56 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"4.48 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"2.24 dm³"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$n(Zn) = 6.5 ÷ 65 = 0.1 mol, and Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ gives a 1 : 1 ratio. V = 0.1 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³ at S.T.P.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2010,14, $q$The most suitable indicator for the titration of a strong acid with a weak base is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methyl orange"},{"label":"B","text":"phenolphthalein"},{"label":"C","text":"universal indicator"},{"label":"D","text":"phenol red"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The equivalence point of a strong acid–weak base titration lies on the acidic side (pH below about 6), so methyl orange (range 3.1–4.4) is appropriate. Phenolphthalein suits weak acid–strong base titrations.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2010,15, $q$In the reaction 2FeCl₃ + SnCl₂ → 2FeCl₂ + SnCl₄, the species that is oxidized is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Fe³⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"Sn²⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"Sn⁴⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"Fe²⁺"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Tin(II) loses two electrons: Sn²⁺ → Sn⁴⁺ + 2e⁻, so it is the reducing species and is oxidized. Fe³⁺ simultaneously gains an electron and is reduced to Fe²⁺.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2010,16, $q$The rate of most chemical reactions increases with a rise in temperature mainly because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a greater fraction of the colliding molecules possesses energy equal to or greater than the activation energy"},{"label":"B","text":"the activation energy of the reaction is lowered"},{"label":"C","text":"the concentration of the reactants increases"},{"label":"D","text":"the reaction becomes more exothermic"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Heating raises the average kinetic energy of the molecules, so many more collisions are effective. The activation energy itself is unchanged unless a catalyst is added.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2010,17, $q$Which metal is extracted industrially by the electrolytic reduction of its molten oxide dissolved in cryolite?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"copper"},{"label":"B","text":"zinc"},{"label":"C","text":"aluminium"},{"label":"D","text":"mercury"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Purified Al₂O₃ is dissolved in molten cryolite to lower the melting point from about 2,000 °C to about 850 °C, then electrolyzed at Hall-Héroult cells. Copper and zinc are won by reduction of their ores in furnaces.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2010,18, $q$Which of the following reagents can be used to distinguish between an aliphatic aldehyde and a ketone?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV) solution"},{"label":"B","text":"bromine water"},{"label":"C","text":"Tollen's reagent"},{"label":"D","text":"universal indicator"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ammoniacal silver oxide (Tollen's reagent) gives a silver mirror with aldehydes but not with ketones. The aldehyde group is easily oxidized; the ketone is not.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2010,19, $q$The functional group present in CH₃CH₂COOH is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"aldehyde group"},{"label":"B","text":"hydroxyl group"},{"label":"C","text":"ester group"},{"label":"D","text":"carboxyl group"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The –COOH group is the carboxyl group and marks the compound as an alkanoic (carboxylic) acid, here propanoic acid. It is responsible for the compound's acidity and reaction with NaHCO₃.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2010,20, $q$20.0 cm³ of hydrochloric acid of unknown concentration neutralizes 25.0 cm³ of 0.10 M sodium hydroxide solution. The concentration of the acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.100 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.125 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.200 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.250 M"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n(NaOH) = 0.025 × 0.10 = 0.0025 mol, and HCl : NaOH is 1 : 1. M(HCl) = 0.0025 ÷ 0.020 = 0.125 mol dm⁻³.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of standard alkali","kind":"math","content":"n(NaOH) = 0.10 mol dm⁻³ × 0.0250 dm³ = 0.0025 mol.","note":"Convert the volume from cm³ to dm³ before multiplying."},{"title":"Use the neutralization ratio","kind":"text","content":"HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O, so n(HCl) reacting = 0.0025 mol.","note":"For monobasic acid against NaOH the mole ratio is 1 : 1."},{"title":"Back-calculate the acid concentration","kind":"math","content":"M(HCl) = 0.0025 ÷ 0.0200 = 0.125 mol dm⁻³.","note":"Divide the moles that reacted by the acid volume in dm³."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2010,21, $q$How many moles of carbon(IV) oxide are produced when one mole of propane, C₃H₈, is completely combusted?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Balancing C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O shows three carbon atoms each ending up in one CO₂ molecule. Hence 1 mol of propane yields 3 mol of CO₂.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2010,22, $q$Aluminium does not (normally) react with concentrated trioxonitrate(V) acid because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"it is a very hard metal"},{"label":"B","text":"its ionization energy is too high for any reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"a thin oxide film forms on its surface and protects it"},{"label":"D","text":"it is an amphoteric metal"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Concentrated HNO₃ is a strong oxidizing agent that rapidly coats aluminium with an impervious Al₂O₃ film; the metal becomes passive. This passivation is why nitric acid can be stored in aluminium containers.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2010,23, $q$An aqueous solution of sodium ethanoate will turn red litmus blue because the salt$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"is acidic in nature"},{"label":"B","text":"is a strong electrolyte"},{"label":"C","text":"is hydrolysed in water to give excess hydroxide ions"},{"label":"D","text":"contains water of crystallization"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻. The ethanoate ion, being the conjugate base of a weak acid, abstracts protons from water and leaves excess OH⁻, so the solution is alkaline.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2010,24, $q$Which gas decolourizes acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) solution from purple to colourless?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"oxygen"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$SO₂ is a good reducing agent: it cuts Mn from +7 to +2, destroying the purple colour. CO₂ and O₂ have no reducing action on permanganate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2010,25, $q$The bond angle in a molecule of water is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"90°"},{"label":"B","text":"109.5°"},{"label":"C","text":"104.5°"},{"label":"D","text":"120°"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The two lone pairs on oxygen repel the two O–H bond pairs more strongly than bond pairs repel each other, compressing the angle from the tetrahedral 109.5° to about 104.5°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2010,26, $q$The best way of obtaining pure, well-formed crystals of a soluble salt from its hot saturated solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"evaporation to complete dryness"},{"label":"B","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"C","text":"decantation"},{"label":"D","text":"allowing slow crystallization as the solution cools"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Slow cooling of a hot saturated solution deposits crystals while impurities remain dissolved in the small quantity of mother liquor. Evaporation to dryness traps impurities and may decompose hydrated salts.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2010,27, $q$During the electrolysis of aqueous copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution using platinum electrodes, the reaction at the cathode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu"},{"label":"B","text":"4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂"},{"label":"D","text":"Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Cu²⁺ is less difficult to discharge than H⁺ from water, so copper plates out: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. Oxygen evolution (option B) belongs to the anode, and option D is an oxidation, which never occurs at a cathode.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2010,28, $q$Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are related as isomers showing$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"positional isomerism"},{"label":"B","text":"chain isomerism"},{"label":"C","text":"functional isomerism"},{"label":"D","text":"geometrical isomerism"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Both have the same carbon skeleton (C₃H₇–) but the –OH group sits on carbon 1 in one and carbon 2 in the other. Moving a group along an unchanged chain is positional isomerism.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2010,29, $q$The flame colour given by a sodium salt in a non-luminous Bunsen flame is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lilac"},{"label":"B","text":"brick red"},{"label":"C","text":"golden yellow"},{"label":"D","text":"apple green"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Sodium colors the flame golden (pale) yellow. Potassium is lilac, calcium is brick red and copper gives a blue-green flame — the basis of the flame test.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2010,30, $q$The number of electrons in the M-shell of an atom of potassium (Z = 19) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"9"},{"label":"C","text":"18"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Potassium is filled 2, 8, 8, 1: the first, second and third shells hold 2, 8 and 8 electrons, with the nineteenth electron in the N-shell. Hence the M-shell carries 8 electrons.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2010,31, $q$100 cm³ of hydrogen is mixed with 60 cm³ of oxygen and the mixture sparked, all volumes being measured at the same temperature and pressure. 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g). The volume of steam formed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"120 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"100 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"200 cm³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The equation shows 2 volumes of H₂ give 2 volumes of steam, so 100 cm³ of H₂ (which consumes only 50 cm³ of O₂) is the limiting gas. Steam formed = 100 cm³, and 10 cm³ of O₂ remains unreacted.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2010,32, $q$A solid which loses all or part of its water of crystallization on exposure to the atmosphere is said to be$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hygroscopic"},{"label":"B","text":"deliquescent"},{"label":"C","text":"efflorescent"},{"label":"D","text":"anhydrous"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Efflorescent substances, such as Na₂CO₃·10H₂O, give up water of crystallization to the air and crumble to powder. Deliquescent substances (like NaOH) instead absorb water until they dissolve; hygroscopic ones merely take up moisture.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2010,33, $q$A charge of 28,950 C is passed through molten aluminium oxide in cryolite (F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹; Al = 27). The mass of aluminium liberated is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.9 g"},{"label":"B","text":"1.8 g"},{"label":"C","text":"2.7 g"},{"label":"D","text":"8.1 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$28,950 ÷ 96,500 = 0.3 F of electrons. Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al, so n(Al) = 0.3 ÷ 3 = 0.1 mol = 0.1 × 27 = 2.7 g.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge in faradays","kind":"math","content":"n(e⁻) = Q ÷ F = 28,950 ÷ 96,500 = 0.3 mol of electrons.","note":"Dividing the Coulombs by the Faraday constant gives moles of electrons."},{"title":"Use the ion charge","kind":"text","content":"Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al, so 3 mol e⁻ deposit 1 mol Al.","note":"Hence n(Al) = 0.3 ÷ 3 = 0.1 mol."},{"title":"Convert to mass","kind":"math","content":"m = n × M = 0.1 × 27 = 2.7 g.","note":"Equivalently m = ZIt with Z = M ÷ (nF) = 27 ÷ 3 = 9 g per faraday-equivalent."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2010,34, $q$In the Haber process, NH₃(g) formed is continuously removed from N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g). This practice increases the yield of ammonia because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"it shifts the equilibrium position to the left"},{"label":"B","text":"removing the product forces the equilibrium to shift forward to replace it"},{"label":"C","text":"the rate of the backward reaction increases"},{"label":"D","text":"it raises the activation energy of the backward reaction"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$By Le Chatelier's principle, deleting a product shifts the equilibrium towards more product. Continual removal keeps the forward reaction going and raises the overall conversion.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2010,35, $q$The product of the reaction between benzene and bromine in the presence of iron(III) bromide is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bromobenzene"},{"label":"B","text":"1,2-dibromocyclohexane"},{"label":"C","text":"bromocyclohexane"},{"label":"D","text":"benzyl bromide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$C₆H₆ + Br₂ →(FeBr₃) C₆H₅Br + HBr is an electrophilic substitution: a ring H is replaced by Br. Ring addition would destroy the stable aromatic system, which benzene resists.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2010,36, $q$Iron sheets used for roofing are coated with zinc by galvanizing in order to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"improve the appearance and strength of the iron"},{"label":"B","text":"make the iron a better electrical conductor"},{"label":"C","text":"prevent rusting, since zinc corrodes in place of the iron"},{"label":"D","text":"harden the surface of the iron"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Zinc is more reactive (electropositive) than iron and corrodes preferentially, protecting the iron even if the coating is scratched. This is sacrificial (electrochemical) protection.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2010,37, $q$When ethyl ethanoate is boiled with aqueous sodium hydroxide, the organic products are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanoic acid and ethanol"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium ethanoate and ethanol"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium ethanoate and ethanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanol and water"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Base hydrolysis of an ester (saponification) cleaves the ester to the alcohol and the sodium salt of the acid: CH₃COOC₂H₅ + NaOH → CH₃COONa + C₂H₅OH. The acid itself is only released on later acidification.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Recognize saponification conditions","kind":"text","content":"Boiling an ester with aqueous NaOH is hydrolysis under alkaline conditions.","note":"With steam/H⁺ the free acid is formed; with NaOH the salt is formed."},{"title":"Split the ester at the C–O bond","kind":"text","content":"CH₃COOC₂H₅ breaks into CH₃COO⁻ (with Na⁺) and C₂H₅OH.","note":"The acid residue travels with Na⁺; the alkoxy part becomes the alcohol."},{"title":"Name the products","kind":"text","content":"Products: sodium ethanoate (the soap-type salt) and ethanol.","note":"Acidifying later would convert the salt into ethanoic acid."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','separation',2010,38, $q$The technique most suitable for separating chalk dust suspended in water is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"evaporation"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"sublimation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Filtration separates an insoluble solid (residue on the filter paper) from the liquid that passes through (the filtrate). Evaporation would recover the solute, not remove suspended matter.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2011,10, $q$A mixture of groundnut oil and water can best be separated using a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"separating funnel"},{"label":"B","text":"filter paper"},{"label":"C","text":"distillation flask"},{"label":"D","text":"centrifuge"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Oil and water are immiscible and form two layers, so a separating funnel allows the denser water to be run off first. Filtration and distillation do not separate immiscible liquid pairs.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2011,11, $q$Atoms of the same element having the same atomic number but different mass numbers are called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"isobars"},{"label":"B","text":"isotopes"},{"label":"C","text":"allotropes"},{"label":"D","text":"isomers"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Isotopes differ only in the number of neutrons, hence in mass number, e.g. Cl-35 and Cl-37. Isomers are different compounds, and allotropes are forms of the same element.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2011,12, $q$The bond in potassium fluoride, KF, is formed by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the sharing of a pair of electrons between K and F"},{"label":"B","text":"the transfer of the single valence electron of potassium to fluorine"},{"label":"C","text":"the pooling of electrons over the whole crystal"},{"label":"D","text":"the sharing of three pairs of electrons"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Potassium (2, 8, 8, 1) gives up its lone valence electron to fluorine (2, 7), forming K⁺ and F⁻ held by electrostatic attraction. Electron sharing would describe a covalent bond instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2011,13, $q$The number of moles in 9.8 g of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H₂SO₄ (M = 98 g mol⁻¹), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.01"},{"label":"B","text":"0.02"},{"label":"C","text":"0.1"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n = mass ÷ molar mass = 9.8 ÷ 98 = 0.1 mol. H₂SO₄ = (2 × 1) + 32 + (4 × 16) = 98 g mol⁻¹.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2011,14, $q$A solution with a pH of 9 is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"strongly acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"weakly acidic"},{"label":"C","text":"neutral"},{"label":"D","text":"alkaline"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$On the pH scale, values above 7 indicate alkalinity. A pH of 9 is mildly alkaline, with [H⁺] = 1 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2011,15, $q$The oxidation number of manganese in potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII), KMnO₄, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+5"},{"label":"B","text":"+6"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+3"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$K is +1 and the four O atoms total −8, so Mn must be +7 for neutrality. The (VII) in the name also records this oxidation state.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2011,16, $q$As a chemical reaction proceeds, its rate steadily decreases because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the temperature of the mixture falls"},{"label":"B","text":"the concentration of the reactants falls as they are used up"},{"label":"C","text":"the activation energy rises as time passes"},{"label":"D","text":"the catalyst is used up"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fewer reactant particles per unit volume means fewer effective collisions per second, and the rate drops. Activation energy is a fixed property of the reaction, not of the time elapsed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2011,17, $q$Which allotrope of phosphorus is stored under water because it ignites spontaneously in air?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"white phosphorus"},{"label":"B","text":"red phosphorus"},{"label":"C","text":"amorphous phosphorus"},{"label":"D","text":"black phosphorus"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$White phosphorus (P₄) has a very low ignition point of about 35 °C and catches fire in air, so it is kept under water. Red phosphorus is stable at room temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2011,18, $q$The general molecular formula of the alkanones (open-chain ketones) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂"},{"label":"B","text":"CₙH₂ₙ"},{"label":"C","text":"CₙH₂ₙO"},{"label":"D","text":"CₙH₂ₙO₂"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$An open-chain ketone has one C=O in place of a CH₂ unit of the alkane, giving CₙH₂ₙO for n ≥ 3, e.g. propanone C₃H₆O. CₙH₂ₙO₂ fits the alkanoic acids and esters.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2011,19, $q$Ethene may be distinguished from ethane by shaking each gas separately with$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bromine water, which is rapidly decolourized by ethene only"},{"label":"B","text":"limewater"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV) solution"},{"label":"D","text":"Fehling's solution"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The C=C double bond adds bromine across it, destroying the red-brown colour; saturated ethane reacts only slowly in light. This is the standard test for unsaturation (acidified KMnO₄ reacts similarly).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2011,20, $q$A hydrocarbon contains 85.7% carbon and 14.3% hydrogen, and its relative molecular mass is 70. The molecular formula of the hydrocarbon is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"C₂H₄"},{"label":"B","text":"C₄H₈"},{"label":"C","text":"C₅H₁₀"},{"label":"D","text":"C₆H₁₂"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In 70 g: C = 60.0 g = 5.0 mol atoms, H = 10.0 g = 10 mol atoms, giving an empirical formula CH₂ (mass 14). Since 70 ÷ 14 = 5, the molecular formula is C₅H₁₀.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Grams of each element","kind":"math","content":"Carbon = 85.7% × 70 g = 60.0 g; hydrogen = 14.3% × 70 g = 10.0 g.","note":"Work with a 70 g sample so the percentages become real masses."},{"title":"Convert to mole ratios","kind":"math","content":"n(C) = 60 ÷ 12 = 5.0; n(H) = 10 ÷ 1 = 10; ratio C : H = 1 : 2.","note":"Divide each mole value by the smallest to get the simplest ratio."},{"title":"Scale to the molar mass","kind":"math","content":"Empirical formula CH₂ = 14 g mol⁻¹; multiplier = 70 ÷ 14 = 5, giving C₅H₁₀.","note":"The molecular formula is a whole-number multiple of the empirical formula."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2011,21, $q$When molten potassium chloride is electrolyzed using inert electrodes, the products at the cathode and anode respectively are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"potassium metal and chlorine gas"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine gas and potassium metal"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen gas and chlorine gas"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium metal and oxygen gas"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Only K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are present in the melt, so K⁺ is reduced at the cathode (K⁺ + e⁻ → K) and Cl⁻ is oxidized at the anode (2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻). No hydrogen or oxygen appears because water is absent.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Identify the ions available","kind":"scene","content":"A molten electrolyte contains only K⁺ and Cl⁻ — no H₂O, hence no H⁺ or OH⁻ rivals.","note":"This is why molten-salt electrolysis differs from aqueous electrolysis."},{"title":"Cathode: reduction of the cation","kind":"text","content":"K⁺ + e⁻ → K; molten silvery potassium collects at the cathode.","note":"Cations always migrate to the cathode and are discharged as metal here."},{"title":"Anode: oxidation of the anion","kind":"text","content":"2Cl⁻ → Cl₂(g) + 2e⁻; greenish-yellow chlorine is given off.","note":"With inert electrodes the anion itself provides the anode reaction."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2011,22, $q$Pellets of sodium hydroxide left open in a humid atmosphere become sticky and eventually turn into a solution because sodium hydroxide is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"efflorescent"},{"label":"B","text":"hygroscopic only"},{"label":"C","text":"deliquescent"},{"label":"D","text":"fluorescent"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Deliquescent substances absorb so much atmospheric moisture that they dissolve in it. NaOH (and CaCl₂) are classics; efflorescent solids instead lose their water of crystallization.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2011,23, $q$In the laboratory, oxygen gas can conveniently be prepared from$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) and dilute hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium chloride and concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen peroxide in the presence of manganese(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$2H₂O₂ →(MnO₂) 2H₂O + O₂; the catalyst speeds the decomposition and the gas is collected over water. The other pairs give H₂, CO₂ and HCl respectively.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2011,24, $q$Which of the following contains a coordinate (dative) bond in addition to covalent bonds?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"methane"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine molecule"},{"label":"D","text":"the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In NH₄⁺ the nitrogen lone pair is donated to H⁺, forming a dative bond; once formed it is indistinguishable from the other three N–H covalent bonds. NaCl is ionic and CH₄, Cl₂ are purely covalent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2011,25, $q$The number of neutrons in the nuclide ⁵⁶₂₆Fe is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"26"},{"label":"B","text":"30"},{"label":"C","text":"56"},{"label":"D","text":"82"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 56 − 26 = 30.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2011,26, $q$In the reaction Cl₂(g) + 2KI(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + I₂(s), the chlorine behaves as a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"reducing agent"},{"label":"B","text":"catalyst"},{"label":"C","text":"dehydrating agent"},{"label":"D","text":"oxidizing agent"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Each Cl₂ molecule accepts two electrons, dropping from 0 to −1 in Cl⁻, and oxidizes I⁻ (−1) to I₂ (0). A species that accepts electrons is the oxidizing agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2011,27, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂COCH₃ is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"butan-2-one"},{"label":"B","text":"butanal"},{"label":"C","text":"butanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"pentan-2-one"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The four-carbon chain carries the C=O on carbon-2 (numbering from the end that gives the ketone group the lowest locant), hence butan-2-one. Its old name is methyl ethyl ketone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2011,28, $q$Powdered marble reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid much faster than a single lump of the same mass because the powder has$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a greater mass per mole"},{"label":"B","text":"a larger surface area exposed for collisions"},{"label":"C","text":"a higher temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"more moles of calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Grinding increases the surface area of the solid, so more acid particles can strike it per second. The amount (moles) of marble is unchanged by grinding.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2011,29, $q$In pure water at 25 °C, the hydrogen ion concentration is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"1 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"C","text":"7 mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"1 × 10⁻¹ mol dm⁻³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Water ionizes slightly: 2H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻ with equal concentrations of 1 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³, giving pH = 7. The product [H⁺][OH⁻] is the ionic product Kw = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2011,30, $q$Stainless steel, used for cutlery, is an alloy principally of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"copper and tin"},{"label":"B","text":"lead and tin"},{"label":"C","text":"copper and zinc"},{"label":"D","text":"iron, chromium and nickel"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Stainless steel is iron alloyed with chromium (and usually nickel), which resists rusting. Cu–Sn is bronze, Pb–Sn is solder and Cu–Zn is brass.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2011,31, $q$A cell is set up with electrodes Zn|Zn²⁺ (E° = −0.76 V) and Cu|Cu²⁺ (E° = +0.34 V). The cell EMF and the anode are respectively$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.42 V and the zinc electrode"},{"label":"B","text":"1.10 V and the zinc electrode"},{"label":"C","text":"1.10 V and the copper electrode"},{"label":"D","text":"0.42 V and the copper electrode"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The more negative electrode (Zn, −0.76 V) is the anode where oxidation occurs. EMF = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V, so the cell pushes electrons from zinc to copper.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Pick the anode by potential","kind":"text","content":"The electrode with the more negative standard potential undergoes oxidation: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻.","note":"Zn (−0.76 V) is therefore the anode; Cu is the cathode."},{"title":"Cathode half-reaction","kind":"text","content":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu deposits copper metal at the cathode.","note":"Reduction always occurs at the cathode."},{"title":"Compute the EMF","kind":"math","content":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode = (+0.34) − (−0.76) = +1.10 V.","note":"Subtracting a negative adds the magnitudes."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','organic',2011,32, $q$Butan-1-ol and 2-methylpropan-1-ol are related as isomers showing$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chain isomerism"},{"label":"B","text":"positional isomerism"},{"label":"C","text":"functional isomerism"},{"label":"D","text":"geometrical isomerism"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Both are C₄H₁₀O alcohols with the –OH on carbon-1, but their carbon skeletons differ (straight chain versus branched). Different skeletons are chain isomerism.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2011,33, $q$5.5 g of a limestone sample reacts with excess dilute hydrochloric acid to give 2.2 g of carbon(IV) oxide. The percentage purity of the calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) in the sample is (CaCO₃ = 100, CO₂ = 44)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40.0%"},{"label":"B","text":"81.8%"},{"label":"C","text":"90.9%"},{"label":"D","text":"100.0%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(CO₂) = 2.2 ÷ 44 = 0.05 mol, so n(CaCO₃) reacted = 0.05 mol = 5.0 g (1 : 1 ratio). Purity = 5.0 ÷ 5.5 × 100 = 90.9%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2011,34, $q$A sample of pure water can be obtained from sea water by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"evaporation"},{"label":"B","text":"distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"decantation"},{"label":"D","text":"chromatography"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Simple distillation boils off the water, which condenses as the distillate free of dissolved salts; evaporation would leave the salt behind instead. It is how fresh water is reclaimed on ships.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2011,35, $q$When a weak acid is titrated against a weak base, it is difficult to choose a suitable indicator because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"both reactants are strong electrolytes"},{"label":"B","text":"there is no sharp change in pH at the equivalent point"},{"label":"C","text":"the salt formed is insoluble"},{"label":"D","text":"the indicators are destroyed by weak electrolytes"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The pH changes only gradually through the equivalence point, so no indicator gives a sharp, reliable colour change. Such titrations are best followed with a pH meter.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2011,36, $q$Which of the following hydrocarbons burns with the least (least smoky) flame?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethane"},{"label":"B","text":"ethene"},{"label":"C","text":"ethyne"},{"label":"D","text":"benzene"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The greater the hydrogen content per carbon, the cleaner the flame: ethane (C₂H₆) burns with a clear flame, while ethene, ethyne and benzene increasingly deposit soot. Ethyne's flame is the smokiest of the four.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2011,37, $q$The mixture used in the thermit process for welding railway lines is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"aluminium powder and iron(III) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"zinc dust and copper(II) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"magnesium powder and silicon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"copper filings and iron(III) oxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Fe₂O₃ + 2Al → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe is strongly exothermic; the molten iron produced welds the rails. Aluminium's great affinity for oxygen drives this displacement.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2011,38, $q$Which of the following substances acts as a reducing agent?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII)"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine gas"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen sulphide"},{"label":"D","text":"oxygen gas"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In H₂S sulphur is at −2 and can be raised to 0, e.g. 2H₂S + SO₂ → 3S + 2H₂O, so H₂S donates electrons and reduces others. KMnO₄, Cl₂ and O₂ are typical oxidizing agents.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);


-- Past Graph :: chemistry 2012-2014 (continuation rows, qnum 10+)

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2012,10, $q$In the fractional distillation of crude oil, the fraction that distils over at the highest temperature range is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"petrol (gasoline)"},{"label":"B","text":"kerosene"},{"label":"C","text":"diesel oil"},{"label":"D","text":"bitumen"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Fractions come off in order of boiling point, which rises with molecular size: gas < petrol < kerosene < diesel < lubricating oils, with bitumen distilled last from the column residue. Hence bitumen has the highest boiling range.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2012,11, $q$The electronic configuration of an atom of iron (Z = 26) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁸"},{"label":"B","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁶ 4s²"},{"label":"C","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰"},{"label":"D","text":"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁴ 4s²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Iron's 26 electrons fill up to argon (18), then 3d⁶ and finish with 4s². The half-filled-to-filled 3d arrangement explains its variable oxidation states (+2 and +3).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2012,12, $q$The type of bonding that exists in solid copper and accounts for its electrical conductivity is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ionic bonding"},{"label":"B","text":"covalent bonding"},{"label":"C","text":"metallic bonding"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen bonding"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In metallic bonding, a lattice of Cu²⁺-like cores sits in a cloud of delocalized valence electrons. These free electrons drift when a potential is applied, which is why metals conduct.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2012,13, $q$The volume occupied at S.T.P. by 3.2 g of sulphur(IV) oxide, SO₂ (S = 32, O = 16; 1 mol gas = 22.4 dm³ at S.T.P.), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"0.56 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"2.24 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"22.4 dm³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n(SO₂) = 3.2 ÷ 64 = 0.05 mol, so volume = 0.05 × 22.4 = 1.12 dm³ at S.T.P.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2012,14, $q$Compared with a solution of pH 6, a solution of pH 4 is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 times more acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"10 times more acidic"},{"label":"C","text":"100 times more acidic"},{"label":"D","text":"1000 times more acidic"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Because pH is logarithmic, each unit of pH differ is a factor of 10 in [H⁺]. Two units therefore give 10² = 100 times more hydrogen ions, i.e. 100 times more acidic.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2012,15, $q$In the reaction MnO₂(s) + 4HCl(aq) → MnCl₂(aq) + Cl₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), the species oxidized is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"MnO₂"},{"label":"B","text":"Cl⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"H₂O"},{"label":"D","text":"Mn²⁺"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Chloride ions lose electrons: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻, rising from −1 to 0, so Cl⁻ is oxidized. Mn drops from +4 in MnO₂ to +2 in Mn²⁺ and is therefore reduced.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2012,16, $q$For the reaction A + B → C, doubling [A] alone doubles the rate, while doubling [B] alone quadruples the rate. The rate equation for the reaction is therefore$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"rate = k[A][B]²"},{"label":"B","text":"rate = k[A]²[B]"},{"label":"C","text":"rate = k[A][B]"},{"label":"D","text":"rate = k[A][B]³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Rate is first order in A (doubling [A] doubles rate) and second order in B (doubling [B] gives 2² = 4 times the rate). Hence rate = k[A][B]².$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2012,17, $q$Sodium is extracted industrially by the electrolysis of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"molten sodium hydroxide"},{"label":"B","text":"molten sodium trioxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"C","text":"molten sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"molten sodium tetraoxosulphate(VI)"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The Downs cell electrolyzes fused NaCl (with CaCl₂ added to lower its melting point), collecting Na at the cathode and Cl₂ at the anode. Brine gives hydrogen instead of sodium metal.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2012,18, $q$An organic liquid X readily decolourizes bromine water but does not react with sodium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV) solution. X most likely contains$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a carboxylic acid group only"},{"label":"B","text":"a carbon-carbon double bond but no carboxylic acid group"},{"label":"C","text":"an aldehyde group"},{"label":"D","text":"an ester group"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Decolourizing bromine water betrays C=C unsaturation, while the negative NaHCO₃ test rules out –COOH; ethene fits this profile. An acid would also fail the first test, so both conditions must be checked.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2012,19, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂COOC₂H₅ is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethyl propanoate"},{"label":"B","text":"propyl ethanoate"},{"label":"C","text":"ethyl ethanoate"},{"label":"D","text":"propanoic propanoate"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The ester splits into CH₃CH₂CO– (propanoate part) and –OC₂H₅ (ethyl group), so the name is ethyl propanoate: alkyl group first, alkanoate last.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2012,20, $q$How long will a current of 0.50 A take to deposit 1.08 g of silver from silver trioxonitrate(V) solution? (F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹; Ag = 108)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"965 s"},{"label":"B","text":"1,930 s"},{"label":"C","text":"3,860 s"},{"label":"D","text":"96,500 s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n(Ag) = 1.08 ÷ 108 = 0.01 mol, requiring 0.01 F since Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag. t = Q ÷ I = (0.01 × 96,500) ÷ 0.50 = 1,930 s.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Mass to moles","kind":"math","content":"n(Ag) = 1.08 g ÷ 108 g mol⁻¹ = 0.01 mol.","note":"Silver monoatomic ion needs one electron per atom."},{"title":"Moles to charge","kind":"math","content":"Q = n(e⁻) × F = 0.01 × 96,500 = 965 C.","note":"Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so 1 faraday deposits 1 mole (108 g) of silver."},{"title":"Charge to time","kind":"math","content":"t = Q ÷ I = 965 ÷ 0.50 = 1,930 s (just over 32 minutes).","note":"m = ZIt rearranged as t = Q/I completes the chain."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2012,21, $q$According to the Brønsted-Lowry theory, an acid is a substance that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"donates a proton"},{"label":"B","text":"accepts a proton"},{"label":"C","text":"produces hydroxide ions in solution"},{"label":"D","text":"donates a pair of electrons"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A Brønsted acid is a proton (H⁺) donor, e.g. HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻, while the accepting species is the conjugate base. Electron-pair donation defines a Lewis base, and option C describes Arrhenius bases.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2012,22, $q$The maximum number of electrons that can occupy the 3d sub-shell is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"14"},{"label":"D","text":"10"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A d sub-shell has five orbitals, each holding 2 electrons with opposite spins, so its capacity is 10. The 2 and 6 figures belong to s and p sub-shells, and 14 to f.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2012,23, $q$The gas with the characteristic rotten-egg smell which blackens lead ethanoate paper is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen sulphide"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonia"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$H₂S is toxic and smells of rotten eggs; with lead(II) ions it gives black PbS, which is the paper test. SO₂ smells of burning sulphur and bleaches, not blackens, the paper.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2012,24, $q$Water boils at 100 °C, far above the boiling point of hydrogen sulphide (−60 °C), because of the presence of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"van der Waals forces"},{"label":"B","text":"covalent bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen bonds between water molecules"},{"label":"D","text":"ionic bonds"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each water molecule can hydrogen-bond to four neighbours through its O–H groups and lone pairs, so extra energy is needed to separate them. H₂S is too weakly polar for significant hydrogen bonding.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2012,25, $q$The mass of sodium hydroxide (M = 40 g mol⁻¹) required to prepare 250 cm³ of a 0.10 M solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 g"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 g"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 g"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 g"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$n = M × V(dm³) = 0.10 × 0.25 = 0.025 mol; m = 0.025 × 40 = 1.0 g.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2012,26, $q$The monomer from which polythene (polyethene) is made by addition polymerization is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethyne"},{"label":"B","text":"ethane"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"D","text":"ethene"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Many ethene molecules open their C=C double bonds and link up: nCH₂=CH₂ → (–CH₂–CH₂–)ₙ. A saturated molecule such as ethane cannot polymerize by addition.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2012,27, $q$Increasing the concentration of reactants speeds up a reaction because it$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increases the frequency with which reactant particles collide"},{"label":"B","text":"lowers the activation energy of the reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"makes the reactant particles heavier"},{"label":"D","text":"increases the amount of products formed"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$More particles per unit volume collide more often per second, so more effective collisions occur in unit time. Concentration cannot change the activation energy — only a catalyst does.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2012,28, $q$Which of the following acids is a weak acid?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"B","text":"trioxonitrate(V) acid"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH, ionizes only partially in water (few H⁺ per mole), which is why it is weak despite being corrosive in the concentrated state. HCl, HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ are the common strong acids.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2012,29, $q$The catalyst used in the contact process for the manufacture of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"finely divided iron"},{"label":"B","text":"nickel"},{"label":"C","text":"copper"},{"label":"D","text":"vanadium(V) oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$V₂O₅ catalyses 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ at about 450 °C and 2 atm in the contact process. Iron is the Haber catalyst and nickel hydrogenates vegetable oils.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2012,30, $q$When ethanol is oxidized with acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI) solution and the product distilled off as soon as it forms, the product is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanal"},{"label":"C","text":"ethene"},{"label":"D","text":"ethyl ethanoate"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Distilling the product as it forms removes ethanal (b.p. 20 °C) before it can be oxidized further. If the mixture were refluxed instead, oxidation would continue to ethanoic acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2012,31, $q$The oxidation number of nitrogen in the trioxonitrate(V) ion, NO₃⁻, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+1"},{"label":"B","text":"+3"},{"label":"C","text":"+5"},{"label":"D","text":"+6"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The three O atoms contribute −6 and the ion carries −1 overall, so N must be +5. The (V) in the name of trioxonitrate(V) acid records the same number.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2012,32, $q$25.0 cm³ of a sodium hydroxide solution is completely neutralized by 20.0 cm³ of 0.10 M hydrochloric acid. The concentration of the alkali is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.080 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.100 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.125 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.800 M"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n(HCl) = 0.10 × 0.020 = 0.002 mol, and HCl : NaOH is 1 : 1. Hence M(NaOH) = 0.002 ÷ 0.025 = 0.080 mol dm⁻³.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of standard acid","kind":"math","content":"n(HCl) = 0.10 mol dm⁻³ × 0.0200 dm³ = 0.002 mol.","note":"The HCl of known concentration is the standard used to standardize the alkali."},{"title":"Apply the reaction ratio","kind":"text","content":"HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O, so n(NaOH) in the pipetted 25.0 cm³ = 0.002 mol.","note":"Monobasic acid against NaOH: 1 : 1 mole ratio."},{"title":"Back-calculate the alkali concentration","kind":"math","content":"M(NaOH) = 0.002 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.080 mol dm⁻³.","note":"Concentration = moles in the aliquot ÷ its volume in dm³."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','separation',2012,33, $q$A mixture of iron filings and powdered sulphur is best separated by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"B","text":"chromatography"},{"label":"C","text":"using a magnet"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Iron is magnetic but sulphur is not, so a magnet draws out the filings cleanly. Physical mixtures of unlike properties separate by exploiting those properties.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2012,34, $q$For the equilibrium A(g) ⇌ B(g), ΔH positive, increasing the temperature will shift the equilibrium$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"to the left"},{"label":"B","text":"to the right (in the forward direction)"},{"label":"C","text":"nowhere; the position is unaffected by temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"both ways equally"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Heating favours the direction that absorbs heat — the endothermic forward reaction here. The system moves right to consume the added thermal energy.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2012,35, $q$The products obtained when an edible fat or oil is boiled with concentrated sodium hydroxide solution (saponification) are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glycerol only"},{"label":"B","text":"a long-chain fatty acid and glycerol"},{"label":"C","text":"a long-chain sodium alkanoate (soap) and glycerol"},{"label":"D","text":"soap and ethanol"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Fat (triglyceride) + 3NaOH → 3RCOONa (soap) + glycerol; under alkaline conditions the acid appears as its sodium salt. Acid hydrolysis would have given the free fatty acid and glycerol.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2012,36, $q$The bleaching action of chlorine water is due to the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrochloric acid formed"},{"label":"B","text":"hypochlorous acid, HOCl, it contains"},{"label":"C","text":"water used to dissolve the chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium chloride formed"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HOCl, and the unstable HOCl releases nascent oxygen that oxidizes dyes to colourless products. Dry chlorine does not bleach, and HCl cannot oxidize colouring matter.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2012,37, $q$What mass of ammonia, NH₃ (M = 17 g mol⁻¹), is formed when 28 g of nitrogen reacts with 3.0 g of hydrogen? (N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃; N = 14, H = 1)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"17 g"},{"label":"B","text":"34 g"},{"label":"C","text":"51 g"},{"label":"D","text":"68 g"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n(N₂) = 28 ÷ 28 = 1.0 mol and n(H₂) = 3.0 ÷ 2 = 1.5 mol; since 1 mol N₂ needs 3 mol H₂, hydrogen is the limiting reagent. H₂ : NH₃ = 3 : 2, so n(NH₃) = 1.0 mol = 17 g.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of each reactant","kind":"math","content":"n(N₂) = 28 ÷ 28 = 1.0 mol; n(H₂) = 3.0 ÷ 2 = 1.5 mol.","note":"The reactant values must be converted to moles before comparison."},{"title":"Find the limiting reagent","kind":"text","content":"The equation N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ demands 3 mol H₂ per mol N₂; only 1.5 mol H₂ is available, so H₂ is limiting.","note":"1.0 mol N₂ would have needed 3.0 mol H₂."},{"title":"Convert limiting reagent to product mass","kind":"math","content":"n(NH₃) = (2 ÷ 3) × 1.5 = 1.0 mol; m = 1.0 × 17 = 17.0 g.","note":"Nitrogen left over: 0.5 mol remains unused."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2012,38, $q$The element whose atoms have the electronic configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴ is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"silicon"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"sulphur"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Sixteen electrons arrange 2, 8, 6, which is sulphur (Z = 16), the third element of group VIA. The six outer electrons give its usual −2 and +4/+6 states.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2013,10, $q$A suitable method for separating the components of a coloured ink into their different dyes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"filtration"},{"label":"B","text":"paper chromatography"},{"label":"C","text":"decantation"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The solvent carries each dye up the paper at its own Rf rate, producing separated spots. Chromatography works precisely because the components differ slightly in solubility and adsorption.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2013,11, $q$The sub-atomic particle that has approximately the same mass as a proton but carries no charge is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"electron"},{"label":"B","text":"positron"},{"label":"C","text":"neutron"},{"label":"D","text":"α-particle"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The neutron has a mass close to the proton's but is electrically neutral, so it does not deflect in electric fields. The electron is far lighter and negatively charged.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2013,12, $q$Which of the following elements is the most electronegative?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"nitrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"fluorine"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Electronegativity rises across a period and up a group, and fluorine tops the scale (Pauling 4.0). Oxygen and chlorine follow, with metallic sodium at the bottom.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2013,13, $q$The volume of oxygen required for the complete combustion of 100 cm³ of methane, measured at the same temperature and pressure, is (CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"200 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"100 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"50 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"400 cm³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$By Avogadro's law, gas volumes at equal temperature and pressure are in the same ratio as their moles. The equation shows 2 volumes of O₂ per volume of CH₄, so 100 cm³ of methane needs 200 cm³ of oxygen.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2013,14, $q$Which of the following oxides is a basic oxide?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"aluminium oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$CaO is a metal oxide of a strongly electropositive element; it reacts with water to give the alkali Ca(OH)₂. CO₂ and SO₂ are acidic oxides, and Al₂O₃ is amphoteric.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2013,15, $q$Which of the following changes is an oxidation?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻"},{"label":"C","text":"Sn⁴⁺ + 2e⁻ → Sn²⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"H⁺ + e⁻ → ½H₂"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Oxidation is electron loss with an increased oxidation number, as in Cl⁻ (−1 → 0). Options A, C and D are all reductions, gaining electrons.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2013,16, $q$For the equilibrium PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g), an increase in the total pressure at constant temperature will$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shift the equilibrium to the left"},{"label":"B","text":"shift the equilibrium to the right"},{"label":"C","text":"have no effect on the position of equilibrium"},{"label":"D","text":"increase the concentration of chlorine"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$One molecule of PCl₅ produces two gaseous molecules, so higher pressure suppresses the side with more molecules — the right side. The equilibrium shifts left towards PCl₅.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2013,17, $q$The chief ore of zinc, from which the metal is commonly extracted, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"haematite"},{"label":"B","text":"zinc blende"},{"label":"C","text":"bauxite"},{"label":"D","text":"cassiterite"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Zinc blende is ZnS; it is roasted to ZnO and reduced to the metal. Haematite, bauxite and cassiterite are the ores of iron, aluminium and tin respectively.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2013,18, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃ is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"but-1-ene"},{"label":"B","text":"but-2-ene"},{"label":"C","text":"butane"},{"label":"D","text":"but-1-yne"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Number the four-carbon chain from the end nearer the double bond so that the C=C gets the lowest locant, which is 1: but-1-ene. The single bond and saturated names (butane) do not apply.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2013,19, $q$Which of the following alcohols is a secondary (sec-) alcohol?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methanol"},{"label":"B","text":"propan-1-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"D","text":"propan-2-ol"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In propan-2-ol the –OH-bearing carbon is joined to two other carbons ((CH₃)₂CHOH), making it secondary. Primary alcohols, like propan-1-ol, have the –OH on a carbon attached to only one other carbon.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2013,20, $q$The pH of a 0.0001 M (1 × 10⁻⁴ M) sodium hydroxide solution at 25 °C is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"13"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$[OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻⁴, so pOH = 4, and pH = 14 − 4 = 10 using Kw = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Hydroxide concentration","kind":"math","content":"NaOH ionizes fully: [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³.","note":"Strong bases give one OH⁻ per formula unit."},{"title":"Convert to pOH","kind":"math","content":"pOH = −log(1 × 10⁻⁴) = 4.","note":"pOH plays the logarithmic role for hydroxide that pH does for H⁺."},{"title":"Use the ionic product of water","kind":"math","content":"pH = 14 − pOH = 14 − 4 = 10 at 25 °C.","note":"pH + pOH = pKw = 14 at room temperature."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2013,21, $q$In electroplating an iron spoon with silver, the spoon is made the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"anode of the cell"},{"label":"B","text":"cathode of the cell"},{"label":"C","text":"electrolyte"},{"label":"D","text":"salt bridge"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The article to be plated is the cathode, where Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag deposits. A silver anode dissolves to replenish Ag⁺, keeping the ion concentration constant.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2013,22, $q$The volume of hydrogen liberated at S.T.P. when 2.3 g of sodium (M = 23 g mol⁻¹) reacts completely with cold water is (1 mol gas = 22.4 dm³ at S.T.P.)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.56 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"2.24 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"11.2 dm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n(Na) = 2.3 ÷ 23 = 0.1 mol. From 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂, two Na give one H₂, so n(H₂) = 0.05 mol and V = 0.05 × 22.4 = 1.12 dm³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2013,23, $q$Which of the following oxides is amphoteric?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium oxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$ZnO dissolves in acids to give salts and in alkalis to give zincates, showing both basic and acidic character. MgO, Na₂O and CaO are purely basic oxides of strongly electropositive metals.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2013,24, $q$The compound responsible for the PERMANENT hardness of water is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"calcium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$CaSO₄ (and MgSO₄) cannot be decomposed by boiling, hence permanent hardness; it is removed by adding washing soda that precipitates CaCO₃. The hydrogen tetraoxocarbonates(IV) cause temporary hardness.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2013,25, $q$Fractional crystallization is the technique used to separate$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"two immiscible liquids"},{"label":"B","text":"two gases of different densities"},{"label":"C","text":"two soluble solids whose solubilities vary differently with temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"an insoluble solid from a liquid"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Because the two salts reach saturation at different temperatures, repeated crystallization enriches the less soluble one in the crystals and the more soluble one in the mother liquor. Simple crystallization would give a mixed crop.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2013,26, $q$How many moles of carbon(IV) oxide are produced per mole of butane, C₄H₁₀, completely combusted?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$2C₄H₁₀ + 13O₂ → 8CO₂ + 10H₂O balances to 4CO₂ per butane: each of its four carbons becomes one CO₂. Hence 1 mol C₄H₁₀ gives 4 mol CO₂.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2013,27, $q$The oxidation number of carbon in sodium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV), NaHCO₃, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+2"},{"label":"B","text":"+4"},{"label":"C","text":"−4"},{"label":"D","text":"+6"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Na (+1) + H (+1) + C (x) + 3(−2) = 0, so x = +4. The (IV) in the IUPAC name of the acid, H₂CO₃, confirms it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2013,28, $q$The rate of reaction between magnesium ribbon and excess dilute hydrochloric acid can be DECREASED by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increasing the temperature of the acid"},{"label":"B","text":"increasing the concentration of the acid"},{"label":"C","text":"using powdered magnesium instead of ribbon"},{"label":"D","text":"cooling the reaction mixture"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Lowering the temperature reduces the kinetic energy of the particles, so fewer collisions reach the activation energy. The other three changes all speed the reaction up.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2013,29, $q$The concentration in mol dm⁻³ of a solution containing 6.0 g of ethanoic acid (M = 60 g mol⁻¹) in 500 cm³ of solution is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.2"},{"label":"B","text":"0.1"},{"label":"C","text":"0.02"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n = 6.0 ÷ 60 = 0.1 mol; concentration = 0.1 ÷ 0.5 = 0.2 mol dm⁻³. Always divide by the volume in dm³, not cm³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2013,30, $q$An unknown liquid X gives effervescence with sodium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV) solution but forms no silver mirror with Tollen's reagent. X is most likely$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanal"},{"label":"D","text":"ethyl ethanoate"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The NaHCO₃ effervescence (CO₂ evolved) marks the –COOH group, while the negative Tollen's test rules out an aldehyde. Ethanoic acid satisfies both observations; ethanal would show a silver mirror, and ethanol would fail the first test.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Read the NaHCO₃ test","kind":"text","content":"Effervescence of CO₂ with NaHCO₃ identifies a carboxylic acid: CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂.","note":"Only fairly strong organic acids — the alkanoic acids — respond this way."},{"title":"Read the Tollen's test","kind":"text","content":"No silver mirror means no aldehyde (–CHO) group is present.","note":"Ethanal would reduce Tollen's reagent and plate silver inside the tube."},{"title":"Match against the options","kind":"text","content":"Ethanoic acid: has –COOH, no –CHO, so it fits both observations.","note":"Ethyl ethanoate (an ester) is neutral and would fail the first test too."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2013,31, $q$Zinc dissolves in aqueous sodium hydroxide with the evolution of a gas which is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Zinc is amphoteric: Zn + 2NaOH + 2H₂O → Na₂[Zn(OH)₄] + H₂. The gas burns with a pop, confirming hydrogen; only very electropositive or amphoteric metals react with alkalis.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2013,32, $q$An element X has two isotopes of mass numbers 10 and 11. If the relative atomic mass of X is 10.8, the percentage abundance of the isotope of mass 10 is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5%"},{"label":"B","text":"10%"},{"label":"C","text":"80%"},{"label":"D","text":"20%"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Let the mass-10 isotope be p%: (10p + 11(100 − p)) ÷ 100 = 10.8, which gives p = 20%. So the isotopes occur as 20% : 80%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2013,33, $q$What mass of copper (M = 64 g mol⁻¹) is deposited when 0.2 mole of electrons passes through a copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.2 g"},{"label":"B","text":"6.4 g"},{"label":"C","text":"12.8 g"},{"label":"D","text":"32.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so each mole of copper uses 2 mol e⁻. n(Cu) = 0.2 ÷ 2 = 0.1 mol, and m = 0.1 × 64 = 6.4 g.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge available","kind":"math","content":"0.2 mol electrons = 0.2 F = 0.2 × 96,500 C of charge.","note":"A faraday is one mole of electrons."},{"title":"Electrode ratio","kind":"text","content":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu: two moles of electrons reduce one mole of Cu²⁺.","note":"n(Cu) = n(e⁻) ÷ 2 = 0.1 mol."},{"title":"Mass deposited","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.1 mol × 64 g mol⁻¹ = 6.4 g.","note":"Halving the electrons would halve the deposit."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','organic',2013,34, $q$Which benzene derivative is used as an antiseptic (carbolic acid)?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"phenol"},{"label":"B","text":"aniline"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrobenzene"},{"label":"D","text":"benzaldehyde"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Phenol, C₆H₅OH, was the first antiseptic used by Lister for wounds and surgical instruments. Aniline is an amine used in dye-making, and nitrobenzene is the product of benzene nitration.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2013,35, $q$For the equilibrium H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) in a 1.0 dm³ vessel at some temperature, the equilibrium concentrations are [H₂] = [I₂] = 2.0 mol dm⁻³ and [HI] = 4.0 mol dm⁻³. The equilibrium constant Kc is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]) = 4² ÷ (2 × 2) = 16 ÷ 4 = 4. Note that Kc here has no units since the concentration powers cancel.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Write the equilibrium expression","kind":"text","content":"For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI: Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]).","note":"Products over reactants, each raised to its coefficient in the equation."},{"title":"Substitute the concentrations","kind":"math","content":"Kc = (4.0)² ÷ (2.0 × 2.0) = 16 ÷ 4.","note":"Squaring [HI] follows from the coefficient 2."},{"title":"Evaluate Kc","kind":"math","content":"Kc = 4 (unitless for this reaction).","note":"Kc > 1 here shows products are favoured at this temperature."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2013,36, $q$In an acidic medium, methyl orange indicator shows the colour$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"yellow"},{"label":"B","text":"red"},{"label":"C","text":"blue"},{"label":"D","text":"violet"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Methyl orange is red at pH below 3.1 and yellow above 4.4, with orange between. In acid solution it is therefore red (never blue or violet — those belong to litmus in alkali and phenolphthalein shades).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2013,37, $q$Copper reacts with concentrated trioxonitrate(V) acid to give a poisonous brown gas which is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nitrogen(II) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"nitrogen(I) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen gas"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Cu + 4HNO₃(conc) → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O; NO₂ is the red-brown gas with a choking smell. With dilute acid, colourless NO is evolved instead, which browns only on meeting air.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2014,10, $q$A pure liquid is best characterized by having a constant and fixed$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"boiling point at a given pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"colour"},{"label":"C","text":"density at all temperatures"},{"label":"D","text":"odour"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A pure liquid boils completely at one fixed temperature for a given pressure; mixtures boil over a range. Boiling point (and melting point for solids) is thus the standard test of purity.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2014,11, $q$The number of valence (outermost) electrons in an atom of phosphorus (Z = 15) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"15"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Phosphorus is configured 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p³, so the third shell — its outermost — holds five electrons. This explains its maximum oxidation state of +5 and hydride PH₃.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2014,12, $q$Which of the following hydrides has the highest boiling point due to extensive hydrogen bonding?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"H₂O"},{"label":"B","text":"H₂S"},{"label":"C","text":"H₂Se"},{"label":"D","text":"H₂Te"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Water's small, very electronegative oxygen allows strong intermolecular hydrogen bonds, so despite being the lightest hydride in its group it boils highest (100 °C vs −60 °C for H₂S). Boiling point otherwise rises down the group as molecular mass increases.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2014,13, $q$The relative molecular mass of calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂ (Ca = 40, O = 16, H = 1), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"58"},{"label":"B","text":"90"},{"label":"C","text":"114"},{"label":"D","text":"74"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$M = 40 + 2(16 + 1) = 40 + 34 = 74 g mol⁻¹. Both hydroxide groups are counted in the formula mass.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2014,14, $q$Which of the following salts is an acid salt?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"K₂SO₄"},{"label":"B","text":"KCl"},{"label":"C","text":"KHSO₄"},{"label":"D","text":"K₃PO₄"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$KHSO₄ (potassium hydrogen tetraoxosulphate(VI)) still contains one replaceable H⁺ from the dibasic acid, which is the criterion for an acid salt. The others have had all ionizable hydrogens replaced.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2014,15, $q$The oxidation number of each chromium atom in the heptaoxodichromate(VI) ion, Cr₂O₇²⁻, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+3"},{"label":"B","text":"+6"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+12"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Let each Cr be x: 2x + 7(−2) = −2, so 2x = +12 and x = +6. The (VI) in the name records this state; +12 would be the total for both atoms, not for one.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2014,16, $q$Which of the following changes will NOT affect the position of a chemical equilibrium?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increasing the total pressure of the gaseous system"},{"label":"B","text":"changing the temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"adding a catalyst"},{"label":"D","text":"changing the concentration of one reactant"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A catalyst speeds the forward and backward reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached sooner but its position never moves. Pressure, temperature and concentration all displace the equilibrium.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2014,17, $q$A brick-red (orange-red) flame colouration on a platinum wire indicates the presence of ions of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"potassium"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium"},{"label":"D","text":"copper"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Calcium salts color a non-luminous flame brick red. Sodium is golden yellow, potassium lilac and copper blue-green.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2014,18, $q$The compound CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH is named$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"butanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"propanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"butan-1-ol"},{"label":"D","text":"butanone"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A four-carbon chain bearing the carboxyl group gives butanoic acid; propanoic acid would have only three carbons. The parent chain includes the carboxyl carbon when counting.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2014,19, $q$The number of chain isomers of butane, C₄H₁₀, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Butane exists as n-butane and 2-methylpropane (isobutane) only. Pentane, with one more carbon, already has the three isomers met earlier.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2014,20, $q$25.0 cm³ of 0.16 M sodium hydroxide is neutralized by 25.0 cm³ of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid. The concentration of the acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.040 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.080 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.160 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.200 M"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n(NaOH) = 0.16 × 0.025 = 0.004 mol; H₂SO₄ is dibasic, so n(H₂SO₄) = 0.002 mol. M(acid) = 0.002 ÷ 0.025 = 0.080 mol dm⁻³ — exactly half the alkali's value because equal volumes react.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of alkali taken","kind":"math","content":"n(NaOH) = 0.16 mol dm⁻³ × 0.0250 dm³ = 0.0040 mol.","note":"'Neutralized by equal volume' hides the true mole ratio."},{"title":"Apply the 2 : 1 stoichiometry","kind":"text","content":"H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O, so the acid moles reacting = 0.0040 ÷ 2 = 0.0020 mol.","note":"Missing this halving is the classic error — it doubles the answer."},{"title":"Concentration of the acid","kind":"math","content":"M(H₂SO₄) = 0.0020 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.080 mol dm⁻³.","note":"Equal volumes reacting means the dibasic acid's molarity is half that of the NaOH."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2014,21, $q$Phenolphthalein begins to turn pink at a pH of about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.4"},{"label":"B","text":"7.0"},{"label":"C","text":"9.0"},{"label":"D","text":"12.0"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Phenolphthalein changes from colourless to pink over pH ≈ 8.3–10.0, centred near 9. That is why it suits titrations whose end point lies on the alkaline side.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2014,22, $q$Ammonia gas is collected by downward displacement of air in an inverted gas jar because ammonia is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"heavier than air"},{"label":"B","text":"less dense than air but very soluble in water"},{"label":"C","text":"insoluble in water"},{"label":"D","text":"reactive with atmospheric oxygen"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Being lighter than air it rises into the inverted jar, and since it dissolves readily in water, collection over water is impossible. Downward delivery exploits the density only.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2014,23, $q$Which of the following species has the smallest ionic radius?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Na"},{"label":"B","text":"Mg"},{"label":"C","text":"Na⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"Mg²⁺"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Mg²⁺ has lost two electrons, leaving 10 electrons pulled by 12 protons; Na⁺ has the same 10 electrons but only 11 protons. The greater effective nuclear charge in Mg²⁺ draws the shell in most tightly.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2014,24, $q$An aqueous solution of ammonium chloride is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"basic"},{"label":"C","text":"neutral"},{"label":"D","text":"amphoteric"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$NH₄Cl is the salt of a strong acid (HCl) and a weak base (NH₃), so the ammonium ion hydrolyses water: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺, leaving excess H₃O⁺ and a pH below 7.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2014,25, $q$When ethanol is heated with excess concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid at 170 °C, the main organic product is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethene"},{"label":"B","text":"ethane"},{"label":"C","text":"ethoxyethane"},{"label":"D","text":"ethyne"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Concentrated H₂SO₄ acts as a dehydrating agent: C₂H₅OH → C₂H₄ + H₂O at about 170 °C. At the lower temperature of 140 °C the acid catalyses condensation to ethoxyethane (ether) instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2014,26, $q$Dry common salt can be obtained from sea water (brine) by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"decantation"},{"label":"B","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"C","text":"centrifugation"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Evaporating the water leaves the dissolved NaCl as dry solid, the principle behind salt pans and solar evaporation. Decantation and centrifugation only act on suspended, undissolved matter.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2014,27, $q$In the reaction Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s), the oxidizing agent is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Zn"},{"label":"B","text":"Zn²⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"Cu"},{"label":"D","text":"Cu²⁺"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Cu²⁺ gains two electrons and is reduced to Cu, so it is the oxidizing agent; zinc, which loses electrons, is the reducing agent. The oxidizing agent is always the species reduced.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2014,28, $q$A 0.25 M solution of sodium hydroxide (M = 40 g mol⁻¹) expressed in grams per dm³ is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"40"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Concentration (g dm⁻³) = molarity × molar mass = 0.25 × 40 = 10 g dm⁻³. A handy check: a 1 M solution of NaOH contains 40 g per dm³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2014,29, $q$In the blast-furnace extraction of iron, the carbon monoxide acts as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the reducing agent that removes oxygen from the oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"an oxidizing agent for the coke"},{"label":"C","text":"the catalyst of the process"},{"label":"D","text":"the flux that removes siliceous impurities"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂: the CO strips oxygen from the ore and is itself oxidized to CO₂. The flux role belongs to limestone, and the fuel is coke burned to generate the CO.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Coke supplies the reducing gas","kind":"scene","content":"Coke burns in the hot air blast: C + O₂ → CO₂; at the hotter zones CO₂ + C → 2CO.","note":"Carbon monoxide is generated inside the furnace itself."},{"title":"Reduction of the ore","kind":"text","content":"Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂; molten iron trickles to the hearth.","note":"CO takes the oxygen, being oxidized from +2 to +4 in carbon."},{"title":"Slag formation","kind":"text","content":"Limestone decomposes (CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂) and CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃ slag floats on the iron.","note":"The flux (CaO) removes the sandy silica as fusible calcium silicate."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','organic',2014,30, $q$Which of the following alkenes exhibits geometrical (cis-trans) isomerism?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"but-1-ene"},{"label":"B","text":"butane"},{"label":"C","text":"propene"},{"label":"D","text":"but-2-ene"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In but-2-ene each of the two doubly bonded carbons carries two different groups (H and CH₃), locking the relative positions of the CH₃ groups into distinct cis and trans forms. Each double-bond carbon of but-1-ene bears two hydrogens, so no cis-trans pair exists.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2014,31, $q$Concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid can be used for drying gases in the laboratory because it is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hygroscopic"},{"label":"B","text":"deliquescent"},{"label":"C","text":"efflorescent"},{"label":"D","text":"volatile"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Concentrated H₂SO₄ absorbs moisture but does not dissolve into a solution as a deliquescent solid does. Its hygroscopic nature suits it for drying gases (except NH₃ and H₂S, with which it reacts).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2014,32, $q$The atomic number of an element is determined by the number of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"neutrons in the nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"nucleons in the atom"},{"label":"C","text":"protons in the nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"neutrons plus electrons"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The atomic number Z equals the number of protons, and in a neutral atom also the number of electrons. The mass number counts protons plus neutrons (the nucleons).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2014,33, $q$When acidified water is electrolyzed with inert electrodes, the volume ratio of hydrogen evolved at the cathode to oxygen at the anode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 2"},{"label":"C","text":"2 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"4 : 1"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Two moles of electrons give 1 mol H₂ at the cathode but only 0.5 mol O₂ at the anode, so H₂ : O₂ = 2 : 1 by volume. This matches Avogadro's law and the composition of water, H₂O.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Cathode half-reaction","kind":"text","content":"2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂: two faradays of charge yield one mole of hydrogen gas.","note":"Hydrogen ion is discharged in preference to Na⁺-type spectator ions; here the acid simply provides H⁺."},{"title":"Anode half-reaction","kind":"text","content":"4OH⁻ (or water) → O₂ + 4e⁻ + 2H₂O: four faradays yield one mole of oxygen.","note":"Water supplies oxygen at the anode while acid keeps the medium conducting."},{"title":"Compare volumes","kind":"math","content":"Per 4 F: H₂ = 2 mol, O₂ = 1 mol, so V(H₂) : V(O₂) = 2 : 1.","note":"Equal moles of gas occupy equal volumes at the same T and P."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','organic',2014,34, $q$The hydrolysis of cane sugar (sucrose) by dilute acid produces$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glucose only"},{"label":"B","text":"fructose only"},{"label":"C","text":"glucose and fructose"},{"label":"D","text":"maltose"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Sucrose is a disaccharide built from one glucose and one fructose unit; acid or the enzyme invertase splits it into equal parts (inverting sugar). Maltose would hydrolyse to glucose alone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2014,35, $q$In studying the reaction between sodium thiosulphate solution and dilute hydrochloric acid, a SHORTER time taken for the cross beneath the beaker to disappear indicates a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"slower rate of reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"no visible change in the mixture"},{"label":"C","text":"greater yield of products"},{"label":"D","text":"faster rate of reaction"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The precipitated sulphur hides the cross; the sooner it does so, the quicker the reaction ran. Rate is inversely proportional to the time measured (rate ∝ 1/time).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2014,36, $q$At the end point of a titration between a strong acid and a strong base, the pH of the solution is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.1"},{"label":"B","text":"9.0"},{"label":"C","text":"7.0"},{"label":"D","text":"13.0"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A strong acid and a strong base form a neutral salt in water (e.g. NaCl), so the equivalence point sits at pH 7 and any of the common indicators may be used. Indicators such as methyl orange (3.1) suit other titration types.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2014,37, $q$The metal stored under kerosene to keep air and moisture away is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium"},{"label":"C","text":"magnesium"},{"label":"D","text":"aluminium"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sodium's extreme reactivity with oxygen and water means it is kept under kerosene, which excludes both. Magnesium and aluminium form protective surface films, so they need no storage under oil.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2014,38, $q$Which of the following polymers is formed by ADDITION polymerization?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nylon"},{"label":"B","text":"terylene"},{"label":"C","text":"polyethene"},{"label":"D","text":"protein"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Polyethene grows by successive opening of C=C bonds of ethene with no by-product; it is an addition polymer. Nylon and terylene (and proteins) condense with elimination of small molecules such as water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);


-- Past Graph :: chemistry 2015-2016 (continuation rows, qnum 10+)

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2015,10, $q$The liquid which passes through the filter paper during filtration and is collected in the receiver is called the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"filtrate"},{"label":"B","text":"residue"},{"label":"C","text":"distillate"},{"label":"D","text":"supernatant liquid"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Filtration gives two products: the solid held back on the paper (residue) and the clear liquid collected below (filtrate). The distillate is the condensed vapour from a distillation instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2015,11, $q$The number of electrons in the L-shell of an atom of silicon (Z = 14) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"14"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Silicon fills 2, 8, 4: the K-shell holds 2, the L-shell 8, and the remaining 4 sit in the M-shell. The L-shell capacity is 8 (2s² 2p⁶).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2015,12, $q$The bond formed by the mutual sharing of a pair of electrons between two atoms is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an ionic bond"},{"label":"B","text":"a covalent bond"},{"label":"C","text":"a metallic bond"},{"label":"D","text":"a hydrogen bond"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Shared electron pairs are the essence of covalent bonding, as in H₂ and CH₄. Ionic bonds transfer electrons, metallic bonds delocalize them, and hydrogen bonds are intermolecular attractions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2015,13, $q$The molar mass of iron(III) tetraoxosulphate(VI), Fe₂(SO₄)₃ (Fe = 56, S = 32, O = 16), is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"200 g mol⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"320 g mol⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"512 g mol⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"400 g mol⁻¹"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$M = 2(56) + 3(32 + 64) = 112 + 288 = 400 g mol⁻¹. Remember the bracket (SO₄) is counted three times over.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2015,14, $q$Which of the following bases is a weak base?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium hydroxide"},{"label":"B","text":"potassium hydroxide"},{"label":"C","text":"aqueous ammonia"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium hydroxide"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ammonia ionizes only partially in water to give NH₃·H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, so it is a weak base. NaOH, KOH and Ca(OH)₂ ionize essentially completely in water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2015,15, $q$In any electrolytic cell, oxidation always takes place at the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cathode"},{"label":"B","text":"anode"},{"label":"C","text":"either electrode"},{"label":"D","text":"inside the electrolyte"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The anode is the electrode at which electrons are removed from species (oxidation), whether the cell is galvanic or electrolytic. Reduction (electron gain) occurs at the cathode.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2015,16, $q$Which of the following reactions is photochemical (needs light to proceed appreciably)?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"rusting of iron"},{"label":"B","text":"combustion of hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"neutralization of HCl by NaOH"},{"label":"D","text":"decomposition of silver chloride in sunlight"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$2AgCl → 2Ag + Cl₂ is driven by absorbed light — photographic films darken for this reason. It is why AgCl reagent bottles are kept in dark cupboards.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2015,17, $q$Which of the following metals does NOT react with cold water but reacts with steam, giving the metal oxide and hydrogen?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"potassium"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium"},{"label":"D","text":"magnesium"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Magnesium's thin oxide coating slows its cold-water attack, but in steam it burns brightly: Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂. Sodium, potassium and calcium all react readily even with cold water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2015,18, $q$The general molecular formula of the alkynes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CₙH₂ₙ₊₂"},{"label":"B","text":"CₙH₂ₙ"},{"label":"C","text":"CₙH₂ₙ₋₂"},{"label":"D","text":"CₙH₂ₙ₋₆"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$One triple bond is equivalent to two degrees of unsaturation, removing four hydrogens from the alkane CₙH₂ₙ₊₂: alkynes are CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, e.g. ethyne C₂H₂. CₙH₂ₙ covers alkenes and cycloalkanes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2015,19, $q$The reaction between methane and chlorine in bright sunlight is an example of a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"substitution reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"addition reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"polymerization reaction"},{"label":"D","text":"condensation reaction"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sunlight splits Cl₂ into radicals, and each step replaces one hydrogen of methane with chlorine: CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl. Alkanes, being saturated, react by substitution rather than addition.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2015,20, $q$When a Daniell cell (Zn|Zn²⁺‖Cu²⁺|Cu) delivers current, the change taking place at the zinc electrode is that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"copper is deposited on it"},{"label":"B","text":"the zinc dissolves, going into solution as Zn²⁺ ions"},{"label":"C","text":"nothing happens at the zinc electrode"},{"label":"D","text":"zinc ions are discharged as zinc metal"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Zinc is the anode: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻, so the electrode corrodes away as ions enter the electrolyte. The released electrons flow round the external circuit to reduce Cu²⁺ at the copper cathode.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Identify the half-cells","kind":"text","content":"Zn|Zn²⁺ is the negative half-cell (anode); Cu²⁺|Cu is the positive half-cell (cathode).","note":"The more reactive metal forms the anode in a simple galvanic cell."},{"title":"Oxidation at the zinc anode","kind":"text","content":"Zn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻; the zinc rod slowly dissolves and its electrode mass falls.","note":"Oxidation is always electron loss."},{"title":"Reduction at the copper cathode","kind":"text","content":"Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s); copper plates onto the cathode and grows heavier.","note":"The salt bridge keeps the two solutions electrically neutral so current flows."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2015,21, $q$The basicity of tetraoxophosphate(V) acid, H₃PO₄, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Basicity counts the ionizable (replaceable) hydrogen atoms per acid molecule. All three H atoms in H₃PO₄ are replaceable, giving salts such as KH₂PO₄, K₂HPO₄ and K₃PO₄.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2015,22, $q$The total number of ATOMS present in 0.10 mol of ozone, O₃, is (Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.02 × 10²²"},{"label":"B","text":"1.806 × 10²³"},{"label":"C","text":"6.02 × 10²³"},{"label":"D","text":"1.806 × 10²⁴"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$There are 0.10 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 6.02 × 10²² ozone molecules, each holding 3 atoms. Atoms = 3 × 6.02 × 10²² = 1.806 × 10²³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2015,23, $q$Which of the following salts gives a neutral aqueous solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"KNO₃"},{"label":"B","text":"NH₄Cl"},{"label":"C","text":"Na₂CO₃"},{"label":"D","text":"(NH₄)₂SO₄"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$KNO₃ is the salt of a strong acid and a strong base, so neither ion hydrolyses water appreciably: the solution stays at pH 7. Ammonium salts go acidic, and sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) goes basic, on hydrolysis.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2015,24, $q$Temporary hardness of water can easily be removed by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"adding washing soda"},{"label":"B","text":"adding alum"},{"label":"C","text":"boiling the water"},{"label":"D","text":"filtration"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Boiling decomposes the soluble hydrogen tetraoxocarbonates(IV): Ca(HCO₃)₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O + CO₂, and the insoluble carbonate settles out. Washing soda is the remedy for permanent hardness.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2015,25, $q$When a secondary alcohol, such as propan-2-ol, is oxidized with acidified potassium heptaoxodichromate(VI), the chief product is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an aldehyde"},{"label":"B","text":"a ketone"},{"label":"C","text":"a carboxylic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"an ester"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Secondary alcohols lose two hydrogens to become ketones, and ketones resist further oxidation because there is no C–H left on the carbonyl carbon. Propan-2-ol therefore stops at propanone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2015,26, $q$The minimum energy which colliding reactant particles must possess before they can react is called the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bond energy"},{"label":"B","text":"ionization energy"},{"label":"C","text":"lattice energy"},{"label":"D","text":"activation energy"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Only collisions whose combined kinetic energy reaches the activation energy barrier are effective. Bond, ionization and lattice energies are fixed, bond-breaking or structure-related quantities instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2015,27, $q$The oxidation number of chlorine in potassium trioxochlorate(V), KClO₃, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+1"},{"label":"B","text":"+3"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+5"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$K is +1 and the three O atoms total −6, so chlorine must be +5 for neutrality. The (V) in the IUPAC name agrees with this state.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2015,28, $q$Excess powdered calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) was added to 50.0 cm³ of 0.10 M hydrochloric acid. The unreacted acid then required 20.0 cm³ of 0.10 M sodium hydroxide for neutralization. The mass of CaCO₃ that reacted is (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.15 g"},{"label":"B","text":"0.25 g"},{"label":"C","text":"0.30 g"},{"label":"D","text":"0.50 g"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Initial n(HCl) = 0.005 mol; n(HCl) left = 0.10 × 0.020 = 0.002 mol; hence 0.003 mol HCl attacked CaCO₃. From CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂, n(CaCO₃) = 0.0015 mol = 0.15 g.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of acid taken","kind":"math","content":"n(HCl) initial = 0.10 × 0.050 = 0.0050 mol.","note":"A back-titration starts from the original amount of acid."},{"title":"Acid left over, from the titration","kind":"math","content":"n(HCl) unused = n(NaOH) = 0.10 × 0.020 = 0.0020 mol.","note":"The NaOH only neutralizes acid that never met the marble."},{"title":"Acid consumed by the carbonate","kind":"math","content":"n(HCl) reacted = 0.0050 − 0.0020 = 0.0030 mol; n(CaCO₃) = 0.0030 ÷ 2 = 0.0015 mol; m = 0.0015 × 100 = 0.15 g.","note":"The 2 : 1 HCl : CaCO₃ ratio is fixed by the equation."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2015,29, $q$In the blast-furnace extraction of iron, the material added mainly to combine with (flux away) the siliceous earthy impurities is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"coke"},{"label":"B","text":"silica"},{"label":"C","text":"iron(III) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"limestone"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$CaCO₃ decomposes to CaO, which reacts with SiO₂ to give fusible calcium silicate slag: CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃. Coke is the fuel/reducer, and iron ore is the material being reduced.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2015,30, $q$Soap lathers poorly in hard water because the stearate (fatty acid) ions of soap precipitate as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"soluble chlorides"},{"label":"B","text":"an insoluble scum of calcium and magnesium salts of the fatty acid"},{"label":"C","text":"insoluble carbonates"},{"label":"D","text":"free fatty acid vapour"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ combine with the anions: 2C₁₇H₃₅COO⁻ + Ca²⁺ → Ca(C₁₇H₃₅COO)₂↓, wasting soap before any lather forms. Removing the ions (boiling or washing soda treatment) restores lathering.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2015,31, $q$The IUPAC name of (CH₃)₂CHCH₂OH is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2-methylpropan-2-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"2-methylbutan-1-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"2-methylpropan-1-ol"},{"label":"D","text":"butan-2-ol"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Expanding the formula gives a three-carbon chain with –OH on carbon-1 and a methyl group on carbon-2: 2-methylpropan-1-ol. Shifting that –OH would give the 2-ol (tertiary) isomer instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2015,32, $q$An atom with the electronic configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ is a stable element belonging to the noble gases and is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"argon"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"C","text":"potassium"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The configuration totals 18 electrons, argon's closed-shell arrangement, which is why argon is virtually unreactive. Chlorine has 17, potassium 19 and calcium 20 electrons.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2015,33, $q$A gas which turns limewater milky, the milkiness disappearing with excess of the gas, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonia"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃↓ (milky) + H₂O, and in excess CO₂ the insoluble carbonate becomes soluble calcium hydrogen tetraoxocarbonate(IV), clearing the milk. This two-stage change identifies CO₂.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2015,34, $q$0.05 g of magnesium was reacted with excess dilute HCl in two runs; the gas evolution was complete in 100 s with 1.0 M acid and in 50 s with 2.0 M acid (same temperature). The results show that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the rate increases as the concentration of the acid increases"},{"label":"B","text":"the rate falls as the concentration of the acid increases"},{"label":"C","text":"the rate is unaffected by the acid concentration"},{"label":"D","text":"the rate depends only on the mass of magnesium used"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Rate ∝ 1/time: run 1 = 1/100 = 0.01 s⁻¹, run 2 = 1/50 = 0.02 s⁻¹ — doubled when [HCl] doubled. Hence rate ∝ [HCl] (first order in acid here), showing concentration speeds reaction.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Turn times into rates","kind":"math","content":"Rate ∝ 1/t: run 1 gives 0.01 s⁻¹ and run 2 gives 0.02 s⁻¹.","note":"A shorter completion time means a faster reaction."},{"title":"Compare with concentration","kind":"text","content":"Doubling [HCl] from 1.0 M to 2.0 M doubled the rate: rate ∝ [HCl] for this reaction.","note":"More H⁺ per unit volume means more frequent effective collisions."},{"title":"Control of variables","kind":"text","content":"The temperature and the magnesium mass were the same in both runs, so only concentration differed.","note":"Good rate studies change one factor at a time."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','organic',2015,35, $q$Which of the following hydrocarbons does NOT decolourize bromine water readily at room temperature?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethene"},{"label":"B","text":"propyne"},{"label":"C","text":"benzene"},{"label":"D","text":"hex-1-ene"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Benzene's completely conjugated ring is unusually stable, so it holds on to its π electrons and resists addition; decolourization needs a catalyst (and gives substitution instead). Ethene, propyne and hex-1-ene add bromine readily across their multiple bonds.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2015,36, $q$By international convention, the standard electrode potential of the standard hydrogen electrode is assigned a value of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.00 V"},{"label":"B","text":"+0.34 V"},{"label":"C","text":"−0.76 V"},{"label":"D","text":"+1.10 V"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The SHE is the arbitrary zero of the electrode-potential scale; all other standard potentials (such as +0.34 V for Cu and −0.76 V for Zn) are measured against it. A cell built from two SHEs would give an EMF of 0.00 V.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2015,37, $q$Which of the following mixtures constitutes a buffer solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"HCl and NaCl"},{"label":"B","text":"CH₃COOH and CH₃COONa"},{"label":"C","text":"NaOH and NaCl"},{"label":"D","text":"H₂O and NH₄Cl"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A buffer needs a weak acid plus the salt of that acid (or a weak base plus its salt): the ethanoic acid/ethanoate pair resists pH change by mopping up added H⁺ or OH⁻. Strong acid or strong base with a neutral salt cannot buffer.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2015,38, $q$The chief ore of tin is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"galena"},{"label":"B","text":"malachite"},{"label":"C","text":"bauxite"},{"label":"D","text":"cassiterite"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Cassiterite is SnO₂; galena is PbS, malachite a copper ore and bauxite the aluminium ore. Tin was mined for centuries on the Jos Plateau.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2016,10, $q$A liquid such as glycerol, which decomposes when heated to its normal boiling point (290 °C), is best purified by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fractional distillation at atmospheric pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"evaporation"},{"label":"C","text":"distillation under reduced pressure"},{"label":"D","text":"filtration"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Lowering the pressure lowers the boiling point, so glycerol distils far below 290 °C without breaking down. Simple and fractional distillation at normal pressure would overheat the liquid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2016,11, $q$The nucleus of an ordinary atom contains$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"protons only"},{"label":"B","text":"neutrons only"},{"label":"C","text":"protons and neutrons"},{"label":"D","text":"electrons and protons"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The nucleus is made of protons, whose number is the atomic number, and neutrons, which add to the mass number. Electrons are excluded, occupying shells far outside the nucleus.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2016,12, $q$Which of the following molecules is linear in shape, with a bond angle of 180°?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"H₂O"},{"label":"B","text":"NH₃"},{"label":"C","text":"CH₄"},{"label":"D","text":"CO₂"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In CO₂ the carbon uses two regions of electron density (two C=O double bonds) and has no lone pairs, giving a straight O=C=O molecule. Water is bent (104.5°), ammonia pyramidal (107°) and methane tetrahedral (109.5°).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2016,13, $q$The volume of carbon(IV) oxide at S.T.P. that contains the same number of molecules as 56.0 dm³ of hydrogen measured under the same conditions is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"22.4 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"28.0 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"112.0 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"56.0 dm³"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$By Avogadro's law, equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules. The gas simply occupies the same 56.0 dm³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2016,14, $q$A solution has a pH of 11. Its hydroxide ion concentration at 25 °C is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 × 10⁻¹¹ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"1 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"C","text":"3 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"1 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$pOH = 14 − pH = 3, so [OH⁻] = 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ using Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴. Note the answer is 10⁻³, not 10⁻¹¹ — that would be the hydrogen ion concentration.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2016,15, $q$In the reaction SO₂(g) + 2H₂S(g) → 3S(s) + 2H₂O(l), the reducing agent is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"SO₂"},{"label":"B","text":"H₂O"},{"label":"C","text":"S"},{"label":"D","text":"H₂S"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Sulphur in H₂S is at −2 and rises to 0 in S, so H₂S loses electrons and reduces SO₂ (whose S falls from +4 to 0). It is the electron-donor — the reducing agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2016,16, $q$Enzymes lose their catalytic activity permanently in the presence of appreciable amounts of ions of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"alkali metals, such as Na⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonium"},{"label":"C","text":"chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"heavy metals, such as Hg²⁺"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Heavy metal ions (Hg²⁺, Pb²⁺, Ag⁺) bind tightly to groups on the enzyme surface and destroy the active site — the enzyme is said to be poisoned. Alkali metal and chloride ions have no such effect.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2016,17, $q$Zinc is used to galvanize iron sheets because zinc is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"less reactive than iron"},{"label":"B","text":"more reactive (electropositive) than iron"},{"label":"C","text":"denser than iron"},{"label":"D","text":"cheaper than iron"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The more electropositive zinc corrodes sacrificially wherever the coating is breached, supplying electrons that keep the iron from dissolving. Progression of galvanic protection depends on zinc being above iron in the reactivity series.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2016,18, $q$The functional group present in ethanal (CH₃CHO) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the aldehyde group"},{"label":"B","text":"the carboxyl group"},{"label":"C","text":"the hydroxyl group"},{"label":"D","text":"the keto group"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The –CHO group (H–C=O with a terminal carbon) is the aldehyde group, and it explains ethanal's positive Tollen's reaction. The keto (C=O) group sits between two carbons in ketones such as propanone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2016,19, $q$Which of the following is an example of a tertiary alcohol?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propan-1-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"propan-2-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"2-methylpropan-2-ol"},{"label":"D","text":"butan-2-ol"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In 2-methylpropan-2-ol, (CH₃)₃COH, the –OH-bearing carbon is bonded to three other carbons — the tertiary condition. Propan-2-ol and butan-2-ol are secondary; propan-1-ol is primary.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2016,20, $q$25.0 cm³ of a vinegar (ethanoic acid) sample required 30.0 cm³ of 0.10 M sodium hydroxide for complete neutralization. The concentration of the acid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.060 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.080 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.120 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.240 M"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(NaOH) = 0.10 × 0.030 = 0.0030 mol; ethanoic acid and NaOH react 1 : 1 (CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O). Hence M(acid) = 0.0030 ÷ 0.025 = 0.12 mol dm⁻³.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of standard base","kind":"math","content":"n(NaOH) = 0.10 mol dm⁻³ × 0.0300 dm³ = 0.0030 mol.","note":"NaOH of known concentration is the standard in this titration."},{"title":"Neutralization ratio","kind":"text","content":"CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O is 1 : 1, so n(acid) in the aliquot = 0.0030 mol.","note":"Ethanoic acid is monobasic despite having several H atoms."},{"title":"Concentration of the vinegar","kind":"math","content":"M(acid) = 0.0030 ÷ 0.0250 = 0.120 mol dm⁻³.","note":"Divide by the vinegar volume in dm³, never by the base volume."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2016,21, $q$The number of moles of electrons needed to reduce 0.5 mol of tetraoxomanganate(VII) ions to manganese(II) ions is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"5.0 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"10.0 mol"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Mn falls from +7 to +2, so each ion takes 5 electrons: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. For 0.5 mol of ions: 0.5 × 5 = 2.5 mol of electrons.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2016,22, $q$The percentage by mass of water of crystallization in CuSO₄·5H₂O (Cu = 64, S = 32, O = 16, H = 1) is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"25.0%"},{"label":"B","text":"45.0%"},{"label":"C","text":"64.0%"},{"label":"D","text":"36.0%"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$M = 160 + 5(18) = 250 g mol⁻¹; water = 90 g. Percentage = 90/250 × 100 = 36.0%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2016,23, $q$Which of the following compounds contains ionic, covalent AND coordinate (dative) bonds?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"methane"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium chloride"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$NH₄Cl has the ionic bond between NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻, three covalent N–H bonds, and a dative bond from nitrogen's lone pair to the H⁺. NaCl and CaCl₂ are purely ionic; CH₄ purely covalent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2016,24, $q$When diluting concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, the acid is added slowly TO THE WATER (and never the reverse) because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the two liquids are immiscible"},{"label":"B","text":"the acid evaporates very quickly"},{"label":"C","text":"the acid is lighter than water"},{"label":"D","text":"the dilution is highly exothermic, and water poured onto the acid spatters dangerously"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The large heat of solution released boils the thin surface layer of water instantly and ejects acid spatter. Adding acid into the bulk of a large body of water spreads the same heat harmlessly — 'add acid to water, as you oughter'. $q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2016,25, $q$Which of the following organic compounds is used as an antifreeze and as a solvent?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethane-1,2-diol"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanal"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanoic acid"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Ethane-1,2-diol (ethylene glycol) lowers the freezing point of water markedly, so it is the antifreeze in car radiators, in addition to its solvent uses. The mono-ol ethanol is a solvent too, but not the standard antifreeze.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2016,26, $q$The dense white fumes formed when ammonia gas meets hydrogen chloride gas are of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonium trioxonitrate(V)"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonium sulphate"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonium hydroxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s): the solid product suspends as thick white smoke. The classic demonstration uses a glass rod dipped in concentrated ammonia solution and another dipped in concentrated hydrochloric acid, brought near each other.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2016,27, $q$The number of moles of oxygen needed for the complete combustion of 0.5 mole of ethanol, C₂H₅OH, is (C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"1.5 mol"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The equation shows 3 mol O₂ per mol of ethanol, so 0.5 × 3 = 1.5 mol of oxygen is consumed. The combustion also gives 1.0 mol CO₂ and 1.5 mol H₂O.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2016,28, $q$For the equilibrium N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g), ΔH = +180 kJ mol⁻¹, the equilibrium yield of NO can be increased by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increasing the total pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"raising the temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"adding a catalyst"},{"label":"D","text":"removing some of the nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The forward reaction is endothermic, so heating pushes the equilibrium to the right. Pressure has no effect (2 gaseous moles on each side), a catalyst changes no position, and removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium left.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Count the gas molecules","kind":"text","content":"Left side: 1 N₂ + 1 O₂ = 2 molecules; right side: 2 NO = 2 molecules.","note":"With equal total moles, a pressure change shifts the position neither way."},{"title":"Heat and the endothermic direction","kind":"text","content":"ΔH is positive for the forward reaction, so added heat is absorbed by forming more NO — the equilibrium moves right.","note":"Le Chatelier: the system responds by consuming the stress (heat)."},{"title":"What a catalyst does here","kind":"text","content":"A catalyst only speeds both directions equally; the NO yield at equilibrium and the position are unchanged.","note":"Kc is fixed by temperature alone."}]$st$,
 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2016,29, $q$Which of the following 0.1 M solutions has the LOWEST pH?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"C","text":"tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium hydroxide"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$H₂SO₄ is diprotic and strong, giving about 2 × 0.1 = 0.2 M H⁺ (pH ≈ 0.7), more acidic than 0.1 M HCl (pH 1). Ethanoic acid ionizes weakly, and the base gives pH near 13.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2016,30, $q$The oxidation number of oxygen in oxygen difluoride, OF₂, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"−2"},{"label":"B","text":"+1"},{"label":"C","text":"+2"},{"label":"D","text":"+4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Fluorine is unmatched in electronegativity and always takes −1, so here O, not F, is the positive partner: O + 2(−1) = 0 gives O = +2. It is the only common case of positive oxygen.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2016,31, $q$Sulphide ores are commonly concentrated from their rocky impurities (gangue) by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"froth flotation"},{"label":"B","text":"magnetic separation"},{"label":"C","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"fractional crystallization"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Powdered ore is agitated with water, oil and air: oil-wetted sulphide particles ride the froth to the surface while the water-wetted gangue sinks. Roasting comes afterwards; flotation is the concentration step.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2016,32, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"butanone"},{"label":"B","text":"butanal"},{"label":"C","text":"butanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"propanal"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Four carbons overall with the –CHO at the end of the chain give butanal; the aldehydic carbon is counted as carbon-1. Butanone would need the C=O inside the chain, and butanoic acid the –COOH group.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2016,33, $q$A current of 0.50 A is passed through silver trioxonitrate(V) solution for 9,650 s. The mass of silver deposited at the cathode is (F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹; Ag = 108)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.7 g"},{"label":"B","text":"5.4 g"},{"label":"C","text":"10.8 g"},{"label":"D","text":"21.6 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Q = It = 0.50 × 9,650 = 4,825 C = 0.05 F. Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag gives n(Ag) = 0.05 mol, so m = 0.05 × 108 = 5.4 g.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge passed","kind":"math","content":"Q = It = 0.50 A × 9,650 s = 4,825 C.","note":"m = ZIt requires currents in amperes and time in seconds."},{"title":"Convert charge to faradays","kind":"math","content":"n(e⁻) = 4,825 ÷ 96,500 = 0.05 mol of electrons.","note":"Each faraday = 96,500 C, one mole of electrons."},{"title":"Deposit mass","kind":"math","content":"Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag consumes 1 e⁻ per atom, so n(Ag) = 0.05 mol and m = 0.05 × 108 = 5.4 g.","note":"Silver ions need only one electron, unlike Cu²⁺ or Al³⁺."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2016,34, $q$The maximum number of electrons that can occupy the N-shell (n = 4) of an atom is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"18"},{"label":"C","text":"50"},{"label":"D","text":"32"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Each shell holds at most 2n² electrons: for n = 4, 2(4²) = 32, spread over one s, three p, five d and seven f orbitals. The values 8 and 18 are the K/M/L-shell ceilings of 2(2²) = 8 and 2(3²) = 18.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2016,35, $q$A gas occupying 200 cm³ at 27 °C is heated to 54 °C at constant pressure. Its new volume is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"183 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"400 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"218 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"200 cm³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Charles' law uses absolute temperature: V₂ = V₁ × T₂/T₁ = 200 × 327 K/300 K = 218 cm³. The trap is dividing by 4 (27 °C) or forgetting to convert Celsius to kelvin.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2016,36, $q$Duralumin, the alloy valued in aircraft construction for its lightness and strength, consists chiefly of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"aluminium, copper, magnesium and manganese"},{"label":"B","text":"iron and carbon"},{"label":"C","text":"copper and tin"},{"label":"D","text":"lead and tin"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Duralumin is an aluminium-base alloy containing about 4% copper with small amounts of magnesium and manganese. Iron–carbon is steel, Cu–Sn is bronze and Pb–Sn is solder.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);


insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2017,10, $q$Molten aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃, is electrolysed using a current of 4.0 A for 1.0 hour. What mass of aluminium is deposited at the cathode? [F = 96500 C mol⁻¹; Al = 27]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.3 g"},{"label":"B","text":"2.7 g"},{"label":"C","text":"4.05 g"},{"label":"D","text":"8.1 g"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Q = It = 4.0 × 3600 = 14400 C gives 0.149 mol of electrons. Since Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al, n(Al) = 0.149 ÷ 3 = 0.0497 mol, i.e. 0.0497 × 27 = 1.3 g. The standard trap is forgetting to divide the electron moles by 3.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Total charge passed","kind":"math","content":"Q = I × t = 4.0 × 3600 = 14400 C","note":"convert minutes to seconds first"},{"title":"Moles of electrons","kind":"math","content":"n(e⁻) = Q ÷ F = 14400 ÷ 96500 = 0.149 mol","note":"divide, never multiply"},{"title":"Apply the half-equation","kind":"text","content":"Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al, so n(Al) = 0.149 ÷ 3 = 0.0497 mol","note":"three electrons reduce one Al³⁺"},{"title":"Mass deposited","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.0497 × 27 ≈ 1.3 g","note":"skipping the ÷ 3 gives the 4.05 g trap"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2017,11, $q$Which of the following salts dissolves in water to give an acidic solution as a result of hydrolysis?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium trioxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"B","text":"potassium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium ethanoate"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$NH₄Cl is the salt of a weak base (NH₃) and a strong acid, so NH₄⁺ hydrolyses to release H₃O⁺ and the solution turns acidic. Cations of strong bases such as Na⁺ and K⁺ do not hydrolyse. Sodium ethanoate is the reverse case and tests basic.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2017,12, $q$The reaction N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) is endothermic. What is the effect on the equilibrium position of raising the temperature?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"it shifts to the right, favouring NO"},{"label":"B","text":"it shifts to the left, favouring reactants"},{"label":"C","text":"the value of Kc falls"},{"label":"D","text":"there is no change in position"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Heat behaves like a reactant in an endothermic change, so supplying heat drives the change forward. Kc for an endothermic equilibrium rises with temperature. Yield of NO therefore increases.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2017,13, $q$The IUPAC name of the alkane CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂CH₃ is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2-ethylbutane"},{"label":"B","text":"hexane"},{"label":"C","text":"3-methylpentane"},{"label":"D","text":"2-methylpentane"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The longest continuous chain contains five carbon atoms, so the parent hydrocarbon is pentane. Numbering from the nearer end places the methyl substituent on carbon 2. Taking a shorter four-carbon chain is what produces the false 2-ethylbutane reading.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2017,14, $q$Crude petroleum is separated into fractions in a fractionating column chiefly because the components differ in their:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"boiling points"},{"label":"B","text":"densities"},{"label":"C","text":"viscosities"},{"label":"D","text":"colours"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The column is hottest at the base, so each fraction condenses on the tray where the temperature falls within its boiling range. Density and viscosity differences follow the same trend but do not drive the separation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2017,15, $q$Element X exists as isotopes ²⁰X (80%) and ²²X (20%). Its relative atomic mass is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20.0"},{"label":"B","text":"20.4"},{"label":"C","text":"21.0"},{"label":"D","text":"21.6"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$RAM = (0.80 × 20) + (0.20 × 22) = 16.0 + 4.4 = 20.4. A simple average of the two isotopes giving 21.0 would ignore the unequal abundances.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2017,16, $q$How many moles of O₂ are required for the complete combustion of 0.2 mole of propane, C₃H₈?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"0.4 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"1.0 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 mol"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O, so each mole of propane burns in 5 moles of oxygen. For 0.2 mol of propane the need is 0.2 × 5 = 1.0 mol. The coefficient 5 belongs to the equation, not directly to the answer.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2017,17, $q$In the periodic table, the first ionisation energy of the elements from sodium to chlorine generally:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increases across the period"},{"label":"B","text":"decreases across the period"},{"label":"C","text":"remains constant"},{"label":"D","text":"first decreases then increases"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Across a period the nuclear charge rises while electrons enter the same shell, so the outer electron is held more tightly. First ionisation energy therefore climbs from Na to Cl.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2017,18, $q$Which of the following oxides is amphoteric?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"aluminium oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"magnesium oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Al₂O₃ reacts with acids as a base and with hot concentrated alkali as an acid. Na₂O and MgO are purely basic, while SO₂ dissolves in water giving an acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2017,19, $q$Which of the following solutions is a weak electrolyte?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium hydroxide solution"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium chloride solution"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanoic acid"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A weak electrolyte ionises only partially in water, so few charge carriers form. Ethanoic acid is a weak acid that sets up the equilibrium CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺; the other three are fully dissociated.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2017,20, $q$The oxidation number of manganese in the permanganate ion, MnO₄⁻, is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+2"},{"label":"B","text":"+4"},{"label":"C","text":"+6"},{"label":"D","text":"+7"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The four oxygen atoms carry 4 × (−2) = −8. For the ion to total −1, the manganese must be +7.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2017,21, $q$At equilibrium, the concentrations in H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) are [H₂] = 0.2, [I₂] = 0.4 and [HI] = 0.8, all in mol dm⁻³. The value of Kc for the system is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0"},{"label":"B","text":"8.0"},{"label":"C","text":"12.5"},{"label":"D","text":"16.0"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂][I₂]) = 0.64 ÷ 0.08 = 8.0. The [HI] term is squared because two HI form each way. Since Δn = 2 − 2 = 0, Kc carries no units.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Write the equilibrium expression","kind":"text","content":"Kc = [HI]² ÷ ([H₂] × [I₂]), with coefficients becoming powers","note":"products over reactants"},{"title":"Substitute the values","kind":"math","content":"Kc = (0.8)² ÷ (0.2 × 0.4) = 0.64 ÷ 0.08","note":"square 0.8 because the coefficient of HI is 2"},{"title":"Compute and check units","kind":"math","content":"Kc = 8.0, and Δn = 2 − 2 = 0, so Kc has no units","note":"zero change in gas moles cancels the units"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2017,22, $q$The polymerisation of ethene to form poly(ethene) is an example of:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"addition polymerisation"},{"label":"B","text":"condensation polymerisation"},{"label":"C","text":"substitution"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogenation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Opening the C=C bonds joins every atom of each monomer into the growing chain, so no small molecule is lost. Condensation polymerisation would instead eliminate a by-product such as water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2017,23, $q$The gas mainly responsible for acid rain when coal containing sulphur impurities is burnt is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(II) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"methane"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Burning the sulphur impurities forms SO₂, which dissolves in cloud droplets as H₂SO₃ and is further oxidised to H₂SO₄. CO₂ only makes rain weakly acidic and is not the principal acid-rain culprit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2017,24, $q$What volume of 0.10 M NaOH will exactly neutralise 25.0 cm³ of 0.05 M tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12.5 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"50.0 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"25.0 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"62.5 cm³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(H₂SO₄) = 0.025 × 0.05 = 0.00125 mol, and the dibasic acid needs twice as many moles of NaOH, 0.0025 mol. At 0.10 M this occupies 0.0025 ÷ 0.10 = 0.025 dm³ = 25.0 cm³. Assuming a 1:1 mole ratio gives the 12.5 cm³ trap.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2017,25, $q$Which of the following substances contains both ionic and covalent bonds?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"B","text":"methane"},{"label":"C","text":"water"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonium chloride"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Within each NH₄⁺ ion the N–H bonds are covalent, while the NH₄⁺ and Cl⁻ ions attract each other ionically. NaCl, CH₄ and H₂O each contain a single bond type only.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2017,26, $q$A mixture of two soluble dyes in the same solvent can best be separated by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chromatography"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"decantation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The dyes differ in solubility and adsorption, so they travel along the absorbent medium at different rates and separate into bands. Filtration and decantation cannot separate dissolved solutes, and both dyes would distil together.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2017,27, $q$The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in the M shell (n = 3) is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"18"},{"label":"D","text":"32"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The 2n² rule gives 2 × 3² = 18 electrons, filling 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹⁰. The figure of 8 applies only to the outermost shells of the first three periods.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2017,28, $q$When a very dilute aqueous solution of sodium chloride is electrolysed using platinum electrodes, the gas liberated at the anode is chiefly:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen chloride"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In dilute solution the OH⁻ ions from water are discharged in preference to the scarce Cl⁻ ions, giving oxygen at the anode. Only concentrated brine supplies enough Cl⁻ for chlorine to be liberated.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2017,29, $q$The enzyme complex present in yeast that converts glucose to ethanol and carbon(IV) oxide during fermentation is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"diastase"},{"label":"B","text":"maltase"},{"label":"C","text":"amylase"},{"label":"D","text":"zymase"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Zymase secreted by yeast catalyses C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. Diastase and amylase act on starch during malting, not on the sugar-to-ethanol step.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2017,30, $q$Increasing the temperature of a reaction increases its rate mainly because:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the activation energy of the reaction is lowered"},{"label":"B","text":"a greater fraction of colliding particles now possess energy equal to or above the activation energy"},{"label":"C","text":"the concentration of the reactants increases"},{"label":"D","text":"every collision now results in a reaction"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Heating redistributes kinetic energy so far more molecules exceed Ea (the Maxwell-Boltzmann tail grows). The activation energy is fixed by the reaction path and does not change with temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2017,31, $q$From magnesium hydroxide to barium hydroxide down Group II, the solubility of the metal hydroxides in water:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"decreases"},{"label":"B","text":"stays the same"},{"label":"C","text":"becomes zero"},{"label":"D","text":"increases"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Group II hydroxides become steadily more soluble down the group, so Ba(OH)₂ is clearly soluble while Mg(OH)₂ is almost insoluble. The reverse trend applies to the Group II tetraoxosulphate(VI) salts.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2017,32, $q$What is the percentage by mass of nitrogen in calcium trioxonitrate(V), Ca(NO₃)₂? [Ca = 40, N = 14, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8.5%"},{"label":"B","text":"17.1%"},{"label":"C","text":"21.2%"},{"label":"D","text":"34.1%"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$M(Ca(NO₃)₂) = 40 + 2(14 + 48) = 164 g mol⁻¹, and the two nitrate groups hold 2 × 14 = 28 g of nitrogen. So %N = 28 ÷ 164 × 100 = 17.1%. Counting one nitrogen only gives 8.5%, and halving the molar mass gives 34.1%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2017,33, $q$Calculate the pH of a 0.01 mol dm⁻³ solution of hydrochloric acid.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$HCl ionises completely and is monobasic, so [H₃O⁺] = 1 × 10⁻² mol dm⁻³ and pH = −log(10⁻²) = 2. A pH of 12 would characterise a dilute strong base instead. Ten-fold dilution would shift the pH by one unit only, to 3.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Complete ionisation","kind":"text","content":"HCl is a strong monobasic acid, so [H₃O⁺] = 0.01 = 1 × 10⁻² mol dm⁻³","note":"every molecule donates one proton"},{"title":"Apply the definition","kind":"math","content":"pH = −log₁₀[H₃O⁺] = −log₁₀(10⁻²) = 2","note":"the index of ten is the pH"},{"title":"Check by dilution logic","kind":"text","content":"a ten-fold dilution raises pH by one unit only, so 0.001 M would give pH 3","note":"pH is logarithmic, never linear"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2017,34, $q$Iodine can be recovered from a mixture of iodine crystals and common salt by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"filtration"},{"label":"B","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"C","text":"decantation"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$On gentle heating iodine sublimes and re-deposits as crystals on a cold surface, leaving sodium chloride behind. The salt is non-volatile and stays in the dish.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2017,35, $q$The number of neutrons in the nucleus of ⁴⁰₁₉K is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"19"},{"label":"B","text":"40"},{"label":"C","text":"21"},{"label":"D","text":"59"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Neutrons = mass number − proton number = 40 − 19 = 21. The subscripted and superscripted numbers must never be swapped in this subtraction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2017,36, $q$Which of the following alkanols is a tertiary alkanol?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propan-1-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"propan-2-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"butan-2-ol"},{"label":"D","text":"2-methylpropan-2-ol"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In 2-methylpropan-2-ol the carbon bearing the OH group is bonded to three other carbon atoms. Butan-2-ol and propan-2-ol carry the OH on a carbon attached to two carbons (secondary), and propan-1-ol is primary.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2017,37, $q$What mass of copper is deposited when 0.5 mole of electrons is passed through a solution of copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI)? [Cu = 64]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8.0 g"},{"label":"B","text":"64.0 g"},{"label":"C","text":"16.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"32.0 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so 0.5 mol of electrons deposits 0.5 ÷ 2 = 0.25 mol of copper. The mass is 0.25 × 64 = 16.0 g; multiplying the electron moles by 2 instead of dividing gives 64 g.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2017,38, $q$Which pair of elements will most readily form an ionic bond?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen and chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon and chlorine"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen and chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium and chlorine"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Sodium readily loses its single outer electron while chlorine eagerly accepts one, producing Na⁺ and Cl⁻ with a large electronegativity difference. Pairs of non-metals such as carbon with chlorine share electrons and bond covalently.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','acids-bases',2018,10, $q$A salt formed by the partial replacement of the replaceable hydrogen atoms of an acid by a metal is called a:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"normal salt"},{"label":"B","text":"acid salt"},{"label":"C","text":"basic salt"},{"label":"D","text":"double salt"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Retaining any acidic hydrogen in the formula, as in NaHSO₄, marks an acid salt. A normal salt has had all its replaceable hydrogens displaced, and a basic salt retains hydroxide ions instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2018,11, $q$The residue drawn from the bottom of a petroleum fractionating column and used for road surfacing is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"kerosene"},{"label":"B","text":"diesel oil"},{"label":"C","text":"bitumen"},{"label":"D","text":"naphtha"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Bitumen is the heaviest, highest-boiling fraction, so it never vapourises and collects at the tower base. Lighter fractions such as kerosene and naphtha rise higher up the column.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2018,12, $q$How many molecules are present in 4.4 g of carbon(IV) oxide? [C = 12, O = 16; Avogadro number = 6.02 × 10²³]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.02 × 10²²"},{"label":"B","text":"6.02 × 10²³"},{"label":"C","text":"1.204 × 10²³"},{"label":"D","text":"2.4 × 10²³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$4.4 g of CO₂ is 4.4 ÷ 44 = 0.1 mol, so the count is 0.1 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 6.02 × 10²² molecules. Using the molar mass as 4.4 g directly produces the 10²³-scale error.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2018,13, $q$An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 2. To which group of the periodic table does it belong?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Group I"},{"label":"B","text":"Group II"},{"label":"C","text":"Group VI"},{"label":"D","text":"Group VIII"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Two electrons in the outermost shell fix the group as II. Being an s-block metal with two outer electrons, it loses both easily to form a 2+ ion.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2018,14, $q$Which of the following properties increases down Group VII of the periodic table from fluorine to iodine?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"atomic radius"},{"label":"B","text":"electronegativity"},{"label":"C","text":"first ionisation energy"},{"label":"D","text":"non-metallic character"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Each halogen adds a whole new shell going down the group, so radii grow steadily. Electronegativity, first ionisation energy, oxidising power and non-metallic character all fall instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2018,15, $q$Which of the following changes is NOT a redox reaction?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"combustion of carbon"},{"label":"B","text":"rusting of iron"},{"label":"C","text":"neutralisation of hydrochloric acid by sodium hydroxide"},{"label":"D","text":"photosynthesis in green plants"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O no oxidation number changes at all; it is a proton transfer. Combustion, rusting and photosynthesis all involve electron transfer with oxygen.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2018,16, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2-methylpentane"},{"label":"B","text":"3-methylpentane"},{"label":"C","text":"2-ethylbutane"},{"label":"D","text":"hexane"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The longest continuous chain has five carbons (pentane), and numbering from the nearer end puts the methyl branch on carbon 3. Starting the count from the other end creates the 2-methylpentane mistake, while six carbons in one chain would be needed for hexane.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2018,17, $q$For the exothermic equilibrium N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), raising the temperature shifts the equilibrium:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"to the right, favouring ammonia"},{"label":"B","text":"in no direction"},{"label":"C","text":"in a way that depends on the catalyst"},{"label":"D","text":"to the left, favouring reactants"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Heat is a product of the forward synthesis, so Le Chatelier's principle sends added heat backwards. That is why the highest ammonia yields come from the lowest practical working temperatures.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2018,18, $q$The gas that relights a glowing splint is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Oxygen vigorously supports combustion, so a glowing splint bursts back into flame in it. Hydrogen burns with a pop, while CO₂ and N₂ extinguish burning splints.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2018,19, $q$What volume of hydrogen, measured at STP, is produced when 6.5 g of zinc reacts with excess dilute hydrochloric acid? [Zn = 65; molar volume at STP = 22.4 dm³]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"2.24 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"4.48 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"22.4 dm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$6.5 g of zinc is 0.1 mol, and Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ liberates 1 mol of H₂ per mol of zinc. So the volume is 0.1 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³; with acid in excess, zinc alone fixes the yield.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2018,20, $q$What is the pH of a 0.02 M solution of sodium hydroxide at 25 °C?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.70"},{"label":"B","text":"2.30"},{"label":"C","text":"11.70"},{"label":"D","text":"12.30"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$NaOH is a strong base, so [OH⁻] = 0.02 mol dm⁻³ and pOH = −log(2 × 10⁻²) = 1.70. Then pH = 14 − 1.70 = 12.30. Quoting the pOH itself as a pH is the classic slip, as is subtracting in the wrong order.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Full dissociation","kind":"text","content":"NaOH is a strong base, so [OH⁻] = 0.02 mol dm⁻³","note":"each formula unit releases one OH⁻"},{"title":"Compute pOH","kind":"math","content":"pOH = −log₁₀(2 × 10⁻²) = 1.70","note":"pH + pOH = 14 at 25 °C"},{"title":"Convert to pH","kind":"math","content":"pH = 14 − 1.70 = 12.30","note":"subtract from 14 in the correct order"},{"title":"Sanity check","kind":"text","content":"a base must give a pH well above 8","note":"answers near 2 belong to acids"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','separation',2018,21, $q$A mixture of iron filings and copper filings can be separated by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"magnetic separation"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"chromatography"},{"label":"D","text":"crystallisation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Iron is ferromagnetic while copper is not, so a magnet lifts the iron filings and leaves the copper. Neither dissolves for crystallisation, and both solids would pass any filter.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2018,22, $q$Nylon is manufactured by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"addition polymerisation"},{"label":"B","text":"condensation polymerisation"},{"label":"C","text":"substitution"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogenation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Nylon builds up from diamine and dicarboxylic acid monomers, splitting out a small molecule (water) at every link. That step-growth loss of a by-product is the defining feature of condensation polymerisation, unlike the atom-economical addition route of poly(ethene).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2018,23, $q$In electroplating an article with silver, the article being plated is made the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"anode"},{"label":"B","text":"cathode"},{"label":"C","text":"electrolyte"},{"label":"D","text":"salt bridge"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Metal deposits wherever reduction occurs, and reduction happens at the cathode where electrons enter the cell. The anode is meanwhile a bar of the plating metal that dissolves to replenish Ag⁺ ions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2018,24, $q$The forces of attraction between neighbouring carbon(IV) oxide molecules in solid dry ice are:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"covalent bonds"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"weak van der Waals (dispersion) forces"},{"label":"D","text":"ionic bonds"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$CO₂ is a symmetrical non-polar molecule, so only temporary dipoles attract neighbouring molecules. These dispersion forces are weak, which is why dry ice sublimes so readily; the strong covalent bonds are strictly within each molecule.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2018,25, $q$4.0 g of hydrogen gas is mixed with 71.0 g of chlorine gas and the mixture is sparked. What mass of hydrogen chloride is formed? [H = 1, Cl = 35.5]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"36.5 g"},{"label":"B","text":"71.0 g"},{"label":"C","text":"73.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"146.0 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(H₂) = 2.0 mol but n(Cl₂) = 1.0 mol, and the 1:1 equation means chlorine is the limiting reactant. One mol of Cl₂ then yields two mol of HCl = 2.0 × 36.5 = 73.0 g. Yield calculations must always use the limiting reagent, never the reagent in excess.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Convert both masses to moles","kind":"math","content":"n(H₂) = 4.0 ÷ 2 = 2.0 mol; n(Cl₂) = 71.0 ÷ 71 = 1.0 mol","note":"M(Cl₂) = 2 × 35.5 = 71 g mol⁻¹"},{"title":"Identify the limiting reactant","kind":"text","content":"the 1:1 equation exhausts 1.0 mol of Cl₂ with only 1.0 mol of H₂, leaving 1.0 mol of H₂ over","note":"chlorine limits, hydrogen is excess"},{"title":"Scale up to the product","kind":"math","content":"Cl₂ + H₂ → 2HCl, so n(HCl) = 2 × 1.0 = 2.0 mol","note":"use the coefficient, 2HCl"},{"title":"Convert moles to mass","kind":"math","content":"m = 2.0 × 36.5 = 73.0 g","note":"M(HCl) = 36.5 g mol⁻¹"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2018,26, $q$The salt formed by the partial replacement of the hydroxide ions of a base by an acid radical is called a:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"normal salt"},{"label":"B","text":"acid salt"},{"label":"C","text":"double salt"},{"label":"D","text":"basic salt"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Salts such as basic zinc chloride, Zn(OH)Cl, still carry hydroxide ions within their formula and are called basic salts. This mirrors acid salts, which retain replaceable hydrogen atoms from the parent acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2018,27, $q$Which sub-atomic particle has a negligible mass and carries a negative charge?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"proton"},{"label":"B","text":"neutron"},{"label":"C","text":"electron"},{"label":"D","text":"alpha particle"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The electron has about 1/1836 of the proton's mass and a charge of −1. Protons and alpha particles are heavy and positive, and neutrons are heavy and neutral.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2018,28, $q$Petrol spilled on water forms a separate floating layer; the two immiscible liquids are best separated by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a separating funnel"},{"label":"B","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"filtration"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Petrol and water are immiscible and form distinct layers, so the funnel simply drains the denser water first. Miscible pairs such as ethanol and water would instead need fractional distillation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2018,29, $q$What mass of sodium hydroxide is required to prepare 500 cm³ of a 0.1 M solution? [Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 g"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 g"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Moles needed = 0.1 × 0.5 = 0.05 mol, and M(NaOH) = 40 g mol⁻¹, so mass = 0.05 × 40 = 2.0 g. Failing to convert 500 cm³ to 0.5 dm³ is the usual slip that halves or doubles the outcome.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2018,30, $q$The oxidation number of nitrogen in trioxonitrate(V) acid, HNO₃, is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+1"},{"label":"B","text":"+2"},{"label":"C","text":"+3"},{"label":"D","text":"+5"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Taking H = +1 and O = −2: x + (−2) × 3 + 1 = 0 gives x = +5. The Roman numeral V in the systematic name states the same oxidation number explicitly.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2018,31, $q$Sodium is stored under kerosene in the laboratory to prevent it from:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"reacting with oxygen and water vapour in the air"},{"label":"B","text":"vaporising at room temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"reacting with the kerosene"},{"label":"D","text":"reducing the kerosene"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Freshly cut sodium reacts almost instantly with air and violently with moisture, forming oxide or peroxide and hydroxide. The kerosene film excludes both air and water, while sodium itself is unreactive towards kerosene.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2018,32, $q$Which of the following compounds will decolourise acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methane"},{"label":"B","text":"ethane"},{"label":"C","text":"ethene"},{"label":"D","text":"chloroethane"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The C=C double bond of ethene is readily oxidised by acidified MnO₄⁻, and the intense purple colour disappears as the manganese is reduced. Saturated species such as the alkanes and chloroethane are far too unreactive towards this oxidant at room temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2018,33, $q$Which change will increase the rate of reaction between dilute HCl and marble chips?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"using larger chips"},{"label":"B","text":"cooling the acid"},{"label":"C","text":"using powdered marble chips"},{"label":"D","text":"adding water to the acid"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Powdering exposes a far larger surface area, so more effective collisions occur each second. Larger chips, dilution and cooling all slow the reaction down.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2018,34, $q$How long will it take a current of 2.0 A to deposit 0.64 g of copper from a copper(II) sulphate solution? [Cu = 64; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"483 s"},{"label":"B","text":"965 s"},{"label":"C","text":"1930 s"},{"label":"D","text":"1930 min"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$0.64 g of copper is 0.01 mol, and Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu needs 0.02 mol of electrons, i.e. Q = 2 × 96500 × 0.01 = 1930 C. At 2.0 A, t = Q ÷ I = 965 s, about 16 minutes.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Mass to moles","kind":"math","content":"n(Cu) = 0.64 ÷ 64 = 0.01 mol","note":"always start from the balanced half-equation"},{"title":"Electrons needed","kind":"math","content":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so n(e⁻) = 2 × 0.01 = 0.02 mol, Q = 0.02 × 96500 = 1930 C","note":"two electrons per Cu²⁺"},{"title":"Charge to time","kind":"math","content":"t = Q ÷ I = 1930 ÷ 2.0 = 965 s","note":"approximately 16 minutes"},{"title":"Trap check","kind":"text","content":"counting one electron per Cu²⁺ would give 483 s","note":"verify the ion charge before dividing"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2018,35, $q$A solution of pH 3 is how many times more acidic than a solution of pH 5?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 times"},{"label":"B","text":"5 times"},{"label":"C","text":"1000 times"},{"label":"D","text":"100 times"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The pH scale is logarithmic, so each unit stands for a factor of ten in [H₃O⁺]. A gap of two units is therefore 10² = 100 times more acidic; note that 2 × 5 arithmetic is meaningless here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2018,36, $q$Which of the following solids conducts electricity when molten but not in the solid state?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"graphite"},{"label":"B","text":"copper"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"diamond"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In solid sodium chloride the Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions are locked in the lattice, but melting frees them to migrate, so the melt conducts. Graphite and copper conduct even while solid, and diamond never conducts.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2018,37, $q$Ethane, C₂H₆, and propane, C₃H₈, belong to which homologous series?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"alkenes"},{"label":"B","text":"alkynes"},{"label":"C","text":"alkanoates"},{"label":"D","text":"alkanes"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Both molecules contain only single C–C bonds and fit the general formula CnH₂n₊₂. Successive members of a homologous series share that general formula and differ by a −CH₂− unit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2018,38, $q$In paper chromatography, the component of a dye mixture that travels furthest up the paper is the one that is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"least soluble in the solvent"},{"label":"B","text":"most soluble in the solvent"},{"label":"C","text":"the heaviest"},{"label":"D","text":"the most volatile"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The moving solvent competes with the stationary paper for each component, and the most soluble (least strongly adsorbed) dye is carried furthest. Molecular mass and volatility have no direct part in the separation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2019,10, $q$2.0 moles of zinc are treated with 3.0 moles of dilute hydrochloric acid. From the equation Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂, how many moles of hydrogen are liberated before the reagent in short supply is used up?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"3.0 mol"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each mole of zinc needs 2 moles of acid, so 3.0 mol of HCl consumes only 1.5 mol of zinc and releases 1.5 mol of H₂. Zinc is in excess here; the acid controls the yield, a textbook limiting-reagent setup.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2019,11, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂COOH is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propanal"},{"label":"B","text":"propanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"propanone"},{"label":"D","text":"propan-1-ol"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The three-carbon chain carrying the COOH group is named from propionic acid as propanoic acid. Propanal would end in CHO, propanone is the three-carbon ketone, and propan-1-ol bears only an OH group.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2019,12, $q$An aqueous solution of sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) is alkaline because:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium ions hydrolyse in water to give OH⁻"},{"label":"B","text":"the carbonate ion is replaced by hydroxide on dissolution"},{"label":"C","text":"carbonate ions hydrolyse, releasing OH⁻ ions"},{"label":"D","text":"water ionises completely in the carbonate solution"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$CO₃²⁻ takes a proton from water to form HCO₃⁻, leaving an excess of OH⁻ in solution. The Na⁺, being the conjugate of a strong base, remains a spectator ion.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2019,13, $q$The number of neutrons in the nucleus of ³¹₁₅P is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"15"},{"label":"B","text":"16"},{"label":"C","text":"31"},{"label":"D","text":"46"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Neutrons = mass number − proton number = 31 − 15 = 16. The subscripts and superscripts must never be swapped when subtracting.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2019,14, $q$Adding a catalyst to a reversible reaction that has reached equilibrium has the effect of:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increasing the equilibrium yield of products"},{"label":"B","text":"speeding up the forward reaction only"},{"label":"C","text":"raising the value of Kc"},{"label":"D","text":"lowering the activation energy and speeding the forward and reverse reactions equally"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A catalyst provides a lower-energy path used identically in both directions, so the same equilibrium is simply reached sooner. The position of equilibrium and Kc remain untouched, and the yield never changes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2019,15, $q$Which element burns in oxygen to produce a gas that turns lime water milky?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"phosphorus"},{"label":"D","text":"iron"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Burning carbon yields CO₂, which precipitates white CaCO₃ in lime water. Hydrogen forms only water, phosphorus gives the solid P₄O₁₀ smoke, and iron produces solid triiron tetraoxide.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2019,16, $q$Which of the following compounds is held together by ionic bonds?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"water"},{"label":"B","text":"potassium fluoride"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"methane"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Potassium, a strongly electropositive metal, transfers its single outer electron to fluorine, leaving K⁺ and F⁻ in an electrostatic lattice. Water, CO₂ and CH₄ consist of discrete molecules sharing electron pairs covalently.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2019,17, $q$What volume of oxygen, measured at the same temperature and pressure, will react completely with 4.0 dm³ of hydrogen to form water steam?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"4.0 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"8.0 dm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O the reacting gas volumes are in the ratio 2 : 1 (Gay-Lussac's law). So 4.0 dm³ of hydrogen needs half its volume, 2.0 dm³, of oxygen.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2019,18, $q$Sand can be separated from a solution of sugar in water by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"filtration"},{"label":"B","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"C","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"chromatography"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Filtration holds the insoluble sand on the filter paper while the sugar solution passes through. The dissolved sugar is then recovered by evaporating or crystallising the filtrate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2019,19, $q$In the reaction Cl₂ + 2KI → 2KCl + I₂, the species oxidised is the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iodide ion"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine molecule"},{"label":"C","text":"potassium ion"},{"label":"D","text":"chloride ion"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Oxidation is electron loss, and each I⁻ loses an electron to become neutral iodine. Chlorine gains those electrons and is reduced, acting as the oxidising agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2019,20, $q$An alkene has a relative molecular mass of 42. Its molecular formula is: [H = 1, C = 12]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"C₂H₄"},{"label":"B","text":"C₃H₆"},{"label":"C","text":"C₃H₈"},{"label":"D","text":"C₄H₈"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Every alkene obeys CnH₂n, which has molar mass 14n, so n = 42 ÷ 14 = 3 and the formula is C₃H₆ (propene). Starting from the alkane formula CnH₂n₊₂ is what produces the C₃H₈ error.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Use the homologous formula","kind":"text","content":"alkenes follow CnH₂n, giving molar mass 14n g mol⁻¹","note":"each CH₂ unit adds 14"},{"title":"Solve for n","kind":"math","content":"n = 42 ÷ 14 = 3","note":"divide, never subtract"},{"title":"Write and check the formula","kind":"text","content":"C₃H₆, propene; the alkane series would need CnH₂n₊₂","note":"H count doubles the carbon count here"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2019,21, $q$1.0 mol of phosphorus pentachloride vapour is heated in a closed 10 dm³ vessel, and at equilibrium 0.40 mol of phosphorus trichloride is present. For PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g), the value of Kc is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.027 mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"0.016 mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"C","text":"0.040 mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"0.267 mol dm⁻³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Each 0.40 mol of PCl₃ formed brings 0.40 mol of Cl₂ into being and consumes 0.40 mol of PCl₅, leaving 0.60 mol. Dividing by the 10 dm³ volume, Kc = (0.040 × 0.040) ÷ 0.060 = 0.027 mol dm⁻³. Working with bare mole numbers gives the 0.267 trap.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Convert moles to concentrations","kind":"math","content":"[PCl₃] = 0.40 ÷ 10 = 0.040 M; [Cl₂] = 0.040 M; [PCl₅] = (1.0 − 0.40) ÷ 10 = 0.060 M","note":"1 PCl₃ made means 1 Cl₂ made and 1 PCl₅ consumed"},{"title":"Write the expression","kind":"text","content":"Kc = [PCl₃][Cl₂] ÷ [PCl₅]","note":"one product of the two product terms"},{"title":"Substitute and compute","kind":"math","content":"Kc = (0.040 × 0.040) ÷ 0.060 = 0.027, units mol dm⁻³ because Δn = +1","note":"gas moles increase from 1 to 2"},{"title":"Avoid the common error","kind":"text","content":"using mole amounts instead of concentrations gives 0.267","note":"the 10 dm³ vessel size matters"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2019,22, $q$Sulphur(IV) oxide dissolves in rain water to form:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"trioxosulphate(IV) acid"},{"label":"B","text":"tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrosulphuric acid"},{"label":"D","text":"sulphur(VI) oxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃, the diprotic trioxosulphate(IV) acid (sulphurous acid), which is the first step towards acid rain. Further oxidation in air can carry it on to tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2019,23, $q$Which of the following species contains exactly 10 electrons?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Mg"},{"label":"B","text":"Na"},{"label":"C","text":"Mg²⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"Cl⁻"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The magnesium atom starts with 12 electrons and loses its two outer ones, leaving the stable 2,8 arrangement of 10 electrons. Neutral sodium has 11 and Cl⁻ has 18.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2019,24, $q$Ethanol and water, which are completely miscible liquids of boiling points 78 °C and 100 °C respectively, are best separated by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"simple distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"a separating funnel"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Their boiling points lie close together, so a fractionating column gives repeated partial condensations that enrich the distillate in ethanol. Simple distillation cannot resolve close-boiling miscible liquids, and the pair never forms two layers.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2019,25, $q$Which of the following molecules contains a double covalent bond?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrogen chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"oxygen"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In O₂ the two atoms share two electron pairs, written O=O. Cl₂, H₂ and HCl each share just one pair between the bonded atoms.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2019,26, $q$What mass of calcium trioxocarbonate(IV), CaCO₃, contains 10 g of calcium? [Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20 g"},{"label":"B","text":"25 g"},{"label":"C","text":"40 g"},{"label":"D","text":"100 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Calcium makes up 40 of the 100 relative mass units of CaCO₃, so the carbonate needed is 10 × 100 ÷ 40 = 25 g. Multiplying by the fraction instead of dividing by it inverts the bridge.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2019,27, $q$In the electrolysis of molten lead(II) bromide using inert electrodes, the product formed at the cathode is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bromine"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"lead(II) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"molten metallic lead"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Pb²⁺ ions migrate to the cathode and are discharged: Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb, pooling as molten metal. Br⁻ travels the other way and is oxidised to red-brown bromine vapour at the anode.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2019,28, $q$Which of the following solutions will turn red litmus paper blue?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dilute hydrochloric acid"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonia solution"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"carbonated water"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Dissolved ammonia reacts with water to give OH⁻ ions, forming the weak base ammonium hydroxide. Hydrochloric acid, ethanoic acid and carbonated water are all acidic and would turn blue litmus red instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2019,29, $q$Vegetable oils are hardened into solid fats by reaction with:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen, using a nickel catalyst"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorine, in bright sunlight"},{"label":"C","text":"oxygen, under high pressure"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon(II) oxide"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The C=C bonds of the unsaturated oil take up hydrogen across a hot nickel catalyst, saturating the chains. This addition of hydrogen raises the melting point and yields solid fats such as margarine.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2019,30, $q$Which of the following elements has the highest electronegativity value?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"fluorine"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Fluorine tops the whole electronegativity scale at about 4.0, because its small size concentrates nuclear pull on the shared pair. Electronegativity rises across a period and up a group, and fluorine sits at the crest of both trends.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2019,31, $q$Which of the following metals reacts vigorously with cold water, liberating hydrogen and leaving an alkaline solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iron"},{"label":"B","text":"zinc"},{"label":"C","text":"copper"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Sodium skates about on the water surface as 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂ proceeds fast enough to ignite the gas. Iron, zinc and copper are too unreactive towards cold water to show this behaviour.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2019,32, $q$An organic compound contains 40.0% carbon, 6.7% hydrogen and 53.3% oxygen by mass. Its empirical formula is: [H = 1, C = 12, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CH₂O"},{"label":"B","text":"CH₄O"},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₆O"},{"label":"D","text":"C₆H₁₂O₆"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Taking a 100 g sample gives moles of C 3.33, H 6.7 and O 3.33, a simplest ratio of 1 : 2 : 1. The empirical formula is therefore CH₂O; glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆, matches that ratio only in a multiple of six and is a molecular formula anyway.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Assume a 100 g sample","kind":"text","content":"the percentages become masses: 40.0 g C, 6.7 g H, 53.3 g O","note":"percentage data becomes gram data"},{"title":"Convert each mass to moles","kind":"math","content":"C: 40.0 ÷ 12 = 3.33; H: 6.7 ÷ 1 = 6.7; O: 53.3 ÷ 16 = 3.33","note":"divide each mass by its relative atomic mass"},{"title":"Divide by the smallest","kind":"math","content":"ratio = 1.0 : 2.0 : 1.0 after dividing all by 3.33","note":"6.7 ÷ 3.33 ≈ 2"},{"title":"Read off the formula","kind":"text","content":"CH₂O; multiply up only when the ratios are near-halves","note":"empirical, not molecular, formula"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2019,33, $q$Which of the following factors affects the rate of a reaction but does NOT affect the position of a reversible reaction's equilibrium?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the presence of a catalyst"},{"label":"B","text":"raising the temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"changing reactant concentrations"},{"label":"D","text":"changing the total pressure in a gaseous equilibrium"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A catalyst shortens the trip to equilibrium equally in both directions and leaves Kc and the yields alone. Temperature, concentration and pressure all shift an equilibrium position as well as altering rates.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2019,34, $q$A charge of 9650 coulombs is passed through a solution of silver trioxonitrate(V). What mass of silver is deposited? [Ag = 108; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.4 g"},{"label":"B","text":"21.6 g"},{"label":"C","text":"10.8 g"},{"label":"D","text":"108.0 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$9650 C is 0.10 F of charge. Since Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, one faraday deposits one mole, so 0.10 mol = 0.10 × 108 = 10.8 g of silver. Divalent ions such as Cu²⁺ would give half as much for the same charge.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge to faradays","kind":"math","content":"Q ÷ F = 9650 ÷ 96500 = 0.10 mol of electrons","note":"count how many 96500 C portions are used"},{"title":"Write the half-equation","kind":"text","content":"Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag; one electron reduces one silver ion","note":"unlike Cu²⁺, which needs two"},{"title":"Scale to mass","kind":"math","content":"n(Ag) = 0.10 mol, m = 0.10 × 108 = 10.8 g","note":"one-to-one ratio with electrons"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2019,35, $q$Pure water can be obtained from sea water by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"evaporation"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"simple distillation"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Distillation vapourises only the water, leaving the dissolved salts behind, and the condenser returns it as liquid distilled water. Evaporation collects the salt instead, and no filtration removes dissolved solutes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2019,36, $q$The functional group present in propanone, CH₃COCH₃, is the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydroxyl group"},{"label":"B","text":"carboxyl group"},{"label":"C","text":"ketone (carbonyl) group"},{"label":"D","text":"amino group"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Propanone carries the C=O carbonyl of a ketone, linking the carbonyl carbon to two other carbons. Alkanals would instead carry the CHO group and alkanols the OH group.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2019,37, $q$The concentration in g dm⁻³ of a sodium hydroxide solution containing 0.05 mol dm⁻³ is: [Na = 23, O = 16, H = 1]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 g dm⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 g dm⁻³"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 g dm⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 g dm⁻³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Multiply the molar concentration by the molar mass: 0.05 × 40 = 2.0 g dm⁻³. Dividing by the molar mass instead would invert the conversion and shrink the value.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2020,10, $q$How much aluminium is deposited when 0.3 F of electricity is passed through molten aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃? [Al = 27; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.9 g"},{"label":"B","text":"2.7 g"},{"label":"C","text":"8.1 g"},{"label":"D","text":"27 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$0.3 F supplies 0.3 mol of electrons, and Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al calls for three electrons per atom. So n(Al) = 0.3 ÷ 3 = 0.1 mol, and the mass is 0.1 × 27 = 2.7 g. Dividing instead by 1 overlooks the trivalent ion charge.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge to electrons","kind":"math","content":"0.3 F means n(e⁻) = 0.3 mol","note":"1 F carries 6.02 × 10²³ electrons"},{"title":"Use the half-equation","kind":"text","content":"Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al in the molten oxide","note":"every 3 electrons yield 1 Al"},{"title":"Scale down and weigh","kind":"math","content":"n(Al) = 0.3 ÷ 3 = 0.1 mol, m = 0.1 × 27 = 2.7 g","note":"the 8.1 g answer forgot to divide"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2020,11, $q$The IUPAC name of the alkanol (CH₃)₂CHCH₂OH is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2-methylpropan-1-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"butan-1-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"2-methylbutan-1-ol"},{"label":"D","text":"propan-2-ol"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The longest chain bearing the OH carbon has three carbons (propan-1-ol), and numbering from the OH end puts the methyl branch on C-2. A four-carbon main chain would have to include the OH group to be valid, which this structure does not allow.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2020,12, $q$Which of the following aqueous solutions will turn blue litmus paper red?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium chloride solution"},{"label":"B","text":"potassium trioxonitrate(V) solution"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium ethanoate solution"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonium tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$(NH₄)₂SO₄ pairs the weak base ammonia with the strong acid H₂SO₄, so NH₄⁺ hydrolyses and releases H₃O⁺. Solutions of strong-base salts such as chloride, trioxonitrate(V) and ethanoate are neutral or alkaline.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2020,13, $q$An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 6. It belongs to group:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Group IV"},{"label":"B","text":"Group II"},{"label":"C","text":"Group VI"},{"label":"D","text":"Group VIII"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The outermost shell holds six electrons, fixing the element in Group VI (the chalcogens). Group number is read directly from the outer-shell count for main-group elements.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2020,14, $q$The atomic radius of the Group I elements increases down the group mainly because:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the nuclear charge grows weaker down the group"},{"label":"B","text":"each successive element adds a complete shell of electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"the electrons become progressively heavier"},{"label":"D","text":"the protons become lighter"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each new period opens a fresh principal shell, pushing the valence electron outwards despite rising nuclear charge. Increased shielding by inner shells reinforces the outward growth of the radius.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2020,15, $q$In the high temperatures of an internal combustion engine, the pollutant mainly formed from air that contributes to acid rain is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon(II) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"methane"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At spark-tube temperatures N₂ and O₂ of air combine to give oxides of nitrogen, chiefly NO which forms NO₂ on cooling. NO₂ dissolves in rain to give acidic solutions contributing to acid rain.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2020,16, $q$What mass of water contains the same number of molecules as 4.4 g of carbon(IV) oxide? [H = 1, C = 12, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.9 g"},{"label":"B","text":"1.8 g"},{"label":"C","text":"4.4 g"},{"label":"D","text":"18.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$4.4 g of CO₂ is 0.1 mol, and equal molecule counts mean equal mole amounts. So 0.1 mol of H₂O weighs 0.1 × 18 = 1.8 g.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2020,17, $q$For the exothermic synthesis N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), the condition that gives the greatest equilibrium yield of ammonia is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"high pressure and high temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"high pressure and low temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"low pressure and high temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"low pressure and low temperature"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Le Chatelier favours the side with fewer gas moles under high pressure and pushes an exothermic equilibrium backwards when heated, so yield rises with pressure and with cold. Moderately warm conditions are used industrially only to secure an acceptable rate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2020,18, $q$Kerosene shaken with water in a separating funnel and the mixture left to stand gives on top:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a layer of water over the kerosene"},{"label":"B","text":"a layer of kerosene over the water"},{"label":"C","text":"one uniform liquid layer"},{"label":"D","text":"a solid residue"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The two liquids are immiscible, and the one of lower density, kerosene, floats above. Opening the tap drains the water layer first, completing the separation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2020,19, $q$The oxidation number of chlorine in the chlorate ion, ClO₃⁻, is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+1"},{"label":"B","text":"+3"},{"label":"C","text":"+5"},{"label":"D","text":"+7"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The three oxygens total 3 × (−2) = −6, so the chlorine must carry x = +5 to give the ion charge of −1. Misreading the ion as ClO₄⁻ produces the spurious +7.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2020,20, $q$Which of the following polymers is produced by condensation polymerisation?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"poly(ethene)"},{"label":"B","text":"poly(chloroethene)"},{"label":"C","text":"nylon-6,6"},{"label":"D","text":"poly(propene)"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Nylon-6,6 forms from a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid with water split out at every amide link. Poly(ethene), poly(chloroethene) and poly(propene) grow by opening double bonds without losing any atoms, i.e. addition polymerisation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2020,21, $q$What is the pH of a 0.001 mol dm⁻³ solution of sodium hydroxide at 25 °C?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"11"},{"label":"D","text":"13"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$NaOH is a strong base giving [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³, so pOH = 3 and pH = 14 − 3 = 11. Quoting the pOH (3) as the pH, or subtracting from 14 before computing pOH, are the classic errors.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Dissociation","kind":"text","content":"NaOH is a strong base, so [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³","note":"complete ionisation of the base"},{"title":"Compute pOH","kind":"math","content":"pOH = −log₁₀(10⁻³) = 3","note":"the index is the pOH"},{"title":"Convert to pH","kind":"math","content":"pH = 14 − 3 = 11 at 25 °C","note":"Kw fixes pH + pOH = 14"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2020,22, $q$In the fractional distillation of liquid air, which of the following boils away first from the liquid mixture?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen, boiling at −183 °C"},{"label":"B","text":"argon, boiling at −186 °C"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen, boiling at −196 °C"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The most volatile component, i.e. the lowest-boiling gas, leaves the liquid first as the temperature rises: nitrogen at −196 °C. Argon and oxygen follow in order of rising boiling points.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2020,23, $q$Oxygen is prepared in the laboratory by heating which substance in the presence of manganese(IV) oxide as catalyst?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"potassium trioxochlorate(V)"},{"label":"D","text":"zinc oxide"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂ proceeds briskly with MnO₂ lowering the decomposition temperature. CaCO₃ gives CO₂, NH₄Cl merely sublimes, and ZnO does not decompose on heating in the apparatus.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2020,24, $q$What mass of chlorine gas occupies 5.6 dm³ at STP? [Cl = 35.5; molar volume at STP = 22.4 dm³]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8.9 g"},{"label":"B","text":"35.5 g"},{"label":"C","text":"71.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"17.75 g"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$5.6 dm³ at STP is 5.6 ÷ 22.4 = 0.25 mol. Chlorine exists as Cl₂, so the mass is 0.25 × 71 = 17.75 g; treating the gas as monatomic (35.5) gives the 8.9 g trap.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2020,25, $q$Which of the following species contains a dative (coordinate covalent) bond?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"methane, CH₄"},{"label":"C","text":"the sodium ion, Na⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"the oxygen molecule, O₂"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$In NH₄⁺ the nitrogen lone pair is donated into the empty orbital of a hydrogen ion, forming the N→H dative bond. CH₄, Na⁺ and O₂ contain only ordinary shared or no bonding at all in these simple pictures.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2020,26, $q$Iron structures under water are protected from rusting by attaching plates of a metal that acts as a sacrifice; the metal is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc"},{"label":"B","text":"copper"},{"label":"C","text":"tin"},{"label":"D","text":"silver"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Zinc is more easily oxidised than iron, so it corrodes preferentially and holds the iron cathodically protected. Copper, tin and silver are less reactive, so with them attached the iron itself would become the anode and rust faster.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2020,27, $q$Ethene can be converted directly to ethanol industrially by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fermentation"},{"label":"B","text":"reduction with hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"hydration with steam over a phosphoric acid catalyst"},{"label":"D","text":"chlorination with chlorine"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Steam adds across the C=C bond of ethene over a phosphoric acid catalyst to give ethanol directly. Fermentation works from sugars, not ethene, and the other treatments yield alkane or chlorinated products.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2020,28, $q$The most suitable indicator for the titration of dilute HCl against ammonia solution is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"phenolphthalein"},{"label":"B","text":"methyl orange"},{"label":"C","text":"litmus"},{"label":"D","text":"starch"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A strong acid versus weak base settles just acidic (pH near 4-5), right where methyl orange changes colour. Phenolphthalein shifts around pH 9, far past the equivalence point, and starch is not an indicator for acids.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2020,29, $q$1.2 g of carbon is burnt completely in a plentiful supply of 19.2 g of oxygen. What mass of carbon(IV) oxide is formed? [C = 12, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.2 g"},{"label":"B","text":"4.4 g"},{"label":"C","text":"20.4 g"},{"label":"D","text":"22.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n(C) = 1.2 ÷ 12 = 0.1 mol and n(O₂) = 0.6 mol, but the 1:1 equation consumes only 0.1 mol of O₂, so carbon limits the reaction. The yield is 0.1 mol of CO₂ = 0.1 × 44 = 4.4 g, and much oxygen stays behind.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of both reactants","kind":"math","content":"n(C) = 1.2 ÷ 12 = 0.1 mol; n(O₂) = 19.2 ÷ 32 = 0.6 mol","note":"convert both, not just one"},{"title":"Apply the equation ratio","kind":"text","content":"C + O₂ → CO₂ needs a 1:1 ratio, so carbon is limiting and 0.5 mol of O₂ is left over","note":"excess never dictates the yield"},{"title":"Mass of product","kind":"math","content":"n(CO₂) = 0.1 mol, m = 0.1 × 44 = 4.4 g","note":"mass must never exceed the reactant that ran out"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2020,30, $q$Which Group II hydroxide is the most soluble in water?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"magnesium hydroxide"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium hydroxide"},{"label":"C","text":"strontium hydroxide"},{"label":"D","text":"barium hydroxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Solubility of Group II hydroxides rises down the group, so Ba(OH)₂, being furthest down, dissolves most readily. Mg(OH)₂, at the top, is almost insoluble.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2020,31, $q$How many unpaired electrons does a nitrogen atom possess in its ground state configuration?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Nitrogen's outer shell is 2s² 2p³, and the three 2p electrons occupy the three separate p orbitals singly (Hund's rule). Hence three unpaired spins; total valence count of 5 would overfill the picture.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2020,32, $q$The average rate of consumption of a reactant in solution is expressed in:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"g"},{"label":"D","text":"dm³ mol⁻¹"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Rate is a change of concentration per unit time, so its unit is mol dm⁻³ per second, written mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. Mol dm⁻³ alone is just concentration, with no time element.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2020,33, $q$Which of the following mixtures can be separated completely by filtration?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a solution of common salt in water"},{"label":"B","text":"a solution of sugar in water"},{"label":"C","text":"a suspension of chalk in water"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanol dissolved in water"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Only undissolved particles are trapped by filter paper, and a chalk suspension settles exactly that way. Dissolved salt, sugar and ethanol pass straight through the paper with the water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2020,34, $q$Copper conducts electricity both as a solid and when molten because it contains:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mobile positive ions in a fixed lattice"},{"label":"B","text":"free protons between atoms"},{"label":"C","text":"pairs of shared outer electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"delocalised mobile valence electrons"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Metallic bonding leaves valence electrons free to drift through the cation lattice, carrying charge in any state. The ions themselves stay fixed in place, and no covalent pairs exist in the metal.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2020,35, $q$During the electrolysis of copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution using copper electrodes, the cathode:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dissolves away"},{"label":"B","text":"remains unchanged"},{"label":"C","text":"decreases in mass"},{"label":"D","text":"increases in mass"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Cu²⁺ ions deposit as metal on the cathode (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu), so it gains mass. It is the copper anode that dissolves, feeding fresh Cu²⁺ into the solution at the same rate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2020,36, $q$Ethanol warmed with ethanoic acid in the presence of a little concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid gives off a sweet smell of:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an ester"},{"label":"B","text":"an ether"},{"label":"C","text":"an alkanal"},{"label":"D","text":"a ketone"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The acid and the alkanol condense with loss of water to ethyl ethanoate, an ester (alkanoate). Ethers need dehydration of two alkanol molecules, and no alkanal or ketone can form here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2020,37, $q$What volume of 0.5 M hydrochloric acid will react completely with 5.3 g of anhydrous sodium trioxocarbonate(IV)? [Na₂CO₃ = 106]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"100 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"200 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"400 cm³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$5.3 g of Na₂CO₃ is 0.05 mol, and each carbonate needs two HCl, giving 0.1 mol of acid required. At 0.5 M that is 0.1 ÷ 0.5 = 0.2 dm³ = 200 cm³; using a 1:1 ratio would halve it to 100 cm³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2020,38, $q$Which of the following pairs represents isotopes of the same element?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"¹²C and ¹⁴N"},{"label":"B","text":"⁴⁰K and ⁴⁰Ar"},{"label":"C","text":"³¹P and ³¹S"},{"label":"D","text":"¹²C and ¹⁴C"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Isotopes share the proton number but differ in neutron number, exactly the ¹²C–¹⁴C case. The other pairs match mass numbers only, changing the element instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2021,10, $q$What mass of oxygen is required for the complete combustion of 5.6 g of ethene? [C₂H₄ = 28, O₂ = 32; C₂H₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 2H₂O]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9.6 g"},{"label":"B","text":"19.2 g"},{"label":"C","text":"28.8 g"},{"label":"D","text":"38.4 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$5.6 g of ethene is 0.2 mol, and the equation calls for 3 mol of O₂ per mol of alkene. The needed oxygen is 0.2 × 3 = 0.6 mol = 19.2 g.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2021,11, $q$An alkane has 4 carbon atoms per molecule. Which of the following compounds belongs to the same homologous series as it does?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"C₄H₁₀"},{"label":"B","text":"C₄H₈"},{"label":"C","text":"C₄H₆"},{"label":"D","text":"C₄H₉OH"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$All alkanes obey the general formula CnH₂n₊₂, and with n = 4 the hydrocarbon is C₄H₁₀, one −CH₂− unit beyond propane. C₄H₈, C₄H₆ and C₄H₉OH belong to the alkene, alkyne and alkanol series instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2021,12, $q$An aqueous solution of sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV), NaHCO₃, is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"strongly acidic"},{"label":"B","text":"neutral"},{"label":"C","text":"strongly basic"},{"label":"D","text":"weakly basic"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV) ion, HCO₃⁻, accepts protons from water more readily than it donates them, so the solution tests slightly alkaline. Its hydrolysis is limited, hence only weakly basic and never strongly so.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2021,13, $q$The mass number and proton number of an atom of X are 23 and 11 respectively. The number of neutrons present is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10"},{"label":"B","text":"11"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"23"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Neutrons = A − Z = 23 − 11 = 12. Subtracting the two downwards wrongly, or quoting Z itself, are the usual slips here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2021,14, $q$In an electrolytic cell, oxidation takes place at the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cathode, in both cell types"},{"label":"B","text":"the electrolyte only"},{"label":"C","text":"anode, in every electrolytic setting"},{"label":"D","text":"salt bridge"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Oxidation is always electron removal, and electrons leave the circuit at the anode wherever oxidation occurs. This holds for electrolytic and voltaic cells alike, whatever the electrode polarity.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2021,15, $q$Which of the following ions, all having 10 electrons, has the largest ionic radius?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Mg²⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"Na⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"F⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"O²⁻"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$These four ions are isoelectronic (10 electrons each), so the one with the smallest nuclear charge pulls in its electron cloud least. O²⁻ (Z = 8) is therefore the largest, with radius shrinking from F⁻ to Na⁺ to Mg²⁺ (Z = 12).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2021,16, $q$In column chromatography, the components of a plant pigment separate chiefly because of differences in their:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"boiling points"},{"label":"B","text":"adsorption on the stationary phase"},{"label":"C","text":"electrical charges"},{"label":"D","text":"melting points"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The moving solvent competes with the solid adsorbent for each pigment, and components held more strongly lag behind. Nothing boils or melts during the run, and charges matter only in special forms of the technique.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2021,17, $q$Increasing the concentration of hydrochloric acid increases its rate of reaction with magnesium ribbon mainly because:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the activation energy is lowered"},{"label":"B","text":"more acid particles collide with the magnesium each second"},{"label":"C","text":"the temperature of the mixture rises"},{"label":"D","text":"the surface area of the magnesium increases"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Crowding more H₃O⁺ particles into each dm³ raises the collision frequency per second at the same energy spread. Activation energy, temperature and surface area are untouched by a concentration change alone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2021,18, $q$Which of the following metals does NOT liberate hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zinc"},{"label":"B","text":"magnesium"},{"label":"C","text":"iron"},{"label":"D","text":"copper"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Copper lies below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so it cannot displace hydrogen from acids. Zinc, magnesium and iron all sit above hydrogen and dissolve with effervescence.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2021,19, $q$What is the percentage by mass of water of crystallisation in sodium trioxocarbonate(IV) decahydrate, Na₂CO₃·10H₂O? [Na = 23, C = 12, H = 1, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"37.1%"},{"label":"B","text":"47.0%"},{"label":"C","text":"62.9%"},{"label":"D","text":"85.7%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$M(Na₂CO₃·10H₂O) = 106 + 180 = 286 g mol⁻¹, of which water is 180 g. Hence %H₂O = 180 ÷ 286 × 100 = 62.9%; the 37.1% error inverts the fraction to answer for the anhydrous part.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2021,20, $q$The polymer formed by the addition polymerisation of tetrafluoroethene is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Teflon (poly(tetrafluoroethene))"},{"label":"B","text":"nylon"},{"label":"C","text":"Dacron"},{"label":"D","text":"Bakelite"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Each tetrafluoroethene monomer, CF₂=CF₂, simply opens its double bond and links up, giving the slippery fluoropolymer PTFE. Nylon, Dacron and Bakelite must condense two different monomers and split out water molecules.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2021,21, $q$How long will a current of 0.5 A take to deposit 1.08 g of silver from a silver trioxonitrate(V) solution? [Ag = 108; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"482.5 s"},{"label":"B","text":"965 s"},{"label":"C","text":"1930 s"},{"label":"D","text":"1930 min"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$1.08 g of silver is 0.01 mol, and Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag demands 0.01 mol of electrons, i.e. Q = 965 C. At 0.5 A, t = Q ÷ I = 965 ÷ 0.5 = 1930 s, about 32 minutes.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Mass to moles","kind":"math","content":"n(Ag) = 1.08 ÷ 108 = 0.01 mol","note":"start from the metal deposited"},{"title":"Electrons from the half-equation","kind":"text","content":"Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so n(e⁻) = n(Ag) = 0.01 mol","note":"monovalent metal: one electron each"},{"title":"Charge required","kind":"math","content":"Q = 0.01 × 96500 = 965 C","note":"1 F per mole of electrons"},{"title":"Time from Q = It","kind":"math","content":"t = 965 ÷ 0.5 = 1930 s","note":"weaker currents stretch the time"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','separation',2021,22, $q$A pure liquid can be separated from the non-volatile solid dissolved in it by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"simple distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"D","text":"paper chromatography"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Only the liquid has an appreciable vapour pressure, so it alone distils over and is condensed pure. Filter paper stops solid particles but cannot hold back anything already dissolved.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2021,23, $q$The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in a p sub-shell is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"14"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A p sub-shell holds three orbitals, 2 electrons each, giving at most 6. The figures 10 and 14 correspond to the d and f sub-shells respectively.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2021,24, $q$A covalent bond is formed by the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"complete transfer of electrons from one atom to another"},{"label":"B","text":"attraction between oppositely charged ions"},{"label":"C","text":"mutual sharing of one or more pairs of electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"delocalisation of electrons among metal cations"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Covalent bonding joins atoms when each contributes at least one electron to a shared pair. Full transfer gives ionic bonding, and delocalised electrons describe the metallic bond instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2021,25, $q$The conjugate base of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H₂SO₄, is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"SO₄²⁻"},{"label":"B","text":"SO₃"},{"label":"C","text":"HSO₃⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"HSO₄⁻"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Removing a single proton from H₂SO₄ leaves the hydrogen tetraoxosulphate(VI) ion, HSO₄⁻. SO₄²⁻ only arises after a second donation, and SO₃ and HSO₃⁻ belong to different species altogether.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2021,26, $q$In the contact process, vanadium(V) oxide is added to the equilibrium 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g). The effect of the catalyst is to:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increase the equilibrium yield of SO₃"},{"label":"B","text":"leave the equilibrium position unchanged while speeding both directions of the change"},{"label":"C","text":"increase the value of Kc"},{"label":"D","text":"shift the equilibrium towards SO₂"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The catalyst offers a lower-energy route used by the forward and reverse changes equally, so Kc and the equilibrium position are untouched. Industrial use of the catalyst is solely to approach equilibrium quickly, since yield is bought with pressure and temperature instead.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"One catalyst, both directions","kind":"text","content":"the same lower-energy path serves the forward and reverse changes alike","note":"catalysis never favours one side"},{"title":"Effect on Kc","kind":"text","content":"Kc and the equilibrium position are unchanged; only the time to reach equilibrium falls","note":"Kc depends on temperature alone"},{"title":"Why industry bothers","kind":"text","content":"a faster approach to the same equilibrium raises the throughput of SO₃, not the yield","note":"classic misconception: catalysts do not increase yield"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2021,27, $q$When a burning magnesium ribbon is plunged into a jar of carbon(IV) oxide, it:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"continues burning, leaving magnesium oxide and specks of carbon"},{"label":"B","text":"forms magnesium carbonate"},{"label":"C","text":"produces magnesium oxide only"},{"label":"D","text":"goes out immediately"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Magnesium is avid enough for oxygen to strip it from CO₂: 2Mg + CO₂ → 2MgO + C. That is why CO₂ extinguishers are unsafe for magnesium fires.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2021,28, $q$What volume does 2.2 g of carbon(IV) oxide occupy at STP? [C = 12, O = 16; molar volume at STP = 22.4 dm³]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.56 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"2.24 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"22.4 dm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$2.2 g of CO₂ is 2.2 ÷ 44 = 0.05 mol, so the volume is 0.05 × 22.4 = 1.12 dm³. Skipping the mole step and pairing grams with molar volume directly invites all the wrong choices here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2021,29, $q$Propan-2-ol, on gentle oxidation, is converted to:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"propanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"propanal"},{"label":"C","text":"propanone"},{"label":"D","text":"propane"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A secondary alkanol oxidises to the corresponding ketone, so propan-2-ol gives propanone (acetone). Alkanals and alkanoic acids arise only from primary alkanols such as propan-1-ol.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2021,30, $q$In the electrolysis of dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid using platinum electrodes, the gas evolved at the cathode is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"sulphur(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"tetraoxosulphate(VI) vapour"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$H₃O⁺ ions from the acid migrate to the cathode and are discharged: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. Oxygen forms at the anode instead, the two changes together amounting to the decomposition of water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2021,31, $q$Which of the following molecules is a polar covalent molecule?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"CH₄"},{"label":"B","text":"Cl₂"},{"label":"C","text":"HCl"},{"label":"D","text":"CO₂"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$HCl pairs atoms of different electronegativity, so the shared pair sits nearer the chlorine and the bond is polar. CH₄, Cl₂ and CO₂ either share between identical atoms or cancel their dipoles by symmetry.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2021,32, $q$Ammonium chloride mixed with sodium chloride can be separated by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration"},{"label":"C","text":"crystallisation"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Gentle heating drives NH₄Cl to sublime and redeposit on a cool surface, while NaCl remains in the dish. Both salts dissolve in water identically, so solution methods would mix them again.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2021,33, $q$How many atoms are present in 0.5 mol of methane, CH₄? [Avogadro number = 6.02 × 10²³]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.01 × 10²³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.505 × 10²⁴"},{"label":"C","text":"3.01 × 10²⁴"},{"label":"D","text":"6.02 × 10²⁴"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$0.5 mol holds 3.01 × 10²³ molecules, and each CH₄ molecule contains 5 atoms. Multiplying gives 5 × 3.01 × 10²³ = 1.505 × 10²⁴ atoms; forgetting the 5 leaves the molecule count of option A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2021,34, $q$0.24 g of magnesium dissolves in dilute HCl in exactly 100 s, with all the hydrogen collected over water at STP conditions. What is the average rate of hydrogen evolution? [Mg = 24; molar volume = 22.4 dm³]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.224 cm³ s⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"22.4 cm³ s⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"2.24 cm³ s⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"224 cm³ s⁻¹"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$0.24 g of Mg is 0.01 mol, giving 0.01 mol of H₂ = 224 cm³ of gas in 100 s. Dividing by time yields 2.24 cm³ s⁻¹. Confusing grams with moles or skipping the 22.4 scaling explains the other choices.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of magnesium","kind":"math","content":"n(Mg) = 0.24 ÷ 24 = 0.01 mol","note":"1:1 with hydrogen: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂"},{"title":"Volume of hydrogen","kind":"math","content":"n(H₂) = 0.01 mol, V = 0.01 × 22400 = 224 cm³ at STP","note":"1000 cm³ per dm³"},{"title":"Divide by time","kind":"math","content":"rate = 224 ÷ 100 = 2.24 cm³ s⁻¹","note":"rates are volume per unit time"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2021,35, $q$Which of the following compounds is NOT a straight-chain alkane?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2,2-dimethylpropane"},{"label":"B","text":"butane"},{"label":"C","text":"pentane"},{"label":"D","text":"hexane"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$2,2-Dimethylpropane carries two methyl branches on a central carbon, so its carbon skeleton is branched, not straight. Butane, pentane and hexane as named here have unbranched chains.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2021,36, $q$A solution has pH = 9. Its pOH and its hydroxide ion concentration are respectively? [Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 and 1 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"9 and 1 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"C","text":"5 and 1 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"9 and 1 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$pOH = 14 − 9 = 5, and [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ follows directly from the definition of pOH. The pH value of 9 belongs to the H₃O⁺ concentration (10⁻⁹), which option B wrongly attaches to the hydroxide scale.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2021,37, $q$An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 8, 1. It is found in period:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The principal shells occupied, four of them, fix the period number, while the single outer electron fixes Group I. This configuration belongs to potassium.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2022,10, $q$A charge of 0.4 F of electricity is passed through molten chromium(III) oxide, Cr₂O₃. What mass of chromium metal is deposited? [Cr = 52; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.31 g"},{"label":"B","text":"6.93 g"},{"label":"C","text":"13.9 g"},{"label":"D","text":"20.8 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$0.4 F supplies 0.4 mol of electrons, and Cr³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Cr needs three per atom, so n(Cr) = 0.4 ÷ 3 = 0.133 mol. The mass is 0.133 × 52 = 6.93 g; dividing by 1 instead of 3 gives the 20.8 g error.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Electrons available","kind":"math","content":"0.4 F = 0.4 mol of electrons","note":"moles of charge counted in faradays"},{"title":"Half-equation sets the ratio","kind":"text","content":"Cr³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Cr in molten Cr₂O₃","note":"trivalent ion, three electrons"},{"title":"Scale to moles of metal","kind":"math","content":"n(Cr) = 0.4 ÷ 3 = 0.133 mol","note":"divide by the electron count"},{"title":"Mass from molar mass","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.133 × 52 = 6.93 g","note":"never multiply the 0.4 by 52 directly"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2022,11, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH₂COCH₃ is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pentan-2-one"},{"label":"B","text":"pentan-3-one"},{"label":"C","text":"pentanal"},{"label":"D","text":"pentan-2-ol"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The five-carbon chain carries a ketone carbonyl, numbered from the nearer end so that the C=O carbon gets the lowest locant, 2. Numbering from the other end, or reading the CHO ending, produces the wrong options.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2022,12, $q$An aqueous solution of aluminium chloride is acidic. This is because:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chloride ions hydrolyse to give H₃O⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"aluminium chloride is itself a strong acid"},{"label":"C","text":"aluminium ions hydrolyse, releasing H₃O⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"aluminium oxide dissolves in the water"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The small, highly charged Al³⁺ polarises water molecules so strongly that a proton is released to the solution. Cl⁻, the conjugate of a strong acid, hydrolyses not at all.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2022,13, $q$Chlorine consists of ³⁵Cl (75%) and ³⁷Cl (25%). Its relative atomic mass is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"35.0"},{"label":"B","text":"35.5"},{"label":"C","text":"36.0"},{"label":"D","text":"37.0"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$RAM = (0.75 × 35) + (0.25 × 37) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.5. The plain average 36.0 would apply only to equal isotopic abundances.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2022,14, $q$Down Group VII, from fluorine to iodine, the oxidising power of the halogens:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"increases"},{"label":"B","text":"decreases"},{"label":"C","text":"remains unchanged"},{"label":"D","text":"first increases then decreases"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Larger atoms with more shells gain electrons less eagerly because inner shells shield the nuclear pull. Fluorine, the smallest halogen, is therefore the strongest oxidising agent, and the power fades towards iodine.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2022,15, $q$What volume of hydrogen is liberated at room conditions (24 dm³ per mol) when 3.25 g of zinc reacts with excess dilute HCl? [Zn = 65]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.2 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"2.4 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"12.0 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"24.0 dm³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$3.25 g of zinc is 0.05 mol, and the equation Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂ gives an equal number of moles of hydrogen. At room conditions that is 0.05 × 24 = 1.2 dm³; quoting the STP value 2.24 dm³ mismatches the stated conditions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2022,16, $q$Sodium chloride crystals are obtained from a saturated brine solution by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"crystallisation"},{"label":"B","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"C","text":"decantation"},{"label":"D","text":"distillation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Evaporating the saturated solution until crystals form, or cooling a hot saturated solution, deposits pure salt crystals. Distillation would merely recover water, and nothing in brine can sublime.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2022,17, $q$For the reaction A + B → C, doubling the concentration of A (with B constant) doubles the rate. The order of the reaction with respect to A is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zero"},{"label":"B","text":"one-half"},{"label":"C","text":"first"},{"label":"D","text":"second"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$When rate ∝ [A]¹ the concentration entering the rate equation carries power 1, so doubling [A] doubles the rate. A second-order dependence would quadruple the rate on doubling, and zero order would leave it unchanged.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2022,18, $q$The gas liberated when sodium peroxide reacts with cold water is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"ammonia"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$2Na₂O₂ + 2H₂O → 4NaOH + O₂, so oxygen fizzes off while the solution turns alkaline. The peroxide ion supplies its own oxidised oxygen; no hydrogen is liberated from this reaction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2022,19, $q$In acidic solution the tetraoxomanganate(VII) ion, MnO₄⁻, is reduced to Mn²⁺. How many electrons does each ion gain?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"5"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Manganese falls from +7 in MnO₄⁻ to +2 in Mn²⁺, a drop of five units. Each unit of oxidation-number fall corresponds to one electron gained, so the half-equation takes 5e⁻ (with 8H⁺ and 4H₂O balancing it).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2022,20, $q$Dacron (polyester) is manufactured by condensing terephthalic acid with:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethanoic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"C","text":"ethene"},{"label":"D","text":"ethane-1,2-diol"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The diol supplies both OH ends needed to link acid molecules into the long ester chain, water being lost at each bond. Ethanol offers only one OH per molecule, and neither ethene nor ethanoic acid can bridge two ester links.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2022,21, $q$Which of the following pairs makes a buffer solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"HCl and NaCl"},{"label":"B","text":"H₂SO₄ and Na₂SO₄"},{"label":"C","text":"CH₃COOH and CH₃COONa"},{"label":"D","text":"NaOH and NaCl"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A buffer needs a weak acid together with its own salt, so CH₃COOH/CH₃COONa holds the pH near 4.7. Strong acid or strong base with their salts offers no reserve of the weak counterpart needed to mop up additions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2022,22, $q$What mass of potassium trioxochlorate(V), KClO₃, must be heated to liberate 11.2 dm³ of oxygen at STP? [KClO₃ = 122.5; molar volume at STP = 22.4 dm³; 2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40.8 g"},{"label":"B","text":"81.7 g"},{"label":"C","text":"30.6 g"},{"label":"D","text":"122.5 g"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$11.2 dm³ of O₂ is 0.5 mol, and the 2 : 3 mole ratio of KClO₃ to O₂ requires ⅔ × 0.5 = 0.333 mol of the salt. That is 0.333 × 122.5 = 40.8 g.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2022,23, $q$An element has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 7. It belongs to group:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"I"},{"label":"B","text":"III"},{"label":"C","text":"VII"},{"label":"D","text":"VIII"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Seven outer electrons place the element in Group VII, the halogens, one electron short of the stable octet. It will readily gain an electron to complete its shell as Cl⁻ does.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2022,24, $q$A liquid that decomposes when heated to its boiling point at atmospheric pressure can best be purified by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"simple distillation at atmospheric pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"distillation under reduced pressure"},{"label":"D","text":"extraction with a suitable solvent"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Lowering the pressure lowers the boiling point, so the liquid vapourises and condenses before reaching its decomposition temperature. Boiling it at 1 atm destroys whatever will not survive the heat, and solvent extraction cannot sharpen a volatile liquid to purity in this context.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2022,25, $q$Which of the following conducts electricity in the solid state?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"graphite"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"iodine"},{"label":"D","text":"diamond"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Graphite's delocalised electrons move along its layered planes even when the solid is intact. NaCl must be molten or dissolved to free its ions, while iodine and diamond are molecular or network-covalent insulators throughout.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2022,26, $q$In the reaction Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, the oxidising agent is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Zn"},{"label":"B","text":"Cu"},{"label":"C","text":"Cu²⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"Zn²⁺"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The oxidising agent is itself reduced, and Cu²⁺ falls to Cu by taking the two electrons zinc gives up. Zn is the reducing agent, while the products have already done their part.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2022,27, $q$Proteins are condensation polymers built from monomers called:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glucose units"},{"label":"B","text":"glycerol molecules"},{"label":"C","text":"nucleotides"},{"label":"D","text":"amino acids"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Amino acids link by peptide (amide) bonds with water eliminated, exactly the acid-plus-amine condensation of proteins. Glucose builds starch or cellulose, glycerol builds fats, and nucleotides build nucleic acids.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2022,28, $q$What mass of anhydrous sodium trioxocarbonate(IV), Na₂CO₃, is needed to prepare 500 cm³ of a 0.2 M solution? [Na₂CO₃ = 106]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.3 g"},{"label":"B","text":"21.2 g"},{"label":"C","text":"10.6 g"},{"label":"D","text":"53 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n = C × V = 0.2 × 0.5 = 0.1 mol, and the mass follows as 0.1 × 106 = 10.6 g. Using 500 as dm³? no—skipping the cm³→dm³ conversion doubles the demand to 21.2 g mistakenly if 500 were treated as 1 dm³.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles from C × V","kind":"math","content":"n = 0.2 mol dm⁻³ × 0.5 dm³ = 0.1 mol","note":"convert 500 cm³ to 0.5 dm³ first"},{"title":"Molar mass check","kind":"math","content":"M(Na₂CO₃) = 2(23) + 12 + 3(16) = 106 g mol⁻¹","note":"add up all atoms"},{"title":"Moles to mass","kind":"math","content":"m = 0.1 × 106 = 10.6 g","note":"weigh, dissolve, make up to the mark"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2022,29, $q$For the exothermic equilibrium 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), which change shifts the equilibrium towards SO₃?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"adding a catalyst"},{"label":"B","text":"raising the temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"decreasing the total pressure"},{"label":"D","text":"increasing the total pressure"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The product side holds fewer gas moles (3 → 2), so squeezing the mixture drives it forwards. Heating undoes an exothermic change, and a catalyst only shortens the journey without moving the position.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2022,30, $q$The major ore of aluminium is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"haematite"},{"label":"B","text":"bauxite"},{"label":"C","text":"galena"},{"label":"D","text":"zinc blende"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Bauxite is hydrated aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃·2H₂O, the feedstock for electrolytic extraction of aluminium. Haematite is iron ore, galena lead sulphide, and zinc blende zinc sulphide.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2022,31, $q$The reaction between an acid and a base to give salt and water only is called:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"neutralisation"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrolysis"},{"label":"C","text":"saponification"},{"label":"D","text":"esterification"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Neutralisation is exactly H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O forming salt and water. Hydrolysis reverses such? splits salts with water, saponification is alkaline fat breakdown, and esterification joins acid with alkanol.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2022,32, $q$Two miscible liquids whose boiling points are very close are best separated by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"simple distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"evaporation"},{"label":"D","text":"sublimation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The fractionating column provides repeated condensation-revaporisation cycles, enriching the vapour in the more volatile component. One simple distillation alone cannot tease liquids so close in volatility apart.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2022,33, $q$What is the molar concentration of a solution containing 4.9 g of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid in 250 cm³ of solution? [H₂SO₄ = 98]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.05 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.10 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.20 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.50 M"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$4.9 g of H₂SO₄ is 4.9 ÷ 98 = 0.05 mol, and concentration = n ÷ V = 0.05 ÷ 0.25 = 0.20 M. Forgetting to convert 250 cm³ to 0.25 dm³ is the error that wrecks every other option.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2022,34, $q$The number of neutrons in an atom of ³⁵₁₇Cl is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"17"},{"label":"B","text":"18"},{"label":"C","text":"35"},{"label":"D","text":"52"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Neutrons = mass number − proton number = 35 − 17 = 18. The proton count 17 fixes the element as chlorine, not the neutron count.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2022,35, $q$Which of the following statements about electrochemical cells is correct?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an electrolytic cell converts chemical energy into electrical energy"},{"label":"B","text":"the anode of a voltaic cell is its positive electrode"},{"label":"C","text":"the cathode of a voltaic cell attracts anions"},{"label":"D","text":"a voltaic cell converts chemical energy into electrical energy"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A voltaic (galvanic) cell runs a spontaneous redox change and exports electrical energy, the reverse of an electrolytic cell. In a voltaic cell the anode is negative, and cations are the species drawn to the cathode.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2022,36, $q$How many structural isomers of pentane, C₅H₁₂, exist?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The three structures are n-pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane. Splitting the chain in more ways simply repeats one of these, since no carbon may carry more than four bonds.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Straight chain first","kind":"text","content":"pentane: CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃, all five carbons in a line","note":"always begin with the unbranched chain"},{"title":"Shorten by one carbon","kind":"text","content":"moving one carbon up as a branch gives 2-methylbutane","note":"branch positions 2 and 3 coincide here"},{"title":"Branch more heavily","kind":"text","content":"2,2-dimethylpropane places all branches on one carbon","note":"three in total; one more split just repeats a shape"}]$st$, 3)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','systems',2000,10, $q$The chamber of the mammalian heart that pumps blood into the aorta is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"right atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"left ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"right ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"left atrium"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The left ventricle has the thickest muscular wall because it drives oxygenated blood at high pressure into the aorta for distribution round the whole body.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2000,11, $q$When the temperature of a medium rises well above the optimum of an enzyme, the enzyme is inactivated mainly because it$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"becomes more active"},{"label":"B","text":"collides with fewer substrate molecules"},{"label":"C","text":"dissolves completely in water"},{"label":"D","text":"loses its three-dimensional shape and active site"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Intense heat breaks the hydrogen and ionic bonds holding the folded protein, so the active site is distorted. Once denatured, the enzyme can no longer bind its substrate.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2000,12, $q$In the food chain grass -> grasshopper -> lizard -> hawk, the organism occupying the second trophic level is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"grasshopper"},{"label":"B","text":"lizard"},{"label":"C","text":"hawk"},{"label":"D","text":"grass"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The grass is the producer at the first level. The grasshopper, a herbivore feeding directly on it, is therefore the primary consumer of the second level.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2000,13, $q$When two heterozygous tall pea plants (Tt x Tt) are crossed, the ratio of tall to short offspring is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 tall : 1 short"},{"label":"B","text":"9 tall : 7 short"},{"label":"C","text":"3 tall : 1 short"},{"label":"D","text":"all tall"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The cross yields TT, Tt, Tt and tt. Since T is completely dominant over t, three genotypes are tall and one is short, giving the classic 3 : 1 monohybrid ratio.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2000,14, $q$In an active cell, the organelle in which aerobic respiration is most intense is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"mitochondrion"},{"label":"C","text":"ribosome"},{"label":"D","text":"chloroplast"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mitochondria contain the enzymes of the Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain, so most of the energy of glucose is released there. This earns it the name powerhouse of the cell.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2000,15, $q$A flower that is pollinated by wind is likely to have$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"large brightly coloured petals"},{"label":"B","text":"sweet scent and nectar"},{"label":"C","text":"heavy sticky pollen grains"},{"label":"D","text":"small dull flowers with light dry pollen"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Wind-pollinated flowers are small and dull, with no scent or nectar. They release abundant light, smooth pollen that air currents can carry, and their stigmas are feathery.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2000,16, $q$The correct pathway of impulses in a simple spinal reflex is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"receptor -> sensory neurone -> relay neurone -> motor neurone -> effector"},{"label":"B","text":"receptor -> motor neurone -> relay neurone -> sensory neurone -> effector"},{"label":"C","text":"effector -> sensory neurone -> relay neurone -> motor neurone -> receptor"},{"label":"D","text":"receptor -> relay neurone -> cerebrum -> sensory neurone -> effector"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The receptor generates the impulse, a sensory neurone carries it to the spinal cord where a relay neurone links it to a motor neurone. The motor neurone then triggers the effector, usually a muscle or gland.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2000,17, $q$When an Elodea leaf cell is placed in a concentrated sugar solution, it$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"becomes turgid"},{"label":"B","text":"undergoes plasmolysis"},{"label":"C","text":"swells and bursts"},{"label":"D","text":"remains unchanged"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The strong sugar solution is hypertonic, so water leaves the cell by osmosis. The protoplasm shrinks and pulls away from the cellulose wall, which is plasmolysis.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),('biology','evolution',2000,18, $q$In Darwin's theory, the phrase "survival of the fittest" refers to organisms$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"that are the biggest in size"},{"label":"B","text":"that are best adapted to their environment and leave offspring"},{"label":"C","text":"that win all fights with rivals"},{"label":"D","text":"that eat the most food"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fitness means being well suited to the prevailing environment so as to survive and reproduce. Size or strength alone is no guarantee of leaving descendants.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2000,19, $q$The largest fraction of carbon dioxide is transported in human blood$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dissolved in the plasma"},{"label":"B","text":"attached to the surface of platelets"},{"label":"C","text":"as carbon monoxide"},{"label":"D","text":"as bicarbonates in the plasma"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Carbon dioxide combines with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into hydrogen carbonate (bicarbonate) ions carried in the plasma. This accounts for about 70 per cent of transport.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2000,20, $q$A heterozygous man of blood group A (IA i) marries a woman of blood group O (i i). The blood groups expected among their children are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50% group A and 50% group O"},{"label":"B","text":"all group A"},{"label":"C","text":"all group O"},{"label":"D","text":"50% group AB and 50% group O"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Group A heterozygous father = IA i; group O mother = i i. Group O can only have the genotype i i.$q$, 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Write the parental genotypes","kind":"text","content":"Group A heterozygous father = IA i; group O mother = i i.","note":"Group O can only have the genotype i i."},{"title":"List the gametes","kind":"text","content":"The father forms IA or i gametes; the mother forms i only.","note":"Each gamete carries one allele of the gene."},{"title":"Combine the gametes","kind":"scene","content":"IA with i gives group A (IA i); i with i gives group O (i i).","note":"In expectation two children are A and two are O."},{"title":"State the outcome","kind":"math","content":"50% group A : 50% group O","note":"No AB child is possible because the mother carries no IB allele."}]$st$, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2000,21, $q$After fertilisation in a flowering plant, the structure that develops into the seed is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ovary"},{"label":"B","text":"anther"},{"label":"C","text":"ovule"},{"label":"D","text":"corolla"}]$o$,
 'C',
