('biology','cellular-processes',2009,22, $q$An enzyme works rapidly at 35 °C but stops working completely at 55 °C. The best explanation is that at 55 °C the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"substrate becomes insoluble"},{"label":"B","text":"enzyme is denatured and its active site loses its shape"},{"label":"C","text":"pH of the reaction mixture falls sharply"},{"label":"D","text":"substrate molecules are broken apart by the heat"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Excess heat breaks the bonds that hold the enzyme's three-dimensional shape, so the active site no longer fits the substrate. This denaturation permanently destroys catalytic ability, unlike mere cooling.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Rising phase","kind":"text","content":"From low temperatures up to the optimum (about 37 °C for human enzymes), heating increases kinetic energy, so enzyme and substrate collide more often and the reaction rate climbs.","note":"Inactivity at low temperature is temporary."},{"title":"Optimum point","kind":"text","content":"At the optimum temperature the rate peaks: the shape of the active site is perfect and collisions are frequent.","note":"For most animal enzymes the optimum is about 37 °C."},{"title":"Beyond the optimum","kind":"text","content":"Above the optimum, heat energy breaks the hydrogen and ionic bonds maintaining the enzyme's tertiary structure.","note":"The active site no longer fits the substrate."},{"title":"Outcome","kind":"text","content":"Denatured enzymes no longer bind substrates, so activity falls abruptly rather than gradually.","note":"Denaturation here is irreversible; cool substrates alone cannot restore it."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','ecology',2009,23, $q$Millipedes and woodlice that feed on fallen decaying leaves in the soil are best described as$q$,
