 $q$Organs with the same structural plan and embryonic origin but performing different functions are homologous; they show descent from a common ancestor. Analogous structures look similar in function but differ in structure.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2009,29, $q$Starting at the vena cava, trace the route of deoxygenated blood as it leaves the heart for the lungs.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"vena cava → left atrium → left ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs"},{"label":"B","text":"vena cava → right ventricle → right atrium → pulmonary vein → lungs"},{"label":"C","text":"vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs"},{"label":"D","text":"vena cava → right atrium → pulmonary vein → lungs"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Blood returning by the vena cava enters the right atrium, passes the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and is pumped out through the pulmonary artery. The pulmonary vein instead returns oxygenated blood to the left atrium.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Entry side","kind":"text","content":"The vena cava is the vein that empties deoxygenated blood from the body into the right atrium of the heart.","note":"The left side of the heart deals only with oxygenated blood."},{"title":"Atrium to ventricle","kind":"text","content":"From the right atrium, blood crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","note":"Valves stop backflow as the chambers contract."},{"title":"Outward journey","kind":"text","content":"The right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary artery, the only artery that carries deoxygenated blood.","note":"It leads straight to the lungs for gaseous exchange."},{"title":"Return leg","kind":"text","content":"Oxygenated blood comes back by the pulmonary vein to the left atrium, never the right.","note":"That completes only one half of the double circulation."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2009,30, $q$The respiratory quotient (RQ) of a respiratory substrate was found to be 0.7. The substrate is most likely$q$,
