 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2011,13, $q$A pea plant heterozygous for both seed shape and seed colour (RrYy) is self-fertilized. How many different phenotypes are expected in the F2 generation?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"9"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Independent assortment lets the R, r, Y and y alleles combine freely, producing 16 equally likely F2 genotypes. These sort into round-yellow, round-green, wrinkled-yellow and wrinkled-green — four phenotypes in the ratio 9:3:3:1.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Gamete types","kind":"text","content":"A dihybrid RrYy makes four kinds of gamete in equal numbers: RY, Ry, rY and ry.","note":"This follows from independent assortment."},{"title":"F2 grid","kind":"math","content":"Selfing gives a 4 × 4 grid with 16 equally likely combinations of genotypes.","note":"For example, RrYy × RrYy yields 16 cells."},{"title":"Phenotype counting","kind":"text","content":"The 16 cells group into 9 round-yellow (RY), 3 round-green, 3 wrinkled-yellow and 1 wrinkled-green.","note":"Round (R) and yellow (Y) are each dominant."},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"Despite 16 genotypes, only 4 distinct phenotypes appear, in the 9:3:3:1 ratio.","note":"This ratio confirmed Mendel's second law."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','evolution',2011,14, $q$The embryos of fish, birds and mammals look almost identical in their early stages, even though the adults differ widely. Such observations support the idea of$q$,
