 $q$The plasma membrane is selectively permeable, admitting some molecules while excluding others. The non-living cell wall of plants is freely permeable and cannot control traffic in this way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2011,27, $q$Which biome would be expected to contain the greatest variety of species?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"desert"},{"label":"B","text":"arctic tundra"},{"label":"C","text":"tropical rain forest"},{"label":"D","text":"temperate grassland"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Constant warmth, abundant rainfall and a layered, unbroken plant cover let countless species coexist in the rain forest. Deserts and tundra are harsh and offer far fewer niches.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2011,28, $q$Unrelated groups of animals that occupy similar habitats often evolve similar body forms. This pattern of evolution is termed$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"common ancestry"},{"label":"B","text":"convergent evolution"},{"label":"C","text":"genetic drift"},{"label":"D","text":"artificial selection"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Different ancestors facing the same environment may reach similar solutions, such as streamlined bodies in fishes and whales. The unfolding of unrelated lines towards one form is convergent evolution.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2011,29, $q$The highest blood pressure in the human circulatory system is found in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"aorta"},{"label":"B","text":"vena cava"},{"label":"C","text":"pulmonary vein"},{"label":"D","text":"capillaries"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The aorta receives the full force of contraction from the thick-walled left ventricle. Pressure falls progressively through arteries, capillaries and veins as blood travels round the circuit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2011,30, $q$In an experiment to show that carbon dioxide is necessary for photosynthesis, a leaf is enclosed in a bell jar over soda lime, kept in light for several hours and then tested with iodine. Why does the leaf remain brown?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the light destroyed the enzyme in the leaf"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen flooded the jar"},{"label":"C","text":"the leaf was boiled for too long"},{"label":"D","text":"soda lime absorbed the carbon dioxide, so no starch was made"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Soda lime removes CO₂ from the enclosed air, leaving the leaf unable to photosynthesize despite adequate light. With no starch formed, iodine gives no blue-black colour.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Destarching","kind":"text","content":"The plant is first kept in darkness for a day so existing starch is used up.","note":"Old starch must not confuse the result."},{"title":"CO₂ removal","kind":"text","content":"The experimental leaf sits in a bell jar containing soda lime, which strongly absorbs carbon dioxide.","note":"A second leaf under plain air serves as control."},{"title":"Light exposure","kind":"text","content":"Both leaves receive sufficient light for several hours, so only the CO₂ supply differs.","note":"Light is not the limiting factor here."},{"title":"Starch test","kind":"text","content":"The soda-lime leaf stains brown (no starch) while the control stains blue-black.","note":"Hence CO₂ is essential for photosynthesis."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2011,31, $q$A couple are both rhesus negative (genotype dd). What rhesus types can their children have?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"all Rh-positive"},{"label":"B","text":"half Rh-positive, half Rh-negative"},{"label":"C","text":"all Rh-negative"},{"label":"D","text":"a mixture of both, unpredictable"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each parent can pass only the d allele, so every child receives dd and is rhesus negative. Rhesus positive children could appear only if at least one parent carried D.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2011,32, $q$The part of the brain that controls the rate of breathing is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cerebrum"},{"label":"B","text":"medulla oblongata"},{"label":"C","text":"cerebellum"},{"label":"D","text":"spinal cord"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The medulla oblongata houses automatic centres, including those for breathing and heartbeat. The cerebellum coordinates posture and movement instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2011,33, $q$Which group of bacteria converts nitrates in the soil back into atmospheric nitrogen?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"denitrifying bacteria such as Pseudomonas"},{"label":"B","text":"nitrifying bacteria"},{"label":"C","text":"ammonifying bacteria"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen-fixing bacteria in nodules"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Denitrifying bacteria reduce nitrates to nitrogen gas, which escapes to the atmosphere and is lost from the soil. Nitrifying bacteria work in the opposite direction, producing nitrates.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2011,34, $q$The mammalian heart is enclosed in a double-walled protective sac named the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pleural membrane"},{"label":"B","text":"peritoneum"},{"label":"C","text":"myocardium"},{"label":"D","text":"pericardium"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The pericardium surrounds the heart and lubricates it with fluid so that heartbeats cause little friction. The myocardium, by contrast, is the muscular wall of the heart itself.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2011,35, $q$The release of a mature ovum from a ripened Graafian follicle is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"menstruation"},{"label":"B","text":"fertilization"},{"label":"C","text":"ovulation"},{"label":"D","text":"parturition"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ovulation is the bursting of the mature Graafian follicle to set the ovum free, usually around mid-cycle. It is triggered mainly by a surge of luteinizing hormone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2011,36, $q$The site of protein synthesis within the cell is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lysosome"},{"label":"B","text":"ribosome"},{"label":"C","text":"vacuole"},{"label":"D","text":"centrosome"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Ribosomes read messenger RNA and assemble amino acids into proteins. Lysosomes digest worn-out material, and the centrosome takes part in cell division.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2011,37, $q$Sperm cells leaving the testes are immature and non-motile; they complete their maturation and are stored in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"epididymis"},{"label":"B","text":"seminal vesicle"},{"label":"C","text":"scrotum"},{"label":"D","text":"urethra"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The long coiled epididymis stores sperm and allows them time to become motile and fertile. The seminal vesicles merely add fluid to semen along the way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2011,38, $q$Oxygenated blood that arrives at the heart from the lungs enters first into the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"right atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"right ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"left ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"left atrium"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The pulmonary veins deliver oxygen-rich blood into the left atrium, which then passes it to the left ventricle. The right atrium receives only deoxygenated blood from the body.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cellular-processes',2010,10, $q$A destarched potted plant has one half of a leaf covered with black paper while the leaf is left in the light. After an iodine test, only the uncovered half turns blue-black. This experiment proves that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"water evaporates through the leaf"},{"label":"B","text":"respiration halts in the dark"},{"label":"C","text":"light is necessary for the manufacture of starch"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon dioxide is converted to starch in the dark"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Destarching removes any starch already present so that only newly made starch is detected. Since starch formed only where the leaf was illuminated, light is required for photosynthesis.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2010,11, $q$Which of these structures is present in plant cells but absent from animal cells?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mitochondrion"},{"label":"B","text":"cellulose cell wall"},{"label":"C","text":"ribosome"},{"label":"D","text":"nucleus"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Plant cells are enclosed by a rigid cellulose cell wall outside the living membrane, giving them a fixed, box-like shape. Animal cells lack walls, though both cell types have mitochondria, ribosomes and nuclei.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2010,12, $q$In the pond food chain algae → tadpoles → fish → heron, the tadpoles occupy which trophic level?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"second (primary consumer)"},{"label":"B","text":"first (producer)"},{"label":"C","text":"third (secondary consumer)"},{"label":"D","text":"fourth (tertiary consumer)"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The algae are the producers that make up the first trophic level. The tadpoles that feed directly on them are primary consumers, occupying the second level.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2010,13, $q$Two heterozygous mice (Aa × Aa) are crossed. The genotypic ratio of their offspring is expected to be$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 1"},{"label":"C","text":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Each parent produces A and a gametes in equal numbers, so the grid yields one AA, two Aa and one aa. The genotypic ratio 1:2:1 is distinct from the phenotypic 3:1.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2010,14, $q$The blood vessel that transports blood away from the kidney is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hepatic vein"},{"label":"B","text":"renal artery"},{"label":"C","text":"renal vein"},{"label":"D","text":"pulmonary vein"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The renal artery brings unfiltered blood into the kidney, and the renal vein carries the filtered blood away. The ureter, not a blood vessel, conveys the urine out.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2010,15, $q$A water plant is illuminated in water containing a little sodium bicarbonate, and gas bubbles are given off from the leaves. The gas collected is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon dioxide"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sodium bicarbonate supplies the carbon dioxide that plants use in photosynthesis. As the process runs, the plant releases oxygen, which is the gas collected and could relight a glowing splint.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2010,16, $q$The structural and functional unit of the mammalian kidney is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nephron"},{"label":"B","text":"neurone"},{"label":"C","text":"alveolus"},{"label":"D","text":"villus"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Each kidney contains many thousands of nephrons. A nephron filters blood at the glomerulus and reclaims water and useful substances, finally producing urine.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2010,17, $q$Which hormone maintains the thickened uterine lining during pregnancy after implantation has occurred?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"follicle-stimulating hormone"},{"label":"B","text":"luteinizing hormone"},{"label":"C","text":"oestrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"progesterone"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The corpus luteum secretes progesterone, which builds up and holds the endometrium in place, preventing menstruation. Oestrogen dominated earlier in the cycle, but progesterone sustains pregnancy.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2010,18, $q$Which chamber of the mammalian heart has the thickest muscular wall?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"right atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"left atrium"},{"label":"C","text":"left ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"right ventricle"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The left ventricle pumps blood around the whole body, so it needs the greatest force and the thickest wall. The right ventricle only drives blood to the nearby lungs.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2010,19, $q$The population of grasshoppers in a large field is best estimated using$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"counting every individual in the field"},{"label":"B","text":"the capture–mark–release–recapture method"},{"label":"C","text":"weighing the soil in the field"},{"label":"D","text":"counting the grass species present"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mobile animals like grasshoppers cannot all be counted directly, so an index of abundance is used. A marked sample released back into the population allows recaptures to estimate the total size.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Capture and mark","kind":"text","content":"Catch a first sample of grasshoppers, count and mark each one, then record the number caught.","note":"Mark only; do not trap permanently."},{"title":"Release","kind":"text","content":"Return the marked individuals to the field so they mix evenly with the rest of the population.","note":"Mixing makes recaptures representative."},{"title":"Recapture","kind":"text","content":"Catch a second sample later and record how many of the animals caught carry the mark.","note":"The proportion marked reflects dilution."},{"title":"Estimate","kind":"text","content":"Total population is estimated as (number marked) × (size of the recapture sample) ÷ (number marked in the recapture sample) — the Lincoln index.","note":"The bigger the samples, the more reliable the estimate."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2010,20, $q$A man with haemophilia (X^h Y) marries a homozygous normal woman (X^H X^H). What proportion of their sons will be haemophilic?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"none (0)"},{"label":"B","text":"all of the sons"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2 of the sons"},{"label":"D","text":"1/4 of the sons"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sons receive their single X chromosome from their mother, and she can pass only X^H. Every son will be normal, though every daughter will be a carrier (X^H X^h).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2010,21, $q$Darwin noted that finches on different islands of the Galapagos had differently shaped beaks, each suited to the food on its island, though all arose from the same ancestral stock. This is an example of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"genetic drift"},{"label":"B","text":"artificial selection"},{"label":"C","text":"vestigial structures"},{"label":"D","text":"adaptive radiation"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$One ancestral finch stock evolved into several diversely adapted species, each exploiting a different niche. The outward spreading of related species into varied roles is adaptive radiation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2010,22, $q$The complete products of aerobic respiration in a mammal's cells are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lactic acid and water"},{"label":"B","text":"ethanol and carbon dioxide"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon dioxide, water and energy"},{"label":"D","text":"carbon dioxide and lactic acid"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In the presence of oxygen, glucose is completely broken down to carbon dioxide and water, releasing energy as ATP. Lactic acid and ethanol are products of anaerobic respiration instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2010,23, $q$Which pairing illustrates mutualism, in which both partners benefit?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cattle egret and cattle"},{"label":"B","text":"Rhizobium bacteria in the root nodules of legumes"},{"label":"C","text":"dodder living on an orange plant"},{"label":"D","text":"roundworm living in a goat's intestine"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Rhizobium gains food and shelter from the nodule while supplying the legume with fixed nitrogen, so both partners profit. The egret benefits alone (commensalism), while dodder and roundworms are parasites.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2010,24, $q$The exchange of nutrients, gases and wastes between the blood and body cells takes place through the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"arterioles"},{"label":"C","text":"venules"},{"label":"D","text":"aorta"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Capillary walls are only one cell thick and blood flows through them slowly, making them ideal for exchange. Large vessels like arterioles and the aorta merely transport blood.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2010,25, $q$In flowering plants, after fertilization the ovule develops into the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"endosperm"},{"label":"B","text":"fruit"},{"label":"C","text":"pericarp"},{"label":"D","text":"seed"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The fertilized ovule matures into the seed, with its zygote becoming the embryo. The ovary swells instead into the fruit, while the endosperm stores food within the seed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2010,26, $q$Which organelle enables plant cells to manufacture their own food but is absent from bacterial cells?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mitochondrion"},{"label":"B","text":"ribosome"},{"label":"C","text":"chloroplast"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll and carry out photosynthesis, a feature of green plant cells. Bacteria are prokaryotes with no membrane-bound organelles, so they have no chloroplasts.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2010,27, $q$The functional role of an organism in its habitat, including what it eats and what eats it, is called its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"habitat"},{"label":"B","text":"ecological niche"},{"label":"C","text":"ecosystem"},{"label":"D","text":"biome"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The habitat is merely the address where an organism lives, while the niche is its profession. Two species cannot occupy exactly the same niche for long in one community.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2010,28, $q$The wings of a butterfly and the wings of a bird are described as analogous structures because they$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"perform a similar function but differ in structural origin"},{"label":"B","text":"possess identical bone arrangements"},{"label":"C","text":"are both vestigial"},{"label":"D","text":"have the same embryonic origin"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Both structures fly, but the insect wing is a thin outgrowth of the body wall while the bird wing is a modified forelimb. Similar function with different structure and origin is analogy, the result of convergent evolution.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2010,29, $q$Trace the order in which the nitrogen compounds in animal excreta must be changed in the soil before plants can use them.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"urea → nitrate → ammonia → nitrite"},{"label":"B","text":"urea → nitrite → ammonia → nitrate"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrate → urea → ammonia → nitrite"},{"label":"D","text":"urea → ammonia → nitrite → nitrate"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Decomposers first convert urea nitrogen into ammonia (ammonification). Nitrifying bacteria then oxidize the ammonia to nitrites and finally to nitrates, which are the forms plant roots absorb.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Ammonification","kind":"text","content":"Saprophytic bacteria and fungi break down urea and other nitrogenous wastes into ammonia.","note":"This recovers nitrogen from dead matter."},{"title":"Nitrite stage","kind":"text","content":"Nitrosomonas oxidizes ammonia to nitrites.","note":"Energy is gained by these specialists."},{"title":"Nitrate stage","kind":"text","content":"Nitrobacter oxidizes the nitrites further to nitrates.","note":"Nitrates are the main plant-usable form."},{"title":"Uptake","kind":"text","content":"Plant roots absorb the nitrates and use them to build amino acids and proteins.","note":"The nitrogen thus re-enters the food chain."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','cellular-processes',2010,30, $q$Anaerobic respiration in yeast cells produces$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"more energy than aerobic respiration"},{"label":"B","text":"lactic acid only"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanol and carbon dioxide"},{"label":"D","text":"glucose without oxygen"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Yeast and other plant-like cells ferment glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide, releasing little energy. Mammalian muscle instead produces lactic acid anaerobically.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2010,31, $q$In the four-o'clock plant, a pure red flower crossed with a pure white flower gives all pink offspring (incomplete dominance). If two pink F1 plants are crossed, the expected phenotypic ratio is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3 red : 1 white"},{"label":"B","text":"1 red : 2 pink : 1 white"},{"label":"C","text":"1 red : 1 pink : 1 white"},{"label":"D","text":"all pink"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Pink flowers are the heterozygotes (RW), so crossing two of them gives RR (red), RW (pink) twice, and WW (white). Neither colour is dominant, so all three phenotypes appear in a 1:2:1 ratio.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2010,32, $q$The skin, lungs and kidneys are all organs of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"excretion"},{"label":"B","text":"respiration"},{"label":"C","text":"digestion"},{"label":"D","text":"circulation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Each of these organs rids the body of metabolic wastes: the skin removes sweat, the lungs expel carbon dioxide and the kidneys filter out urea. Excretion is their shared function.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2010,33, $q$The ultimate source of energy for almost all ecosystems on earth is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the soil"},{"label":"B","text":"the wind"},{"label":"C","text":"decomposition of organic matter"},{"label":"D","text":"sunlight"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Green producers capture solar energy by photosynthesis and store it as food. All the energy that flows through food chains therefore originally comes from the sun.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2010,34, $q$The valve found between the left atrium and the left ventricle of the mammalian heart is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"tricuspid valve"},{"label":"B","text":"aortic valve"},{"label":"C","text":"bicuspid (mitral) valve"},{"label":"D","text":"pulmonary valve"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The bicuspid valve has two flaps and separates the left atrium from the left ventricle. The corresponding valve on the right side has three flaps and is the tricuspid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2010,35, $q$A woman who is a carrier of haemophilia (X^H X^h) marries a man with normal blood clotting (X^H Y). What is the probability that their first child will be a haemophilic son?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1/2"},{"label":"B","text":"1/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/8"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The mother passes X^H and X^h eggs in equal numbers, while the father passes X^H or Y sperm in equal numbers. A haemophilic son needs both events at once: half times half equals one-quarter.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Mother's gametes","kind":"text","content":"The carrier mother produces two kinds of eggs: X^H and X^h, each with probability 1/2.","note":"She is unaffected because one X is normal."},{"title":"Father's gametes","kind":"text","content":"The normal father produces X^H sperm and Y sperm, each with probability 1/2.","note":"He donates only the normal allele."},{"title":"Son requirement","kind":"text","content":"A son arises when a Y sperm meets an egg, an event with probability 1/2.","note":"Daughters arise when an X^H sperm fertilizes an egg."},{"title":"Combine the events","kind":"math","content":"P(son and haemophilic) = P(Y sperm) × P(X^h egg) = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.","note":"The events are independent, so the probabilities multiply."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','evolution',2010,36, $q$Fossils of the same species of reptile have been unearthed in West Africa and in South America. This evidence supports the idea that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the two continents were once joined together"},{"label":"B","text":"reptiles evolved twice, independently"},{"label":"C","text":"fossils can migrate across oceans"},{"label":"D","text":"the continents have always been apart"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A land reptile could not cross a wide ocean, so identical fossils on facing continents point to a former land connection. The matching fossil record is evidence that the continents have drifted apart.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2010,37, $q$Cell sap in a plant cell is found mainly in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"cytoplasm"},{"label":"C","text":"mitochondrion"},{"label":"D","text":"central vacuole"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The large fluid-filled central vacuole holds cell sap, a solution of water, salts and sugars. Its outward pressure against the cell wall keeps herbaceous plants firm.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2010,38, $q$The organ that manufactures bile for the digestion of fats is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"stomach"},{"label":"C","text":"liver"},{"label":"D","text":"large intestine"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The liver cells continuously secrete bile, which the gall bladder stores and concentrates. The pancreas makes enzymes but not bile.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2012,10, $q$Lysosomes are sometimes described as 'suicide bags' because they$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"contain enzymes that could digest the cell itself"},{"label":"B","text":"store water instead of enzymes"},{"label":"C","text":"manufacture energy for the cell"},{"label":"D","text":"contain green pigment"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Lysosomes hold powerful hydrolytic enzymes normally kept isolated from the rest of the cell. If the membrane ruptures, the cell's own structures are digested.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2012,11, $q$The theory that organisms develop features by using them and pass the enlarged or shrunken organs to their offspring ('use and disuse') was put forward by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Charles Darwin"},{"label":"B","text":"Gregor Mendel"},{"label":"C","text":"Alfred Wallace"},{"label":"D","text":"Jean-Baptiste Lamarck"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Lamarck imagined a giraffe's stretching for high leaves lengthening its neck, and the length being inherited. Modern genetics shows acquired characters are not passed on in this way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2012,12, $q$Plants with thick, fleshy stems and sunken stomata, such as the cactus, are best suited to a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pond habitat"},{"label":"B","text":"swamp"},{"label":"C","text":"desert habitat"},{"label":"D","text":"saline estuary"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Desert plants are xerophytes, structured to store water and cut down loss through transpiration. Swamps and ponds instead favour hydrophytes, which are adapted to excess water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2012,13, $q$A man of blood group AB (I^A I^B) marries a woman of blood group O (i i). Which blood groups are possible in their children?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"AB only"},{"label":"B","text":"A and B only"},{"label":"C","text":"O only"},{"label":"D","text":"all four groups"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The father's two alleles I^A and I^B pass separately while the mother contributes only i. Children are I^A i (group A) or I^B i (group B), and can be neither AB nor O.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Father's gametes","kind":"text","content":"The AB father produces I^A sperm and I^B sperm in equal numbers.","note":"Codominant alleles segregate in meiosis."},{"title":"Mother's gametes","kind":"text","content":"The group O mother has genotype i i, so every egg carries only i.","note":"A double recessive makes one gamete type."},{"title":"offspring genotypes","kind":"math","content":"The two possible zygote types are I^A i and I^B i.","note":"Cells of a two-row Punnett grid."},{"title":"Phenotypes","kind":"text","content":"I^A i shows the A phenotype and I^B i shows the B phenotype, both in equal proportion.","note":"No AB or O child is possible in this mating."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','systems',2012,14, $q$The artery that carries blood from the heart to all parts of the body except the lungs is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"aorta"},{"label":"B","text":"pulmonary artery"},{"label":"C","text":"hepatic portal vein"},{"label":"D","text":"pulmonary vein"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The aorta receives blood from the left ventricle and distributes it through systemic branches. The pulmonary artery is the only artery that runs to the lungs.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2012,15, $q$The onset of puberty in girls is marked by$q$,
