('biology','genetics',2012,33, $q$A woman who is completely colour-blind (X^b X^b) marries a man with normal colour vision (X^B Y). What can be said of their sons?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"all sons will have normal vision"},{"label":"B","text":"all sons will be colour-blind"},{"label":"C","text":"half the sons will be colour-blind"},{"label":"D","text":"sons cannot inherit the trait from the mother"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The mother's eggs all carry one X^b. A son, receiving her X plus a Y from his father, will be X^b Y and colour-blind; every daughter will be a carrier at worst.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Mother's gametes","kind":"text","content":"A colour-blind woman (X^b X^b) can produce only one kind of egg: X^b.","note":"Homozygous recessive gives a single gamete type."},{"title":"Father's gametes","kind":"text","content":"The normal man produces X^B sperm and Y sperm in equal numbers.","note":"His X carries the dominant normal allele."},{"title":"Son formation","kind":"text","content":"A Y sperm plus an X^b egg produces an X^b Y son.","note":"Sons get their only X from the mother."},{"title":"Outcome","kind":"text","content":"Since every son receives the recessive X^b, all sons are colour-blind; daughters (X^B X^b) are carriers.","note":"Sex-linked recessives show criss-cross inheritance: father to daughter, mother to son."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','systems',2012,34, $q$The largest artery in the human body is the$q$,
