 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2013,33, $q$Two parents, each of group A and each heterozygous (I^A i), marry. What is the probability that their child will be group O?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/4"},{"label":"D","text":"3/4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each parent contributes I^A or i in equal shares. Only the combination i from both parents yields ii, the genotype of group O, which occurs in one of the four possibilities.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Parent genotypes","kind":"text","content":"Both parents are I^A i, carrying a dominant A allele and a recessive i allele.","note":"Each shows the A phenotype."},{"title":"Gamete types","kind":"text","content":"Each parent makes I^A and i gametes in equal numbers.","note":"Segregation separates the alleles."},{"title":"Grid combination","kind":"math","content":"The four zygote types are I^A I^A, I^A i, I^A i and i i.","note":"Group A appears three times; group O once."},{"title":"Probability","kind":"text","content":"Only i i gives group O, so the chance per child is 1/4.","note":"Each pregnancy is independent."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','systems',2013,34, $q$The organ whose secretion is the most strongly acid digestive juice in the human alimentary canal is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mouth"},{"label":"B","text":"stomach"},{"label":"C","text":"small intestine"},{"label":"D","text":"large intestine"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Gastric juice secreted by the stomach lining contains hydrochloric acid at about pH 2. The acid activates pepsinogen, kills microbes and provides the acid medium pepsin needs.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2013,35, $q$The single cell formed immediately after a sperm fertilizes an ovum is called the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"zygote"},{"label":"B","text":"morula"},{"label":"C","text":"ovum"},{"label":"D","text":"polar body"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Fusion of the two haploid gamete nuclei restores the diploid number in the zygote. The zygote then divides repeatedly to become first a ball of cells and later an embryo.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2013,36, $q$Which of these natural resources is renewable?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"crude oil"},{"label":"B","text":"natural gas"},{"label":"C","text":"coal"},{"label":"D","text":"wildlife"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Wildlife and forest resources can reproduce and rebuild their numbers if properly managed. Fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas form over millions of years and cannot be replaced within a human lifetime.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2013,37, $q$The final products of protein digestion in the human alimentary canal are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glucose molecules"},{"label":"B","text":"fatty acids and glycerol"},{"label":"C","text":"amino acids"},{"label":"D","text":"maltose"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Proteins pass from peptides to even smaller fragments and, under peptidases, end as amino acids. These are the small units absorbed through the villi into the blood.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cellular-processes',2014,10, $q$Which set of conditions must all be present before a seed can germinate?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"light, wind and fertiliser"},{"label":"B","text":"dryness, warmth and shade"},{"label":"C","text":"air, moisture and warmth"},{"label":"D","text":"oil, salt and low temperature"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Known experiments with viable seeds show germination fails if any of air, water or suitable warmth is withheld. Light is not required by most seeds, neither is soil nor fertiliser.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Moisture","kind":"text","content":"Water softens the seed coat and activates the enzymes that digest stored food.","note":"Without water the seed stays dormant."},{"title":"Air","kind":"text","content":"Oxygen in the air is needed for the vigorous respiration that powers embryo growth.","note":"Seeds in boiled (oxygen-free) water fail to sprout."},{"title":"Warmth","kind":"text","content":"A moderate temperature keeps the enzymes working efficiently.","note":"Far below or above the optimum, germination stops."},{"title":"Conclusion","kind":"text","content":"All three conditions act together, which is why a seed may be viable yet dormant until conditions coincide.","note":"Most seeds do not need light."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2014,11, $q$A man of blood group A and genotype I^A i marries a woman of blood group B and genotype I^B i. Which blood groups may their children show?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"A and B only"},{"label":"B","text":"A, B, AB and O"},{"label":"C","text":"AB only"},{"label":"D","text":"O only"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each parent passes either a major allele (I^A or I^B) or the recessive i. All four genotype types can therefore appear, giving children of groups A, B, AB and O in a 1:1:1:1 phenotypic ratio.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Gametes","kind":"text","content":"The I^A i father makes I^A and i sperm; the I^B i mother makes I^B and i eggs.","note":"Each parent contributes two alternatives."},{"title":"Combinations","kind":"math","content":"The grid yields I^A I^B, I^A i, I^B i and i i.","note":"Each combination has probability 1/4."},{"title":"Phenotypes","kind":"text","content":"I^A I^B shows as AB, I^A i as A, I^B i as B, and i i as O.","note":"Four distinct blood groups result."},{"title":"Ratio","kind":"text","content":"Thus children can belong to any of the four ABO groups, each with equal likelihood.","note":"This is the classic backcross pattern in ABO inheritance."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','ecology',2014,12, $q$Nitrifying bacteria in the soil carry out the conversion of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ammonia into nitrites and then into nitrates"},{"label":"B","text":"nitrates into nitrogen gas"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen gas into ammonia"},{"label":"D","text":"dead plants into ammonia only"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Nitrosomonas oxidizes ammonia to nitrite and Nitrobacter oxidizes nitrite to nitrate. The nitrates produced are the nitrogen form most available to higher plants.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2014,13, $q$The testes in man are housed in the scrotal sac outside the body cavity because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"they need sunlight to work"},{"label":"B","text":"the abdominal cavity is too small"},{"label":"C","text":"hormones are produced only outside the body"},{"label":"D","text":"sperm formation requires a temperature a little below that of the body"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Spermatogenesis goes on best a few degrees below the body temperature. The positioning of the testes in the scrotum provides this cool environment, while abdominal heat halts sperm production.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2014,14, $q$Blood returning to the heart from the head and the arms drains through the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"inferior vena cava"},{"label":"B","text":"hepatic vein"},{"label":"C","text":"superior vena cava"},{"label":"D","text":"renal vein"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The superior vena cava collects blood from the head, neck and upper limbs and empties it into the right atrium. Blood from the legs below the diaphragm flows through the inferior vena cava instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2014,15, $q$An enzyme acts fastest near pH 7 but stops working altogether at pH 2. This observation shows that enzyme activity is sensitive to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"the degree of acidity or alkalinity (pH)"},{"label":"C","text":"light intensity"},{"label":"D","text":"pressure"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Every enzyme has its own pH optimum at which its active site holds its working shape. Away from that pH, charged groups in the protein are disturbed and the enzyme loses, sometimes irreversibly, its activity.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2014,16, $q$The living matter of a cell, made up of the cytoplasm together with its nucleus, is collectively known as the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"protoplasm"},{"label":"B","text":"cell sap"},{"label":"C","text":"cell wall"},{"label":"D","text":"intercellular spaces"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Protoplasm is the general term for the jelly-like living substance of the cell, including nucleus and cytoplasm. Cell sap and cell wall are non-living inclusions of plant cells.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2014,17, $q$The hormone chiefly responsible for the strong contraction of the uterine muscles during labour is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"progesterone"},{"label":"B","text":"oestrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"luteinizing hormone"},{"label":"D","text":"oxytocin"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The pituitary releases pulses of oxytocin when labour begins, and the uterus contracts rhythmically in response. The same hormone later helps eject milk from the mammary glands.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2014,18, $q$The branch of study that compares the structural plans of the bodies of different species to trace their relationships is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"palaeontology"},{"label":"B","text":"cytology"},{"label":"C","text":"comparative anatomy"},{"label":"D","text":"taxonomy"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Matching organ arrangements, like the pentadactyl limb across vertebrates, argue for shared ancestry. This comparative anatomy is a classical line of evidence for evolution alongside fossils and embryology.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2014,19, $q$The digested end-products of fats entering the bloodstream are absorbed in the small intestine chiefly into the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"red blood cells"},{"label":"B","text":"lacteals of the villi"},{"label":"C","text":"lumen of the colon"},{"label":"D","text":"hepatic artery"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fatty acids and glycerol recombine into tiny fat droplets inside the villus cells and pass into the lacteals, which are lymph vessels. Glucose and amino acids, by contrast, go straight into the blood capillaries.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2014,20, $q$A tall pea plant crossed with another tall pea plant produced some dwarf offspring. The genotypes of the parents must be$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"both heterozygous Tt"},{"label":"B","text":"both homozygous TT"},{"label":"C","text":"TT × tt"},{"label":"D","text":"both tt"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A dwarf offspring is tt and must receive a t gene from each parent. Since both parents are themselves tall, each must carry T and t, that is both are heterozygous Tt.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Interpreting the offspring","kind":"text","content":"A dwarf child is tt, so each parent must have supplied a t allele.","note":"tt parents would themselves be dwarf."},{"title":"Tall phenotype","kind":"text","content":"Because the parents look tall, each must carry at least one T allele.","note":"TT and Tt both look the same."},{"title":"Genotype fixing","kind":"text","content":"Together the two facts make each parent Tt.","note":"Tt × Tt yields 3 tall : 1 dwarf."},{"title":"Checking the alternative","kind":"text","content":"TT × TT or TT × tt can never produce tt offspring.","note":"Hence the observed dwarfs rule those matings out."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','reproduction',2014,21, $q$In mammals, the gland that produces and secretes milk is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"placenta"},{"label":"B","text":"ovary"},{"label":"C","text":"liver"},{"label":"D","text":"mammary gland"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The mammary glands contain secretory lobules that make milk under the influence of prolactin after birth. Muscular contraction of the gland then ejects the milk for the young.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2014,22, $q$In cleaning up an oil spill, bacteria that attack and degrade petroleum are deliberately spread over the area. This clean-up method is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"incineration"},{"label":"B","text":"dredging"},{"label":"C","text":"bioremediation"},{"label":"D","text":"distillation"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Bioremediation employs living organisms, usually microorganisms, to break harmful pollutants into harmless products. Hydrocarbon-oxidizing bacteria digest the oil, converting it to water and carbon dioxide over time.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2014,23, $q$The gas produced during respiration that turns lime water milky is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon dioxide"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Respiring organisms release carbon dioxide, which reacts with calcium hydroxide to form white calcium carbonate. Limewater therefore serves as a test for this gas.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2014,24, $q$The hypothalamus monitors the water content of the blood through special cells sensitive to the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"osmotic pressure of the blood"},{"label":"B","text":"loudness of sound"},{"label":"C","text":"strength of bone tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"colour of light"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$When the blood grows too concentrated, the osmoreceptors of the hypothalamus signal the pituitary, which releases ADH. The kidney tubules then reabsorb more water, saving it back into the blood.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2014,25, $q$The menstrual cycle is governed mainly by hormones from the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"liver"},{"label":"C","text":"adrenal cortex"},{"label":"D","text":"pituitary gland and the ovaries"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The pituitary supplies FSH and LH, which ripen the follicle and trigger ovulation. Together with oestrogen and progesterone from the developing ovary, these hormones run the monthly cycle.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2014,26, $q$The components of blood chiefly responsible for sealing injured vessels and starting clotting are the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"leucocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"erythrocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"platelets"},{"label":"D","text":"plasma"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Platelets (thrombocytes) aggregate where a vessel is torn and help thrombokinase convert prothrombin into thrombin. Fibrin then forms a mesh that nails the clot in place.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2014,27, $q$Genes such as those for colour blindness and haemophilia that are associated with one sex more than the other are said to be sex-linked; they are located on the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"autosomes"},{"label":"B","text":"sex chromosomes, especially the X chromosome"},{"label":"C","text":"cytoplasm of the egg"},{"label":"D","text":"mitochondria"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The X chromosome is large and carries many genes, while the tiny Y barely does. Recessive alleles on the X therefore show up in sons whenever the mother passes them.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2014,28, $q$According to Darwin, the mechanism that brings about evolutionary change is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"natural selection acting on heritable variation"},{"label":"B","text":"use and disuse of organs"},{"label":"C","text":"sudden mutation alone"},{"label":"D","text":"special creation of adapted forms"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Darwin proposed that individuals with favourable variations survive competition and reproduce more successfully. Their features then spread through the population over the generations.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2014,29, $q$In the mammalian nephron, glucose filtered out of the blood is reabsorbed back into the blood mostly at the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glomerulus"},{"label":"B","text":"loop of Henle"},{"label":"C","text":"collecting duct"},{"label":"D","text":"proximal convoluted tubule"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The filtrate reaching the proximal tubule carries glucose, amino acids and salts, all useful. Active transport here recovers them completely, so healthy urine contains no glucose.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2014,30, $q$The oxygen set free during photosynthesis comes from the splitting (photolysis) of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon dioxide"},{"label":"B","text":"glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"water"},{"label":"D","text":"starch"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In the light reaction, light energy splits water into oxygen, hydrogen ions and electrons. Experimental studies using labelled water confirm that the liberated oxygen is from water, not CO₂.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2014,31, $q$The ABO blood group system is said to be controlled by multiple alleles because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the alleles blend in each person"},{"label":"B","text":"the gene exists in three allelic forms that give four blood phenotypes"},{"label":"C","text":"only recessive alleles are involved"},{"label":"D","text":"blood group half comes from each of four genes"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A single gene governs the system in three allelic variants: I^A, I^B and i. Any person carries only two of them, and the combinations yield the four phenotypes A, B, AB and O.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2014,32, $q$In the human male reproductive system, the tube that carries mature sperm from the epididymis to where it joins the urethra is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"vas deferens"},{"label":"B","text":"ureter"},{"label":"C","text":"fallopian tube"},{"label":"D","text":"seminal vesicle"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The vas deferens runs from the epididymis up and over the bladder to the urethra, carrying sperm for ejaculation. The ureter, by contrast, carries urine from the kidney to the bladder.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2014,33, $q$The stable final community reached at the end of a long series of vegetation changes is called a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pioneer community"},{"label":"B","text":"seral stage"},{"label":"C","text":"transient community"},{"label":"D","text":"climax community"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Succession begins with simple pioneer organisms and moves through changing seral stages. It ends in a climax community that can maintain itself in the prevailing climate for a long time.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2014,34, $q$Deoxygenated blood arriving in the heart from the body tissues enters first the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"left atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"pulmonary vein"},{"label":"C","text":"right atrium"},{"label":"D","text":"left ventricle"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Venous blood collected by the vena cavae flows into the right atrium, the first chamber on its return. From there it passes to the right ventricle for the pulmonary circuit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2014,35, $q$A glowing splint at the mouth of a gas jar begins to relight brightly. The gas in the jar must be$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nitrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen, which supports burning"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon dioxide"},{"label":"D","text":"methane"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Relighting a splint is the classic positive test for oxygen, since oxygen supports combustion. Carbon dioxide and nitrogen would extinguish it instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2014,36, $q$Which structure helps an animal cell divide but is absent from most higher plant cells?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the centrosome with its centrioles"},{"label":"B","text":"chloroplasts"},{"label":"C","text":"cellulose cell walls"},{"label":"D","text":"central vacuoles"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$During cell division the centrosome, with its paired centrioles, organises the spindle in animal cells. Higher plants lack centrioles and still form spindles without them.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2014,37, $q$The great variety of different species of plants and animals found within a given area is best described as its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ecosystem"},{"label":"B","text":"niche"},{"label":"C","text":"habitat"},{"label":"D","text":"biodiversity"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Biodiversity refers to the total variety of living things, from genes and species to ecosystems in an area. Conservation programmes target measures that preserve this variety.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2014,38, $q$Which sequence correctly follows oxygenated blood as it leaves the heart for the tissues of the body?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"vena cava → head and arms → aorta"},{"label":"B","text":"pulmonary vein → lungs → aorta"},{"label":"C","text":"aorta → systemic circulation → vena cava"},{"label":"D","text":"aorta → lungs → left atrium"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A blood cell leaving the left ventricle enters the aorta and travels through the body's capillaries, exchanging gases and nutrients. It then returns by the vena cava, completing the systemic circuit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','evolution',2015,10, $q$The evolutionary theory claiming that the long neck of the giraffe resulted from its continuous stretching to reach high leaves, and that this stretched condition was inherited by its offspring, is associated with$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Charles Darwin"},{"label":"B","text":"Gregor Mendel"},{"label":"C","text":"the theory of continental drift"},{"label":"D","text":"inheritance of acquired characters"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Lamarck's doctrine of use and disuse held that organs developed through effort are passed on to offspring. Modern genetics shows that practice cannot alter the genes carried by germ cells.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2015,11, $q$The hormone that stimulates the mammary glands to produce milk after childbirth is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxytocin"},{"label":"B","text":"oestrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"prolactin"},{"label":"D","text":"progesterone"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Prolactin from the anterior pituitary drives the secretory cells of the mammary glands to manufacture milk. Oxytocin instead ejects already-produced milk during suckling.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2015,12, $q$Ribosomes occur either free in the cytoplasmic sap or attached to the surface of the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mitochondria"},{"label":"B","text":"endoplasmic reticulum"},{"label":"C","text":"cell membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"nuclear membrane"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Many ribosomes stud the rough endoplasmic reticulum, which then delivers the newly made proteins onward. Free ribosomes supply proteins for use inside the cell itself.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2015,13, $q$An F1 dihybrid pea plant with round yellow seeds (RrYy) is crossed with a double recessive plant having wrinkled green seeds (rryy). The expected phenotypic ratio is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 round-yellow : 1 round-green : 1 wrinkled-yellow : 1 wrinkled-green"},{"label":"B","text":"9 round-yellow : 3 round-green : 3 wrinkled-yellow : 1 wrinkled-green"},{"label":"C","text":"3 round-yellow : 1 wrinkled-green"},{"label":"D","text":"all round-yellow"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The heterozygote produces four gamete types in equal numbers (RY, Ry, rY, ry), while the recessive parent gives only ry gametes. Each combination therefore appears with equal frequency, a 1:1:1:1 test-cross ratio.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Dihybrid gametes","kind":"text","content":"Independent assortment lets RrYy generate four gamete types: RY, Ry, rY and ry.","note":"Each type appears with probability 1/4."},{"title":"Tester parent","kind":"text","content":"The rryy plant produces only ry gametes, revealing whichever allele comes with them.","note":"This is why a double recessive doubles as a test parent."},{"title":"Offspring grid","kind":"math","content":"The four equally likely offspring genotypes are RrYy, Rryy, rrYy and rryy.","note":"One offspring from each gamete type."},{"title":"Phenotypic ratio","kind":"text","content":"Hence round-yellow : round-green : wrinkled-yellow : wrinkled-green = 1:1:1:1.","note":"The 1:1:1:1 test ratio confirms heterozygosity in the other parent."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','cellular-processes',2015,14, $q$Loss of water as vapour from the surfaces of plant leaves, chiefly through stomata, is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"translocation"},{"label":"B","text":"osmosis"},{"label":"C","text":"respiration"},{"label":"D","text":"transpiration"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Transpiration is the evaporation of water from the mesophyll cells to the air through the stomata. It generates the pull that lifts water up the xylem.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2015,15, $q$The physical place where an organism actually lives is termed its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"niche"},{"label":"B","text":"community"},{"label":"C","text":"habitat"},{"label":"D","text":"biome"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The habitat is the address of the organism in nature, such as a pond or forest canopy. Its niche, unlike this, describes the occupation and links it plays in the community.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2015,16, $q$Most aquatic animals get rid of their nitrogenous waste mainly in the form of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"uric acid"},{"label":"B","text":"ammonia"},{"label":"C","text":"urea"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen gas"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fish and other water-dwelling animals release ammonia, the toxic but cheap nitrogen end-product, straight into the surrounding water. Land animals, lacking this luxury, convert it to urea or uric acid.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2015,17, $q$After ovulation has taken place, the empty Graafian follicle develops into the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"corpus luteum"},{"label":"B","text":"a new follicle"},{"label":"C","text":"endometrium"},{"label":"D","text":"fallopian funnel"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The ruptured follicle fills with yellow cells to form the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone. If pregnancy does not occur, this structure degenerates and menstruation follows.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2015,18, $q$The 'lub-dub' sounds heard when the heart is examined with a stethoscope are produced by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"contraction of the cardiac muscles alone"},{"label":"B","text":"rush of blood along the arteries"},{"label":"C","text":"beating of the pericardial membrane against the lungs"},{"label":"D","text":"closing of the heart valves"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The valve-closings generate two distinct sounds in each cardiac cycle: the atrioventricular valves snapping shut ('lub') and the semilunar valves ('dub'). The muscular wall itself gives off no sound.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2015,19, $q$Fossils are most commonly found in$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"igneous rocks formed from lava"},{"label":"B","text":"volcanic ash only"},{"label":"C","text":"sedimentary rocks laid down as layers"},{"label":"D","text":"dry wind-blown dust"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Bodies buried in soft sediments are pressed gently as layer after layer accumulates, and so must be preserved. Lava and ash would burn or destroy the remains instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2015,20, $q$A man heterozygous for the free earlobe allele (Ff) marries a woman with attached earlobes (ff). The fraction of their children expected to have free earlobes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"all of the children"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"3/4"},{"label":"D","text":"1/4"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The father produces F and f sperm in equal numbers; the mother contributes only f. The offspring will be Ff (free) or ff (attached) in equal proportions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2015,21, $q$Decomposers are essential to every ecosystem chiefly because they$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"recycle nutrients locked in dead matter back to the soil"},{"label":"B","text":"manufacture their own food by photosynthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"attack and kill living plants"},{"label":"D","text":"trap sunlight energy for the community"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Without decomposers, the remains of organisms would pile up and their nutrients stay locked away. By breaking down dead material, they return minerals to plants and keep the matter circulating.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2015,22, $q$In a plant grown under normal conditions, adding more carbon dioxide to the air$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"stops photosynthesis at once"},{"label":"B","text":"makes light totally unnecessary"},{"label":"C","text":"causes respiration to exceed photosynthesis"},{"label":"D","text":"raises the rate of photosynthesis until another factor, such as light, becomes limiting"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Blackman's law of limiting factors holds that the scarcest essential factor sets the rate of photosynthesis. When carbon dioxide is raised, photosynthesis increases only until light or other factors becomes the bottleneck.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2015,23, $q$The chamber of the mammalian heart that pumps blood out through the aorta is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"right atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"right ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"left ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"left atrium"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The left ventricle, with the thickest myocardium, drives blood through the aorta round the entire body. The right ventricle supplies only its light-duty, short pulmonary circuit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2015,24, $q$The central nervous system of man consists of the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"brain and the cranial nerves"},{"label":"B","text":"brain and spinal cord"},{"label":"C","text":"spinal cord and the sense organs"},{"label":"D","text":"spinal cord and all the peripheral nerves"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The central nervous system is made up of the brain and spinal cord, staffed by interneurones and motor neurones. The numerous peripheral nerves connecting the central nerves to body parts form the peripheral system instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2015,25, $q$A woman who is a carrier of haemophilia (X^H X^h) marries a man with normal clotting (X^H Y). What is the probability that their first child, if a daughter, will be a carrier?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1/2"},{"label":"B","text":"1/4"},{"label":"C","text":"all daughters certainly"},{"label":"D","text":"none of the daughters"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Daughters always receive their father's X^H. From such a mother they receive X^H or X^h in equal proportions, so exactly half of the daughters will be carriers.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Father's contribution","kind":"text","content":"The normal father passes his X^H to every daughter.","note":"Daughters are therefore protected from haemophilia itself."},{"title":"Mother's gametes","kind":"text","content":"The carrier mother produces X^H and X^h eggs in equal numbers.","note":"She is healthy because one X is normal."},{"title":"Daughter genotypes","kind":"math","content":"Daughters may be X^H X^H (normal) or X^H X^h (carrier), each with probability 1/2.","note":"The paternal X is fixed as X^H."},{"title":"Probability","kind":"text","content":"So among daughters, half are carriers and half are fully normal.","note":"By contrast, half the sons would be haemophilic."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','ecology',2015,26, $q$In the food chain maize → locust → lizard → snake, which organism is a secondary consumer?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"maize"},{"label":"B","text":"locust"},{"label":"C","text":"snake"},{"label":"D","text":"lizard"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The maize plant is the producer, and the locust that feeds on it a primary consumer. The lizard that eats the locust is therefore the secondary (carnivorous) consumer.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Producers first","kind":"text","content":"Maize manufactures its own food by photosynthesis and anchors the chain at trophic level 1.","note":"All matter and energy in the chain begins here."},{"title":"Primary consumers","kind":"text","content":"The locust feeds directly on the maize as a herbivore.","note":"This makes it the primary consumer at level 2."},{"title":"Secondary consumers","kind":"text","content":"The lizard gains energy by preying upon the locust.","note":"Carnivores occupy level 3."},{"title":"Tertiary consumers","kind":"text","content":"The snake, which eats the lizard, occupies the next level as a tertiary consumer.","note":"Energy dwindles sharply through each successive step."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','reproduction',2015,27, $q$The transfer of pollen grains from the anther of a flower to its own or another flower's stigma is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fertilization"},{"label":"B","text":"germination"},{"label":"C","text":"pollination"},{"label":"D","text":"transpiration"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Pollination merely delivers the male gamete-bearing grains to a receptive stigma; actual fertilization follows only after a pollen tube grows. Wind, water, insects, birds and bats all serve as pollination agents.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2015,28, $q$Strains of the malarial parasite resistant to a once-effective drug have spread widely. The best evolutionary explanation is that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the drug created resistant genes immediately inside the parasites"},{"label":"B","text":"parasites already carrying resistant genes survived the treatment and multiplied"},{"label":"C","text":"all parasites mutated at the same time"},{"label":"D","text":"the mosquitoes themselves became resistant"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Resistant mutants already existed in the parasite population before the drug arrived. The drug killed the susceptible ones, leaving resistant forms to reproduce and dominate by natural selection.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2015,29, $q$Bile, produced in the liver and stored in the gall bladder, is emptied for use in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"duodenum"},{"label":"B","text":"stomach"},{"label":"C","text":"ileum only"},{"label":"D","text":"colon"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The bile duct empties into the first part of the small intestine, the duodenum, where its salts emulsify incoming fat. Waves of gall bladder contraction deliver bile whenever fatty food arrives.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2015,30, $q$Which organ stores urine temporarily until it is voided?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"kidney"},{"label":"B","text":"ureter"},{"label":"C","text":"urethra"},{"label":"D","text":"urinary bladder"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The urinary bladder receives urine drop by drop through the ureters and holds it until about 400 ml has gathered. Stretching of its wall then prompts the urge to urinate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2015,31, $q$The cell theory asserts that organisms are made of cells and, in addition, that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cells may spring from lifeless matter by spontaneous generation"},{"label":"B","text":"cells exist only in multicellular organisms"},{"label":"C","text":"all cells come from the division of pre-existing cells"},{"label":"D","text":"every cell in a body is exactly the same"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Schwann, Schleiden and later Virchow established that cells are the units of life and arise only from other cells by division. Spontaneous generation was thus abandoned.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2015,32, $q$Certain alleles located on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together rather than separately. This phenomenon is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"crossing over"},{"label":"B","text":"linkage"},{"label":"C","text":"independent assortment"},{"label":"D","text":"mutation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Genes occupying neighbouring positions on a chromosome travel together during cell division, so their characters appear together in offspring. Crossing over can still separate them in meiosis.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2015,33, $q$The fruits of plants such as cocklebur, covered with hooked spines, are adapted for dispersal by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"animals that brush against the plant"},{"label":"B","text":"wind"},{"label":"C","text":"water"},{"label":"D","text":"explosive release"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Hooked spines grip the fur of passing animals or even cloth, and the fruit rides along until it is scraped off elsewhere. Such fruits are hence dispersed by animals (zoocarpy).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2015,34, $q$Which of the following practices best shows conservation of natural resources?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"burning the same forest continuously for farmland"},{"label":"B","text":"fishing with fine nets that catch young fish as well"},{"label":"C","text":"mining without restoring the mined land"},{"label":"D","text":"replanting harvested forest lands and recycling used paper"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Conservation uses resources wisely so that future generations still enjoy them. Replanting forests and recycling paper keep the resource base renewing itself instead of running down.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2015,35, $q$In a germinating seed, the part that emerges first and later develops into the primary root system is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"plumule"},{"label":"B","text":"cotyledon"},{"label":"C","text":"radicle"},{"label":"D","text":"epicotyl"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The radicle grows out of the seed first, downwards to form the main root. The plumule, guard from above, later gives rise to the shoot or aerial system.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2015,36, $q$The tiny gap between the end of one neurone and the beginning of the next, across which impulses pass by chemical signals, is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dendrite"},{"label":"B","text":"synapse"},{"label":"C","text":"axon"},{"label":"D","text":"nephron"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Impulses arriving at a synapse trigger the release of transmitter chemicals that diffuse across the gap. Synapses ensure signals travel in one direction only in the nerve network.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2015,37, $q$Which blood vessels contain valves that prevent the backflow of blood?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"veins"},{"label":"B","text":"arteries"},{"label":"C","text":"capillaries"},{"label":"D","text":"arterioles"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Blood in the veins flows slowly and indirectly against gravity, so flap valves hold it on its way back to the heart. Arteries need none because ventricular pressure keeps the flow one-way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2015,38, $q$The form of chemical energy in which the energy released from the breakdown of glucose is directly stored in cells is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glucose"},{"label":"B","text":"ADP"},{"label":"C","text":"DNA"},{"label":"D","text":"ATP"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Respiration packs the energy of food into ATP, which cells spend on work ranging from muscle contraction to active transport. ATP is therefore the cell's universal energy currency.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cellular-processes',2016,10, $q$The products of photosynthesis that make the process fundamental to life on earth are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glucose (food) and oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon dioxide and water vapour"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen and ammonia"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanol and lactic acid"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Green plants manufacture glucose, the food that feeds almost every food chain, and release oxygen in the process. Both products are basic to the survival of other organisms.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2016,11, $q$The branch of science that studies extinct life forms through their preserved remains is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ecology"},{"label":"B","text":"embryology"},{"label":"C","text":"taxonomy"},{"label":"D","text":"palaeontology"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Palaeontologists describe fossils and use their positions in rock layers to read the history of life. That record underlines the claim that simpler organisms lived before more complex ones.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2016,12, $q$Which of the following organisms is a prokaryote?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"yeast"},{"label":"B","text":"Amoeba"},{"label":"C","text":"a bacterium"},{"label":"D","text":"an onion root cell"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Bacteria keep their DNA naked in the cytoplasm without a nuclear membrane, a prokaryotic trait. Yeast, Amoeba and onion cells all have true nuclei and are eukaryotes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2016,13, $q$The coronary arteries of the heart supply blood chiefly to the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lungs"},{"label":"B","text":"heart muscle itself"},{"label":"C","text":"small intestine"},{"label":"D","text":"brain"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The cardiac muscle works ceaselessly and cannot live on the blood passing through its own chambers. Coronary arteries branching off the aorta feed it, and their blockage causes a heart attack.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2016,14, $q$In birds and reptiles, the developing embryo obtains most of its nourishment while inside the egg from the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"yolk"},{"label":"B","text":"egg shell"},{"label":"C","text":"air space"},{"label":"D","text":"albumen only"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The yolk is a concentrated store of fat and protein laid down by the mother. The albumen chiefly cushions the embryo and supplies water, while the shell shelters it and permits breathing.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2016,15, $q$A national park is established mainly to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mine minerals under supervision"},{"label":"B","text":"attract commercial resorts"},{"label":"C","text":"permit controlled hunting of wildlife"},{"label":"D","text":"protect natural resources and wildlife in their natural setting"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Parks preserve complete ecosystems, pollinators and species free from farming, logging or hunting. Such reserves conserve plants, animals and land resources for posterity.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2016,16, $q$Root hair cells absorb mineral ions from the soil even when their concentration inside the cell is already higher than outside. This uptake depends on$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"simple diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"osmosis"},{"label":"C","text":"active transport using energy from respiration"},{"label":"D","text":"surface tension"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Movement of a substance against its own concentration gradient cannot happen by osmosis or diffusion. The cell spends respiration energy (ATP) to pump the ions in actively.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2016,17, $q$Urea is formed in the body of mammals mainly in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"kidney"},{"label":"B","text":"liver"},{"label":"C","text":"skin"},{"label":"D","text":"bladder"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The liver deaminates surplus amino acids and combines the nitrogen with CO₂ to make urea. The kidney, supplied with urea in the blood, excretes it in urine but does not manufacture it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2016,18, $q$Which of the following is a biotic component of an ecosystem?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bacteria in the soil"},{"label":"B","text":"temperature"},{"label":"C","text":"rainfall"},{"label":"D","text":"soil pH"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Biotic factors are the living members of an ecosystem, from microbes to plants and animals. Temperature, rainfall and soil pH are non-living (abiotic) conditions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2016,19, $q$The cell wall that surrounds plant cells is built essentially of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"starch"},{"label":"B","text":"protein"},{"label":"C","text":"fat"},{"label":"D","text":"cellulose"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Cellulose fibres woven in cross-hatched fashion give the wall its strength, and permeability lets water and salts pass freely. Starch and fats are storage materials inside cells instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2016,20, $q$The genotype of a person of blood group O is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"I^A I^B"},{"label":"B","text":"I^A i"},{"label":"C","text":"i i"},{"label":"D","text":"I^B i"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Only the double recessive genotype i i produces neither the A nor the B antigen, hence group O. Every genotype carrying I^A or I^B expresses one or both of those antigens.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2016,21, $q$In a maize grain (a monocot seed), the greater part of the stored food lies in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"plumule"},{"label":"B","text":"endosperm"},{"label":"C","text":"testa"},{"label":"D","text":"epicotyl"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Cereal grains reserve most of their food in the endosperm, with the scutellum absorbing it during germination. Bean and other dicot seeds instead pack their food into thick cotyledons.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2016,22, $q$Water is drawn up the xylem of tall trees against gravity chiefly by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"transpiration pull aided by the cohesion and adhesion of water molecules"},{"label":"B","text":"pull of gravity on heavier soil water"},{"label":"C","text":"active pumping of water by the leaf cells"},{"label":"D","text":"diffusion of dissolved air through the stem"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Water evaporating from the leaf stomata tugs on the water column below, and the strong cohesion between molecules holds that column unbroken. Adhesion to the xylem walls stops it collapsing, so the water rises as one piece.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Evaporation anchor","kind":"text","content":"Transpiration at the leaves lowers the pressure in the water columns running up the xylem.","note":"This tension is the engine of ascent."},{"title":"Cohesion","kind":"text","content":"Individual water molecules attract one another (cohesion) and remain connected in an unbroken column.","note":"Hydrogen bonds join the molecules like beads on a string."},{"title":"Adhesion","kind":"text","content":"The column also clings to the hydrophilic xylem walls by adhesion.","note":"Wall wetting prevents the column from breaking under tension."},{"title":"Rise of water","kind":"text","content":"With a continuous thread of water, evaporation at the leaf draws new water upward from root to shoot.","note":"Root pressure makes at most a small contribution."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','evolution',2016,23, $q$Organic evolution may best be described as the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"theory that has since been abandoned by scientists"},{"label":"B","text":"man-made creation of new species in laboratories"},{"label":"C","text":"ability of individual organisms to change at will within their lifetime"},{"label":"D","text":"gradual development of new species from pre-existing ones over long periods"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Evolution is the slow, hereditary change in populations through generations, producing new species from older ones. It works through inherited variation and selection, not through changes within an individual's lifetime.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2016,24, $q$The excretory organs found in the flatworm Planaria are the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nephridia"},{"label":"B","text":"Malpighian tubules"},{"label":"C","text":"flame cells"},{"label":"D","text":"green glands"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In the flatworms, water and wastes are swept through a network of canals by beating flame cells. Nephridia belong to annelids like the earthworm and Malpighian tubules to insects.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2016,25, $q$A rhesus negative woman (dd) is married to a rhesus positive man who is homozygous (DD). Which statement about their children is correct?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"all the children will be rhesus negative"},{"label":"B","text":"all the children will be rhesus positive (Dd)"},{"label":"C","text":"half the children will be rhesus negative"},{"label":"D","text":"none of the children can inherit the rhesus factor"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The homozygous DD father contributes a D allele to every child while the mother contributes only d, so every child is Dd and rhesus positive. Because the mother is rhesus negative, her future pregnancies run the risk of haemolytic disease after sensitization.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Parental genotypes","kind":"text","content":"Rhesus positive (DD) is dominant; the mother is homozygous recessive (dd).","note":"Only the D allele produces the rhesus antigen."},{"title":"Father's gametes","kind":"text","content":"A DD father produces one type of sperm, carrying D.","note":"The mother contributes one type of egg, carrying d."},{"title":"Children","kind":"math","content":"Every child is Dd and therefore rhesus positive.","note":"No child can be rhesus negative here."},{"title":"Clinical note","kind":"text","content":"A sensitization in an earlier pregnancy puts later Dd babies at risk of haemolytic disease.","note":"This arises because a d mother can form anti-D antibodies."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','ecology',2016,26, $q$Many interlocking food chains in a community, each feeding into the next, together form a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"food web"},{"label":"B","text":"pyramid of numbers"},{"label":"C","text":"habitat"},{"label":"D","text":"biome"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Because most animals eat more than one kind of food, food chains interlock in a web of relationships. The web paints a fuller picture of community feeding than any single chain can.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2016,27, $q$The part of the sperm that produces the enzymes which help it penetrate the egg during fertilization is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"tail"},{"label":"B","text":"middle piece"},{"label":"C","text":"neck"},{"label":"D","text":"acrosome at the front of the head"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The acrosome sits at the tip of the sperm head and releases enzymes that dissolve the egg's membranes. The middle piece carries mitochondria and the tail supplies the propulsion.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2016,28, $q$The glomerular filtrate in the kidney lacks large blood proteins although it carries glucose, salts and urea. The reason is that the proteins$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"are digested by enzymes in the blood"},{"label":"B","text":"are stored away in the liver before reaching the kidney"},{"label":"C","text":"are too large to pass through the pores of the glomerulus"},{"label":"D","text":"are conveyed to the bladder directly"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Filtration at the glomerulus separates particles by size. Plasma proteins such as albumin remain behind in the blood, so urine apart from disease never contains them.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2016,29, $q$The class of mammalian teeth chiefly specialised for tearing flesh is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"incisors"},{"label":"B","text":"canines"},{"label":"C","text":"molars"},{"label":"D","text":"premolars"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Canines are long, pointed and grip prey, as seen in the flesh-eating group. Incisors cut, and premolars and molars grind and crush the food.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','ecology',2016,30, $q$The concentration of non-degradable poisonous substances, such as mercury, as they move up successive trophic levels is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"biological magnification (bioaccumulation)"},{"label":"B","text":"eutrophication"},{"label":"C","text":"succession"},{"label":"D","text":"biodegradation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Such poisons, already present in the water in tiny amounts, remain in the tissues and are passed to the next consumer each time feeding occurs. Top carnivores therefore accumulate the most concentrated doses.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cell-structure',2016,31, $q$Which structure is present in BOTH prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nucleus bounded by a membrane"},{"label":"B","text":"mitochondria"},{"label":"C","text":"chloroplasts"},{"label":"D","text":"ribosomes"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$All cells, even bacteria, build proteins with ribosomes. The other organelles listed are membrane-bound features of eukaryotic cells and are missing in bacteria.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','genetics',2016,32, $q$A heritable change in the structure of a chromosome or of a gene itself is termed a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"natural selection"},{"label":"B","text":"adaptation"},{"label":"C","text":"mutation"},{"label":"D","text":"hybridization"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Mutations arise spontaneously in copying errors in the DNA, or are induced by agents like radiation. Only such inherited changes supply new alleles among which natural selection can pick.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','systems',2016,33, $q$Lymph differs from blood chiefly because it does not contain$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"plasma"},{"label":"B","text":"red blood cells"},{"label":"C","text":"white blood cells"},{"label":"D","text":"water"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Lymph is a pale fluid filtered from blood plasma within the tissue spaces, carrying white blood cells and dissolved nutrients. Red corpuscles never pass out of the capillaries, so the lymph does not have them.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','reproduction',2016,34, $q$Gestation (pregnancy) from fertilization to birth in man lasts on average$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"about 9 months (about 40 weeks)"},{"label":"B","text":"about 6 months"},{"label":"C","text":"about 4 months"},{"label":"D","text":"about 12 months"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The human embryo develops for about 38 to 40 weeks inside the uterus. Great variation exists, but term labour typically comes after these 40 weeks, which is roughly 9 months.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','evolution',2016,35, $q$Of the following types of information, the one now most valued as evidence for close evolutionary relationship between two species is their similarity in$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"preferred habitats"},{"label":"B","text":"feeding habits"},{"label":"C","text":"body size"},{"label":"D","text":"DNA and protein sequences"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Modern comparative molecular work compares the order of nucleotides in DNA and of amino acids in proteins. The closer two species are, the more alike their sequences are expected to be, revealing their common ancestry.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2016,36, $q$Before its green shoot appears above the soil, a germinating seedling obtains the energy it needs for growth from$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"photosynthesis by its thin green leaves"},{"label":"B","text":"mineral salts absorbed from the soil"},{"label":"C","text":"its stored food reserves in the endosperm or cotyledons"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen fixed by bacteria in the nodule"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The buried, lightless seedling respires its stored starch or oil, releasing the energy needed for growth. Photosynthesis contributes only after the leaves emerge and turn green in the light.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2016,37, $q$The part of the brain responsible for intelligence, memory and reasoning powers in man is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cerebellum"},{"label":"B","text":"cerebrum (cerebral hemispheres)"},{"label":"C","text":"medulla oblongata"},{"label":"D","text":"spinal cord"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The cerebral hemispheres, best developed in man, house the centres of memory, thinking and voluntary action. The medulla handles breathing and other involuntary duties while the cerebellum smooths movements.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2017,10, $q$Which of the following organelles is directly concerned with the synthesis of proteins?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Ribosome"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitochondrion"},{"label":"C","text":"Lysosome"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Ribosomes, whether free or attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum, are the sites where amino acids are joined into polypeptides. Mitochondria release energy, lysosomes digest worn-out material, and the Golgi body packages secretions.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2017,11, $q$The organelle that contains its own DNA and is also the main site of energy release in the cell is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nucleolus"},{"label":"B","text":"mitochondrion"},{"label":"C","text":"lysosome"},{"label":"D","text":"vacuole"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A mitochondrion carries a small circular DNA molecule of its own and runs aerobic respiration, releasing most of the cell's ATP. Its possession of separate genes supports the idea that it originated from a free-living bacterium.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2017,12, $q$Ribosomes are manufactured within the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nuclear envelope"},{"label":"B","text":"chromatin"},{"label":"C","text":"nucleolus"},{"label":"D","text":"cytoplasm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The nucleolus, the dense rounded body inside the nucleus, assembles ribosomes from rRNA and proteins. These are then exported to the cytoplasm, where they carry out protein synthesis.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2017,13, $q$Chromosomes arrange themselves along the equator of the spindle during which stage of mitosis?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Prophase"},{"label":"B","text":"Anaphase"},{"label":"C","text":"Telophase"},{"label":"D","text":"Metaphase"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In metaphase the fully condensed chromosomes lie single-file at the cell equator, attached to spindle fibres. Anaphase follows, separating the chromatids towards opposite poles.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2017,14, $q$An enzyme-controlled reaction eventually stops completely when the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"substrate is completely used up"},{"label":"B","text":"enzyme concentration increases"},{"label":"C","text":"product concentration decreases"},{"label":"D","text":"temperature is held at the optimum"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Once every substrate molecule has been converted there is nothing left for the enzyme to act on, although the enzyme itself remains intact. Supplying fresh substrate would set the reaction going again.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2017,15, $q$The oxidation of glucose to carbon dioxide and water, with the release of usable energy, occurs during$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"photosynthesis"},{"label":"B","text":"aerobic respiration"},{"label":"C","text":"anaerobic respiration"},{"label":"D","text":"transpiration"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Aerobic respiration takes up oxygen and oxidises glucose completely to carbon dioxide and water, releasing many ATP molecules. Fermentation stops short of this and leaves ethanol or lactic acid as end-products.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2017,16, $q$Movement of oxygen from the alveolar air into the blood flowing past occurs by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"active transport"},{"label":"B","text":"osmosis"},{"label":"C","text":"diffusion"},{"label":"D","text":"phagocytosis"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each gas diffuses down its own concentration gradient, from high to low concentration, until near-equilibrium is reached. No cellular energy is spent, which rules out active transport.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2017,17, $q$Which blood vessel carries deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary vein"},{"label":"B","text":"Aorta"},{"label":"C","text":"Vena cava"},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary artery"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Arteries carry blood away from the heart, and the pulmonary artery is the one artery that normally transports oxygen-poor blood. Its partner, the pulmonary vein, returns oxygen-rich blood from the lungs.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2017,18, $q$Emulsification of fats during digestion in man is brought about by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bile"},{"label":"B","text":"pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"renin"},{"label":"D","text":"mucus"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Bile salts break large fat globules into tiny droplets, enlarging the surface available to lipase. Bile itself contains no digestive enzyme; the actual hydrolysis is done by lipase.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2017,19, $q$Gas exchange in the lungs is highly efficient mainly because the alveoli are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"thick-walled with large lumens"},{"label":"B","text":"thin-walled, moist, extremely numerous and wrapped in capillaries"},{"label":"C","text":"rigid, being reinforced with cartilage rings"},{"label":"D","text":"lined with tough connective tissue"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A thin, moist membrane keeps the diffusion path for gases very short, while countless alveoli provide an enormous surface. A dense capillary network constantly sweeps fresh blood past them.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2017,20, $q$Water, glucose, salts and urea are forced from the glomerulus into the Bowman's capsule during urine formation by the process of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"selective reabsorption"},{"label":"B","text":"tubular secretion"},{"label":"C","text":"ultrafiltration"},{"label":"D","text":"osmoregulation"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$High pressure in the glomerular capillaries drives small molecules through the filtration barrier into the capsule. Blood cells and large plasma proteins are held back in the blood.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2017,21, $q$Reabsorption of water from the glomerular filtrate is controlled by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"thyroxine"},{"label":"B","text":"insulin"},{"label":"C","text":"adrenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"antidiuretic hormone"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$ADH, released by the pituitary whenever the blood becomes too concentrated, makes the kidney collecting ducts permeable to water. Water reabsorption then rises and the urine grows more concentrated.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2017,22, $q$Which sequence correctly traces the path of a nerve impulse during a simple spinal reflex?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Receptor -> sensory neurone -> spinal cord -> motor neurone -> effector"},{"label":"B","text":"Effector -> spinal cord -> receptor -> sensory neurone"},{"label":"C","text":"Receptor -> motor neurone -> spinal cord -> sensory neurone -> effector"},{"label":"D","text":"Effector -> sensory neurone -> spinal cord -> motor neurone -> receptor"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The sensory (afferent) neurone carries the impulse into the spinal cord, from which motor (efferent) neurones stimulate a muscle or gland. The receptor senses the stimulus and the effector carries out the response.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2017,23, $q$Work through a cross between a tall hybrid pea plant (Tt) and a true-breeding dwarf plant (tt). Which phenotypic ratio should appear among the offspring?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3 tall : 1 dwarf"},{"label":"B","text":"1 tall : 1 dwarf"},{"label":"C","text":"all tall"},{"label":"D","text":"2 tall : 1 dwarf"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A heterozygote test-crossed with a homozygous recessive gives half tall and half dwarf offspring. The same 1:1 pattern appears whatever the dominant character being followed.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Sort out the gametes","kind":"math","content":"The Tt parent makes T and t gametes in equal numbers; the tt parent makes t gametes only.","note":"Each gamete carries one allele per gene."},{"title":"Combine the gametes","kind":"math","content":"T with t gives Tt, and t with t gives tt - two outcomes in a 1:1 ratio.","note":"Tt offspring are tall because T masks t."},{"title":"Convert to phenotypes","kind":"text","content":"Half the offspring are tall and half are dwarf, so the phenotype ratio is 1:1.","note":"Tt x tt is the classical test cross."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2017,24, $q$Children of a father with blood group AB and a mother with blood group O could belong to blood group(s)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"O or AB only"},{"label":"B","text":"AB only"},{"label":"C","text":"A or B only"},{"label":"D","text":"O only"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The AB father contributes either the A allele or the B allele, while the O mother can contribute only the o allele. The children are therefore AO (group A) or BO (group B), never AB and never O.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2017,25, $q$In humans, the sex of a developing baby is determined by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the number of ova released"},{"label":"B","text":"the autosomes of the zygote"},{"label":"C","text":"the mother's nutrition during pregnancy"},{"label":"D","text":"the sex chromosome carried by the fertilising sperm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Every human ovum carries an X chromosome, while sperm carry either an X or a Y. An XX zygote develops as a female and an XY zygote as a male.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2017,26, $q$A couple both having the sickle cell trait (AS) plan a family. Work out the chance that any child of theirs will suffer sickle cell anaemia.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 in 4"},{"label":"B","text":"1 in 2"},{"label":"C","text":"3 in 4"},{"label":"D","text":"1 in 1"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Between two AS carriers the offspring genotypes come out as 1 AA : 2 AS : 1 SS. One out of every four conceptions is therefore expected to be SS, and the risk repeats at each pregnancy.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Set up the parents","kind":"text","content":"Both parents are AS, so each produces A gametes and S gametes in equal numbers.","note":"The S allele is recessive to the normal allele A."},{"title":"Build the Punnett square","kind":"math","content":"Combining the gametes gives AA, AS, AS and SS, each with a 1 in 4 chance.","note":"The four outcomes are equally likely."},{"title":"Pick out the sufferers","kind":"text","content":"Only SS children have sickle cell anaemia, so the risk per child is one in four.","note":"Each pregnancy repeats the same 25 per cent risk independently."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','reproduction',2017,27, $q$The male reproductive part of a flower is made up of the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"gynoecium"},{"label":"B","text":"anthers borne on filaments"},{"label":"C","text":"corolla"},{"label":"D","text":"ovary with its style"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each stamen is a filament topped by an anther, in which the pollen grains that carry the male gametes are produced. The gynoecium, made of carpels, is the female counterpart.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2017,28, $q$The transfer of pollen grains from the anther of one plant to the stigma of another plant of the same species is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"self-pollination"},{"label":"B","text":"asexual reproduction"},{"label":"C","text":"cross-pollination"},{"label":"D","text":"germination"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Cross-pollination joins gametes from two different plants, usually with wind, water or an animal as the carrying agent. Pollen landing on the stigma of its own flower would be self-pollination.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2017,29, $q$In mammals, the oxygen and digested nutrients reaching the developing foetus pass through the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"amnion"},{"label":"B","text":"uterine muscle"},{"label":"C","text":"umbilical artery"},{"label":"D","text":"placenta"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Maternal and foetal bloodstreams run side by side in the placenta without mixing, exchanging gases and nutrients across very thin membranes. The umbilical artery actually carries waste-laden blood away from the foetus rather than bringing nutrients in.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2017,30, $q$In the food chain maize -> locust -> lizard -> hawk, the locust is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a primary consumer"},{"label":"B","text":"a producer"},{"label":"C","text":"a secondary consumer"},{"label":"D","text":"a decomposer"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The locust feeds directly on the producer, maize, and therefore occupies the second trophic level. The lizard, which eats the locust, is the secondary consumer.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2017,31, $q$A network of interlinked food chains within an ecosystem is best described as a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"trophic level"},{"label":"B","text":"food web"},{"label":"C","text":"pyramid of numbers"},{"label":"D","text":"nutrient cycle"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Since few organisms feed on just one kind of food, many food chains interlock. The resulting feeding network is called a food web.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2017,32, $q$In the nitrogen cycle, the bacterium that converts ammonia into nitrite is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Azotobacter"},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomonas"},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrosomonas"},{"label":"D","text":"Rhizobium"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Nitrosomonas oxidises ammonia to nitrite, after which Nitrobacter oxidises nitrite to nitrate. Pseudomonas denitrifies, while Azotobacter and Rhizobium are nitrogen fixers.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2017,33, $q$Using the ten-per-cent rule, work out the energy arriving at the hawk in the chain grass -> grasshopper -> lizard -> hawk, given that the grass layer stores 10000 kJ.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1000 kJ"},{"label":"B","text":"100 kJ"},{"label":"C","text":"1 kJ"},{"label":"D","text":"10 kJ"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Only about one-tenth of the energy held by a trophic level is passed on to the next. Three transfers after the grass therefore leave roughly 10 kJ with the hawk.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Line up the levels","kind":"scene","content":"Grass (producer) -> grasshopper (primary consumer) -> lizard (secondary consumer) -> hawk (tertiary consumer), starting from 10000 kJ.","note":"Each arrow marks one energy transfer."},{"title":"Multiply by 0.1 step by step","kind":"math","content":"Grasshopper: 10000 x 0.1 = 1000 kJ. Lizard: 1000 x 0.1 = 100 kJ. Hawk: 100 x 0.1 = 10 kJ.","note":"Nine-tenths of the energy leaves each level."},{"title":"Check the result","kind":"text","content":"Only 10 kJ of the original 10000 kJ can reach the hawk.","note":"The lost energy reappears as heat given off in respiration."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','ecology',2017,34, $q$The loss of water as vapour from the aerial parts of a plant, mainly through the leaves, is termed$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"transpiration"},{"label":"B","text":"guttation"},{"label":"C","text":"percolation"},{"label":"D","text":"condensation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Water evaporates from the moist walls of the mesophyll cells and escapes as vapour through the stomata. Guttation, in contrast, squeezes liquid water out at the leaf margins.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2017,35, $q$Flowers that are pollinated by wind usually$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"bear large showy petals and a strong scent"},{"label":"B","text":"are small and dull, shedding abundant light dry pollen"},{"label":"C","text":"offer nectar cups that reward visiting insects"},{"label":"D","text":"carry heavy pollen in sticky clusters"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Wind-pollinated flowers do not need petals, scent or nectar because no carrier animal is involved. They compensate by releasing clouds of light pollen towards feathery stigmas.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2017,36, $q$Preserved remains or impressions of organisms that lived long ago, found in layers of sedimentary rock, are called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"homologous organs"},{"label":"B","text":"vestigial organs"},{"label":"C","text":"fossils"},{"label":"D","text":"minerals"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Fossils include bones, shells, impressions and trails laid down layer upon layer, with deeper layers holding older forms. This ordered record shows gradual change in life through time.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2017,37, $q$The forelimbs of man, whale and bat share one basic bone pattern although they grip, paddle and fly respectively. Such structures are best described as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"analogous structures"},{"label":"B","text":"vestigial structures"},{"label":"C","text":"acquired characters"},{"label":"D","text":"homologous structures"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Homologous structures keep the same underlying plan because of shared ancestry, even as their functions diverge. Analogous structures solve the same task from different starting designs, as in bird and insect wings.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2017,38, $q$Which of the following human blood cells is without a nucleus?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Leucocyte"},{"label":"B","text":"Erythrocyte"},{"label":"C","text":"Monocyte"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocyte"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mature red blood cells pack in haemoglobin after throwing out their nuclei. Every type of white cell listed retains a full nucleus.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);
