('biology','cellular-processes',2018,15, $q$On a bright sunny day a green leaf releases more oxygen than it consumes because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"its stomata stay firmly closed all day"},{"label":"B","text":"photosynthesis and respiration exactly balance"},{"label":"C","text":"photosynthesis proceeds faster than respiration in strong light"},{"label":"D","text":"the leaf stores up oxygen from the previous night"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Photosynthesis liberates oxygen while consuming carbon dioxide, and in full sunlight it outpaces respiration. The leaf therefore gives off more oxygen than its own cells use up.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2018,16, $q$Trace the sequence of anaerobic breakdown in yeast and decide which products form when glucose is fermented without oxygen.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Lactic acid only"},{"label":"B","text":"Carbon dioxide only"},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate and water"},{"label":"D","text":"Ethanol and carbon dioxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Yeast ferments glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide in the absence of oxygen. Muscle tissue, lacking the yeast enzymes, produces lactic acid instead.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Split the glucose first","kind":"text","content":"Glycolysis converts glucose into two molecules of pyruvate with a net gain of 2 ATP.","note":"Glycolysis itself needs no oxygen."},{"title":"Let the yeast enzymes act","kind":"text","content":"Yeast decarboxylates pyruvate to acetaldehyde with release of carbon dioxide, then reduces it to ethanol.","note":"No oxygen is consumed in this stage."},{"title":"Reject the muscle route","kind":"text","content":"Human muscle reduces pyruvate to lactate instead, because it lacks the yeast decarboxylase step.","note":"That is why lactic acid is the wrong product here."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2018,17, $q$The term that describes an enzyme's ability to work on only one kind of substrate is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"specificity"},{"label":"B","text":"denaturation"},{"label":"C","text":"sensitivity"},{"label":"D","text":"catalysis"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Each enzyme's active site fits one substrate, or a narrow family of close relatives, and this quality is called specificity. A change in the shape of the active site, for example by heat, destroys the fit.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2018,18, $q$Oxygen-poor blood returning from the body tissues settles first into which chamber of the heart?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Left atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"Left ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"Right atrium"},{"label":"D","text":"Right ventricle"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Systemic veins empty into the right atrium, which then hands the blood on to the right ventricle. The left side of the heart deals with the freshly oxygenated blood arriving from the lungs.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2018,19, $q$The digestion of proteins in man begins in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mouth"},{"label":"B","text":"small intestine"},{"label":"C","text":"large intestine"},{"label":"D","text":"stomach"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Pepsin in gastric juice starts splitting proteins in the acid of the stomach. The mouth digests only starch, while the intestine continues protein breakdown with trypsin and peptidases.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2018,20, $q$The tiny finger-like projections lining the wall of the ileum that absorb digested food are called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"villi"},{"label":"B","text":"alveoli"},{"label":"C","text":"nephrons"},{"label":"D","text":"bronchioles"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Villi hugely enlarge the absorptive surface of the ileum, and each one contains a lacteal plus a capillary network. Digested nutrients cross this enriched surface into the blood and lymph.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2018,21, $q$Water rises up a tall tree trunk mainly because of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"root pressure alone"},{"label":"B","text":"transpiration pull aided by the cohesion of water molecules"},{"label":"C","text":"capillarity of the phloem tubes"},{"label":"D","text":"gravity acting on the descending sap"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Water lost through the stomata pulls up a continuous column of water in the xylem, held together by cohesion and stuck to the walls by adhesion. Root pressure plays only a minor role in tall trees.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2018,22, $q$Weigh the listed environmental factors and decide under which condition a leaf would lose water fastest by transpiration.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"High humidity with still air"},{"label":"B","text":"Low temperature with moist air"},{"label":"C","text":"Moving wind with bright sun and moderately low humidity"},{"label":"D","text":"Dew saturating the leaf at night"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Transpiration rises with wind, warmth and light, but falls when the air is humid or still. The combination of a breeze, heat and open stomata gives the steepest water loss.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Evaluate humidity and air movement","kind":"text","content":"Wind strips away the humid shell of vapour round the leaf, keeping the outside air drier than the leaf interior.","note":"Stagnant air lets vapour accumulate and slows loss."},{"title":"Evaluate temperature and light","kind":"text","content":"A warm leaf holds more vapour inside, and sunlight keeps the stomata wide open.","note":"Closed stomata at night protect the leaf almost completely."},{"title":"Compare the four lists","kind":"text","content":"Wind plus warmth with sunlight beats every other option, so C has the highest transpiration rate.","note":"Night dew implies closed stomata and saturated air, the slowest possible case."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','systems',2018,23, $q$Between which structures listed below does the actual exchange of gases with the blood occur?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Larynx and trachea"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchioles and larynx"},{"label":"C","text":"Trachea and bronchi"},{"label":"D","text":"Alveoli and blood capillaries"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The alveoli lie against a dense net of capillaries, and gases diffuse across the jointly very thin membranes. Every airway above the alveoli merely conducts air in and out.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2018,24, $q$The organelle in fresh-water Protista such as Paramecium and Amoeba that pumps out excess water is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"contractile vacuole"},{"label":"B","text":"food vacuole"},{"label":"C","text":"nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"gullet"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$By contracting rhythmically, the contractile vacuole expels the water that keeps flooding in by osmosis. It also rids the cell of a little of its soluble waste.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2018,25, $q$The main benefit human beings derive from sweating is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the elimination of solid wastes"},{"label":"B","text":"getting rid of excess body heat along with some salts"},{"label":"C","text":"supplying the skin with oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"toughening the surface of the skin"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Evaporation of sweat carries a great deal of heat away from the body surface. Small amounts of urea and salts also leave dissolved in the water.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2018,26, $q$Where would you find the relay (intermediate) neurone of a spinal reflex?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Along the limb muscle"},{"label":"B","text":"Just outside the spinal cord"},{"label":"C","text":"Entirely within the central nervous system"},{"label":"D","text":"In the effector organ itself"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Relay neurones lie wholly within the central nervous system and link sensory neurones to motor neurones. The dorsal root ganglion houses the sensory cell bodies, while muscle contains only effectors.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2018,27, $q$Which endocrine gland influences the activities of most of the other endocrine glands?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenal"},{"label":"C","text":"Islets of Langerhans"},{"label":"D","text":"Pituitary"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Pituitary hormones such as TSH and ACTH direct the thyroid and adrenal glands to pour out their own secretions. This supervisory role earns the pituitary its title of master gland.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2018,28, $q$Cross two pea plants each heterozygous for both seed shape (Rr) and seed colour (Yy), then choose the expected phenotypic ratio among the offspring.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"3 : 1"},{"label":"C","text":"1 : 2 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"9 : 7"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Two independently assorting heterozygous gene pairs combined give the classical dihybrid ratio of 9:3:3:1. Ratios depart from this standard pattern only when the genes are linked or the alleles interact.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Handle one gene at a time","kind":"text","content":"Rr x Rr gives 3 round : 1 wrinkled, and Yy x Yy gives 3 yellow : 1 green.","note":"Segregation operates independently for each gene."},{"title":"Multiply the outcomes","kind":"math","content":"3 x 3 = 9 round yellow; 3 x 1 = 3 round green; 1 x 3 = 3 wrinkled yellow; 1 x 1 = 1 wrinkled green.","note":"The genes assort independently because they sit on different chromosomes."},{"title":"Check the total","kind":"text","content":"The four categories together use all 16 cells of the Punnett square, matching 9:3:3:1.","note":"Two independent dominant traits always give this classic ratio."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','genetics',2018,29, $q$A tall pea plant crossed with a dwarf one produced only tall offspring. The genotype of the tall parent is most probably$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"heterozygous (Tt)"},{"label":"B","text":"homozygous tall (TT)"},{"label":"C","text":"homozygous dwarf (tt)"},{"label":"D","text":"a selection of tall seeds from an earlier crop"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$If the tall parent were Tt, roughly half of the offspring with the tt plant would be dwarf. A family made up entirely of tall plants points to a TT parent, though a larger family would confirm it.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2018,30, $q$Red-green colour blindness caused by a recessive allele is far commoner in human males than in females because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the allele is carried on the Y chromosome"},{"label":"B","text":"males have two X chromosomes on which the allele is doubled"},{"label":"C","text":"the recessive allele lies on the X chromosome, and males have only one X"},{"label":"D","text":"affected fathers transmit the allele to all their sons"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A single X-borne recessive allele is expressed in males, since they lack a second X to mask it. Females must receive the allele on both X chromosomes to be affected.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2018,31, $q$Sickle cell anaemia arises from$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the loss of one autosome"},{"label":"B","text":"nondisjunction of the sex chromosomes"},{"label":"C","text":"crossing over during meiosis"},{"label":"D","text":"a point mutation in the gene for haemoglobin"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A single base change in the haemoglobin gene makes the beta chain behave wrongly under low oxygen. The red cells then become rigid and sickle-shaped and clog tiny vessels.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2018,32, $q$Raising a new sweet potato plant from a runner or a stem cutting is described as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"vegetative propagation"},{"label":"B","text":"pollination"},{"label":"C","text":"double fertilisation"},{"label":"D","text":"seed dispersal"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Vegetative propagation builds new plants from vegetative parts, without gametes or seeds. Runners, cuttings and tubers all work this way, and every daughter plant is a genetic copy of its parent.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2018,33, $q$The hormone that repairs and builds up the uterine lining after menstruation is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"progesterone"},{"label":"B","text":"oestrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"testosterone"},{"label":"D","text":"oxytocin"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Rising oestrogen from the growing follicles makes the uterine lining thicken during the first half of the cycle. Progesterone from the corpus luteum then maintains the prepared lining later on.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2018,34, $q$A typical seed of a flowering plant consists of the embryo, the seed coat (testa) and the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"stigma"},{"label":"B","text":"nucellus"},{"label":"C","text":"endosperm"},{"label":"D","text":"carpel"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The endosperm stores food for the growing embryo, as in maize and castor seeds. In beans and other non-endospermic seeds the food has already been moved into the cotyledons.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2018,35, $q$After fertilisation in a flowering plant, the ovule develops into the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fruit"},{"label":"B","text":"pericarp"},{"label":"C","text":"anther"},{"label":"D","text":"seed"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The fertilised ovule matures into the seed, with its coats derived from the integuments. The ovary, meanwhile, enlarges into the surrounding fruit.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2018,36, $q$The total number of individuals of one species living in a given area at a given time constitutes the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"population"},{"label":"B","text":"community"},{"label":"C","text":"ecosystem"},{"label":"D","text":"biosphere"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A population is one species counted in one place at one time. Adding all the populations in the area gives the community.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2018,37, $q$The natural dwelling place of an organism, with its particular set of conditions, is its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"niche"},{"label":"B","text":"habitat"},{"label":"C","text":"biosphere"},{"label":"D","text":"biome"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The habitat is where the organism lives, described by its physical surroundings. The niche, in contrast, describes the job the organism does within that place.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2018,38, $q$Which of the following is an abiotic component of a fresh-water habitat?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"The fish population"},{"label":"B","text":"Water weeds"},{"label":"C","text":"Dissolved oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"Frog spawn"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Dissolved oxygen, temperature and light are physical and chemical features of the water. Fish, weeds and frog spawn are all living components.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2019,10, $q$Cells of the kidney tubule, which reabsorb glucose by active transport, would be expected to contain unusually large numbers of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mitochondria"},{"label":"B","text":"chloroplasts"},{"label":"C","text":"vacuoles"},{"label":"D","text":"nuclei"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Active transport runs on ATP, and mitochondria are the chief suppliers of ATP. Tissues that pump hard, like kidney tubules and muscle, are packed with them.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2019,11, $q$The green pigment that traps light energy for photosynthesis is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carotene"},{"label":"B","text":"chlorophyll"},{"label":"C","text":"melanin"},{"label":"D","text":"haemoglobin"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue-violet light and converts it to chemical energy inside the chloroplast. Carotene is an accessory pigment, melanin protects skin, and haemoglobin carries oxygen in blood.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2019,12, $q$The jelly-like medium in which the organelles of a cell are suspended is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cell sap"},{"label":"B","text":"tissue fluid"},{"label":"C","text":"cytoplasm"},{"label":"D","text":"lymph"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Cytoplasm is the semi-fluid ground substance filling the cell outside the nucleus. Cell sap sits inside vacuoles, and lymph or tissue fluid bathes cells outside the body.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2019,13, $q$A cell that is not dividing spends most of its time in which stage of the cell cycle?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Prophase"},{"label":"B","text":"Telophase"},{"label":"C","text":"Metaphase"},{"label":"D","text":"Interphase"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Between divisions the cell grows, doubles its organelles and copies its DNA during interphase. The four mitotic stages that follow take up only a small part of the cycle.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2019,14, $q$Mineral ions are taken up by plant roots even against a concentration gradient by means of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"active transport"},{"label":"B","text":"diffusion"},{"label":"C","text":"osmosis"},{"label":"D","text":"capillarity"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Uphill uptake needs energy from respiration, which the root supplies as ATP. Diffusion and osmosis can only follow gradients downhill, which is why they cannot explain it.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2019,15, $q$Aerobic respiration may be summarised as glucose plus oxygen giving$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"water and nitrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon dioxide, water and energy"},{"label":"C","text":"lactic acid only"},{"label":"D","text":"proteins and heat"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Complete oxidation of glucose yields carbon dioxide, water and a large crop of ATP. Fermentation, which lacks oxygen, instead leaves ethanol or lactic acid behind.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2019,16, $q$Beyond the optimum temperature an enzyme-catalysed reaction sharply slows because the enzyme is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrolysed into amino acids"},{"label":"B","text":"phosphorylated"},{"label":"C","text":"denatured"},{"label":"D","text":"diluted"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Heat shakes apart the weak bonds holding the enzyme's shape, so the active site no longer fits the substrate. The damaged protein cannot be refitted by cooling, since denaturation is permanent.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2019,17, $q$Blood returning from the lower limbs to the heart enters through the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pulmonary artery"},{"label":"B","text":"pulmonary vein"},{"label":"C","text":"superior vena cava"},{"label":"D","text":"inferior vena cava"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The inferior vena cava gathers deoxygenated blood from the parts of the body below the heart. The superior vena cava drains the head, neck and arms instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2019,18, $q$Which blood vessels have walls only one cell thick and allow exchange of materials with the tissues?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Capillaries"},{"label":"B","text":"Arteries"},{"label":"C","text":"Veins"},{"label":"D","text":"Venules"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A single flattened endothelial layer makes the capillary wall ideal for diffusion. Arteries and veins need thicker muscular and elastic walls to withstand higher pressures.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2019,19, $q$The heart muscle itself is supplied with oxygenated blood by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pulmonary veins"},{"label":"B","text":"coronary arteries"},{"label":"C","text":"aorta branches to the limbs"},{"label":"D","text":"hepatic veins"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Coronary arteries leave the aorta at its base and run over the heart surface. Their blockage starves the muscle and brings on a heart attack.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2019,20, $q$Which of the following enzymes is secreted by the pancreas?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"B","text":"Rennin"},{"label":"C","text":"Trypsin"},{"label":"D","text":"Ptyalin"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Trypsin, pancreatic amylase and lipase pour from the pancreas into the duodenum. Pepsin and rennin come from the stomach, while ptyalin is salivary.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2019,21, $q$Excess amino acids are broken down, with the nitrogen released as urea, in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"stomach"},{"label":"B","text":"pancreas"},{"label":"C","text":"ileum"},{"label":"D","text":"liver"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The liver deaminates surplus amino acids, splitting off the amino group for conversion to urea. The carbon remainder is then used in respiration or stored as fat or glycogen.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2019,22, $q$The tangled knot of capillaries tucked inside the Bowman's capsule of the nephron is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glomerulus"},{"label":"B","text":"medulla"},{"label":"C","text":"ureter"},{"label":"D","text":"collecting duct"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The glomerulus sits inside the cup-shaped capsule, and blood pressure there drives ultrafiltration. The filtrate drains on through the tubules to the collecting duct.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2019,23, $q$The part of the brain that controls breathing rate, heart beat and swallowing is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cerebrum"},{"label":"B","text":"medulla oblongata"},{"label":"C","text":"cerebellum"},{"label":"D","text":"hypothalamus"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Vital reflex centres for breathing, heart rate and swallowing lie in the medulla oblongata. The cerebellum balances posture, and the cerebrum handles conscious thought.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2019,24, $q$The amount of light entering the eye is adjusted by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"retina"},{"label":"B","text":"cornea"},{"label":"C","text":"iris"},{"label":"D","text":"lens"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Muscle fibres in the iris alter the diameter of the pupil, closing it in bright light and opening it in dim light. The cornea and lens focus the image, and the retina receives it.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2019,25, $q$Examine how a nerve impulse crosses from one neurone to the next at a synapse and decide what carries it across the gap.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Blood plasma flowing in a fine capillary"},{"label":"B","text":"The fatty myelin sheath stretching across"},{"label":"C","text":"Red blood cells squeezing through"},{"label":"D","text":"A chemical neurotransmitter released into the cleft"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Synapses are narrow clefts crossed by chemical messengers, not by blood or by stretching membranes. Release and receptor binding hand the impulse on within a fraction of a second.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Watch the impulse arrive","kind":"scene","content":"An impulse sweeps down the axon and reaches the bulb at the end of the neurone.","note":"The message travels electrically along the fibre."},{"title":"Calcium lets vesicles fuse","kind":"text","content":"Vesicles of neurotransmitter, such as acetylcholine, fuse with the membrane and pour into the synaptic cleft.","note":"No blood vessel crosses the cleft itself."},{"title":"Cross over and restart","kind":"text","content":"The chemical binds receptors on the next neurone and starts a fresh impulse there.","note":"The myelin sheath only insulates the transmitting fibre."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','genetics',2019,26, $q$When both alleles of a gene pair are fully expressed side by side in a heterozygote, the condition is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"co-dominance"},{"label":"B","text":"complete dominance"},{"label":"C","text":"polygenic inheritance"},{"label":"D","text":"multiple allelism"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$In co-dominance both characters show, as in the AB blood group. Complete dominance would let one allele alone mask its partner.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2019,27, $q$The ABO blood group system in man is controlled by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"three independent genes"},{"label":"B","text":"three alleles of one gene"},{"label":"C","text":"two alleles of one gene"},{"label":"D","text":"six alleles of two genes"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$One gene carries the three alleles I superscript A, I superscript B and i. Each person inherits only two of them, which produces the four known groups.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2019,28, $q$Mendel observed that the two factors of a character separate during gamete formation, so that each gamete receives only one. This is his law of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"dominance"},{"label":"B","text":"linkage"},{"label":"C","text":"segregation"},{"label":"D","text":"independent assortment"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Segregation splits the allele pair at meiosis, one allele to gamete. Independent assortment is the companion rule for two different gene pairs.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2019,29, $q$To establish the genotype of a plant showing a dominant trait, the plant should be crossed with one that is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"heterozygous for the same trait"},{"label":"B","text":"homozygous dominant for the trait"},{"label":"C","text":"of the same genotype"},{"label":"D","text":"homozygous recessive for the trait"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Crossing with a homozygous recessive (a test cross) exposes any hidden recessive allele. A 1:1 offspring ratio signals a heterozygous parent, and all-dominant offspring signals a homozygous one.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2019,30, $q$Meiosis is the type of cell division responsible for the formation of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"gametes in animals and spores in plants"},{"label":"B","text":"identical body cells for growth"},{"label":"C","text":"zygotes straight from body cells"},{"label":"D","text":"new red blood cells in the marrow"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Meiosis halves the chromosome number and shuffles genes, which suits gamete and spore production. Mitosis, with its exact copies, serves growth and repair instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2019,31, $q$Work through the effect of meiosis on chromosome number: a body cell containing 16 chromosomes forms gametes with how many chromosomes?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"16"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"32"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Meiosis halves the diploid number, so 16 chromosomes give gametes with 8. Fertilisation then adds the two gametes back together to restore 16.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Note the starting number","kind":"text","content":"The body cell is diploid, carrying 16 chromosomes in 8 matched pairs.","note":"Body cells arise by mitosis, which keeps the count."},{"title":"Halve it at meiosis","kind":"math","content":"16 divided by 2 = 8 chromosomes per gamete.","note":"Meiosis separates the members of every pair."},{"title":"Restore on fertilisation","kind":"text","content":"Two gametes with 8 each fuse at fertilisation to rebuild 16 in the zygote.","note":"Doubling would give 32, which no egg receives."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','reproduction',2019,32, $q$In the human female reproductive system, fertilisation of the ovum normally takes place in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"uterus"},{"label":"B","text":"cervix"},{"label":"C","text":"oviduct"},{"label":"D","text":"vagina"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Sperm meet the released ovum in the oviduct (fallopian tube), usually in its upper part. The fertilised egg then travels on to implant in the uterus.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2019,33, $q$The role an organism plays in its habitat, including what it eats and how it lives, defines its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ecological niche"},{"label":"B","text":"habitat"},{"label":"C","text":"population size"},{"label":"D","text":"biome"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The niche is the organism's job or profession within its community, while the habitat is simply where it lives. Two species may share one habitat yet occupy different niches.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2019,34, $q$Trace the course of succession in an abandoned field and pick the community that finally becomes stable and self-replacing.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Pioneer community of weeds and grasses"},{"label":"B","text":"Climax community of woodland trees"},{"label":"C","text":"Abiotic base of bare soil"},{"label":"D","text":"Community of decomposers alone"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Succession moves from short-lived pioneers through woody stages to a climax community that maintains itself. Only the climax holds its composition without being replaced.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Pioneers arrive","kind":"scene","content":"Weeds and grasses colonise the bare ground first and improve the soil with their litter.","note":"The abiotic soil base holds only microbes and seeds."},{"title":"Woody stage follows","kind":"text","content":"Shrubs then young trees shade out the sun-loving pioneers step by step.","note":"Each stage modifies the habitat for the next."},{"title":"Stability is reached","kind":"text","content":"The climax community, woodland, replaces itself indefinitely unless the climate shifts.","note":"Succession ends in this balanced, self-maintaining state."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','ecology',2019,35, $q$Organisms such as bacteria and fungi that break down the remains of dead plants and animals to recycle nutrients are called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"producers"},{"label":"B","text":"primary consumers"},{"label":"C","text":"decomposers"},{"label":"D","text":"autotrophs"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Decomposers digest dead organic matter and return mineral nutrients to the soil for reuse by plants. Producers and autotrophs make their own food instead of consuming remains.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2019,36, $q$Green plants remove carbon dioxide directly from the atmosphere during$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"digestion"},{"label":"B","text":"combustion"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrification"},{"label":"D","text":"photosynthesis"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Photosynthesis fixes atmospheric carbon dioxide into sugars within the chloroplasts. Respiration by plants and animals, combustion and decay return the gas to the air.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2019,37, $q$The association in which fungal threads wrap round the roots of a higher plant and help it absorb water and minerals is known as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a mycorrhiza"},{"label":"B","text":"a lichen"},{"label":"C","text":"parenchyma"},{"label":"D","text":"a root nodule"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A mycorrhiza is a partnership between a fungus and plant roots, both partners gaining. Root nodules instead shelter nitrogen-fixing Rhizobium, and a lichen pairs a fungus with an alga.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2020,10, $q$Prokaryotic cells differ from eukaryotic cells chiefly in lacking$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cytoplasm"},{"label":"B","text":"a nuclear membrane round the hereditary material"},{"label":"C","text":"ribosomes"},{"label":"D","text":"a hereditary molecule"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In prokaryotes such as bacteria the DNA lies naked in the cytoplasm with no nuclear envelope. Ribosomes, cytoplasm and DNA are all present in both cell kinds.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2020,11, $q$Chloroplasts are most abundant in which part of a typical leaf?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Palisade mesophyll cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Epidermal cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Root cap cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Xylem fibres"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The palisade layer sits just under the upper epidermis, where sunlight first strikes. Epidermis, root cap and xylem lie away from the light-catching zone.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2020,12, $q$A selectively permeable membrane is one that allows$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"all materials to pass at equal rates"},{"label":"B","text":"no substances whatsoever to cross it"},{"label":"C","text":"the selective passage of some substances while holding back others"},{"label":"D","text":"whole solid food particles into the cell"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The membrane chooses between molecules, letting water pass freely while restricting larger or charged particles. This selectivity underlies osmosis and the cell's intake control.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2020,13, $q$Which organelle holds enzymes capable of digesting worn-out parts of the cell itself?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondrion"},{"label":"B","text":"Chloroplast"},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"Lysosome"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes and break down damaged organelles and engulfed particles. Recycling the debris keeps the cell's internal budget in order.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2020,14, $q$The raw materials from which photosynthesis builds sugar are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon dioxide and water"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen and glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"starch and water"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen and light"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Carbon dioxide enters through the stomata and water arrives from the roots, and light energy binds them into glucose. Oxygen appears as a by-product, not an ingredient.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2020,15, $q$The light-independent (dark) stage of photosynthesis takes place in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"thylakoid lumen"},{"label":"B","text":"stroma of the chloroplast"},{"label":"C","text":"nuclear sap"},{"label":"D","text":"stomata"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The enzymes that fix carbon dioxide into carbohydrate work in the stroma surrounding the grana. The thylakoid membranes, with their chlorophyll, run the light-requiring stage instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2020,16, $q$Follow the fate of pyruvate in a hard-working human muscle deprived of oxygen and decide what it is converted into.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Ethanol"},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"Lactic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Carbon dioxide and water"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Without oxygen the muscle reduces pyruvate to lactic acid, recovering some NAD for glycolysis. Piling-up lactate is what makes tired muscles ache; only yeast produces ethanol.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Glycolysis runs without oxygen","kind":"text","content":"Glucose is split to pyruvate in the cytoplasm, with a small net gain of ATP.","note":"This stage is the same in yeast and in muscle."},{"title":"Oxygen short, muscle switches route","kind":"text","content":"Pyruvate is reduced directly to lactic acid, which regenerates the NAD needed to keep glycolysis going.","note":"No carbon dioxide is released on this route."},{"title":"Contrast the alternatives","kind":"text","content":"Yeast meanwhile decarboxylate pyruvate to ethanol, and full oxidation to CO2 and water needs oxygen.","note":"So ethanol, and complete oxidation, are the wrong tracks here."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2020,17, $q$As the concentration of substrate offered to an enzyme is raised, the reaction rate$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"decreases steadily to zero"},{"label":"B","text":"falls off at once"},{"label":"C","text":"remains completely unchanged"},{"label":"D","text":"increases up to a limit set by the enzyme concentration"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$More substrate means more enzyme-substrate collisions at first, so the rate climbs. Once every active site is busy, extra substrate cannot help and the curve flattens.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2020,18, $q$Which chamber of the heart pumps blood to the whole body through the aorta?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Left ventricle"},{"label":"B","text":"Right ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"Right atrium"},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary artery"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The left ventricle has the thickest muscular wall because it drives blood round the entire systemic circuit. The right ventricle only pushes blood the short distance to the lungs.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2020,19, $q$Which blood constituent is the actual oxygen carrier?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Leucocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"Haemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrinogen"},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocytes"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Haemoglobin in the red cells binds oxygen loosely and reversibly, forming oxyhaemoglobin. Fibrinogen clots the blood, while the white cells defend the body.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2020,20, $q$Which statement describes inhalation (inspiration) in man correctly?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"The diaphragm relaxes and domes upward"},{"label":"B","text":"The volume of the chest cavity decreases"},{"label":"C","text":"The diaphragm flattens and the chest cavity expands"},{"label":"D","text":"The ribs push stale air out"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Contraction flattens the diaphragm and raises the ribs, enlarging the chest cavity. The pressure inside then falls below atmospheric, and air flows in until the two are equal.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2020,21, $q$The lining of the stomach is not digested by its own acid and pepsin because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"its wall is made of enamel"},{"label":"B","text":"its lining cells secrete a protective layer of mucus"},{"label":"C","text":"secretin neutralises all the acid produced"},{"label":"D","text":"the food inside stays completely dry"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mucus forms a slimy barrier that keeps pepsin and acid off the living tissue. When this barrier fails, an ulcer develops.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2020,22, $q$After a pollen grain lands on a compatible stigma it germinates and grows into the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pollen tube, which carries the male gametes down to the ovule"},{"label":"B","text":"ovary wall"},{"label":"C","text":"embryo sac"},{"label":"D","text":"filament"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The tube digests its way through the style, conducting the two male gametes to the embryo sac. One gamete fuses with the egg, and the second joins with the fusion nucleus.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2020,23, $q$The female part of a flower that catches and holds pollen grains is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"style"},{"label":"B","text":"anther"},{"label":"C","text":"stigma"},{"label":"D","text":"filament"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The stigma, usually sticky or feathery, sits at the top of the style to trap pollen. The anther is the male pollen-producing organ at the other end of the process.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2020,24, $q$The fusion of a male gamete with the egg cell in an ovule directly produces the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"style"},{"label":"B","text":"corolla"},{"label":"C","text":"pollen grain"},{"label":"D","text":"zygote"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The zygote formed by this fusion divides to build the embryo of the new plant. The ovule later matures into the seed around it.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2020,25, $q$The length of the gestation period in man is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"three months"},{"label":"B","text":"nine months"},{"label":"C","text":"twelve months"},{"label":"D","text":"six months"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The human foetus spends roughly nine months, about 280 days, in the uterus. Cattle and other large mammals carry their young for a similar span.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2020,26, $q$All the living organisms and physical factors of a defined area, interacting as one system, make up the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ecosystem"},{"label":"B","text":"community"},{"label":"C","text":"habitat"},{"label":"D","text":"niche"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$An ecosystem joins a community with its non-living surroundings, energy flow included. Community alone means just the living part, and habitat is merely the place.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2020,27, $q$Which of the following is a biotic factor in an ecosystem?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Rainfall"},{"label":"B","text":"Predators"},{"label":"C","text":"Humidity"},{"label":"D","text":"Temperature"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Biotic factors are living influences; predators, competitors and decomposers all qualify. Rainfall, humidity and temperature are physical (abiotic) conditions.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2020,28, $q$The first trophic level of any food chain is occupied by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carnivores"},{"label":"B","text":"primary consumers"},{"label":"C","text":"producers"},{"label":"D","text":"decomposers"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Green plants manufacture food from sunlight and form the base of every chain. Herbivores rise to the second level only by eating them.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2020,29, $q$Setting land aside as national parks, game reserves and sanctuary forests is a direct form of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"timber extraction"},{"label":"B","text":"shifting cultivation"},{"label":"C","text":"recreational tourism"},{"label":"D","text":"wildlife conservation"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Protecting whole habitats in parks keeps rare plants and animals alive in their natural settings. Logging, cultivation and unmanaged tourism would do the opposite.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2020,30, $q$The quadrat method of population study is most suitable for estimating numbers of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sessile organisms such as grasses in a meadow"},{"label":"B","text":"migratory birds crossing the field"},{"label":"C","text":"fast-running mammals in the area"},{"label":"D","text":"flying insects above the canopy"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A quadrat is a fixed frame laid on the ground and works only for stationary organisms, plants above all. Mobile animals flee or drift out of the frame and so are badly sampled.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2020,31, $q$The maximum population of a species that a particular environment can support indefinitely is termed its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"biotic potential"},{"label":"B","text":"carrying capacity"},{"label":"C","text":"population density"},{"label":"D","text":"mortality"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Carrying capacity is set by the resources available, chiefly food, water and space. Populations that overshoot it crash as the resources run short.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2020,32, $q$The physical appearance and other visible characters of an organism constitute its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"genotype"},{"label":"B","text":"genome"},{"label":"C","text":"phenotype"},{"label":"D","text":"allele"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The phenotype is what the genes allow the body to show, from tallness to flower colour. The genotype is the actual gene pair carried, which the phenotype may only partly reveal.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2020,33, $q$Work through the cross of two parents both with blood group A but heterozygous (AO x AO), and decide which blood groups their children could show.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"A and O only"},{"label":"B","text":"AB only"},{"label":"C","text":"A and AB only"},{"label":"D","text":"all four groups"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Crossing AO with AO yields the genotypes AA, AO, AO and oo, a 3-to-1 ratio of A to O. Neither parent carries the B allele, so groups B and AB are impossible.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Write the gametes","kind":"text","content":"Each AO parent produces A gametes and o gametes in equal numbers.","note":"The o allele makes group O only when paired with another o."},{"title":"Combine them","kind":"math","content":"The children come out AA, AO, AO and oo in a 3 : 1 ratio.","note":"AA and AO give group A; oo gives group O."},{"title":"Rule out the rest","kind":"text","content":"Because no parent carries I superscript B, no child can be B or AB.","note":"Group B alleles simply cannot appear in this cross."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2020,34, $q$The combination of sex chromosomes that develops into a normal human female is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"XX"},{"label":"B","text":"XY"},{"label":"C","text":"XO"},{"label":"D","text":"YY"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Two X chromosomes make a female; an X with a Y makes a male. An XO combination arises only abnormally, and YY cannot survive because the Y carries almost no vital genes.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2020,35, $q$Follow the feeding relations of the chain maize -> weaver bird -> snake -> mongoose step by step and state the trophic level of the snake.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Producer (first level)"},{"label":"B","text":"Primary consumer (second level)"},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary consumer (third level)"},{"label":"D","text":"Decomposer"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Counting from the producer upward, the snake is the second animal feeder and so occupies the third trophic level. It is a secondary consumer devoted to preying on the grain-eating bird.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Fix the base","kind":"scene","content":"Maize is the producer making food from sunlight, so it stands at level one.","note":"Every chain begins with an autotroph."},{"title":"Count the feeders upward","kind":"text","content":"Weaver bird eats maize = level 2 (primary consumer); snake eats the weaver = level 3.","note":"Each step of eating raises the level by one."},{"title":"Place the mongoose","kind":"text","content":"The mongoose, eating the snake, belongs at level 4 as a tertiary consumer.","note":"Highest-level consumers are vulnerable to any collapse below them."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','systems',2020,36, $q$The digestion of starch in the human alimentary canal begins in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"stomach"},{"label":"B","text":"mouth"},{"label":"C","text":"duodenum"},{"label":"D","text":"ileum"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Salivary amylase (ptyalin) starts splitting cooked starch into maltose while food is still chewed. The stomach's acidity halts the enzyme, and pancreatic amylase resumes the work in the duodenum.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2020,37, $q$Insulin, the hormone that lowers blood glucose, is secreted by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"islets of Langerhans of the pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"adrenal medulla"},{"label":"C","text":"thyroid gland"},{"label":"D","text":"pituitary gland"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The beta cells of the islets release insulin whenever the blood sugar climbs. Secreting too little, or responding weakly, produces the condition called diabetes mellitus.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2020,38, $q$The part of the eye on which the image of an object is finally focused is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cornea"},{"label":"B","text":"pupil"},{"label":"C","text":"iris"},{"label":"D","text":"retina"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The cornea and lens combine to throw a sharp inverted image on the retina. Light-sensitive cells there then convert it into nerve impulses for the brain.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2021,10, $q$The control centre of the cell, which houses the chromosomes and directs protein synthesis, is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ribosome"},{"label":"B","text":"centrosome"},{"label":"C","text":"nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"nucleolus"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The nucleus holds the DNA-bearing chromosomes and sends out the instructions for protein building. The nucleolus is merely its ribosome-making body.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2021,11, $q$Which organelle in plant cells serves chiefly for the storage of starch?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Amyloplast"},{"label":"B","text":"Chloroplast"},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondrion"},{"label":"D","text":"Chromoplast"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Amyloplasts are colourless leucoplasts that stockpile starch in tubers and seeds. Chloroplasts trap light, chromoplasts hold pigments, and mitochondria release energy.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2021,12, $q$Osmosis may be defined as the movement of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"gases through the stomata of a leaf"},{"label":"B","text":"solutes against a concentration gradient"},{"label":"C","text":"glucose in both directions across a membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"water across a semi-permeable membrane from the dilute to the more concentrated side"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Osmosis is diffusion restricted to water across a barrier that blocks the solute. The water continues until the concentrations on the two sides balance out.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2021,13, $q$The simple sugar first made in photosynthesis is soon converted and stored in the green leaf mainly as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"starch"},{"label":"B","text":"glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"protein"},{"label":"D","text":"oil"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Leaves convert their sugar product into starch for storage, since starch is insoluble and harmless to accumulate. Glucose is the sugar first made, but it is not left lying about.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2021,14, $q$Which of these processes is carried out by both plant and animal cells?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"label":"B","text":"Respiration"},{"label":"C","text":"Transpiration"},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrogen fixation"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Every living cell respires, animals and plants alike, to release energy from food. Photosynthesis, transpiration and nitrogen fixation are activities reserved for green plants and microbes.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2021,15, $q$The description of enzyme action as a lock-and-key fit refers to the relationship between the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cell and its tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"leaf and its stem"},{"label":"C","text":"enzyme and its substrate"},{"label":"D","text":"blood and its plasma"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The key (substrate) fits only the lock (enzyme's active site), so the reaction is specific. Keys other than the matching one cannot open the lock and remain unchanged.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2021,16, $q$Trace the closed route of systemic circulation round the body and choose the correct order of the vessels the blood passes through.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"viens -> capillaries -> arteries -> back to the heart"},{"label":"B","text":"capillaries -> arteries -> veins -> back to the heart"},{"label":"C","text":"arteries -> veins -> capillaries -> back to the heart"},{"label":"D","text":"arteries -> capillaries -> veins -> back to the heart"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Blood leaves by arteries, slows for exchange in the capillaries and returns through the veins. Any ordering that swaps the capillaries out of the middle position breaks the circuit.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Leave the heart","kind":"scene","content":"The ventricle drives blood into the arteries, which branch ever smaller away from the heart.","note":"Arteries always carry blood away from the heart."},{"title":"Exchange at the tissues","kind":"text","content":"Flow slows in the tiny capillaries, where oxygen, nutrients and wastes are exchanged through the one-cell-thick walls.","note":"Capillaries are the only exchange vessels."},{"title":"Return to the heart","kind":"text","content":"Capillaries rejoin into venules and then veins, which empty into the atrium and restart the circuit.","note":"Veins always carry blood back towards the heart."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','systems',2021,17, $q$The structure that stops food from slipping into the windpipe during swallowing is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"epiglottis"},{"label":"B","text":"glottis"},{"label":"C","text":"bronchus"},{"label":"D","text":"pharynx"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$As the bolus passes, a flap called the epiglottis folds over the opening of the larynx. Without it, food would breach the airway and cause choking or coughing.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2021,18, $q$The end-products of the complete digestion of proteins are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"glucose molecules"},{"label":"B","text":"fatty acids and glycerol"},{"label":"C","text":"amino acids"},{"label":"D","text":"vitamins"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Proteins are cleaved by pepsin, trypsin and peptidases stage by stage down to amino acids. Glucose comes from carbohydrates, and fatty acids with glycerol from fats.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2021,19, $q$The mineral element required for building haemoglobin in the blood is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iodine"},{"label":"B","text":"iron"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Every haem group in haemoglobin cradles one iron atom, so shortage brings on anaemia. Iodine serves the thyroid, and calcium hardens bone and teeth.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2021,20, $q$The plant tissue that transports manufactured food from the leaves to other organs is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"xylem"},{"label":"B","text":"cortex"},{"label":"C","text":"stomata"},{"label":"D","text":"phloem"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Phloem conducts sugars and other soluble foods from source to sink, a movement called translocation. Xylem instead drives water and mineral salts upward.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2021,21, $q$The organ chiefly responsible for the removal of excess body heat during sweating is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"skin"},{"label":"B","text":"kidney"},{"label":"C","text":"liver"},{"label":"D","text":"spleen"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sweat glands in the skin pour water to the surface, and its evaporation cools the body cheaply. The kidney and liver process wastes, but neither sheds heat this way.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2021,22, $q$The central nervous system is made up of the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cranial and spinal nerves only"},{"label":"B","text":"sense organs"},{"label":"C","text":"brain and spinal cord"},{"label":"D","text":"muscles and glands"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Brain and spinal cord process incoming information and issue the commands. The nerves of the body belong to the peripheral system, and muscles and glands are effectors.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2021,23, $q$Endocrine glands differ from exocrine glands because endocrine glands$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"possess ducts to the surface"},{"label":"B","text":"are ductless and pour their hormones into the blood"},{"label":"C","text":"secrete digestive enzymes into the gut"},{"label":"D","text":"are fastened to the skeleton"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Without ducts, hormones must travel in the bloodstream to reach their target organs. Exocrine glands, with ducts, deliver enzymes and sweat onto surfaces.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2021,24, $q$Any change in the environment that a living organism detects and reacts to is called a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"response"},{"label":"B","text":"effector"},{"label":"C","text":"impulse"},{"label":"D","text":"stimulus"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The stimulus is the change or signal received. The reaction the organism makes is the response, delivered by an effector such as a muscle or gland.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2021,25, $q$A pure-breeding black mouse (BB) is crossed with a pure-breeding white mouse (bb), black being dominant. The entire first filial generation will be$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"black"},{"label":"B","text":"white"},{"label":"C","text":"grey"},{"label":"D","text":"half black and half white"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Every offspring receives B from the black parent and b from the white one, so all are Bb. With B dominant, the heterozygotes all show black coats; grey would require blending inheritance, which single dominant genes do not give.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2021,26, $q$Test which of the listed conditions is inherited as a sex-linked (X-borne) character in man, then justify the choice.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Sickle cell anaemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Albinism"},{"label":"C","text":"Haemophilia"},{"label":"D","text":"Measles"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Haemophilia is the classical X-linked recessive character, afflicting mostly males. Sickle cell anaemia and albinism are autosomal, while measles is an infection, not a gene.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Locate the gene","kind":"text","content":"The gene for haemophilia sits on the X chromosome, so sons inherit the condition from carrier mothers.","note":"Fathers pass an X only to daughters."},{"title":"Follow the pattern","kind":"text","content":"Affected males are far commoner than affected females, since a male needs just one faulty X.","note":"This male bias is the signature of X-linkage."},{"title":"Eliminate the rest","kind":"text","content":"Sickle cell and albinism are autosomal traits, and measles is not hereditary at all.","note":"Only haemophilia rides the sex chromosomes."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2021,27, $q$Genes occupy fixed positions on chromosomes known as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"codons"},{"label":"B","text":"loci"},{"label":"C","text":"centromeres"},{"label":"D","text":"nucleotides"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The place a gene holds on a chromosome is its locus, the plural being loci. Codons are base triplets and nucleotides are the building blocks of DNA itself.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2021,28, $q$Taken together, the anther and the filament constitute the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"stamen"},{"label":"B","text":"pistil"},{"label":"C","text":"corolla"},{"label":"D","text":"sepal"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Filament plus anther equals stamen, the male unit of the flower. Pistil names the female carpel unit, jointed atop the ovary.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2021,29, $q$In a flowering plant the structures housing the female gametes are found within the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"anther"},{"label":"B","text":"stigma"},{"label":"C","text":"pollen tube"},{"label":"D","text":"ovule"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The ovule encloses the embryo sac, whose egg cell is the true female gamete. On fertilisation the ovule ripens into the seed, sheltering the embryo within it.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2021,30, $q$After fertilisation in a flowering plant the ovary enlarges and matures into the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"seed"},{"label":"B","text":"fruit"},{"label":"C","text":"filament"},{"label":"D","text":"anther"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The ovary swells into the fruit, which ripens to protect and disperse the seeds inside. Each contained ovule has already turned into a seed.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2021,31, $q$The gradual, orderly replacement of one community by another over time is described as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pollution"},{"label":"B","text":"mutation"},{"label":"C","text":"succession"},{"label":"D","text":"conservation"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Succession runs from bare pioneer stages towards a stable climax community. Each stage alters the surroundings so as to favour the next wave of colonisers.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2021,32, $q$Organisms able to manufacture their own food from simple inorganic substances are called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"autotrophs"},{"label":"B","text":"heterotrophs"},{"label":"C","text":"saprophytes"},{"label":"D","text":"parasites"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Autotrophs, chiefly green plants, build organic matter from carbon dioxide and water with light energy. All the other groups must take ready-made food from other organisms.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2021,33, $q$Examine the three conventional ecological pyramids and decide which one can never be inverted whatever the ecosystem.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Pyramid of numbers"},{"label":"B","text":"Pyramid of biomass"},{"label":"C","text":"Pyramid of energy"},{"label":"D","text":"Pyramid of height"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Whatever the habitat, each trophic transfer sheds energy as heat, so less energy reaches each higher level. This irreversible loss keeps the pyramid of energy permanently upright.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Numbers can flip","kind":"text","content":"One old tree can support thousands of insects, so a number pyramid may stand on a narrow base.","note":"Counting heads misleads."},{"title":"Biomass may dip too","kind":"text","content":"Standing crop varies with the season of measurement, so biomass pyramids can occasionally invert.","note":"Dry weight is a snapshot, not a flow."},{"title":"Energy must narrow","kind":"text","content":"Ten per cent or less of the energy passes up each level, so the energy pyramid is always upright.","note":"Energy leaves the chain as heat and cannot be recycled."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','ecology',2021,34, $q$The interspecific relationship in which both partners gain a clear benefit is called$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"commensalism"},{"label":"B","text":"mutualism"},{"label":"C","text":"predation"},{"label":"D","text":"saprophytism"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mutualism pays both partners, as between a cow and its rumen microbes. In commensalism one side gains while the other neither gains nor loses, and in predation one partner dies.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2021,35, $q$The strikingly similar early embryos of fish, birds and humans are taken as evidence of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"common ancestry"},{"label":"B","text":"special independent creation of each group"},{"label":"C","text":"inheritance of acquired characters"},{"label":"D","text":"adaptation to the same climate"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sharing a common ancestor, the groups re-enact a like developmental plan early in life before diverging. This embryological agreement is one of the classical arguments for evolution.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2021,36, $q$Organisms congregating in patches or clusters within their habitat, as termites do round rotten wood, shows which type of dispersion?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Random dispersion"},{"label":"B","text":"Uniform dispersion"},{"label":"C","text":"Regular dispersion"},{"label":"D","text":"Clumped (contagious) dispersion"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Clumped dispersion gathers individuals where resources or social grouping draw them. Uniform spacing implies territorial defence, and random spacing brings no pattern at all.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2022,10, $q$The organelle in which aerobic respiration takes place in a eukaryotic cell is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ribosome"},{"label":"B","text":"mitochondrion"},{"label":"C","text":"nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Aerobic oxidation of glucose down to carbon dioxide and water is completed inside mitochondria. Their folded inner membranes (cristae) give the respiratory enzymes more working surface.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2022,11, $q$Which structure determines exactly what enters and leaves a living cell?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"Cell wall"},{"label":"C","text":"Chloroplast"},{"label":"D","text":"Cell (plasma) membrane"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The selectively permeable plasma membrane screens every cross-border movement of the cell. The cellulose wall is fully permeable and merely braces the plant cell.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2022,12, $q$Which of these is found in a plant cell but never in an animal cell?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Chloroplast"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitochondrion"},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Only plant cells carry out photosynthesis, so only they contain chloroplasts. Every eukaryote shares mitochondria, a nucleus and a Golgi apparatus.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2022,13, $q$The splitting of water molecules during the light stage of photosynthesis releases$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon dioxide"},{"label":"B","text":"glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"nitrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"oxygen"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Photolysis breaks water into hydrogen for reducing power and oxygen as a by-product. The oxygen thus released is the same gas that refreshed the early atmosphere.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2022,14, $q$The products formed when respiration proceeds aerobically in a cell are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lactic acid and oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"glucose and oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon dioxide, water and energy"},{"label":"D","text":"urea and heat"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Complete aerobic oxidation yields carbon dioxide and water and releases the energy locked in glucose. Fermentation instead leaves lactic acid or alcohol partly unoxidised.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2022,15, $q$Pepsin, the protein-splitting enzyme of the stomach, works best in a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"strongly acidic medium"},{"label":"B","text":"alkaline medium such as the duodenum provides"},{"label":"C","text":"distilled neutral water"},{"label":"D","text":"boiling water medium"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Pepsin is built for the stomach, where hydrochloric acid holds the pH near 2. Trypsin, mildly alkaline in the intestine, is the enzyme that needs the opposite setting.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2022,16, $q$Stomata open in daylight chiefly because the guard cells$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lose water and turn flaccid"},{"label":"B","text":"gain water and become turgid"},{"label":"C","text":"become coated with cuticle"},{"label":"D","text":"divide rapidly in the light"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Photosynthesis and ion uptake raise the solute level in guard cells, so water enters and they swell. Their thick inner walls bow the pore open; flaccid guard cells close it instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2022,17, $q$Peristalsis, by which food is moved along the alimentary canal, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"rhythmic waves of muscular contraction passing along the gut wall"},{"label":"B","text":"chemical breakdown of proteins by gastric juice"},{"label":"C","text":"absorption of nutrients into the lacteals"},{"label":"D","text":"a deliberate squeezing of food by the tongue when swallowing"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Circular muscles contract behind each bolus and relax ahead of it, pushing food forward involuntarily. Digestion, absorption and tongue action are all different processes.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2022,18, $q$The teeth of an adult mammal that are adapted for grinding food are the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"molars and premolars"},{"label":"B","text":"incisors"},{"label":"C","text":"canines"},{"label":"D","text":"milk teeth"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Broad, ridged molars and premolars crush and grind between them. Incisors nip, canines grip and tear, and milk teeth are simply the first temporary set.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2022,19, $q$During vigorous exercise the heart beats faster chiefly in order to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lower the blood pressure"},{"label":"B","text":"cool the skin"},{"label":"C","text":"speed up digestion"},{"label":"D","text":"deliver more oxygen and glucose to the muscles"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Hard-working muscles burn oxygen and glucose rapidly and would suffocate without extra supply. A faster beat delivers more blood, and hence more fuel, per minute.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2022,20, $q$Poor vision at night, known as night blindness, results from a deficiency of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"vitamin A"},{"label":"B","text":"vitamin B1"},{"label":"C","text":"vitamin C"},{"label":"D","text":"vitamin D"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Vitamin A builds the visual purple of the retina, without which dim light cannot be seen. Lack of B1 brings beriberi, of C brings scurvy, and of D brings rickets.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2022,21, $q$The semi-solid chyme formed during digestion collects first in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oesophagus"},{"label":"B","text":"stomach"},{"label":"C","text":"ileum"},{"label":"D","text":"rectum"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Churning with acid and pepsin reduces the swallowed food to a creamy chyme in the stomach. It is then squirted in small amounts into the duodenum for the next stage.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2022,22, $q$Follow the filtration step of urine formation and determine which constituents are held back when blood is filtered in the glomerulus.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose"},{"label":"B","text":"Water"},{"label":"C","text":"Blood cells and large plasma proteins"},{"label":"D","text":"Urea"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ultrafiltration holds back blood cells because they cannot squeeze through the three-layered filter. Most plasma proteins remain too, so the filtrate is protein-free apart from traces.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Pressure drives filtration","kind":"scene","content":"High blood pressure in the glomerular capillaries forces fluid through the vessel walls into the Bowman's capsule.","note":"This is ultrafiltration, not simple diffusion."},{"title":"Small molecules pass","kind":"text","content":"Water, glucose, salts, amino acids and urea are all small enough to sieve through.","note":"The filtrate resembles diluted blood plasma."},{"title":"The barrier rejects the bulky","kind":"text","content":"Blood cells are far too large, and most plasma proteins also stay behind.","note":"The filtrate must therefore contain no cells of any kind."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2022,23, $q$Osmoregulation, the careful balancing of body water and salts, is carried out mainly by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"kidneys"},{"label":"B","text":"lungs"},{"label":"C","text":"skin"},{"label":"D","text":"pancreas"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The nephrons trim the filtrate precisely, reclaiming the water the body needs. The lungs and skin may vent moisture, but neither regulates the body's salt budget.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2022,24, $q$Which description fits reflex actions?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Slow actions performed deliberately"},{"label":"B","text":"Actions under conscious control throughout"},{"label":"C","text":"Responses that depend on prior learning"},{"label":"D","text":"Swift, automatic responses not consciously directed"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A reflex travels a fixed inborn pathway and finishes before conscious thought could start. Blinking and the knee jerk typify this automatic speed.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2022,25, $q$Work out the first-generation offspring of a cross between a red-flowered four o'clock plant (RR) and a white-flowered one (rr), noting that this species shows incomplete dominance.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"all red"},{"label":"B","text":"all pink"},{"label":"C","text":"all white"},{"label":"D","text":"equal numbers of red and white"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$With incomplete dominance the single R dose of pigment turns every Rr offspring pink. Red or white appear only in the later generations of homozygotes.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Fix the gametes","kind":"text","content":"The RR plant yields only R gametes and the rr plant only r gametes.","note":"Each parent is homozygous, so no mixture arises."},{"title":"Combine in F1","kind":"text","content":"Every offspring is Rr, carrying half the colour dose of RR.","note":"Neither allele dominates the other here."},{"title":"Read the colour","kind":"text","content":"An intermediate pigment load gives pink flowers for all the F1 plants.","note":"Red would require complete dominance, which this species lacks."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2022,26, $q$A father of blood group O (oo) married a mother of group AB. What fraction of their children should belong to group A?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"none of them"},{"label":"B","text":"one quarter"},{"label":"C","text":"one half"},{"label":"D","text":"all of them"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The A allele can come only from the AB mother and o only from the father, so offspring are AO or BO. Split evenly, that predicts half group A and half group B.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"List the gametes","kind":"text","content":"The O father gives only o; the AB mother gives A and B in equal shares.","note":"The o allele cannot make A alone."},{"title":"Form the children","kind":"math","content":"Half the matings give A o (group A) and half give B o (group B).","note":"No child can be AB or O in this cross."},{"title":"State the fraction","kind":"text","content":"Exactly one half of the children are expected to be group A.","note":"Quarter fractions arise from heterozygote-by-heterozygote crosses, not this one."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2022,27, $q$Two genes sitting on the same chromosome fail to assort independently. Their joint behaviour is explained by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mutation"},{"label":"B","text":"linkage"},{"label":"C","text":"inbreeding"},{"label":"D","text":"outbreeding"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Linked genes ride the same chromosome and are parted only occasionally, by crossing over. Mutation changes genes outright, while inbreeding merely raises homozygosity.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2022,28, $q$The part of a germinating seed that grows upward into the shoot is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"radicle"},{"label":"B","text":"endosperm"},{"label":"C","text":"plumule"},{"label":"D","text":"seed coat"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The plumule is the embryonic shoot-tip destined to carry the first leaves. The radicle, its downward partner, builds the primary root instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2022,29, $q$After ovulation, progesterone is secreted mainly by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"corpus luteum"},{"label":"B","text":"Graafian follicle before it ruptures"},{"label":"C","text":"uterine wall itself"},{"label":"D","text":"oviduct"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The rupture point of the follicle heals into the corpus luteum, a temporary gland rich in progesterone. Its hormone keeps the uterine lining ready in case of pregnancy.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2022,30, $q$The amniotic fluid surrounding a mammalian embryo functions to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nourish the mother"},{"label":"B","text":"produce hormones for the embryo"},{"label":"C","text":"convey oxygen to the embryo"},{"label":"D","text":"cushion the embryo against shock"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The watery fluid acts as a shock absorber and keeps the embryo from drying out or sticking to its sac. Gases and nutrients travel by way of the placenta, not the amniotic fluid.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2022,31, $q$Grazing, burrowing and falling prey to foxes together describe the rabbit's place in its community, that is, its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"habitat"},{"label":"B","text":"niche"},{"label":"C","text":"biome"},{"label":"D","text":"territory"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The niche is the full functional role, covering food, habits and predators alike. Habitat is just the address, and a biome is a whole climatic region.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2022,32, $q$In a sandy desert, the environmental factor that most strongly limits plant growth is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"temperature"},{"label":"B","text":"availability of soil water"},{"label":"C","text":"wind speed"},{"label":"D","text":"oxygen concentration"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Desert plants suffer chiefly from drought, not from heat alone or lack of oxygen. Xerophytes survive by storing water and cutting losses through reduced leaves.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2022,33, $q$Which of the following natural resources is renewable?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Forest trees"},{"label":"B","text":"Crude oil under the ground"},{"label":"C","text":"Coal seams"},{"label":"D","text":"Natural gas reservoirs"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Trees regrow if felling is matched by planting, making forests renewable. Fossil fuels took ages to form and cannot be replaced on any human timescale.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2022,34, $q$Mosquito populations that survived early spraying with DDT grew steadily more resistant to it. The spread of this resistance is an example of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"artificial selection"},{"label":"B","text":"genetic drift"},{"label":"C","text":"co-evolution"},{"label":"D","text":"natural selection"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The insecticide killed the susceptible insects and left the naturally resistant ones to breed. Survivors multiplied, and the population shifted to resistance without any human choice involved.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2022,35, $q$The similar streamlined bodies of a fish and of a whale, built from quite different skeletons, are examples of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"analogous structures"},{"label":"B","text":"homologous structures"},{"label":"C","text":"vestigial organs"},{"label":"D","text":"identical genes"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Analogous structures solve the same problem from different origins and point to convergent evolution. Homologous structures share an origin regardless of function.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2022,36, $q$The gas chiefly responsible for acid rain when it dissolves in atmospheric moisture is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"methane"},{"label":"C","text":"sulphur dioxide"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Burning coal and oil releases sulphur dioxide, which turns into dilute sulphuric acid in the clouds. Nitrogen oxides contribute too, but sulphur dioxide is the classical culprit.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2023,10, $q$Besides the nucleus, which organelles of a eukaryotic cell contain their own DNA?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Lysosomes"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts"},{"label":"C","text":"Ribosomes"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi bodies"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Mitochondria and chloroplasts each hold a small circle of DNA and replicate independently. The arrangement recalls their capture of free-living bacteria early in evolution.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2023,11, $q$The actual assembly of amino acids into proteins happens on the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chloroplasts"},{"label":"B","text":"lysosomes"},{"label":"C","text":"ribosomes"},{"label":"D","text":"centrioles"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Messenger RNA threads through ribosomes, which stitch the amino acids together in the coded order. Chloroplasts capture light, lysosomes digest, and centrioles organise the spindle.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2023,12, $q$An adult grain weevil is able to hollow out a maize grain because its mouthparts are modified mainly for$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"boring"},{"label":"B","text":"sucking"},{"label":"C","text":"chewing"},{"label":"D","text":"lapping"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The long rostrum of the weevil bores a tunnel into the grain before the tissues are eaten. Sucking and lapping parts belong to butterflies and houseflies.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2023,13, $q$The mouthparts of the adult housefly are adapted for$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"boring into wood"},{"label":"B","text":"chewing solid leaves"},{"label":"C","text":"piercing skin and drawing blood"},{"label":"D","text":"sponging up liquid food"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The housefly lowers its fleshy proboscis on to moist surfaces and sponges the liquid up through fine channels. It cannot bite or pierce, so it must liquefy soluble food first.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2023,14, $q$Which passages branch from the trachea and carry air into the two lungs?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Oesophagus"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchi"},{"label":"C","text":"Larynx"},{"label":"D","text":"Alveoli"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The trachea forks into two bronchi, one entering each lung, which branch further into bronchioles. The oesophagus lies behind the windpipe and carries food.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2023,15, $q$Trace the route of the filtrate from the Bowman's capsule onwards and pick the correct sequence of nephron parts.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Loop of Henle -> Bowman's capsule -> proximal tubule -> distal tubule -> collecting duct"},{"label":"B","text":"Proximal tubule -> glomerulus -> loop of Henle -> collecting duct -> distal tubule"},{"label":"C","text":"Bowman's capsule -> proximal convoluted tubule -> loop of Henle -> distal convoluted tubule -> collecting duct"},{"label":"D","text":"Collecting duct -> loop of Henle -> Bowman's capsule -> proximal tubule -> distal tubule"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Filtrate leaves the capsule for the proximal tubule, tips down the loop of Henle and returns by the distal tubule to the collecting duct. Water and useful solutes are recovered along this whole line.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2023,16, $q$Follow the transmission of malaria from an infected girl to a new victim through the female Anopheles mosquito and decide how the parasite actually enters the fresh blood.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Through the saliva injected during the blood meal"},{"label":"B","text":"Through the mosquito's faeces smeared on the wound"},{"label":"C","text":"On the scales shed from its body"},{"label":"D","text":"Through eggs laid inside the vein"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The mosquito is only a carrier: its saliva, injected while it probes for blood, ferries the sporozoites into a new host. Faeces, scales and eggs play no part in transmission.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Draw the infective blood","kind":"scene","content":"The female Anopheles pierces the skin of an infected person and draws blood holding gametocytes of Plasmodium.","note":"Only the female feeds on blood, needing protein for eggs."},{"title":"Parasites develop inside the mosquito","kind":"text","content":"In about two weeks the gametocytes fertilise, multiply and travel to the salivary glands as sporozoites.","note":"The cycle speeds up in warm weather."},{"title":"Pass the parasite on","kind":"text","content":"When the mosquito bites the next person, sporozoites flow out with the saliva into the new wound.","note":"Bites are itchy, and scratching pushes saliva into tissues."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2023,17, $q$The pupal stage in the life cycle of the mosquito is commonly called the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"wriggler"},{"label":"B","text":"instar"},{"label":"C","text":"maggot"},{"label":"D","text":"tumbler"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The comma-shaped pupa darts through water by flipping movements, hence tumbler. Wriggler names the larva instead, and the maggot belongs to the fly's own earlier stages.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2023,18, $q$A food web is best described as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"many interlocking food chains in an ecosystem"},{"label":"B","text":"one straight pathway of energy flow"},{"label":"C","text":"a list of only producers and decomposers"},{"label":"D","text":"just one predator tied to one prey"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Because most animals eat several kinds of food, their chains criss-cross into a web. Such networks make the community hardier than any single chain could be.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2023,19, $q$In wind-pollinated flowers the stigma is typically$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sticky and buried inside the petals"},{"label":"B","text":"short and hidden in the flower"},{"label":"C","text":"feathery and well exposed to the moving air"},{"label":"D","text":"guarded deep inside a nectar cup"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A feathery stigma combs passing pollen grains straight from the breeze. No showy petals or scent are needed where no insect visits.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2023,20, $q$Which pair of plants relies chiefly on wind pollination?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Mango and pawpaw"},{"label":"B","text":"Maize and grasses"},{"label":"C","text":"Rose and hibiscus"},{"label":"D","text":"Pawpaw and tomato"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Maize tassels let loose clouds of light pollen that the feathery silks and grass stigmas trap. The showy insect-pollinated blooms listed offer nectar and scent.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2023,21, $q$Which of the following represents ex situ (off-site) conservation?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"National parks"},{"label":"B","text":"Forest reserves"},{"label":"C","text":"Game sanctuaries"},{"label":"D","text":"Gene banks and botanical gardens"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Gene banks and botanical gardens preserve plant stock away from its original habitat, hence ex situ. Parks and reserves guard whole ecosystems in place (in situ).$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2023,22, $q$In the food chain maize -> aphid -> ladybird -> spider, the spider occupies the role of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a tertiary consumer"},{"label":"B","text":"a primary consumer"},{"label":"C","text":"a secondary consumer"},{"label":"D","text":"a producer"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The spider stands fourth in line, feeding on the carnivorous ladybird, so it is a tertiary consumer. The aphid (herbivore) and ladybird (its eater) fill the lower links.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2023,23, $q$Free-living soil bacteria that can fix atmospheric nitrogen into the soil include the genus$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrobacter"},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrosomonas"},{"label":"C","text":"Azotobacter"},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudomonas"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Azotobacter (and Clostridium with it) fixes nitrogen without any plant partner. Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter merely oxidise nitrogen compounds, and Pseudomonas denitrifies them.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2023,24, $q$Work out why the pyramid of energy narrows sharply at every step of a food chain and justify the most important reason for the loss.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Consumers reproduce too rapidly"},{"label":"B","text":"Producers are always more numerous"},{"label":"C","text":"Energy multiplies on its way up the chain"},{"label":"D","text":"Most energy is spent in respiration and lost as heat at each level"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$At each trophic level the energy captured by producers is one-way traffic downhill. Respiration burns the better part of every meal, so barely a tenth is handed upward and the pyramid tapers.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Follow a meal through a herbivore","kind":"text","content":"A grasshopper burns most of the energy in its grass food for life processes and sheds it as heat.","note":"Breathing, movement and keeping warm all cost energy."},{"title":"Note the small surplus","kind":"text","content":"Only about one-tenth of the energy eaten goes into growth, and that is the part a predator can harvest.","note":"The ten-per-cent rule follows from this book-keeping."},{"title":"Watch it repeat","kind":"text","content":"The same loss recurs when the predator eats and again above it, so the pyramid must keep narrowing.","note":"Energy never flows back down the chain."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','genetics',2023,25, $q$Investigate how genetically modified organisms are actually produced and choose the description that fits.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"By grafting one plant on to the stock of another"},{"label":"B","text":"By inserting a chosen foreign gene into the organism's own genetic material"},{"label":"C","text":"By exposing seeds to strong sunlight"},{"label":"D","text":"By treating crops with growth hormones"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Genetic modification means joining a selected gene into the organism's own DNA, after which the new character is expressed. Grafting and hormone dressing never transfer genes.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Pick the useful gene","kind":"text","content":"A gene carrying the wanted character, for example pest resistance, is cut out of its source DNA.","note":"Restriction enzymes make the cuts."},{"title":"Insert it into the organism","kind":"text","content":"The gene is spliced into a carrier and delivered into the cells of the target organism.","note":"Bacteria serve both as carriers and as multiplying hosts."},{"title":"Confirm and grow on","kind":"text","content":"Cells that have accepted the gene are grown on and checked for the new character.","note":"Grafting and hormones never move genes this way."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2023,26, $q$Gene therapy works by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"replacing a faulty gene with a working copy"},{"label":"B","text":"transfusing whole blood into the patient"},{"label":"C","text":"irradiating the diseased tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"choosing the sex of the baby"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The treatment delivers a normal copy of the defective gene into the patient's cells. Since the fault itself is corrected at the gene level, the disorder may be permanently eased.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2023,27, $q$Insulin for the treatment of diabetes is nowadays obtained mainly from$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pancreas tissue of slaughtered pigs only"},{"label":"B","text":"cow pancreas extracts alone"},{"label":"C","text":"bacteria engineered to carry the human insulin gene"},{"label":"D","text":"a synthetic mixture of amino acids"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The human insulin gene is inserted into bacteria, which then manufacture the hormone in vast vats. The product is cheap, pure and identical to the natural protein.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2023,28, $q$For a monohybrid cross showing complete dominance, the phenotypic ratio in the second filial generation is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1"},{"label":"C","text":"1 : 2 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"3 : 1"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Selfing the F1 hybrids gives three dominant to one recessive phenotype. The 1:2:1 pattern is the genotypic ratio, and 9:3:3:1 belongs to a dihybrid cross.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2023,29, $q$In the sooty industrial towns of England the peppered moth population shifted from pale to dark forms over decades. The mechanism behind the change was chiefly$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"natural selection through differential predation"},{"label":"B","text":"genetic drift in small flocks"},{"label":"C","text":"deliberate breeding by man"},{"label":"D","text":"seasonal migration"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$On soot-blackened trees birds picked off the conspicuous pale moths, leaving the camouflaged dark ones to breed. Selection by predators drove the whole population shade by shade.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2023,30, $q$The coccyx at the base of the human vertebral column, a remnant of a tail, is an example of a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fossil structure"},{"label":"B","text":"homologous organ"},{"label":"C","text":"vestigial organ"},{"label":"D","text":"renewed organ"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A vestigial organ is shrunken and functionless in its present owner though useful in ancestors. The coccyx, the appendix, wisdom teeth and ear muscles all fall in this class.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2023,31, $q$At a movable joint, bone is bound to bone by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"tendons"},{"label":"B","text":"ligaments"},{"label":"C","text":"cartilage"},{"label":"D","text":"synovial membrane"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Ligaments are inelastic straps holding the two bones of a joint in place. Tendons instead anchor muscle to bone, and cartilage smooths the joint surfaces.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2023,32, $q$Double fertilisation in a flowering plant produces$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a single zygote only"},{"label":"B","text":"four haploid spores"},{"label":"C","text":"a zygote and an endosperm nucleus"},{"label":"D","text":"twin embryos"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$One male gamete fuses with the egg and the second with the two polar nuclei, starting the food-storing endosperm. No other kingdom of plants shows this twin fusion.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2023,33, $q$The hormone responsible for the development of the beard, deep voice and other male secondary sexual characters is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"testosterone"},{"label":"B","text":"oestrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"progesterone"},{"label":"D","text":"prolactin"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Testosterone is made by the interstitial cells of the testes at puberty. Oestrogen and progesterone belong to the female cycle, and prolactin drives milk secretion.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2023,34, $q$Which gland governs the basal metabolic rate of the body through its secretion?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenal cortex"},{"label":"C","text":"Pituitary"},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Thyroxine from the thyroid sets the general pace of chemical activity in the tissues. Over-secretion overruns the body, and under-secretion slows it into sluggishness.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2023,35, $q$The part of the brain mainly concerned with balance, posture and fine coordination of movement is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cerebrum"},{"label":"B","text":"cerebellum"},{"label":"C","text":"medulla oblongata"},{"label":"D","text":"hypothalamus"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The cerebellum smooths and steadies movements commanded from the cortex. Damage here makes walking unsteady even though strength and sensation persist.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2023,36, $q$The organ of Corti, which converts sound vibrations into nerve impulses, lies within the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cochlea"},{"label":"B","text":"semicircular canals"},{"label":"C","text":"eustachian tube"},{"label":"D","text":"tympanic membrane"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Waves in the fluid of the spiralled cochlea rock the hair cells of the organ of Corti. The semicircular canals handle balance, and the eustachian tube only equalises pressure.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2023,37, $q$The component of blood chiefly responsible for starting the clotting process is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"erythrocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"leucocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"platelets, acting with the plasma clotting factors"},{"label":"D","text":"lymph"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Platelets disintegrate at a wound and trigger the fibrin-forming cascade of plasma proteins. Erythrocytes carry oxygen and leucocytes defend against microbes.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2023,38, $q$Which nitrogenous base is present in RNA but absent from DNA?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Adenine"},{"label":"B","text":"Thymine"},{"label":"C","text":"Guanine"},{"label":"D","text":"Uracil"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$RNA swaps its thymine for uracil when pairing the coded message. Adenine, guanine and cytosine appear in both nucleic acids.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2024,10, $q$Which organelle has its cristae folded to enlarge its inner surface area for respiration?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondrion"},{"label":"B","text":"Chloroplast"},{"label":"C","text":"Golgi apparatus"},{"label":"D","text":"Lysosome"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The inner membrane of a mitochondrion is thrown into cristae, on which the respiratory enzymes stand. More folds mean more enzyme room and a stronger power output.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2024,11, $q$Mature sieve-tube elements, which conduct food in phloem, notably lack$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chloroplasts"},{"label":"B","text":"companion cells beside them"},{"label":"C","text":"cellulose walls"},{"label":"D","text":"true nuclei"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The nuclei of sieve tubes break down as the cells mature, leaving cytoplasm to stream freely. This emptiness suits the carrying of dissolved food.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2024,12, $q$In a white blood cell that has engulfed bacteria, digestion of the ingested microbes will be carried out by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mitochondrion"},{"label":"B","text":"chloroplast"},{"label":"C","text":"lysosome"},{"label":"D","text":"ribosome"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Lysosomes fuse their packet of hydrolytic enzymes with the engulfed particle and digest it. Ribosomes only build proteins, and chloroplasts are foreign to animal cells.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2024,13, $q$In bright morning light a green leaf is likely to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"take in oxygen faster than carbon dioxide"},{"label":"B","text":"absorb more carbon dioxide than it gives off"},{"label":"C","text":"release more carbon dioxide than oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"cease all exchange of gases"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$With photosynthesis running full tilt, the leaf consumes carbon dioxide and frees oxygen briskly. Net uptake of carbon dioxide therefore exceeds its output.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2024,14, $q$Salivary amylase (ptyalin) acts on$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cooked starch"},{"label":"B","text":"proteins"},{"label":"C","text":"fats"},{"label":"D","text":"raw cellulose"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Ptyalin hydrolyses cooked starch first to dextrins and ultimately to maltose. It pays no attention to protein, fat or the cellulose it cannot reach.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2024,15, $q$Which part of respiration happens in both aerobic and anaerobic conditions?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"The Krebs (citric acid) cycle"},{"label":"B","text":"The electron transport chain"},{"label":"C","text":"Direct oxidation of pyruvate within mitochondria"},{"label":"D","text":"Glycolysis in the cytoplasm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Glycolysis, the initial splitting of glucose to pyruvate, needs no oxygen at all. Everything after it differs for the two routes, so glycolysis is the shared stage.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Start the breakdown","kind":"text","content":"Glycolysis splits glucose into two pyruvate molecules without needing any oxygen.","note":"It happens in the cytoplasm of every cell."},{"title":"Split off at pyruvate","kind":"text","content":"Aerobic cells forward pyruvate into the mitochondria; oxygen-starved cells divert it to fermentation.","note":"Here the two routes part ways."},{"title":"Confirm the common ground","kind":"text","content":"Only glycolysis is shared by both routes, whatever happens afterwards.","note":"Krebs cycle and electron transport need oxygen."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','cellular-processes',2024,16, $q$The main structures through which gases enter and leave a green land plant are the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"lenticels only"},{"label":"B","text":"bark"},{"label":"C","text":"stomata"},{"label":"D","text":"cuticle"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Stomatal pores on the leaves admit carbon dioxide and let oxygen and vapour out. Lenticels help in woody stems, but the stomata carry the main traffic.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2024,17, $q$Most of the water absorbed with food in the human gut is reclaimed in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"colon"},{"label":"B","text":"oesophagus"},{"label":"C","text":"duodenum"},{"label":"D","text":"buccal cavity"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The colon compacts the residue by drawing off most of its water. Removal fails only in diarrhoea, when the contents pass through too quickly.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2024,18, $q$The longest bone in the human body is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"tibia"},{"label":"B","text":"humerus"},{"label":"C","text":"radius"},{"label":"D","text":"femur"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The femur, the thigh bone, is the single longest and heaviest bone of the skeleton. It carries the weight of the body between hip and knee.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2024,19, $q$Worn-out red blood cells are chiefly broken down and their pigment recycled in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"spleen"},{"label":"C","text":"kidney"},{"label":"D","text":"stomach"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The spleen's pulp filters out exhausted red cells and dismantles their haemoglobin. The liver assists, but the pancreas, kidney and stomach handle different work entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2024,20, $q$The human skull is built chiefly to give$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"protection to the brain"},{"label":"B","text":"support to the lower limbs"},{"label":"C","text":"attachment to the thigh muscles"},{"label":"D","text":"flexibility to the torso"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Skull bones shield the brain, one of the most delicate organs. Limb support and trunk flexibility belong to the girdle and spine instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2024,21, $q$Within the liver, urea is manufactured chiefly during the process of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"protein synthesis"},{"label":"B","text":"deamination of surplus amino acids"},{"label":"C","text":"glycogenesis"},{"label":"D","text":"lipolysis"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Deamination strips the amino groups off surplus amino acids, and the liver converts them into urea. The carbon skeletons left over serve as fuel or storage.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2024,22, $q$Some nerve fibres are wrapped in a fatty insulation that quickens the impulse. This is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"myelin sheath"},{"label":"B","text":"axon membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"dendrite"},{"label":"D","text":"synapse"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Myelin wraps the axon and lets the impulse leap from one exposed node to the next. Unmyelinated fibres conduct the same message far more slowly.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2024,23, $q$The autonomic (involuntary) nervous system controls the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chewing of food"},{"label":"B","text":"reading of a printed page"},{"label":"C","text":"wiggling of the toes"},{"label":"D","text":"heartbeat and the peristalsis of the gut"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Heart rate, gut movement, sweating and pupil size all run themselves without conscious orders. Chewing, reading and toe-wiggling are under voluntary direction.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2024,24, $q$Goitre, a swelling in the neck, results from a deficiency of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iron"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium"},{"label":"C","text":"iodine in the diet"},{"label":"D","text":"vitamin D"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Without iodine the thyroid cannot complete thyroxine and enlarges in a futile effort. Supplying the missing element from salt or diet brings the gland under control.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2024,25, $q$Two tall hybrid pea plants (Tt x Tt) are cross-pollinated. What fraction of their offspring should be dwarf?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"none at all"},{"label":"B","text":"one quarter"},{"label":"C","text":"one half"},{"label":"D","text":"three quarters"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Selfing two heterozygotes gives genotypes in the ratio 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt. Only the single tt class is dwarf, so one quarter of the offspring are expected to carry it.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Set out the parental gametes","kind":"text","content":"Each Tt parent makes T gametes and t gametes in equal numbers.","note":"Segregation hands one allele per gamete."},{"title":"Combine them","kind":"math","content":"The offspring split TT, Tt, Tt and tt in a 1 : 2 : 1 genotypic ratio.","note":"Only the tt class shows the recessive dwarf character."},{"title":"Convert to a fraction","kind":"text","content":"One of the four possible outcomes is tt, so one-quarter are dwarf.","note":"Three-quarters turn out tall, being either TT or Tt."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','genetics',2024,26, $q$Human blood group AB, in which both A and B antigens appear on the red cells, illustrates$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"complete dominance"},{"label":"B","text":"sex-linkage"},{"label":"C","text":"co-dominance of two alleles"},{"label":"D","text":"recessive inheritance"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Neither A nor B masks the other when both are inherited, so both show openly. This side-by-side expression is the classic case of co-dominance.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2024,27, $q$Each human gamete, whether a sperm or an ovum, carries how many chromosomes?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"23"},{"label":"B","text":"46"},{"label":"C","text":"22"},{"label":"D","text":"48"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Meiosis halves the human diploid number of 46 to 23 per gamete. Fusion at fertilisation restores the full 46 in the zygote.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2024,28, $q$The human testes are carried outside the body cavity in the scrotum because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"that reduces their weight on the body"},{"label":"B","text":"infection is avoided there"},{"label":"C","text":"urination is helped"},{"label":"D","text":"sperm production needs a temperature slightly below that of the body core"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Normal sperm formation halts at the internal temperature of about 37 degrees C, so the scrotum hangs the testes in cooler air. A testis left undescended within the warmth usually stays sterile.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2024,29, $q$Menstruation is triggered when$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fertilisation fails and the uterine lining is shed"},{"label":"B","text":"two ova are released at once"},{"label":"C","text":"sperm reach the ovary itself"},{"label":"D","text":"progesterone level stays permanently high"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Without a fertilised egg the corpus luteum fades, progesterone falls and the built-up lining breaks away. Implantation of a zygote would hold the lining in place instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2024,30, $q$The association between nitrogen-fixing bacteria of the genus Rhizobium and leguminous plants is an example of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"parasitism"},{"label":"B","text":"commensalism"},{"label":"C","text":"mutualism"},{"label":"D","text":"competition"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Root nodules shelter the bacteria with sugars while they supply the plant with fixed nitrogen. Both sides profit, which is the definition of mutualism.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2024,31, $q$Broad thin leaves with generous air spaces (aerenchyma) belong to plants described as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"xerophytes"},{"label":"B","text":"halophytes"},{"label":"C","text":"mesophytes of dry ridges"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrophytes"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Hydrophytes live in water and so need no waxy armour; their tissues hold floating air spaces instead. Xerophytes, lean against drought, do exactly the opposite.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2024,32, $q$Organisms that feed at more than one trophic level within the same food web include the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"omnivores"},{"label":"B","text":"strict producers"},{"label":"C","text":"strict herbivores"},{"label":"D","text":"top carnivores only"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Omnivores eat both plant and animal food and so sit at several levels at once. Strict herbivores stay at level two, and pure producers never leave level one.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2024,33, $q$Assess the share of the decomposers in the carbon cycle and pick the accurate statement.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"They trap carbon dioxide into sugars for the community"},{"label":"B","text":"They break down dead organic matter and return carbon dioxide to the atmosphere"},{"label":"C","text":"They lock carbon permanently inside shells and wood"},{"label":"D","text":"They turn carbon dioxide straight into nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$By consuming the dead, decomposers respire their carbon back into the air as carbon dioxide. Photosynthesis, not decay, is what re-binds the gas into sugars.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Set the decomposers on the remains","kind":"scene","content":"Bacteria and fungi colonise fallen leaves and carcasses and digest their organic matter.","note":"This releases the energy stored in the dead tissue."},{"title":"Follow the carbon","kind":"text","content":"Respiration by these microbes releases the carbon back as carbon dioxide.","note":"Green plants alone can re-fix the gas into sugar."},{"title":"Rule out the wrong tracks","kind":"text","content":"Fixing carbon into sugars is photosynthesis, not decay, and burial in shells takes ages of geology.","note":"Carbon dioxide cannot become nitrogen by any biological act."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','evolution',2024,34, $q$Darwin's studies of the Galapagos finches, which carry beaks of different shapes on different islands, illustrate$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"convergence of unrelated birds"},{"label":"B","text":"one constant beak shape"},{"label":"C","text":"adaptive radiation from one stock"},{"label":"D","text":"regression to a single type"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$One ancestral finch stock spread to the islands and branched into seed-crackers, insect-probers and cactus-specialists. Filling varied niches in this way is adaptive radiation.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2024,35, $q$Homologous structures found in different animal groups supply evidence of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"adaptation to identical surroundings"},{"label":"B","text":"descent from a common ancestor"},{"label":"C","text":"coincidental similarity called convergence"},{"label":"D","text":"use and disuse of the organs"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The same bone layout argues a shared ancestor that both lines inherited it from. Convergence explains analogous, not homologous, resemblance.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2024,36, $q$Depletion of the ozone layer high in the atmosphere is caused mainly by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon dioxide from engines"},{"label":"B","text":"methane from paddy fields"},{"label":"C","text":"water vapour"},{"label":"D","text":"chlorofluorocarbons from old refrigerants and sprays"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Once aloft, chlorofluorocarbons release chlorine atoms that split ozone molecules. Carbon dioxide warms the planet, but CFCs are the ozone destroyers.$q$,
 'single', null, 1);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('biology','cell-structure',2025,10, $q$In a eukaryotic cell the hereditary material is chiefly housed in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cytoplasm"},{"label":"B","text":"nucleus"},{"label":"C","text":"ribosome"},{"label":"D","text":"cell wall"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Chromosomes with their DNA lie within the nuclear envelope. Small amounts of DNA also sit in mitochondria and chloroplasts, but the nucleus is the main archive.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cell-structure',2025,11, $q$ATP, the energy currency spent by cell activities, is recovered mainly in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ribosomes"},{"label":"B","text":"vacuoles"},{"label":"C","text":"mitochondria"},{"label":"D","text":"nucleoli"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Aerobic respiration, which regenerates ATP in bulk, takes place inside mitochondria. Ribosomes build protein, and the nucleolus builds ribosomes in turn.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2025,12, $q$A spray of perfume released in one corner of a room soon reaches the far corner by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"diffusion"},{"label":"B","text":"osmosis"},{"label":"C","text":"transpiration"},{"label":"D","text":"condensation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The scented molecules drift from the crowded corner to emptier air until they spread evenly. Diffusion, unlike osmosis, needs no membrane at all.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2025,13, $q$The biological agent that speeds up a biochemical reaction in the body without being used up is called a$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"substrate"},{"label":"B","text":"enzyme"},{"label":"C","text":"product"},{"label":"D","text":"coenzyme"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Enzymes are protein catalysts that lower the energy barrier of a reaction. The substrate is what is changed, and the product is the outcome.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','cellular-processes',2025,14, $q$Although chlorophyll strongly absorbs red and blue-violet light, leaves look green because the pigment$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"absorbs red light only"},{"label":"B","text":"reflects green light to the eye"},{"label":"C","text":"stores blue light for later"},{"label":"D","text":"emits fresh green light"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Chlorophyll reflects, rather than absorbs, much of the green band. The reflected light reaching the eye paints the leaf green.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2025,15, $q$The excretory organ that rids the blood of urea, excess salts and surplus water is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"heart"},{"label":"B","text":"liver"},{"label":"C","text":"kidney"},{"label":"D","text":"lungs"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Nephrons in the kidney filter the blood and fine-tune what is retained or voided as urine. The liver makes the urea but does not excrete it, and the lungs vent only carbon dioxide.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2025,16, $q$The straw-coloured fluid part of blood that carries dissolved nutrients and wastes is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"plasma"},{"label":"B","text":"haemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"lymph"},{"label":"D","text":"serum"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Plasma is over nine-tenths water holding salts, glucose, proteins and urea in solution. Serum is plasma minus its clotting protein, and lymph is the related tissue fluid.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2025,17, $q$Oxygenated blood arriving from the lungs enters which chamber of the heart?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Right atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"Right ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"Left ventricle"},{"label":"D","text":"Left atrium"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The pulmonary veins discharge their blood into the left atrium first. It then drops into the left ventricle for the drive round the body.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2025,18, $q$The final digestion of fats in man, down to fatty acids and glycerol, is accomplished in the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"small intestine"},{"label":"B","text":"stomach"},{"label":"C","text":"mouth"},{"label":"D","text":"large intestine"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Bile emulsifies the fats and pancreatic lipase completes their splitting within the small intestine. No enzyme upstream reaches fats in any useful amount.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2025,19, $q$The eustachian tube runs from the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"outer ear to the brain"},{"label":"B","text":"middle ear to the pharynx"},{"label":"C","text":"inner ear to the larynx"},{"label":"D","text":"larynx to the trachea"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$This tube connects the middle-ear cavity with the throat to equalise pressure on both sides of the drum. Yawning opens it, which explains why the ears clear then.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','systems',2025,20, $q$Trace the first steps of a Plasmodium infection after the sporozoites enter with the mosquito's bite and state where the parasite first settles and multiplies.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"The red blood cells"},{"label":"B","text":"The brain tissue"},{"label":"C","text":"The salivary glands of the person"},{"label":"D","text":"The cells of the liver"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The injected sporozoites head straight for the liver cells, where they multiply for about a week before breaking into the blood. The red-cell stage, and hence the fever, comes later.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Arrival in the blood","kind":"scene","content":"Sporozoites injected with the mosquito's saliva ride the bloodstream within minutes.","note":"They spend little time free in the blood."},{"title":"Landing in the liver","kind":"text","content":"Within an hour the parasites enter liver cells and multiply repeatedly for about a week.","note":"No fever shows during this quiet phase."},{"title":"Reinvasion of the blood","kind":"text","content":"The released parasites then flood out to invade red blood cells, and the fever cycles begin.","note":"That is when diagnosis by a blood smear succeeds."}]$st$,
 2),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2025,21, $q$The waste product which the lungs excrete daily in greatest quantity is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon dioxide"},{"label":"B","text":"urea"},{"label":"C","text":"uric acid"},{"label":"D","text":"excess salts"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Respiration builds up carbon dioxide continuously and the blood ferries it to the lungs. Urea, uric acid and salts are taken out by the kidneys and the skin instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2025,22, $q$Sensory (afferent) neurones transmit impulses from the sense organs towards$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the muscles"},{"label":"B","text":"the glands"},{"label":"C","text":"the central nervous system"},{"label":"D","text":"the effector organs"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Afferent fibres carry information inwards to the brain and spinal cord for processing. The onward journey to muscles and glands is handled by motor neurones.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','excretion-nerve',2025,23, $q$During a frightening emergency, which gland pours adrenaline into the bloodstream?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid gland"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenal gland"},{"label":"C","text":"Pituitary gland"},{"label":"D","text":"Thymus"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The adrenal glands sit one on each kidney and release adrenaline (epinephrine) at moments of stress. The hormone quickens the heart and sharpens breathing for action.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2025,24, $q$The outermost whorl of a typical complete flower, made of sepals, is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"corolla"},{"label":"B","text":"androecium"},{"label":"C","text":"gynoecium"},{"label":"D","text":"calyx"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Working from the outside inward, the whorls run calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium. The sepals of the calyx protect the young unopened bud.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2025,25, $q$Showy petals, scent and nectar in flowers serve mainly to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"attract the pollinating agents"},{"label":"B","text":"shield the ripening seeds"},{"label":"C","text":"absorb water for the ovary"},{"label":"D","text":"store food for the developing ovule"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Colours, perfumes and nectar rewards draw insects, birds and bats that carry pollen between plants. Insect-pollinated flowers spend much of their budget on these advertisements.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','reproduction',2025,26, $q$The incubation period of the eggs of the domestic fowl, from laying to hatching, is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"7 days"},{"label":"B","text":"14 days"},{"label":"C","text":"21 days"},{"label":"D","text":"30 days"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A brooding hen or an incubator needs three weeks of steady warmth to hatch a chick. Ducks take about a month, and canaries come out at a fortnight.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','genetics',2025,27, $q$A cross is made between two round-seeded heterozygous pea plants (Rr x Rr). Work through the possible genotypes of their second-generation offspring and give the genotypic ratio.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9 : 3 : 3 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 2 : 1"},{"label":"C","text":"3 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"1 : 1"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Combining R and r gametes from both parents gives RR, Rr, Rr and rr. That is a genotypic ratio of 1 : 2 : 1, though the visible ratio of characters stays 3 : 1.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"List the gametes","kind":"text","content":"Each Rr parent forms R and r gametes in equal numbers.","note":"Segregation is the rule for every gene pair."},{"title":"Fill the combinations","kind":"math","content":"The Punnett square yields RR, Rr, Rr and rr - a 1 : 2 : 1 spread of genotypes.","note":"Phenotypes collapse into 3 round to 1 wrinkled."},{"title":"Name the ratio asked for","kind":"text","content":"Genotypes, not phenotypes, are requested here, so the answer is 1 : 2 : 1.","note":"Confusing the two ratios is the commonest slip."}]$st$,
 3),
('biology','genetics',2025,28, $q$Down syndrome in humans results from$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an extra copy of chromosome 21"},{"label":"B","text":"a missing chromosome 21"},{"label":"C","text":"a point mutation in the haemoglobin gene"},{"label":"D","text":"the loss of the Y chromosome"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Trisomy 21, three instead of two copies of chromosome 21, produces the condition. The fault is a chromosomal error, not a single-gene change.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','evolution',2025,29, $q$The sickle cell allele survives at high frequency in populations where malaria is endemic because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"people affected with sickle cell anaemia reproduce fastest"},{"label":"B","text":"the allele is carried on the Y chromosome"},{"label":"C","text":"homozygous normal individuals resist malaria best"},{"label":"D","text":"heterozygous (AS) individuals resist malaria better than normal homozygotes"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In malarial districts the AS carrier gains a selective edge, catching less severe infection than AA people. This balanced advantage keeps what is a fatal allele in homozygotes from disappearing.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2025,30, $q$The producers of most open-water (pelagic) food chains are the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"phytoplankton"},{"label":"B","text":"zooplankton"},{"label":"C","text":"fish fry"},{"label":"D","text":"water fleas of the shallows"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Floating microscopic algae photosynthesise near the surface and feed the whole oceanic chain. Zooplankton grazing them are consumers, not producers.$q$,
 'single', null, 1),
('biology','ecology',2025,31, $q$When the insecticide DDT enters a lake food chain, its concentration becomes greatest in the body of the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"producers (algae)"},{"label":"B","text":"top carnivores, such as the fish-eating birds"},{"label":"C","text":"decomposing bacteria"},{"label":"D","text":"pond snails and other herbivores"}]$o$,
 'B',
