 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2008,36, $q$In a right-angled triangle, the side opposite the 30° angle is 6 cm. Find the length of the hypotenuse.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6√3 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"12 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"9 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"3√3 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The 30-60-90 triangle has sides in the ratio 1 : √3 : 2, so the hypotenuse is twice the side opposite 30°: 12 cm.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2008,37, $q$An isosceles triangle has a vertex angle of 40°. Find each base angle.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50°"},{"label":"B","text":"40°"},{"label":"C","text":"80°"},{"label":"D","text":"70°"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The two base angles are equal and share the remaining 180° − 40° = 140°, so each is 70°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
 ('mathematics','calculus',2008,38, $q$A particle moves along a line so that its displacement after t seconds is s = t² + 3t metres. Find its velocity at t = 2 s.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"7 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"9 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"3 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Velocity is v = ds/dt = 2t + 3, so at t = 2 s the velocity is 4 + 3 = 7 m/s (and the constant acceleration is 2 m/s²).$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Velocity is the derivative","kind":"math","content":"v = ds/dt = 2t + 3","note":"Differentiate displacement with respect to time"},{"title":"Substitute t = 2","kind":"math","content":"v = 2(2) + 3 = 7 m/s","note":"Starting velocity (t = 0) is 3 m/s"},{"title":"Note the acceleration","kind":"math","content":"dv/dt = 2 m/s², constant","note":"Acceleration is the derivative of velocity"}]$st$, 3);


