('mathematics','quadratics',2009,30, $q$If (x + 1) is a factor of x³ + kx² + 2x + 4, find k.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"-1"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$By the factor theorem, P(-1) = 0. Substituting gives k + 1 = 0, so k = -1.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Apply the factor theorem","kind":"text","content":"For (x + 1) to be a factor of P(x) = x³ + kx² + 2x + 4, we need P(-1) = 0.","note":"The root of the factor sets the substitution value."},{"title":"Substitute x = -1","kind":"math","content":"P(-1) = -1 + k - 2 + 4 = k + 1","note":"Collect the constants carefully: -1 - 2 + 4 = +1."},{"title":"Solve for k","kind":"math","content":"k + 1 = 0, so k = -1","note":"A positive answer here signals a sign slip in the substitution."}]$st$,
 3),
