('mathematics','indices-logs',2012,37, $q$Solve 3^(2x) - 12(3^x) + 27 = 0.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"x = 1 or x = 2"},{"label":"B","text":"x = 3 or x = 9"},{"label":"C","text":"x = -1 or x = -2"},{"label":"D","text":"x = 1 only"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$With u = 3^x the equation factors as (u - 3)(u - 9) = 0, giving x = 1 and x = 2. The values 3 and 9 are the roots in u, not in x.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Substitute u = 3^x","kind":"text","content":"With u = 3^x, we have 3^(2x) = (3^x)² = u², so the equation becomes u² - 12u + 27 = 0.","note":"This turns the exponential into an ordinary quadratic."},{"title":"Factorise","kind":"math","content":"u² - 12u + 27 = (u - 3)(u - 9) = 0, so u = 3 or u = 9","note":"Both roots are positive, so both matter for 3^x."},{"title":"Undo the substitution","kind":"math","content":"3^x = 3 gives x = 1, and 3^x = 9 = 3² gives x = 2","note":"Reporting u values (3, 9) as the answer is the classic finish error."}]$st$,
 3),
