('mathematics','calculus',2013,35, $q$Evaluate ∫₀³ (x² + 1) dx.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"15"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The antiderivative is x³/3 + x, which gives 9 + 3 = 12 at x = 3. Forgetting the +1 term gives 9.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Antidifferentiate","kind":"math","content":"∫ (x² + 1) dx = x³/3 + x + C","note":"Constant 1 integrates to x, not to 1."},{"title":"Substitute the upper limit","kind":"math","content":"At x = 3: 27/3 + 3 = 9 + 3 = 12","note":"Compute the cube term and the x term separately before adding."},{"title":"Subtract the lower limit","kind":"math","content":"At x = 0: 0 + 0 = 0, so the integral is 12","note":"Omitting the constant's integral is what produces the 9 distractor."}]$st$,
 3),
('mathematics','quadratics',2013,36, $q$Find the values of p for which x² + px + 16 = 0 has equal roots.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8 only"},{"label":"B","text":"-8 only"},{"label":"C","text":"+8 and -8"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Equal roots require p² - 64 = 0, so p = ±8. Both symmetric values work because only p² appears in the discriminant.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2013,37, $q$Express x² + 8x + 3 in the form (x + p)² + q.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"(x + 4)² - 13"},{"label":"B","text":"(x + 4)² + 13"},{"label":"C","text":"(x - 4)² - 13"},{"label":"D","text":"(x + 8)² - 13"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Completing the square gives (x + 4)² - 13 since 3 - 16 = -13. Halving the wrong coefficient gives (x + 8)².$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Take half the coefficient of x","kind":"text","content":"8 ÷ 2 = 4, so the squared part is (x + 4)² with a plus sign.","note":"The sign inside matches the sign of the x coefficient."},{"title":"Expand to see what must be adjusted","kind":"math","content":"(x + 4)² = x² + 8x + 16","note":"Compared with the given expression, 16 must come down to 3."},{"title":"Adjust the constant","kind":"math","content":"x² + 8x + 3 = (x + 4)² - 13","note":"3 - 16 = -13, hence the minus before the 13."}]$st$,
 3),
('mathematics','geometry',2013,38, $q$A circle has radius 25 cm and a chord 40 cm long. How far is the chord from the centre?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"15 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"10 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The chord bisected gives a half-chord of 20 cm, so d² = 25² - 20² = 225 and d = 15 cm. Here 15 and 20 have swapped roles, and 5 is just 25 - 20.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

