 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2014,26, $q$Of 200 students, 120 offer Economics and 100 offer Geography. If 40 offer neither subject, how many offer both?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60"},{"label":"B","text":"40"},{"label":"C","text":"80"},{"label":"D","text":"20"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Students offering at least one subject: 200 - 40 = 160. Then both = 120 + 100 - 160 = 60.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Trim the universe","kind":"math","content":"Students offering at least one subject = 200 - 40 = 160","note":"The 'neither' students belong to neither circle of the Venn diagram."},{"title":"State the inclusion rule","kind":"math","content":"n(E ∪ G) = n(E) + n(G) - n(E ∩ G)","note":"Adding the two subject totals counts the overlap twice."},{"title":"Solve the overlap","kind":"math","content":"160 = 220 - n(E ∩ G), so n(E ∩ G) = 60","note":"Sanity check: 120 - 60 = 60 take only Economics, 100 - 60 = 40 take only Geography, and 60 + 40 + 60 + 40 = 200."}]$st$,
 3),
('mathematics','geometry',2014,27, $q$Each interior angle of a regular polygon is 108°. How many sides has the polygon?$q$,
