('mathematics','progressions',2018,18, $q$An arithmetic progression begins 18, 15.5, 13, ... and ends at −7. How many terms are in the progression?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"13"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The common difference is −2.5. Solving 18 + (n − 1)(−2.5) = −7 gives n − 1 = 10, so there are 11 terms.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Find the common difference","kind":"math","content":"d = 15.5 − 18 = −2.5","note":"Each term drops by 2.5."},{"title":"Set up the nth term","kind":"text","content":"Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d with a = 18, and the last term equals −7.","note":"Count how many steps of −2.5 fit."},{"title":"Solve for n","kind":"math","content":"18 + (n − 1)(−2.5) = −7 → (n − 1)(−2.5) = −25 → n − 1 = 10 → n = 11","note":"Dividing −25 by −2.5 gives 10."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2018,19, $q$The second term of a geometric progression is 4 and the sum to infinity is 16. Find the first term.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$ar = 4 and a/(1 − r) = 16, so a = 16(1 − r). Then 16r(1 − r) = 4, giving (2r − 1)² = 0, r = ½ and a = 8.$q$,
