('mathematics','progressions',2021,18, $q$The sum of three numbers in geometric progression is 21 and their product is 216. Find the largest number.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"15"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$For terms a, ar, ar², the product is (ar)³ = 216, so ar = 6. The sum condition gives a(1 + r + r²) = 21, leading to 2r² − 5r + 2 = 0, so r = 2 or ½. The terms are 3, 6, 12, and the largest is 12.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Use the product","kind":"math","content":"a × ar × ar² = a³r³ = (ar)³ = 216, so the middle term is ar = ∛216 = 6","note":"For a GP in order, the product is the cube of the middle term."},{"title":"Set up the sum","kind":"math","content":"a(1 + r + r²) = 21 with a = 6/r gives 6(1 + r + r²)/r = 21 → 2r² − 5r + 2 = 0","note":"Divide through by 3r after clearing fractions."},{"title":"Solve for the ratio","kind":"math","content":"(2r − 1)(r − 2) = 0, so r = 2 or ½; the terms are 3, 6, 12 either way","note":"The largest number is 12."}]$st$, 3),
('mathematics','progressions',2021,19, $q$The sum to infinity of a geometric progression is 27 and the common ratio is 2/3. Find the first term.$q$,
