 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2022,17, $q$The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic progression is Sₙ = 3n² + n. Find the common difference.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The nth term is Sₙ − Sₙ₋₁ = (3n² + n) − (3(n − 1)² + (n − 1)) = 6n − 2, so the difference between consecutive terms is 6.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2022,18, $q₦15,700 invested at r% per annum amounts to ₦16,956 after one year, compounded annually. Find the value of r.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"9"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The interest for one year is ₦16,956 − ₦15,700 = ₦1,256. Since rate = interest/principal × 100, r = 1256/15700 × 100 = 8.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2022,19, $q$The sixth term of a geometric progression is 96 and the common ratio is 2. Find the second term.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12"},{"label":"B","text":"24"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Since T₆ = ar⁵ = 96 and 2⁵ = 32, the first term is a = 3. The second term is T₂ = ar = 6.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Relate the sixth term to the first","kind":"math","content":"T₆ = a r⁵ = 96 with r = 2, so a = 96/32 = 3","note":"2⁵ = 32."},{"title":"Find the second term","kind":"math","content":"T₂ = a r = 3 × 2 = 6","note":"Multiply the first term by the common ratio."},{"title":"Verify the progression","kind":"text","content":"The GP begins 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96, which confirms the given sixth term.","note":"Consistency check against the question data."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2022,20, $q$Find the range of values of x for which x² − 2x − 15 < 0.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"x < −3 or x > 5"},{"label":"B","text":"−3 < x < 5"},{"label":"C","text":"−5 < x < 3"},{"label":"D","text":"x > 5"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Factorize: (x − 5)(x + 3) < 0. The product is negative between the roots, so −3 < x < 5.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2022,21, $q$Solve the equation x² + 7x + 12 = 0.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"x = −3 or 4"},{"label":"B","text":"x = 3 or −4"},{"label":"C","text":"x = −3 or −4"},{"label":"D","text":"x = 3 or 4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The numbers with sum 7 and product 12 are 3 and 4, so (x + 3)(x + 4) = 0. Hence x = −3 or −4.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2022,22, $q$A farmer has 36 m of wire fence to enclose a rectangular pen. What is the largest area that can be enclosed?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"72 m²"},{"label":"B","text":"81 m²"},{"label":"C","text":"90 m²"},{"label":"D","text":"100 m²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The perimeter condition 2(x + y) = 36 gives y = 18 − x, so the area is A = x(18 − x). Maximizing: dA/dx = 18 − 2x = 0 gives x = 9, since a square maximizes area. The maximum area is 9 × 9 = 81 m².$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Express the fence condition","kind":"text","content":"For sides x and y in metres: 2(x + y) = 36, so y = 18 − x.","note":"The wire surrounds all four sides."},{"title":"Write the area function","kind":"math","content":"A = x(18 − x) = 18x − x²","note":"Area equals length times width."},{"title":"Maximize the area","kind":"math","content":"dA/dx = 18 − 2x = 0 gives x = 9, and A = 9 × 9 = 81 m²","note":"The rectangle of maximum area is a square."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2022,23, $q$If sin θ = −3/5 for 270° < θ < 360°, find cos θ.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"−4/5"},{"label":"B","text":"4/5"},{"label":"C","text":"3/5"},{"label":"D","text":"−3/4"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ = 1 − 9/25 = 16/25, so cos θ = ±4/5. In the fourth quadrant cosine is positive, so cos θ = 4/5.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2022,24, $q$If tan θ = 1 for 0° ≤ θ ≤ 180°, find θ.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"45°"},{"label":"B","text":"135°"},{"label":"C","text":"90°"},{"label":"D","text":"30°"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The tangent is positive only in the first and third quadrants, and the third is outside the range. Hence θ = 45°, since tan 135° = −1.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2022,25, $q$The angle of elevation of the top of a tree from a point 12 m away on level ground is 45°. Find the height of the tree.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6 m"},{"label":"B","text":"12√2 m"},{"label":"C","text":"12 m"},{"label":"D","text":"24 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Since tan 45° = 1, the height equals the horizontal distance: h = 12 × tan 45° = 12 m.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2022,26, $q$Find dy/dx if y = cos 2x.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"−2 sin 2x"},{"label":"B","text":"2 sin 2x"},{"label":"C","text":"−sin 2x"},{"label":"D","text":"cos 2x/2"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$By the chain rule, dy/dx = −sin 2x × 2 = −2 sin 2x. The inner derivative of 2x is 2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2022,27, $q$Find the gradient of the curve y = 2x³ − 3x² + x at the point where x = 1.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"1"},{"label":"D","text":"−1"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$dy/dx = 6x² − 6x + 1. At x = 1 this is 6 − 6 + 1 = 1, so the gradient is 1.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2022,28, $q$The revenue from selling x units of a product is R(x) = 20x − x² (in thousands of naira). Find the value of x that maximizes the revenue.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5"},{"label":"B","text":"15"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"20"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Setting dR/dx = 20 − 2x = 0 gives x = 10. The second derivative is −2, confirming a maximum, with revenue 100 thousand naira.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2022,29, $q$Evaluate ∫₀^(π/2) (cos x − sin x) dx.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"0"},{"label":"D","text":"−1"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$An antiderivative is sin x + cos x. At π/2 it is 1 + 0 = 1 and at 0 it is 0 + 1 = 1, so the integral is 1 − 1 = 0.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2022,30, $q$The mean of the five numbers 3, 5, x, 9 and 11 is 8. Find the value of x.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"14"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The total must be 5 × 8 = 40, and 3 + 5 + 9 + 11 = 28. Hence x = 40 − 28 = 12.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2022,31, $q$If the variance of a set of data is 16, find its standard deviation.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"256"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The standard deviation is the square root of the variance: √16 = 4. Data are typically less dispersed than the variance figure suggests.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2022,32, $q$The ages of 20 teachers are grouped: 25–29 years (2 teachers), 30–34 years (6 teachers), 35–39 years (8 teachers) and 40–44 years (4 teachers). Using the class midpoints, find the mean age.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"35.0"},{"label":"B","text":"35.5"},{"label":"C","text":"36.0"},{"label":"D","text":"34.5"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The midpoints are 27, 32, 37 and 42. The weighted total is 27(2) + 32(6) + 37(8) + 42(4) = 710, so the mean is 710/20 = 35.5 years.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"List the midpoints","kind":"text","content":"The midpoints of the classes are 27, 32, 37 and 42 years.","note":"Halfway values stand in for each class."},{"title":"Total the weighted ages","kind":"math","content":"27×2 + 32×6 + 37×8 + 42×4 = 54 + 192 + 296 + 168 = 710","note":"Multiply each midpoint by its frequency."},{"title":"Divide by the number of teachers","kind":"math","content":"Mean age = 710/20 = 35.5 years","note":"Divide by the total frequency of 20."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2022,33, $q$Find the distance between the points A(−2, 3) and B(4, −5).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 units"},{"label":"B","text":"8 units"},{"label":"C","text":"14 units"},{"label":"D","text":"12 units"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Apply d = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²] = √[6² + (−8)²] = √(36 + 64) = 10 units.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Measure the horizontal and vertical changes","kind":"text","content":"From A(−2, 3) to B(4, −5): the x-change is 4 − (−2) = 6 and the y-change is −5 − 3 = −8.","note":"The two changes form the legs of a right triangle."},{"title":"Apply the distance formula","kind":"math","content":"AB = √(6² + (−8)²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10","note":"This is Pythagoras applied to the coordinate grid."},{"title":"State the result","kind":"text","content":"The distance between A and B is exactly 10 units.","note":"A 6-8-10 right triangle, a multiple of 3-4-5."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2022,34, $q$Find the area of the triangle whose vertices are (1, 1), (6, 1) and (4, 5).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8 square units"},{"label":"B","text":"20 square units"},{"label":"C","text":"10 square units"},{"label":"D","text":"12 square units"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The side from (1, 1) to (6, 1) is horizontal with length 5, and the third vertex is 4 units above it vertically. The area is ½ × 5 × 4 = 10.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2022,35, $q$From a point P, 13 cm from the centre O of a circle of radius 5 cm, tangents are drawn to the circle. Find the length of each tangent.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"12 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"13 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"8 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The tangent, the radius and OP form a right angle at the contact point: PT = √(13² − 5²) = √144 = 12 cm. This is the 5-12-13 triangle.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2022,36, $q$A trapezium has parallel sides of 8 cm and 12 cm and the perpendicular distance between them is 6 cm. Find its area.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"54 cm²"},{"label":"B","text":"66 cm²"},{"label":"C","text":"72 cm²"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm²"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Area = ½ (sum of parallel sides) × height = ½(8 + 12) × 6 = 60 cm².$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

-- Past Graph generator: JAMB UTME 2003-2008 multiple-choice insertion script
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','kinematics',2003,10, $q$A body starts from rest and moves with a uniform acceleration of 2 m/s². What distance does it cover in the 6th second of its motion?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"11 m"},{"label":"B","text":"12 m"},{"label":"C","text":"22 m"},{"label":"D","text":"36 m"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The distance covered in the nth second is u + (a/2)(2n - 1), which here is (2/2)(2×6 - 1) = 11 m. A common trap is choosing 36 m, which is the total distance covered in 6 seconds, not the sixth second alone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2003,11, $q$Two forces of magnitudes 5 N and 12 N act on a body at right angles to each other. The magnitude of their resultant is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"7 N"},{"label":"B","text":"13 N"},{"label":"C","text":"17 N"},{"label":"D","text":"60 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$For perpendicular forces the resultant is the square root of (5² + 12²) = the square root of 169 = 13 N. Adding the magnitudes to get 7 N and multiplying them to get 60 N are the classic traps for a vector quantity.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2003,12, $q$A boy of mass 50 kg runs up a staircase and climbs a vertical height of 4 m in 8 s. Taking g = 10 m/s², his average power output is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"250 W"},{"label":"B","text":"25 W"},{"label":"C","text":"160 W"},{"label":"D","text":"4000 W"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The work done against gravity is mgh = 50 × 10 × 4 = 2000 J, and dividing by the 8 s taken gives 250 W. Option D forgets to divide the work by the time.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2003,13, $q$A vernier calipers has a least count of 0.01 cm. Its main scale reads 2.4 cm while the 6th vernier division coincides with a main scale graduation. The length measured is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.06 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"2.46 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"3.00 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"2.40 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The vernier contribution is 6 × 0.01 = 0.06 cm, so the reading is 2.4 + 0.06 = 2.46 cm. Options that ignore either the coincidence or the least count miss the vernier scale entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2003,14, $q$A sound wave of frequency 500 Hz has a wavelength of 0.66 m in air. The speed of the wave is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"330 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"165 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"500 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"760 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Wave speed is frequency multiplied by wavelength: 500 × 0.66 = 330 m/s. Option C confuses the frequency of the source with the speed of the wave it produces.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2003,15, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 12 V drives a current of 2 A round a circuit and its terminal p.d. is 11 V. The internal resistance of the battery is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 ohm"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 ohm"},{"label":"C","text":"5.5 ohm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 ohm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The lost volt inside the cell is E - V = 12 - 11 = 1 V, so r = lost volt / current = 1/2 = 0.5 ohm. The 5.5-ohm option comes from wrongly dividing the terminal p.d. by the current.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2003,16, $q$An object is placed 30 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 20 cm. The distance of the image from the mirror is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"60 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"120 cm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$From 1/f = 1/u + 1/v, 1/v = 1/20 - 1/30 = 1/60, so the real inverted image lies 60 cm from the mirror. Its magnification is 2, but the distance, not the size, is asked.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2003,17, $q$The count rate of a radioactive source falls from 160 counts per minute to 20 counts per minute in 12 days. The half-life of the source is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 days"},{"label":"B","text":"3 days"},{"label":"C","text":"6 days"},{"label":"D","text":"4 days"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The count rate falls by a factor of 8, and 8 = 2³ corresponds to three half-lives. Half-life = 12/3 = 4 days; dividing 12 by 8 instead of 3 gives the wrong 1.5-day scale.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2003,18, $q$The sleeve of a micrometer screw gauge reads 4.5 mm and the 28th division on the 0.01 mm thimble coincides with the reference line. The diameter of the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.28 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"4.53 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"4.78 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"7.30 mm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A micrometer reading is the sleeve reading plus the thimble contribution, taken division by division.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Record the sleeve reading","kind":"text","content":"The last graduation visible on the sleeve before the thimble edge is 4.5 mm, so the sleeve contributes 4.5 mm.","note":"Read the main scale only up to the edge of the thimble, never beyond it."},{"title":"Convert the thimble divisions","kind":"math","content":"Each thimble division is 0.01 mm, so 28 divisions add 28 × 0.01 = 0.28 mm.","note":"One full turn of 100 divisions advances the spindle by exactly 1 mm."},{"title":"Add the two readings","kind":"math","content":"Diameter = 4.5 + 0.28 = 4.78 mm.","note":"Option D treats 28 divisions as 2.8 mm instead of 0.28 mm."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','kinematics',2003,19, $q$A car travels 40 km due east and then 30 km due north. The magnitude of its resultant displacement is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 km"},{"label":"B","text":"35 km"},{"label":"C","text":"50 km"},{"label":"D","text":"70 km"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The two legs are perpendicular, so the displacement is the square root of (40² + 30²) = 50 km. Option D is the algebraic sum of the distances, which treats a vector like a scalar.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2003,20, $q$A uniform metre rule is pivoted at its centre and a 2 N weight hangs from the 20 cm mark. At which mark must a 3 N weight be hung to balance the rule?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the 30 cm mark"},{"label":"B","text":"the 70 cm mark"},{"label":"C","text":"the 80 cm mark"},{"label":"D","text":"the 60 cm mark"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Balancing moments about the centre: 2 × 30 = 3 × d, so d = 20 cm on the side opposite the load, which is the 70 cm mark. Mark 30 cm lies on the same side as the 2 N weight and cannot restore balance.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2003,21, $q$What is the kinetic energy of a 4 kg body moving with a speed of 5 m/s?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20 J"},{"label":"B","text":"100 J"},{"label":"C","text":"10 J"},{"label":"D","text":"50 J"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$K.E. = (1/2)mv² = (1/2) × 4 × 25 = 50 J. Option B omits the factor of one half and doubles the answer.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2003,22, $q$How much heat is required to melt 0.2 kg of ice at 0 °C? (Specific latent heat of fusion of ice = 3.3 × 10⁵ J/kg)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.6 × 10³ J"},{"label":"B","text":"1.65 × 10⁴ J"},{"label":"C","text":"3.3 × 10⁴ J"},{"label":"D","text":"6.6 × 10⁴ J"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Q = ml = 0.2 × 3.3 × 10⁵ = 6.6 × 10⁴ J. Melting at 0 °C involves no temperature change, so no specific heat term enters the calculation.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2003,23, $q$The resistance of a uniform wire is 8 ohms. If the wire is stretched uniformly to twice its original length, its new resistance is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4 ohms"},{"label":"B","text":"32 ohms"},{"label":"C","text":"16 ohms"},{"label":"D","text":"2 ohms"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Stretching to double the length halves the cross-sectional area, and R is proportional to L/A, so the resistance rises four times to 32 ohms. The volume of the wire is unchanged during stretching.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2003,24, $q$In a resonance tube experiment, successive resonances occur at air column lengths of 15.0 cm and 45.0 cm. The wavelength of the sound in the tube is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"15 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"30 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"45 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The difference between successive resonant lengths is half a wavelength, so 30.0 cm = λ/2 and λ = 60 cm. Option B wrongly treats the 30 cm separation as the full wavelength.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2003,25, $q$A transformer with 600 primary turns is connected to a 240 V supply. How many turns must the secondary coil have to deliver 12 V?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 turns"},{"label":"B","text":"12 turns"},{"label":"C","text":"72 turns"},{"label":"D","text":"30 turns"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Ns = Np × Vs/Vp = 600 × 12/240 = 30 turns, since the turns ratio equals the voltage ratio. A step-down transformer must have fewer secondary turns than primary turns, so options above 30 cannot fit.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2003,26, $q$The refractive index of glass is 1.5. The critical angle for the glass-air boundary is approximately$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"30°"},{"label":"B","text":"48°"},{"label":"C","text":"42°"},{"label":"D","text":"60°"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At the critical angle sin C = 1/n = 1/1.5 = 0.67, which corresponds to about 42°. Option B arises from reading the sine table for 0.5 or using the complement of the angle.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2003,27, $q$A ball is projected with a speed of 20 m/s at 30° above the horizontal. Taking g = 10 m/s², the maximum height reached is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 m"},{"label":"B","text":"10 m"},{"label":"C","text":"17 m"},{"label":"D","text":"20 m"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The vertical component is 20 sin 30° = 10 m/s, and the maximum height is its square divided by 2g: 100/20 = 5 m. Option C uses the whole speed as if the ball were projected vertically.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2003,28, $q$Light of energy 3.0 eV falls on a metal whose work function is 2.0 eV. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 eV"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$From Einstein's photoelectric equation, K.E.max = photon energy - work function = 3.0 - 2.0 = 1.0 eV. Option D wrongly adds the two energies.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2003,29, $q$During electrolysis a steady current of 2 A flows for 500 s. If the electrochemical equivalent of the deposited substance is 3.3 × 10⁻⁷ kg/C, the mass deposited is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.3 × 10⁻⁵ kg"},{"label":"B","text":"3.3 × 10⁻⁴ kg"},{"label":"C","text":"1.65 × 10⁻⁴ kg"},{"label":"D","text":"6.6 × 10⁻⁴ kg"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$From Faraday's law m = ZIt = 3.3 × 10⁻⁷ × 2 × 500 = 3.3 × 10⁻⁴ kg. The charge passed is It = 1000 C, and option C omits the 2 A factor from that charge.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2003,30, $q$A 2 kg trolley moving at 3 m/s collides with and sticks to a stationary 1 kg trolley. Their common speed after impact is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Momentum is conserved: 2 × 3 = (2 + 1)v, so v = 2 m/s. Kinetic energy is not conserved in this inelastic collision, so no energy equation applies here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2003,31, $q$A fixed mass of gas is heated at constant volume from 27 °C to 87 °C. If its initial pressure is 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, its new pressure is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.45 × 10⁵ Pa"},{"label":"B","text":"1.74 × 10⁵ Pa"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 × 10⁵ Pa"},{"label":"D","text":"1.2 × 10⁵ Pa"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Pressure increases in the ratio of absolute temperatures: P2 = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 360/300 = 1.2 × 10⁵ Pa. Forgetting to convert to kelvin and using 87/27 directly gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2003,32, $q$The period of a simple pendulum of length 0.9 m is approximately (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.9 s"},{"label":"B","text":"0.6 s"},{"label":"C","text":"3.8 s"},{"label":"D","text":"5.7 s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$T = 2 × 3.14 × the square root of (0.9/10) = 2 × 3.14 × 0.3, which is about 1.9 s. Option C doubles the period, confusing it with a single swing between the two extreme positions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2003,33, $q$A man drags a box 4 m along the floor with a force of 50 N directed at 60° above the horizontal. The work done by this force on the box is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50 J"},{"label":"B","text":"100 J"},{"label":"C","text":"173 J"},{"label":"D","text":"200 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Work uses the force component along the displacement: here the horizontal component of the 50 N pull acts through 4 m.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Resolve the pull into components","kind":"math","content":"Horizontal component = 50 cos 60° = 50 × 0.5 = 25 N; the vertical component is 50 sin 60° which is about 43 N.","note":"The box moves horizontally, so only the horizontal component contributes to work."},{"title":"Apply the work formula","kind":"math","content":"W = force along the motion × distance = 25 × 4 = 100 J.","note":"Option D multiplies the whole 50 N force by 4 m and ignores the 60° angle."},{"title":"Check against the distractors","kind":"text","content":"173 J pairs the 4 m displacement with the 43 N vertical component, and 50 J uses only half of the horizontal component.","note":"A quick check: the work done must lie between 0 and 200 J, the maximum possible force × distance product."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','magnetism',2003,34, $q$A wire of length 0.4 m carrying a current of 2 A lies at right angles to a magnetic field of flux density 0.5 T. The force on the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 N"},{"label":"B","text":"0.4 N"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 N"},{"label":"D","text":"0.25 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$F = BIL = 0.5 × 2 × 0.4 = 0.4 N when the wire and the field are perpendicular. Option D looks like a rearrangement but belongs to a formula for force on a charge, not a current element.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2003,35, $q$An object is placed 15 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. The distance of the real image from the lens is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"6 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"25 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"30 cm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/10 - 1/15 = 1/30, so v = 30 cm and the image is real and inverted. The magnification happens to be 2, but the question asks for the image distance.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2003,36, $q$Two 6-ohm resistors are connected in parallel and the combination is joined in series with a 3-ohm resistor across a 12 V battery of negligible internal resistance. The current drawn from the battery is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.8 A"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 A"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The parallel pair has an equivalent resistance of 3 ohms, so the total is 3 + 3 = 6 ohms and I = 12/6 = 2 A. Option B divides by the parallel pair alone and option D treats the whole circuit as if it were 12 ohms.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2003,37, $q$A block of wood of density 800 kg/m³ and volume 200 cm³ floats in water of density 1000 kg/m³. Taking g = 10 m/s², the upthrust on the block is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.0 N"},{"label":"B","text":"1.6 N"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 N"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$For a floating body in equilibrium, the upthrust equals the block's weight, found from its mass and the density of the wood.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Find the mass of the block","kind":"math","content":"m = density × volume = 800 × 200 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.16 kg.","note":"1 cm³ = 10⁻⁶ m³, so 200 cm³ is 2 × 10⁻⁴ m³."},{"title":"Find the weight","kind":"math","content":"Weight = mg = 0.16 × 10 = 1.6 N.","note":"This is the load the water must balance."},{"title":"Apply the floating condition","kind":"text","content":"A floating body is in equilibrium, so upthrust = weight = 1.6 N regardless of how deeply it sits.","note":"2.0 N is the upthrust if fully submerged; only 80% of the block is below the surface, matching the ratio 800/1000."}]$st$,
 3);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2004,10, $q$A vernier calipers with a least count of 0.01 cm has a positive zero error of 0.02 cm. For a rod the main scale reads 3.7 cm and the 4th vernier division coincides. The true length of the rod is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.70 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"3.72 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"3.74 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"3.76 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The observed reading is 3.7 + 4 × 0.01 = 3.74 cm, and a positive zero error reads high, so it must be subtracted: 3.74 - 0.02 = 3.72 cm. Option D adds the zero error with the wrong sign.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2004,11, $q$A stone is released from rest at a height of 80 m above level ground. Taking g = 10 m/s², the time it takes to reach the ground is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4 s"},{"label":"B","text":"2 s"},{"label":"C","text":"8 s"},{"label":"D","text":"16 s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$From h = (1/2)gt², t = the square root of (2 × 80/10) = the square root of 16 = 4 s. Option B drops the factor of 2 from the formula.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2004,12, $q$A resultant horizontal force of 20 N acts on a 5 kg block resting on a frictionless floor. The acceleration of the block is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.4 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"4 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"25 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"100 m/s²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$a = F/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s². Option C multiplies force and mass instead of dividing.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2004,13, $q$A sound wave travelling at 340 m/s has a frequency of 680 Hz. Its wavelength is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 m"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 m"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 m"},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 m"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$λ = v/f = 340/680 = 0.5 m. Multiplying instead of dividing gives a value far too long for an audible wave.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2004,14, $q$A capacitor of capacitance 4 microfarads is charged to a p.d. of 50 V. The charge stored is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 × 10⁻⁴ C"},{"label":"B","text":"2 × 10⁻² C"},{"label":"C","text":"8 × 10⁻⁵ C"},{"label":"D","text":"1.25 × 10⁻³ C"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Q = CV = 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 50 = 2 × 10⁻⁴ C. Convert microfarads to farads before multiplying; skipping that step produces option B.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2004,15, $q$A machine has a velocity ratio of 5 and an efficiency of 80%. Its mechanical advantage is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25"},{"label":"B","text":"1.6"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"20"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Efficiency = MA/VR, so MA = 0.8 × 5 = 4. Option B divides instead of multiplying, and a real machine must have MA less than its VR.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2004,16, $q$A converging lens throws a real image, the same size as the object, onto a screen. If the object stands 40 cm from the lens, the focal length of the lens is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"40 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"80 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A same-size real image has magnification 1, which requires u = v = 2f, so f = 40/2 = 20 cm. Option C mistakes the object distance for the radius scale of the lens.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2004,17, $q$In a photoelectric experiment, light of photon energy 6.0 eV liberates electrons with maximum kinetic energy 2.5 eV. The work function of the metal is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"6.0 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"8.5 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"3.5 eV"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The photon energy splits into the escape energy plus the kinetic energy: work function = 6.0 - 2.5 = 3.5 eV. Option C adds the energies and would mean the electrons owed energy rather than gaining it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2004,18, $q$A micrometer screw gauge reads 3.0 mm on the sleeve with the 25th thimble division (each 0.01 mm) on the reference line. The instrument has a positive zero error of 0.03 mm. The true diameter of the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.19 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"3.22 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"3.25 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"3.28 mm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The observed reading and the zero correction must be combined with the correct signs before trusting the result.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Read the observed diameter","kind":"math","content":"Observed reading = 3.0 + 25 × 0.01 = 3.25 mm.","note":"Each thimble division is 0.01 mm, so 25 divisions contribute 0.25 mm."},{"title":"Apply the zero correction","kind":"text","content":"The jaws touch with the scale already at +0.03 mm, so every reading is too large by 0.03 mm: true = 3.25 - 0.03 = 3.22 mm.","note":"A positive zero error is subtracted; option D adds it instead."},{"title":"Sense-check the result","kind":"text","content":"The corrected value must be slightly smaller than the observed reading, which rules out 3.28 mm.","note":"3.19 mm would correspond to a zero error of 0.06 mm, double the stated value."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','kinematics',2004,19, $q$An arrow is projected at 20 m/s at an angle of 45° above level ground. Taking g = 10 m/s², its horizontal range is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20 m"},{"label":"B","text":"30 m"},{"label":"C","text":"40 m"},{"label":"D","text":"80 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At 45° the range formula gives R = u² sin(2 × 45°)/g = 400 × 1/10 = 40 m, the largest range for this launch speed. Option D doubles the range by mixing up the angle-doubling inside the formula.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2004,20, $q$A resultant force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg body initially at rest for 3 s. The velocity acquired by the body is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"15 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"30 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"5 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"10 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The impulse 10 × 3 = 30 N s changes the momentum by 30 kg m/s, so v = 30/2 = 15 m/s. Option C is the acceleration, filled into the wrong slot.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2004,21, $q$A cell of e.m.f. 2.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm delivers a current of 1.5 A. The terminal potential difference of the cell is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.75 V"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 V"},{"label":"C","text":"2.75 V"},{"label":"D","text":"1.25 V"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The lost volt inside the cell is Ir = 1.5 × 0.5 = 0.75 V, so the terminal p.d. = 2.0 - 0.75 = 1.25 V. Option C adds the lost volt to the e.m.f., which would ignore the internal resistance altogether.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2004,22, $q$A gas occupies 300 cm³ at 27 °C. When heated to 87 °C at constant pressure, its new volume is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"347 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"354 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"360 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"375 cm³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Volumes compare as absolute temperatures: V2 = 300 × 360/300 = 360 cm³. Option A adds the 60 °C rise directly in celsius and mixes the two scales.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2004,23, $q$A 0.5 kg mass hangs from a spring of stiffness 50 N/m and is pulled down slightly and released. The period of the resulting simple harmonic motion is approximately (g = 10 m/s²)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.63 s"},{"label":"B","text":"0.32 s"},{"label":"C","text":"1.26 s"},{"label":"D","text":"6.3 s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$T = 2 × 3.14 × the square root of (m/k) = 2 × 3.14 × the square root of (0.5/50) = 2 × 3.14 × 0.1, about 0.63 s. Option C doubles the period, and option D is ten times too large, forgetting the mass in kilograms.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2004,24, $q$A straight conductor 0.5 m long moves at 4 m/s at right angles to a uniform magnetic field of 0.2 T. The e.m.f. induced across the ends of the conductor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 V"},{"label":"B","text":"1.6 V"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 V"},{"label":"D","text":"0.4 V"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$E = Blv = 0.2 × 0.5 × 4 = 0.4 V, the rate at which field lines are cut. If the conductor moved along the field, the induced e.m.f. would be zero.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2004,25, $q$A vernier scale has 10 divisions coinciding in length with 9 divisions of a main scale whose smallest division is 1 mm. The least count of the instrument is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.9 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"0.1 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"1.1 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.01 mm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$One vernier division spans 0.9 mm of main scale, so the least count is 1.0 - 0.9 = 0.1 mm. The least count is the difference between the two divisions, not their ratio.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2004,26, $q$A body moves at a steady 10 m/s for 5 s and then slows uniformly to rest in the next 5 s. Its total distance travelled is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50 m"},{"label":"B","text":"25 m"},{"label":"C","text":"75 m"},{"label":"D","text":"100 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The area under the velocity-time graph is 10 × 5 for the steady part plus (1/2 × 10 × 5) for the slowing part: 50 + 25 = 75 m. Option A counts only the steady part, as if the car stopped instantly.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2004,27, $q$A spring of stiffness 200 N/m is compressed 0.1 m from its natural length and held. The elastic energy stored in the spring is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 J"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 J"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 J"},{"label":"D","text":"20 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$E = (1/2)kx² = (1/2) × 200 × 0.01 = 1.0 J. Option D squares the displacement but forgets the one-half factor.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2004,28, $q$A pin placed 15 cm in front of a concave mirror produces an image twice as tall as the pin. The image of the pin is formed$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"7.5 cm behind the mirror"},{"label":"B","text":"30 cm in front of the mirror"},{"label":"C","text":"45 cm in front of the mirror"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm behind the mirror"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Magnification = image distance / object distance, so v = 2 × 15 = 30 cm, a real inverted image in front of the mirror. Option A inverts the magnification ratio.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2004,29, $q$A radioactive source has a half-life of 8 days. The fraction of its original nuclei remaining after 24 days is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1/2"},{"label":"B","text":"1/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/6"},{"label":"D","text":"1/8"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Twenty-four days span 24/8 = 3 half-lives, leaving (1/2)³ = 1/8 of the nuclei. Decay is exponential, so subtracting elapsed time from a fixed rate, as in option C, misleads.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2004,30, $q$Three resistors of 2 ohms, 3 ohms and 6 ohms are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance of the combination is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 ohm"},{"label":"B","text":"0.5 ohm"},{"label":"C","text":"5.5 ohms"},{"label":"D","text":"11 ohms"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$1/R = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 1, so R = 1 ohm, always smaller than the smallest branch. Option D is the plain series sum and ignores the parallel rule.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2004,31, $q$A 1000 kg car travelling at 20 m/s is brought uniformly to rest by its brakes in 4 s. The braking force is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2000 N"},{"label":"B","text":"4000 N"},{"label":"C","text":"5000 N"},{"label":"D","text":"50000 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Compute the deceleration first, then convert it to a force through the mass of the car.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Find the deceleration","kind":"math","content":"a = change of speed ÷ time = 20 ÷ 4 = 5 m/s².","note":"The final speed is zero because the car comes to rest."},{"title":"Apply Newton's second law","kind":"math","content":"Braking force F = ma = 1000 × 5 = 5000 N.","note":"Option D treats the numbers as energy values; the units must stay in newtons here."},{"title":"Confirm with the stopping distance","kind":"math","content":"Distance = average speed × time = (20 + 0)/2 × 4 = 40 m, consistent with a = 5 m/s².","note":"Using final speed × time = 80 m assumes uniform motion, which a braking car does not have."}]$st$,
 3),
('physics','waves-sound',2004,32, $q$Two tuning forks of frequencies 256 Hz and 260 Hz are sounded together. The beat frequency heard is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"4 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"258 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"516 Hz"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The beat frequency is the difference of the two frequencies: 260 - 256 = 4 Hz. Option C mixes the two notes instead of taking their difference.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2004,33, $q$How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 2 kg of water from 20 °C to 70 °C? (Specific heat capacity of water = 4200 J/kg K)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.2 × 10⁴ J"},{"label":"B","text":"1.68 × 10⁵ J"},{"label":"C","text":"4.2 × 10⁵ J"},{"label":"D","text":"8.4 × 10⁵ J"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Q = mcΔT = 2 × 4200 × 50 = 4.2 × 10⁵ J. Option B substitutes the initial temperature instead of the 50 °C rise.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2004,34, $q$The magnetic force on a current-carrying conductor in a uniform magnetic field is greatest when the conductor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"parallel to the field"},{"label":"B","text":"at 45° to the field"},{"label":"C","text":"at right angles to the field"},{"label":"D","text":"antiparallel to the field"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$F = BIL sin(angle), and the sine reaches its maximum of 1 at 90°. For parallel or antiparallel alignment the sine is zero and the force vanishes entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2004,35, $q$An electric heater rated 2000 W is used for 30 minutes. The energy it consumes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 kWh"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 kWh"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 kWh"},{"label":"D","text":"60 kWh"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Thirty minutes is 0.5 h, so energy = 2.0 kW × 0.5 h = 1.0 kWh. Option D multiplies by minutes instead of hours.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2004,36, $q$The speed of light in glass is 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s and in vacuum it is 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. The refractive index of the glass is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.67"},{"label":"B","text":"3.0"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0"},{"label":"D","text":"1.5"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Refractive index = speed in vacuum / speed in medium = 3.0/2.0 = 1.5, always greater than 1 for a material. Option A inverts the ratio and would imply light speeding up in glass.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2004,37, $q$A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m/s in 10 s. The distance covered in this time is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"100 m"},{"label":"B","text":"200 m"},{"label":"C","text":"50 m"},{"label":"D","text":"400 m"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Average velocity = (0 + 20)/2 = 10 m/s, so distance = 10 × 10 = 100 m. Option B multiplies the final speed by the time, valid only for uniform motion.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2004,38, $q$An electric heater rated 2000 W operates in water boiling steadily at 100 °C. The mass of water it vaporizes in 5 minutes is (specific latent heat of vaporization = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg)$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.27 kg"},{"label":"B","text":"2.7 kg"},{"label":"C","text":"0.13 kg"},{"label":"D","text":"0.53 kg"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Energy supplied divided by the specific latent heat gives the mass boiled off.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Compute the heat supplied","kind":"math","content":"Energy = power × time = 2000 × 300 = 6 × 10⁵ J.","note":"5 minutes must become 300 s before substituting."},{"title":"Divide by the latent heat","kind":"math","content":"m = E/l = 6 × 10⁵ ÷ 2.26 × 10⁶, about 0.27 kg.","note":"Option C keeps the time in minutes and loses a factor of 60."},{"title":"Sense-check the answer","kind":"text","content":"Boiling off roughly a large cup of water in five minutes on a 2 kW heater is reasonable.","note":"Option B is ten times too large; that would require about 10 kW of power."}]$st$,
 2);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','kinematics',2005,10, $q$A cyclist travelling at 12 m/s applies the brakes and slows uniformly at 3 m/s². The distance she travels before stopping is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12 m"},{"label":"B","text":"18 m"},{"label":"C","text":"24 m"},{"label":"D","text":"36 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Using v² = u² - 2as with v = 0 gives 0 = 144 - 6s, so s = 24 m. Braking distance grows as the square of speed, so no small fraction of the speed can give a small stopping distance here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2005,11, $q$A crate of weight 200 N is dragged across a floor at constant velocity by a horizontal force of 60 N. The coefficient of friction between the crate and the floor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.3"},{"label":"B","text":"0.03"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0"},{"label":"D","text":"1.2"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$At constant velocity the friction exactly balances the pull, so the coefficient = 60/200 = 0.3. Constant velocity means zero resultant force, not zero friction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2005,12, $q$A pump raises 200 kg of water through a vertical height of 15 m in 20 s. Taking g = 10 m/s², its useful power output is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 kW"},{"label":"B","text":"0.75 kW"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 kW"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 kW"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Useful work per second = mg × h ÷ t = 200 × 10 × 15 ÷ 20 = 1500 W = 1.5 kW. Power is work per unit time, so the 20 s must be divided out.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2005,13, $q$Resistors of 4 ohms and 12 ohms are connected in parallel across a battery of negligible internal resistance supplying 8 V. The current in the 4-ohm resistor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 A"},{"label":"B","text":"3.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 A"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In parallel each branch sees the full 8 V, so the 4-ohm branch carries 8/4 = 2 A. Dividing the current equally between the branches is wrong because their resistances differ.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2005,14, $q$A vernier calipers of least count 0.01 cm has a negative zero error of 0.03 cm. For a cylindrical rod the main scale reads 5.2 cm and the 7th vernier mark coincides. The true diameter of the rod is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.24 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"5.27 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"5.30 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"5.33 cm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The observed reading is 5.2 + 7 × 0.01 = 5.27 cm; a negative zero error reads low by 0.03 cm when the jaws close, so it is added back: 5.27 + 0.03 = 5.30 cm. Option B forgets the correction entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2005,15, $q$A string fixed at both ends and 0.5 m long vibrates in its fundamental mode at 200 Hz. The speed of transverse waves on the string is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"200 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"100 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"400 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"50 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$In the fundamental mode the string holds half a wavelength, so λ = 2 × 0.5 = 1.0 m and v = fλ = 200 m/s. Halving the speed, as option D does, would fit a string of quite different length.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2005,16, $q$An object is placed 36 cm from a concave mirror of focal length 12 cm. The magnification of the image formed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0"},{"label":"B","text":"0.5"},{"label":"C","text":"1.0"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/12 - 1/36 = 1/18, so v = 18 cm and m = v/u = 18/36 = 0.5. A magnification of 2 would require the object inside the focal length instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2005,17, $q$In the Rutherford alpha-particle scattering experiment most of the alpha particles passed straight through the thin gold foil. This showed that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"atoms are indivisible spheres"},{"label":"B","text":"electrons are embedded in a sphere of positive charge"},{"label":"C","text":"electrons revolve in fixed orbits"},{"label":"D","text":"the atom is mostly empty space with a small dense positive nucleus"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Nearly all alpha particles passed straight through because the atom is overwhelmingly empty, with a tiny massive positive nucleus at its centre. A uniformly filled atom would have deflected almost every alpha particle.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2005,18, $q$A micrometer screw gauge has a least count of 0.01 mm and a zero error of +0.02 mm. Measuring a wire, its sleeve reads 2.0 mm and the 45th thimble division coincides. The true diameter of the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.38 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"2.43 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"2.45 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"2.47 mm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The observed reading and the zero correction combine with opposite signs when the error is positive.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Read the combined scale","kind":"math","content":"Observed reading = 2.0 + 45 × 0.01 = 2.45 mm.","note":"The 0.01 mm thimble supplies the fraction of a millimetre."},{"title":"Correct for the zero error","kind":"text","content":"The jaws touch with the scale already at +0.02 mm, so every reading is high by 0.02 mm: true = 2.45 - 0.02 = 2.43 mm.","note":"Positive zero errors always come off the observed reading."},{"title":"Verify against the alternatives","kind":"text","content":"The true value must sit slightly below 2.45 mm, which rules out every option that adds the error or ignores the thimble.","note":"2.47 mm corresponds to adding the zero error, and 2.45 mm keeps it uncorrected."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','kinematics',2005,19, $q$A boat that can move at 4 m/s in still water points directly across a river flowing at 3 m/s. The resultant speed of the boat observed from the bank is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"7 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"12 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The 4 m/s heading and the 3 m/s current act along perpendicular directions, so the resultant is the square root of (16 + 9) = 5 m/s. Velocities add only as vectors; a direct sum forgets their directions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2005,20, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 24 V and internal resistance 1.0 ohm drives a current of 4 A. The resistance of the external circuit is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3.0 ohms"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 ohms"},{"label":"C","text":"5.0 ohms"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 ohms"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$From E = I(R + r), 24 = 4 × (R + 1), so R = 5 ohms. The internal drop is Ir = 4 V, leaving 20 V across the external resistance: 20/4 also gives 5 ohms.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2005,21, $q$A mass of gas at 27 °C and pressure 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa occupies 3.0 × 10⁻³ m³. If it is compressed to 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³ and heated to 127 °C, its new pressure is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 × 10⁵ Pa"},{"label":"B","text":"2.7 × 10⁵ Pa"},{"label":"C","text":"1.8 × 10⁵ Pa"},{"label":"D","text":"8.0 × 10⁵ Pa"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The general gas law uses absolute temperatures: P2 = P1 × (V1/V2) × (T2/T1) = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 3 × 400/300 = 8.0 × 10⁵ Pa. Compression to one third alone would have tripled the pressure, and the heating multiplies it once more.$q$,
 'single', null, 3),
('physics','work-energy',2005,22, $q$A 0.5 kg ball is thrown vertically upward and rises 8 m above the point of projection. Taking g = 10 m/s², the kinetic energy given to the ball at throw is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8 J"},{"label":"B","text":"20 J"},{"label":"C","text":"40 J"},{"label":"D","text":"80 J"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At the highest point the kinetic energy has all become potential energy mgh = 0.5 × 10 × 8 = 40 J, so that was its launch value. With air resistance neglected, nothing else consumes energy on the way up.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2005,23, $q$A wire of length 0.5 m carrying a current of 3 A lies at right angles to a magnetic field of flux density 0.4 T. The force on the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.6 N"},{"label":"B","text":"0.24 N"},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 N"},{"label":"D","text":"4.2 N"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$F = BIL = 0.4 × 0.5 × 3 = 0.6 N for a right-angled wire. Option A pairs the force with only two of the three factors.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2005,24, $q$When a beam of monochromatic light passes from air into glass, the quantity that remains unchanged is its$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"speed"},{"label":"B","text":"wavelength"},{"label":"C","text":"direction"},{"label":"D","text":"frequency"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The source fixes the frequency, and it survives the change of medium unchanged, while speed and wavelength both fall in glass. Option A reverses the truth: it is the speed that alters.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2005,25, $q$Of the following radiations, the one with the shortest wavelength is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ultraviolet"},{"label":"B","text":"gamma rays"},{"label":"C","text":"infrared"},{"label":"D","text":"radio waves"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Gamma rays carry the shortest wavelengths and the highest photon energies of the listed radiations. Radio waves sit at the opposite, longest-wavelength end of the spectrum.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2005,26, $q$In a stationary wave on a stretched string the distance between two adjacent nodes is 5 cm. The wavelength of the wave is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"10.0 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"20.0 cm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Adjacent nodes are half a wavelength apart, so λ = 2 × 5 = 10 cm. Option B mistakes the node spacing itself for the full wavelength.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2005,27, $q$A passenger falls forward when a moving bus is suddenly braked. This happens because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the seat pushes the passenger forward"},{"label":"B","text":"the passenger's body continues in uniform motion while the bus slows"},{"label":"C","text":"the engine pulls the passenger forward"},{"label":"D","text":"gravity suddenly acts forward"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The passenger's body keeps its velocity while the bus loses speed below it, so the upper body tips forward. No forward force is required to keep a body moving; inertia does all the work.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2005,28, $q$A capacitor of capacitance 2 microfarads is charged to a potential difference of 100 V. The energy stored in it is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.01 J"},{"label":"B","text":"0.02 J"},{"label":"C","text":"0.1 J"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 J"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$E = (1/2)CV² = (1/2) × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 100² = 0.01 J. Option B equals CV², which is the energy drawn from the source rather than what remains stored on the capacitor.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2005,29, $q$The instrument most suitable for measuring the internal diameter of the narrow neck of a test tube is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"micrometer screw gauge"},{"label":"B","text":"metre rule"},{"label":"C","text":"pair of external calipers"},{"label":"D","text":"vernier calipers used with its upper jaws"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The upper jaws of vernier calipers open inside an aperture and read the internal diameter to 0.01 cm. A micrometer cannot span a test tube, and a metre rule lacks the ranging jaws needed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2005,30, $q$A pulley system of velocity ratio 4 raises an 80 kg load using an effort of 250 N. Taking g = 10 m/s², the efficiency of the machine is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60%"},{"label":"B","text":"75%"},{"label":"C","text":"80%"},{"label":"D","text":"125%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Efficiency compares the useful load multiplied with the effort applied, through the velocity ratio of the machine.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Convert the load to a force","kind":"math","content":"Load = mg = 80 × 10 = 800 N.","note":"Effort is a force, so the load must be expressed as one too."},{"title":"Find the mechanical advantage","kind":"math","content":"MA = load ÷ effort = 800 ÷ 250 = 3.2.","note":"MA counts how many times the machine multiplies the effort."},{"title":"Divide by the velocity ratio","kind":"math","content":"Efficiency = MA ÷ VR = 3.2 ÷ 4 = 0.8 = 80%.","note":"Option D is the inverse ratio VR/MA, which no real machine can exceed or even reach."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','dynamics',2005,31, $q$A 1 kg ball moving horizontally at 4 m/s strikes a vertical wall and rebounds along the same line at 4 m/s. The magnitude of its change of momentum is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8 kg m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"4 kg m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"16 kg m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$On rebound the momentum swings from +4 kg m/s to -4 kg m/s, so the magnitude of the change is 8 kg m/s. Using only 4 kg m/s forgets the reversal of direction; scalar speeds cannot be subtracted here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2005,32, $q$Water is widely used as a coolant in engines and reactors chiefly because of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"its high density"},{"label":"B","text":"its high specific heat capacity"},{"label":"C","text":"its low boiling point"},{"label":"D","text":"its poor thermal conductivity"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A high specific heat capacity lets water absorb a large quantity of heat for a small temperature rise. That is exactly what a coolant circulating round an engine requires.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2005,33, $q$An observer runs directly towards a stationary loudspeaker sounding a fixed note. Compared with the note emitted, the observer hears$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a lower frequency"},{"label":"B","text":"the same frequency"},{"label":"C","text":"a lower speed"},{"label":"D","text":"a higher frequency"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Running into the wavefronts packs them closer in time, so the observer hears a higher frequency. The source still emits its own frequency; only the received count changes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2005,34, $q$When white light passes through a triangular glass prism, the colour deviated the most is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"violet"},{"label":"B","text":"red"},{"label":"C","text":"yellow"},{"label":"D","text":"green"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Violet light travels slowest in glass and so is refracted most, emerging deviated beyond every other colour. Red, being fastest, suffers the least deviation and sits at the other edge.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2005,35, $q$Two lamps rated 60 W and 100 W at 240 V are connected in series across a 240 V supply. The lamp that glows brighter is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the 60 W lamp"},{"label":"B","text":"the 100 W lamp"},{"label":"C","text":"both equally bright"},{"label":"D","text":"neither lamp, since the supply is shared"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$In series the two lamps share one current, so each dissipates power in proportion to its own resistance, which puts the lead with the low-power lamp here.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Rank the resistances","kind":"math","content":"R = V² ÷ P, so the 60 W lamp has 240² ÷ 60 = 960 ohm while the 100 W lamp has 240² ÷ 100 = 576 ohm.","note":"For a fixed rated voltage, the lower-power lamp is wound with the larger resistance."},{"title":"Compare the dissipated powers","kind":"math","content":"In series the current is common, and P = I²R, so the larger resistance dissipates more power: the 60 W lamp wins.","note":"Option B answers the parallel case, where the higher power rating wins instead."},{"title":"Check the rating fallacy","kind":"text","content":"Neither lamp reaches its rated power in series; the pair divides unevenly because their resistances differ.","note":"Ratings apply only at the full 240 V across each lamp alone."}]$st$,
 3),
('physics','magnetism',2005,36, $q$A transformer with 240 V across its primary draws a primary current of 0.2 A. Its secondary delivers 12 V to a load carrying 2 A. The efficiency of the transformer is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40%"},{"label":"B","text":"60%"},{"label":"C","text":"50%"},{"label":"D","text":"100%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Input power = 240 × 0.2 = 48 W and output power = 12 × 2 = 24 W, so efficiency = 24/48 = 50%. Compare powers, never currents alone, when judging a transformer.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2005,37, $q$On a velocity-time graph, the slope of the line at any instant represents the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"distance travelled"},{"label":"B","text":"acceleration"},{"label":"C","text":"displacement"},{"label":"D","text":"speed reached"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The gradient of a velocity-time graph is the rate of change of velocity, which is acceleration. The area under that graph, not its slope, gives the distance.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2005,38, $q$A radioactive nuclide of mass number 232 emits an alpha particle. The mass number of the daughter nuclide is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"228"},{"label":"B","text":"230"},{"label":"C","text":"232"},{"label":"D","text":"236"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$An alpha particle is a helium nucleus of mass number 4, so the daughter has 232 - 4 = 228. Additions rather than subtractions would describe absorption, not decay.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2006,10, $q$A vernier calipers has 50 vernier divisions equal in total length to 49 divisions of a main scale graduated in millimetres. Its least count is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.02 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"0.05 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 mm"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$One vernier division spans 49/50 = 0.98 mm, so the least count is 1.0 - 0.98 = 0.02 mm. The least count is a difference in length between matching divisions, not their sum.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2006,11, $q$A ball is thrown vertically upward with a speed of 30 m/s. Taking g = 10 m/s², the total time before it returns to the thrower is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3 s"},{"label":"B","text":"4.5 s"},{"label":"C","text":"6 s"},{"label":"D","text":"12 s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The time to the top is u/g = 3 s, and descent takes the same time again, giving 6 s. Option A stops at the highest point and option D doubles the up-leg only.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2006,12, $q$A stone is whirled in a horizontal circle of radius 0.5 m at 2 revolutions per second. Taking pi squared as 9.9, its centripetal acceleration is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"8.0 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"79.0 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"158.0 m/s²"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The angular speed is 2 × pi × f = 4 pi rad/s, so a = omega²r = 16 × 9.9 × 0.5, about 79 m/s². Option B forgets to square the angular speed before multiplying by the radius.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2006,13, $q$A wire of length 2.0 m and cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² has resistivity 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ ohm-m. Its resistance is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 ohm"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 ohm"},{"label":"C","text":"10.0 ohms"},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 ohm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$R = rho L/A = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ × 2 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 1.0 ohm. Every length must be in metres and every area in square metres before the substitution.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2006,14, $q$Air in a pipe closed at one end resonates strongly when the air column is 0.25 m long. Taking the speed of sound as 340 m/s, the frequency of the tuning fork in use is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"85 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"170 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"340 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"680 Hz"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A closed pipe holds a quarter wavelength at its first resonance, so λ = 4 × 0.25 = 1.0 m and f = 340 ÷ 1.0 = 340 Hz. The open-pipe formula v ÷ 2L would give the decoy 680 Hz.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2006,15, $q$A man pushes against a rigid wall with a horizontal force of 100 N for 10 s. The work done by his force on the wall is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0 J"},{"label":"B","text":"100 J"},{"label":"C","text":"1000 J"},{"label":"D","text":"10000 J"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Work requires a displacement along the line of the force, and the wall does not move. Effort without displacement transfers no energy to the wall.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2006,16, $q$A man holds his face 20 cm from a concave shaving mirror of focal length 30 cm. The image of his face is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"real and diminished"},{"label":"B","text":"real and magnified"},{"label":"C","text":"virtual and diminished"},{"label":"D","text":"virtual and magnified"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The face sits within the focal length: 1/v = 1/30 - 1/20 is negative, giving a virtual image 60 cm behind the mirror with magnification 3. Real images need the object beyond the focal point of a concave mirror.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2006,17, $q$In a photoelectric experiment the intensity of the incident light of fixed frequency is increased. The quantity that increases is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons"},{"label":"B","text":"the number of photoelectrons emitted per second"},{"label":"C","text":"the threshold frequency of the surface"},{"label":"D","text":"the work function of the metal"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Brighter light of the same frequency delivers more photons per second, so more electrons escape per second. Each photon still carries the same energy, so the maximum kinetic energy, threshold and work function are all unchanged.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2006,18, $q$A ball is kicked from level ground at 10 m/s at 37° to the horizontal (sin 37° = 0.6, cos 37° = 0.8). Taking g = 10 m/s², its horizontal range is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.8 m"},{"label":"B","text":"8.0 m"},{"label":"C","text":"9.6 m"},{"label":"D","text":"19.2 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The range follows from the two velocity components and the symmetrical flight time under gravity.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Resolve the launch velocity","kind":"math","content":"Vertical component = 10 × 0.6 = 6 m/s; horizontal component = 10 × 0.8 = 8 m/s.","note":"The horizontal component stays constant throughout the flight."},{"title":"Time the flight","kind":"math","content":"Time to the top = 6 ÷ 10 = 0.6 s, and descent mirrors it, so the total flight lasts 1.2 s.","note":"The landing vertical speed equals the launch value in magnitude, directed downward."},{"title":"Multiply across","kind":"math","content":"Range = 8 × 1.2 = 9.6 m; for scale, the greatest height is 36 ÷ 20 = 1.8 m.","note":"Option D reads the flight time as 2.4 s, as if gravity never ended the flight."}]$st$,
 3),
('physics','electricity',2006,19, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 9.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm is connected to a heater of resistance 8.5 ohms. The potential difference across the heater is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8.0 V"},{"label":"B","text":"8.5 V"},{"label":"C","text":"9.0 V"},{"label":"D","text":"9.5 V"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The circuit totals 9.0 ohms, so I = 9/9 = 1.0 A. The terminal p.d. across the heater is E - Ir = 9.0 - 0.5 = 8.5 V.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2006,20, $q$Food cooks more slowly in an open pot on a high mountain than at sea level because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the air is colder at high altitude"},{"label":"B","text":"the specific heat capacity of water falls with altitude"},{"label":"C","text":"the reduced atmospheric pressure lowers the boiling point of water"},{"label":"D","text":"the flame is starved of oxygen"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Boiling occurs when the vapour pressure of the water matches the surrounding pressure, and thinner air means boiling at a lower temperature. The pot boils sooner but cooler, so cooking drags on.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2006,21, $q$A 0.15 kg ball strikes a wall head-on at 20 m/s and rebounds along its original line at 10 m/s. The magnitude of the impulse it receives is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 N s"},{"label":"B","text":"3.0 N s"},{"label":"C","text":"9.0 N s"},{"label":"D","text":"4.5 N s"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The momentum reverses from +3 kg m/s to -1.5 kg m/s, so the change is 4.5 kg m/s and the impulse equals 4.5 N s. A reversal of direction demands a vector subtraction, not a difference of speeds.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2006,22, $q$A pendulum bob of mass 0.2 kg is pulled aside to a point 0.5 m above its lowest position and released. Taking g = 10 m/s², its speed at the lowest point is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"3.2 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"5.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"10 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The lost potential energy becomes kinetic: (1/2)v² = 10 × 0.5, so v = the square root of 10, about 3.2 m/s. Option A is the energy, 1.0 J, quoted as if it were a speed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2006,23, $q$How many neutrons are in an atom of the nuclide uranium-238, whose proton number is 92?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"92"},{"label":"B","text":"238"},{"label":"C","text":"330"},{"label":"D","text":"146"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Neutrons = mass number - proton number = 238 - 92 = 146. The proton count alone covers only part of the nucleons.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2006,24, $q$Which practice minimizes parallax error when reading a length against a metre rule?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"placing the eye vertically above the mark being read"},{"label":"B","text":"starting every measurement from the zero graduation"},{"label":"C","text":"holding the rule at a slant toward the eye"},{"label":"D","text":"reading with the head tilted to one side"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Parallax arises when the line of sight meets the scale at an angle, so the eye must sit directly over the mark. Beginning at a different graduation concerns zero error, a separate fault.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2006,25, $q$A lamp is rated 60 W at 240 V. Its resistance while operating normally is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4 ohms"},{"label":"B","text":"240 ohms"},{"label":"C","text":"960 ohms"},{"label":"D","text":"1440 ohms"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At the rated conditions R = V²/P = 240² ÷ 60 = 960 ohms. Option A divides voltage by power, confusing the lamp resistance with the current formula I = P/V.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2006,26, $q$A man claps his hands in front of a cliff and hears the echo 2 s later. Taking the speed of sound as 340 m/s, his distance from the cliff is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"85 m"},{"label":"B","text":"170 m"},{"label":"C","text":"680 m"},{"label":"D","text":"340 m"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The sound travels to the cliff and back within the 2 s, so the one-way distance is 340 × 2 ÷ 2 = 340 m. Option C forgets the return journey in the timing.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2006,27, $q$Compared with violet light of the same intensity travelling in the same piece of glass, red light has$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a larger refractive index"},{"label":"B","text":"a smaller refractive index"},{"label":"C","text":"the same refractive index"},{"label":"D","text":"a refractive index of zero"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Red light travels fastest in glass, bends least, and so has the smaller refractive index of the pair. Violet takes the larger value, which is why prisms spread red widest at the top.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2006,28, $q$A capacitor of 100 microfarads is charged from a 12 V battery through a resistor. The energy finally stored in the capacitor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.2 × 10⁻³ J"},{"label":"B","text":"7.2 × 10⁻³ J"},{"label":"C","text":"14.4 × 10⁻³ J"},{"label":"D","text":"0.72 × 10⁻³ J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The charge first, then the energy: half of charge times voltage is what remains on the plates.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Store the charge","kind":"math","content":"Q = CV = 100 × 10⁻⁶ × 12 = 1.2 × 10⁻³ C.","note":"Convert microfarads to farads before multiplying."},{"title":"Convert charge to energy","kind":"math","content":"E = QV ÷ 2 = 1.2 × 10⁻³ × 12 ÷ 2 = 7.2 × 10⁻³ J.","note":"Option C is QV and forgets the half; only half of the source's delivery ends up stored."},{"title":"Judge the resistor's role","kind":"text","content":"The series resistor fixes only the charging rate, never the final energy.","note":"The stored energy depends on C and V alone, so the resistor value is a decoy."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','dynamics',2006,29, $q$A 4 kg box on a horizontal floor experiences a coefficient of friction of 0.25. A horizontal pull of 12 N is applied. Taking g = 10 m/s², the acceleration of the box is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 m/s²"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Friction = 0.25 × 40 = 10 N opposes the 12 N pull, leaving a net force of 2 N and a = 2 ÷ 4 = 0.5 m/s². Dividing the net force by the weight instead of the mass produces option D.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2006,30, $q$Which observation provides the clearest evidence that light behaves as particles?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"interference fringes from a double slit"},{"label":"B","text":"diffraction of electron beams"},{"label":"C","text":"photoelectric emission of electrons from a metal surface"},{"label":"D","text":"the bending of light as it enters glass"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Photoelectric emission pairs each photon with a single ejected electron, an exchange that a continuous wave picture cannot explain. Interference and refraction instead belong to the wave account of light.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2006,31, $q$100 g of water at 20 °C is mixed with 100 g of water at 60 °C in a calorimeter of negligible heat capacity. The final temperature of the mixture is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"30 °C"},{"label":"B","text":"40 °C"},{"label":"C","text":"45 °C"},{"label":"D","text":"50 °C"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Equal masses exchanging heat end at the midpoint of the two temperatures: 40 °C. Any other value would leave one portion both warmer and a net gainer of heat, which cannot be.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2006,32, $q$A particle executing simple harmonic motion has frequency 2 Hz and amplitude 0.05 m. Its maximum speed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.31 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"0.63 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"1.26 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"6.3 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The speed peaks at the centre of the oscillation: v = 2 × 3.14 × f × A = 2 × 3.14 × 2 × 0.05, about 0.63 m/s. Option C doubles it by treating the amplitude as a diameter or by doubling the frequency as well.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2006,33, $q$A current of 2 A passes through a silver voltameter for 1000 s. If the electrochemical equivalent of silver is 1.1 × 10⁻⁶ kg/C, the mass of silver deposited is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.2 × 10⁻³ kg"},{"label":"B","text":"1.1 × 10⁻³ kg"},{"label":"C","text":"2.2 × 10⁻² kg"},{"label":"D","text":"2.2 × 10⁻⁴ kg"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The charge passed is It = 2000 C, so m = ZIt = 1.1 × 10⁻⁶ × 2000 = 2.2 × 10⁻³ kg. Option D loses the factor of 2 from the current.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2006,34, $q$An eclipse of the sun occurs when$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the earth casts its shadow onto the sun"},{"label":"B","text":"the moon passes directly between the sun and the earth"},{"label":"C","text":"the earth passes directly between the sun and the moon"},{"label":"D","text":"the sun and moon reverse their positions in the sky"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A solar eclipse needs the moon's shadow to sweep across the earth, so the moon must lie between sun and earth. Option C describes a lunar eclipse rather than a solar one.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2006,35, $q$The resistance of a metallic conductor increases as its temperature rises because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the drift speed of the free electrons increases"},{"label":"B","text":"the number of free electrons decreases"},{"label":"C","text":"the amplitude of vibration of the metal ions increases"},{"label":"D","text":"the cross-sectional area of the conductor grows"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Hotter ions vibrate more widely and collide with the drifting electrons more often, so the current suffers greater obstruction. The population of free electrons in a metal changes very little over ordinary temperature ranges.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2006,36, $q$A car covers the first half of a journey at 20 m/s and the second half at 30 m/s. Its average speed for the whole journey is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"25 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"50 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"24 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Equal distances, not equal times, split the journey, so the slower leg gets more of the clock.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Weigh the two halves","kind":"text","content":"The fast half is finished sooner, so most of the journey time is spent at 20 m/s.","note":"This tips the average toward the smaller speed."},{"title":"Apply the definition","kind":"math","content":"Average speed = total distance ÷ total time = 2 ÷ (1/20 + 1/30) = 24 m/s.","note":"Equivalently, the harmonic mean: 2uv ÷ (u + v)."},{"title":"Reject the speed mean","kind":"text","content":"The plain mean, 25 m/s, would be right only for equal times at the two speeds.","note":"The stated condition fixes equal distances, which changes the weighting."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','magnetism',2006,37, $q$Two long straight parallel wires in air carry currents in opposite directions. The wires will$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"repel each other"},{"label":"B","text":"attract each other"},{"label":"C","text":"exert no magnetic force on each other"},{"label":"D","text":"repel only while the currents differ in size"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$With opposite currents the magnetic fields in the space between the wires reinforce and the wires are pushed apart. Same-direction currents behave in the opposite way and attract.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2007,10, $q$The zero of a vernier scale lies between 6.1 cm and 6.2 cm on a main scale of least count 0.01 cm per vernier division, and the 3rd vernier mark coincides. The corrected reading is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6.03 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"6.10 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"6.13 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"6.30 cm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The main scale supplies 6.1 cm and the coincidence adds 3 × 0.01 = 0.03 cm, giving 6.13 cm. Option D treats each vernier division as worth 0.1 cm rather than 0.01 cm.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2007,11, $q$A ball is thrown horizontally at 15 m/s from the top of a cliff 45 m high. Taking g = 10 m/s², the horizontal distance it travels before striking the ground is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"45 m"},{"label":"B","text":"30 m"},{"label":"C","text":"15 m"},{"label":"D","text":"60 m"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The fall alone sets the time: t = the square root of (2 × 45 ÷ 10) = 3 s. The horizontal travel at 15 m/s then covers 45 m, unchanged by the vertical drop.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2007,12, $q$Two forces of 6 N and 8 N act on a body at right angles to each other. The magnitude of their resultant is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 N"},{"label":"B","text":"10 N"},{"label":"C","text":"14 N"},{"label":"D","text":"48 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$For perpendicular forces the resultant is the square root of (36 + 64) = 10 N. Option A is their difference and option C their sum; neither uses Pythagoras' rule for a vector triangle.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2007,13, $q$A wave travels along a rope at 2.0 m/s with a frequency of 25 Hz. Its wavelength is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"8.0 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"12.5 cm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$λ = v ÷ f = 2 ÷ 25 = 0.08 m = 8 cm. Option D inverts the ratio and reports 12.5 rather than 0.125 of a metre,$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2007,14, $q$Two 12-ohm resistors in parallel are connected in series with a 4-ohm resistor across a 20 V battery of negligible internal resistance. The total current drawn is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 A"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"5.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 A"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The parallel pair behaves as 6 ohms, so the circuit totals 10 ohms and I = 20 ÷ 10 = 2 A. Treating the network as a single 5-ohm load produces option B.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2007,15, $q$A crate is hauled 5 m along horizontal ground by a rope pulled with a force of 40 N at 53° above the horizontal (cos 53° = 0.6). The work done by the pull on the crate is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60 J"},{"label":"B","text":"120 J"},{"label":"C","text":"160 J"},{"label":"D","text":"200 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The horizontal component 40 × 0.6 = 24 N is the only part acting along the motion, so W = 24 × 5 = 120 J. Option D multiplies the whole 40 N by the displacement and ignores the 53° pull.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2007,16, $q$An object stands 10 cm from a converging lens of focal length 15 cm. The image formed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6 cm from the lens, real"},{"label":"B","text":"10 cm from the lens, real"},{"label":"C","text":"30 cm from the lens on the same side as the object, virtual"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm from the lens, real"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Since 1/v = 1/15 - 1/10 is negative, v = -30 cm: a virtual image 30 cm from the lens on the object side, upright and three times as tall. An object inside the focal length of a converging lens never throws a real image onto a screen.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2007,17, $q$A radioactive source has an initial activity of 640 counts per second and a half-life of 5 hours. Its activity after 15 hours is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40 counts per second"},{"label":"B","text":"80 counts per second"},{"label":"C","text":"160 counts per second"},{"label":"D","text":"320 counts per second"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fifteen hours is three half-lives, so the activity falls by the factor 2³ = 8 to 640 ÷ 8 = 80. Option A applies one further half-life than the time allows.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2007,18, $q$A wire of length 0.3 m carrying a current of 4 A is inclined at 30° to a uniform magnetic field of flux density 0.5 T. The magnetic force on the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.15 N"},{"label":"B","text":"0.3 N"},{"label":"C","text":"0.6 N"},{"label":"D","text":"3.0 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The inclination of the current to the field scales the magnetic force by the sine of the angle.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Read the angle factor","kind":"math","content":"F = BIL sin(angle), and sin 30° = 0.5 halves whatever a perpendicular wire would feel.","note":"The maximum value 0.6 N belongs to a 90° arrangement."},{"title":"Multiply the three factors","kind":"math","content":"F = 0.5 × 4 × 0.3 × 0.5 = 0.3 N.","note":"Option C applies the formula with sin 90° in place of sin 30°."},{"title":"Link to the motor principle","kind":"text","content":"This same BIL force spins the sides of a motor coil inside its field.","note":"If the current ran parallel to the field, the force would collapse to zero."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','kinematics',2007,19, $q$A car covers the first 30 km of a trip in 20 minutes and the remaining 40 km in 40 minutes. Its average speed for the whole trip is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60 km/h"},{"label":"B","text":"66 km/h"},{"label":"C","text":"75 km/h"},{"label":"D","text":"70 km/h"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Total distance 70 km over 1 hour gives 70 km/h. The two stage speeds, 90 km/h and 60 km/h, held for unequal times, make their plain mean of 75 wrong.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2007,20, $q$An electric iron rated 1.5 kW operates on a 240 V supply. The current it draws is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.0 A"},{"label":"B","text":"6.25 A"},{"label":"C","text":"8.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"12.5 A"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$I = P ÷ V = 1500 ÷ 240 = 6.25 A. Option D simply repeats the calculation with the voltage half of what supplies the iron.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2007,21, $q$While a block of ice at 0 °C melts steadily, the heat supplied to it$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"raises the potential energy of the molecules as the crystal structure breaks up"},{"label":"B","text":"raises the kinetic energy of the molecules rapidly"},{"label":"C","text":"raises the temperature of the ice gradually"},{"label":"D","text":"turns the ice straight into vapour"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The heat breaks the orderly arrangement of the solid, raising molecular potential energy while their kinetic energy holds steady. That is why a thermometer reads 0 °C throughout the melting.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2007,22, $q$A 2 kg trolley moving at 6 m/s overtakes and collides elastically head-on with an identical trolley at rest. The speed of the second trolley after the collision is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"3 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"6 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"12 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$For equal masses in a head-on elastic collision the velocities interchange completely: the struck trolley leaves at 6 m/s and the striker stops dead. Sharing 3 m/s each fits momentum but squanders kinetic energy, so it describes an inelastic collision.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2007,23, $q$A micrometer screw gauge has a pitch of 0.5 mm and 50 equal divisions on its thimble. Its least count is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.001 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"0.01 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"0.05 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.1 mm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Least count = pitch ÷ number of divisions = 0.5 ÷ 50 = 0.01 mm. Option C confuses the pitch division with the thimble fraction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2007,24, $q$Sound cannot travel through a vacuum because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"it is a mechanical wave that needs a material medium"},{"label":"B","text":"it is an electromagnetic wave"},{"label":"C","text":"it travels only along straight lines"},{"label":"D","text":"the vacuum absorbs all the sound"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sound is carried by the to-and-fro motion of particles of a material medium. A vacuum offers nothing to vibrate, so no sound can cross it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2007,25, $q$In a wheel and axle the radius of the wheel is 30 cm and that of the axle is 5 cm. The velocity ratio of the machine is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.17"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"25"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$VR = radius of wheel ÷ radius of axle = 30 ÷ 5 = 6, since both turn together through one distinct angle. Option A reports the inverse ratio, which would shrink rather than multiply an effort.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2007,26, $q$A coin lies at the bottom of a water tank 2.0 m deep. Taking the refractive index of water as 1.33, the apparent depth of the coin is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 m"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 m"},{"label":"C","text":"2.7 m"},{"label":"D","text":"3.0 m"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Apparent depth = real depth ÷ refractive index = 2 ÷ 1.33 = 1.5 m. Option C multiplies by the index instead, sinking the coin deeper than it actually rests.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2007,27, $q$Two cells, each of e.m.f. 2.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm, are connected in series with a 3-ohm resistor. The current in the circuit is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 A"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 A"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In series the e.m.f.s add to 4.0 V and the internal resistances to 1.0 ohm, so I = 4 ÷ 4 = 1 A. Option D assumes the cells have no internal resistance at all.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2007,28, $q$According to the Bohr model of the atom, when an electron drops from a higher to a lower energy level, the atom$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"absorbs a photon of any energy"},{"label":"B","text":"keeps the lost energy in store for later use"},{"label":"C","text":"gains electrical charge"},{"label":"D","text":"emits a photon of definite energy"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The energy gap leaves the atom as one photon of fixed energy, producing the sharp lines of the element's spectrum. Only an upward jump absorbs light, and only at matching energies.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2007,29, $q$A ball is thrown vertically downward at 10 m/s from a high building and strikes the ground 3 s later. Taking g = 10 m/s², the height of the building is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"45 m"},{"label":"B","text":"60 m"},{"label":"C","text":"75 m"},{"label":"D","text":"120 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The throw starts the ball off with a head start that the free-fall formula must include.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Find the landing speed","kind":"math","content":"v = u + gt = 10 + 10 × 3 = 40 m/s downward.","note":"The initial 10 m/s adds to the free-fall gain of 30 m/s."},{"title":"Average the speed","kind":"math","content":"Average speed = (10 + 40) ÷ 2 = 25 m/s.","note":"Endpoint averaging is valid because the acceleration is constant."},{"title":"Multiply by the time","kind":"math","content":"Height = 25 × 3 = 75 m.","note":"Option A ignores the initial push, and option D assumes the ball falls at its final speed throughout."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','magnetism',2007,30, $q$A steel pin becomes a magnet when a bar magnet is stroked along it repeatedly in one direction. This happens because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the pin gains electric charge from the magnet"},{"label":"B","text":"the magnetic domains within the steel align in one direction"},{"label":"C","text":"the pin is warmed by the friction of stroking"},{"label":"D","text":"the domains of the steel are scattered at random"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Stroking in a single direction lines up the tiny magnetic domains of the steel so their fields add. Repeated to-and-fro stroking would cancel them and leave the pin almost unmagnetized.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2007,31, $q$Resistors of 100 ohms and 200 ohms carry the same current while connected in series. The ratio of the power dissipated in the 200-ohm resistor to that in the 100-ohm resistor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$With a common current, P = I²R grows in direct proportion to resistance, so the ratio is 200 ÷ 100 = 2. Option D squares the resistance ratio, as though the two resistors shared the voltage instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2007,32, $q$A fixed mass of gas at 600 mmHg pressure occupies 200 cm³. Compressed at constant temperature to 100 cm³, its pressure becomes$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"300 mmHg"},{"label":"B","text":"600 mmHg"},{"label":"C","text":"1200 mmHg"},{"label":"D","text":"2400 mmHg"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Boyle's law gives P1V1 = P2V2: 600 × 200 = P2 × 100, so P2 = 1200 mmHg. Halving the volume must double the absolute pressure of the gas.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2007,33, $q$A tuning fork of frequency 500 Hz produces the first resonance in a closed pipe at an air column length of 16.5 cm. Taking the end correction as negligible, the speed of sound in air is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"165 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"330 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"660 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"1320 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$At the first resonance the pipe holds a quarter wavelength, so λ = 4 × 0.165 = 0.66 m and v = fλ = 330 m/s. Option C treats the 16.5 cm as half the wavelength instead of a quarter.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2007,34, $q$A 0.2 kg stone is whirled in a horizontal circle of radius 0.4 m at a steady 5 m/s. The centripetal force on the stone is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 N"},{"label":"B","text":"6.25 N"},{"label":"C","text":"25 N"},{"label":"D","text":"12.5 N"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Substitute straight into F = mv² ÷ r: 0.2 × 25 ÷ 0.4 = 12.5 N. Option B skips the squaring of the speed and takes 5 as if it were the square.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2007,35, $q$A vernier calipers has a positive zero error of 0.04 cm and a least count of 0.01 cm. Measuring a rod, the main scale reads 4.5 cm and the 6th vernier division coincides. The true measurement of the rod is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.48 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"4.52 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"4.56 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"4.60 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The observed reading is 4.5 + 6 × 0.01 = 4.56 cm, which is high by the 0.04 cm zero error: true = 4.56 - 0.04 = 4.52 cm. Option A goes further and subtracts the error a second time.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2007,36, $q$A converging lens has a focal length of 25 cm. Its power is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.04 dioptre"},{"label":"B","text":"0.25 dioptre"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 dioptres"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 dioptres"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Power = 1 ÷ focal length in metres = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4 D. Option A divides by the number 25 and never converts centimetres to metres.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2007,37, $q$An ammeter designed for a laboratory circuit should have a very low resistance so that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"it does not alter the current it is measuring"},{"label":"B","text":"it can record very small voltages"},{"label":"C","text":"it can stand connection across a battery"},{"label":"D","text":"its pointer moves over the whole scale"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$An ammeter sits in series with the load, so any sizeable resistance of its own would choke the current it is meant to report. A voltmeter does the opposite and needs a huge resistance across the load.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2007,38, $q$A 0.5 kg block of aluminium at 80 °C is dropped into 1.0 kg of water at 20 °C. Taking specific heat capacities of aluminium and water as 900 and 4200 J/kg K, the final temperature of the mixture is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"22 °C"},{"label":"B","text":"26 °C"},{"label":"C","text":"32 °C"},{"label":"D","text":"50 °C"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Heat lost by the hot metal must equal heat gained by the cool water, and the water's much larger heat capacity pins the end result near its own starting temperature.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Write the two heat terms","kind":"math","content":"Heat lost by aluminium = 0.5 × 900 × (80 - T); heat gained by water = 1.0 × 4200 × (T - 20).","note":"T is the shared final temperature of the mixture."},{"title":"Equate and expand","kind":"math","content":"450 × (80 - T) = 4200 × (T - 20) gives 36000 - 450T = 4200T - 84000.","note":"An insulated mixing vessel allows no heat to escape to the surroundings."},{"title":"Solve for T","kind":"math","content":"4650T = 120000, so T is about 25.8, close to 26 °C.","note":"Any value above the water's start or below it must be rejected on sight."}]$st$,
 3);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2008,10, $q$A vernier calipers has 10 vernier divisions coinciding in length with 9 divisions of a main scale whose smallest division is 0.5 mm. The least count of the instrument is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.01 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"0.05 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"0.1 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 mm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each vernier division spans 9 × 0.5 ÷ 10 = 0.45 mm, so the least count is 0.5 - 0.45 = 0.05 mm. The least count compares one vernier mark with one main-scale mark, division against division.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2008,11, $q$A train starting from rest accelerates uniformly at 0.5 m/s² until its speed reaches 25 m/s. The distance covered during this acceleration is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"625 m"},{"label":"B","text":"312.5 m"},{"label":"C","text":"1250 m"},{"label":"D","text":"5000 m"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$From v² = 2as, s = 625 ÷ 1 = 625 m. The run lasts 50 s, and option B halves the distance by pretending the journey ended at 25 s.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2008,12, $q$A man of mass 60 kg stands on a scale inside a lift accelerating downward at 2 m/s². Taking g = 10 m/s², the reading registered by the scale is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"120 N"},{"label":"B","text":"480 N"},{"label":"C","text":"600 N"},{"label":"D","text":"720 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$With the platform sinking beneath him the floor pushes with m(g - a) = 60 × 8 = 480 N. Option C is his true weight, and it applies only to a lift moving steadily or at rest.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2008,13, $q$A water wave travels at 1.5 m/s with a wavelength of 0.75 m. Its frequency is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"1.125 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 Hz"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$f = v ÷ λ = 1.5 ÷ 0.75 = 2 Hz. Multiplying the two quantities instead of dividing them produces option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2008,14, $q$Three capacitors, each of 6 microfarads, are connected in series. The capacitance of the combination is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 microfarads"},{"label":"B","text":"6 microfarads"},{"label":"C","text":"18 microfarads"},{"label":"D","text":"36 microfarads"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Three equal capacitors in series give one third of the single value: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 microfarads. Option C is what connecting them in parallel would yield instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2008,15, $q$A 0.3 kg ball is dropped from rest at a height of 20 m. Taking g = 10 m/s² and ignoring air resistance, its speed on hitting the ground is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"14 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"20 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"40 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Equating kinetic and potential energy gives (1/2)v² = 10 × 20, so v = 20 m/s. Option D squares the velocity figure instead of taking its square root.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2008,16, $q$An object is placed 60 cm from a concave mirror of focal length 40 cm. The magnification of the image formed is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0"},{"label":"C","text":"1.5"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$1/v = 1/40 - 1/60 = 1/120, so v = 120 cm and m = v/u = 120 ÷ 60 = 2, a real inverted image twice the size. Option A is the virtual-image case with the object inside the focal length.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2008,17, $q$Photoelectrons are emitted with a maximum kinetic energy of 3.0 eV. The stopping potential needed to halt them is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 V"},{"label":"B","text":"3.0 V"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0 V"},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 V"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The stopping potential must absorb the entire maximum kinetic energy of a single electron: 3.0 eV corresponds to 3.0 V. Kinetic energies quoted in electron-volts convert one-to-one into volts this way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2008,18, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 12 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm drives a current of 2 A round a circuit. The terminal potential difference of the battery is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 V"},{"label":"B","text":"12 V"},{"label":"C","text":"13 V"},{"label":"D","text":"11 V"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Some of the e.m.f. is always spent pushing the current through the battery's own resistance first.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Charge the internal drop","kind":"math","content":"Lost volt = Ir = 2 × 0.5 = 1 V.","note":"The size of the current decides how much e.m.f. is swallowed inside."},{"title":"Subtract from the e.m.f.","kind":"math","content":"Terminal p.d. = 12 - 1 = 11 V.","note":"Option B copies the e.m.f. from the label and never acknowledges r."},{"title":"Size the external resistor","kind":"math","content":"R = V ÷ I = 11 ÷ 2 = 5.5 ohm.","note":"A 2 V internal drop would require a 1-ohm cell, double the stated value."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','kinematics',2008,19, $q$Two cars 600 m apart travel towards each other on a straight road at 15 m/s and 25 m/s. The time that elapses before they meet is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6 s"},{"label":"B","text":"12 s"},{"label":"C","text":"15 s"},{"label":"D","text":"24 s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Head-on motion closes the gap at the sum of the speeds, 40 m/s, so the meeting occurs after 600 ÷ 40 = 15 s. Option D divides the gap by only one car's speed and lets the other car idle.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2008,20, $q$A gas occupies 2.0 litres at 27 °C. Heated to 127 °C at constant pressure, its new volume is about$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.67 litre"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 litres"},{"label":"C","text":"2.67 litres"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 litres"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Volumes follow absolute temperatures: V2 = 2 × 400 ÷ 300 = 2.67 litres. Option A inverts the temperature ratio and shrinks a gas that has been heated.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2008,21, $q$A rifle of mass 4 kg fires a 0.02 kg bullet horizontally at 300 m/s. Taking the rifle initially at rest, its speed of recoil is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.75 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Momentum conservation gives 4 × v = 0.02 × 300, so v = 1.5 m/s backwards. The rifle's much larger mass tames its recoil, and option D results from swapping the two masses.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2008,22, $q$Which of the following is a derived physical quantity?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"mass"},{"label":"B","text":"length"},{"label":"C","text":"density"},{"label":"D","text":"time"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Density is built from two base quantities, mass and volume, so it is derived. Mass, length and time are among the base quantities themselves.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2008,23, $q$A uniform pipe open at both ends is 1.0 m long and sounds its fundamental note in air. Taking the speed of sound as 340 m/s, the frequency emitted is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"340 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"680 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"85 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"170 Hz"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$An open pipe holds half a wavelength at its fundamental: λ = 2 m, so f = 340 ÷ 2 = 170 Hz. Option C is what a pipe of the same length closed at one end would sound.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2008,24, $q$White light splits into its component colours as it passes through a glass prism because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the prism absorbs part of the light"},{"label":"B","text":"different colours travel at different speeds in glass and so refract differently"},{"label":"C","text":"the source emits light in separate pulses of colour"},{"label":"D","text":"the wedge shape of the prism splits rays mechanically"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each colour keeps its own frequency but travels at its own speed in glass, so each has its own refractive index. That spread of refractive indices is what prisms exploit to fan the spectrum out.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2008,25, $q$Two lamps, each rated 12 V and 36 W, are connected in series across a 24 V supply. The current in the circuit is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 A"},{"label":"B","text":"3.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"12 A"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each lamp has resistance 144 ÷ 36 = 4 ohms, so the series pair totals 8 ohms and I = 24 ÷ 8 = 3 A. Option C reconnects the lamps in parallel across the same 24 V, but the question fixes them in series.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2008,26, $q$A boy of weight 600 N runs up a flight of stairs of vertical height 3 m in 5 s. His average useful power is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"36 W"},{"label":"B","text":"180 W"},{"label":"C","text":"360 W"},{"label":"D","text":"1800 W"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$P = work ÷ time = 600 × 3 ÷ 5 = 360 W. Option D multiplies the force by the time instead of dividing by it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2008,27, $q$Of the three common radioactive emissions, the least penetrating is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"alpha particles"},{"label":"B","text":"beta particles"},{"label":"C","text":"gamma rays"},{"label":"D","text":"X-rays"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A sheet of paper stops alpha particles long before beta needs a thicker plate or gamma passes through thick lead. Alpha therefore ranks as the least penetrating of the radiations.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2008,28, $q$A projectile is launched over level ground with vertical component 16 m/s and horizontal component 12 m/s. Taking g = 10 m/s², its time of flight is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.6 s"},{"label":"B","text":"3.2 s"},{"label":"C","text":"3.8 s"},{"label":"D","text":"6.4 s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The vertical component alone fixes the flight time, doubled between launch and landing.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Time the upward leg","kind":"math","content":"Time to the top = vertical component ÷ g = 16 ÷ 10 = 1.6 s.","note":"At the summit the vertical velocity reads zero for an instant."},{"title":"Double for the whole flight","kind":"math","content":"Time of flight = 2 × 1.6 = 3.2 s.","note":"Level launch and landing make the descent mirror the ascent exactly."},{"title":"Cross-check the geometry","kind":"math","content":"Range = 12 × 3.2 = 38.4 m and the greatest height = 256 ÷ 20 = 12.8 m.","note":"Option A stops at the summit instead of completing the return journey."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','magnetism',2008,29, $q$A straight wire of length 0.5 m carries a current of 4 A at right angles to a magnetic field of flux density 0.3 T. The force on the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.18 N"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 N"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0 N"},{"label":"D","text":"0.6 N"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$F = BIL = 0.3 × 4 × 0.5 = 0.6 N for the perpendicular arrangement stated. Option A drops one of the three factors from the product.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2008,30, $q$Heat reaches all parts of a liquid or gas mainly by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"convection"},{"label":"B","text":"conduction"},{"label":"C","text":"radiation"},{"label":"D","text":"evaporation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$In fluids, warmed layers expand, grow lighter, and rise while cooler fluid sinks to replace them, setting up convection currents. Conduction needs a fixed array of particles that stay in place, as in solids.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2008,31, $q$A resultant force of 20 N acts on a 5 kg body at rest for 0.25 s. The velocity acquired by the body is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"4.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Impulse = F × t = 20 × 0.25 = 5 N s, and dividing by the 5 kg mass leaves v = 1 m/s. Option C is the speed after a full second under the same force, not 0.25 s.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2008,32, $q$The diameter of a wire is measured five times and the readings are 2.12, 2.14, 2.13, 2.15 and 2.12 mm. The best estimate of the diameter is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.12 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"2.14 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"2.66 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"2.13 mm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The readings total 10.66 mm, and dividing by 5 gives 2.132, quoted as 2.13 mm. Option C merely sums the readings and never divides.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2008,33, $q$A ship's sonar sends an ultrasound pulse straight down and receives its echo from the sea bed 2 s later. Taking the speed of sound in sea water as 1500 m/s, the depth of the water is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"750 m"},{"label":"B","text":"1500 m"},{"label":"C","text":"3000 m"},{"label":"D","text":"600 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The pulse goes down and back inside the 2 s, so depth = 1500 × 2 ÷ 2 = 1500 m. Option C charges the return journey's time into the one-way distance yet again.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2008,34, $q$A ray of light strikes a plane mirror with an angle of incidence of 30°. The angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"15°"},{"label":"B","text":"30°"},{"label":"C","text":"60°"},{"label":"D","text":"90°"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The reflected ray turns through twice the angle of incidence measured from the normal: 2 × 30 = 60°. Option B simply restates the incidence angle and ignores the reflected one entirely.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2008,35, $q$A machine lifts a 120 kg load through a height of 2 m while an effort of 400 N moves 8 m. Taking g = 10 m/s², the efficiency of the machine is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60%"},{"label":"B","text":"80%"},{"label":"C","text":"133%"},{"label":"D","text":"75%"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Efficiency divides the work got out by the work put in, measured in the same units of joules.$q$,
 'multi',
 $st$[{"title":"Total the useful output","kind":"math","content":"Output work = load × height = 120 × 10 × 2 = 2400 J.","note":"g = 10 turns the 120 kg load into a 1200 N force."},{"title":"Total the effort input","kind":"math","content":"Input work = effort × distance moved = 400 × 8 = 3200 J.","note":"The machine trades distance for force, so the effort travels 8 m to lift 2 m."},{"title":"Divide and convert","kind":"math","content":"Efficiency = 2400 ÷ 3200 = 0.75 = 75%.","note":"Option C inverts the ratio; no real machine can pass 100%."}]$st$,
 2),
('physics','electricity',2008,36, $q$An ideal transformer steps 240 V down to 24 V and its primary coil has 2000 turns. If the secondary delivers a current of 5 A, the current in the primary is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.05 A"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"50 A"},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 A"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$An ideal transformer passes power through unchanged: 240 × Ip = 24 × 5, so Ip = 0.5 A. Stepping the voltage down a factor of 10 necessarily steps the current up by the same factor, and option B leaves the currents equal.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2008,37, $q$The vast energy output of the sun is produced chiefly by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"nuclear fusion of hydrogen nuclei"},{"label":"B","text":"nuclear fission of heavy uranium-type nuclei"},{"label":"C","text":"the chemical burning of hydrogen in oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"slow gravitational shrinkage alone"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$In the sun's core, hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium, converting a small loss of mass into vast energy. Fission of heavy nuclei belongs to reactors, and chemical burning is far too feeble to light a star.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);
﻿insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2015,10, $q$A vernier caliper has a least count of 0.01 cm. On measuring the diameter of a metal rod, the main scale reads 2.4 cm and the 7th vernier division coincides with a main scale division. The diameter of the rod is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.41 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"2.47 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"2.71 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"2.77 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The vernier coincidence adds 7 × 0.01 cm to the main scale reading of 2.40 cm, giving 2.47 cm. Subtracting in the wrong order produces options C and D.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2015,11, $q$The pitch of a micrometer screw gauge is 0.5 mm and its circular scale has 50 divisions. The least count of the gauge is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.001 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"0.005 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"0.01 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.05 mm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Least count = pitch / number of circular divisions = 0.5/50 = 0.01 mm. Option D divides the pitch by 10 only.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2015,12, $q$A stone is projected horizontally from the top of a cliff 80 m high with a speed of 30 m/s. Taking g = 10 m/s², the horizontal distance from the foot of the cliff at which the stone strikes the ground is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60 m"},{"label":"B","text":"90 m"},{"label":"C","text":"120 m"},{"label":"D","text":"240 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The time of fall depends only on the vertical motion: t is the square root of 2h/g, that is the square root of 16, which is 4 s. The horizontal range is u × t = 30 × 4 = 120 m. Option B corresponds to a 3 s fall.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2015,13, $q$A car starts from rest and its velocity-time graph has three stages: uniform acceleration to 20 m/s in 5 s, a constant velocity of 20 m/s for 5 s, and uniform deceleration to rest in another 10 s. The total distance travelled by the car is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"200 m"},{"label":"B","text":"250 m"},{"label":"C","text":"300 m"},{"label":"D","text":"350 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The distance is the total area under the velocity-time graph: 50 + 100 + 100 = 250 m. Averaging the speed over the whole 20 s instead invites the classic error of dropping one triangular area.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Split at the slope changes","kind":"text","content":"Distance equals the total area enclosed between the velocity-time curve and the time axis.","note":"Treat each stage of the motion separately."},
{"title":"Acceleration stage","kind":"math","content":"First area = 0.5 × 5 × 20 = 50 m","note":"A triangle of base 5 s and height 20 m/s."},
{"title":"Constant-speed stage","kind":"math","content":"Second area = 20 × 5 = 100 m","note":"A rectangle of constant height 20 m/s."},
{"title":"Deceleration stage and total","kind":"math","content":"Third area = 0.5 × 10 × 20 = 100 m, so total = 50 + 100 + 100 = 250 m","note":"Add all three contributions."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2015,14, $q$A car moving with uniform acceleration from rest covers 180 m in 6.0 s. Its acceleration and final velocity respectively are$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.0 m/s² and 30 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"7.5 m/s² and 45 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"10 m/s² and 60 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"12 m/s² and 72 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$From s = 0.5 a t², the acceleration is a = 2 × 180/36 = 10 m/s². The final velocity is a × t = 60 m/s. Pair values in which v is less than at are inconsistent and can be rejected at sight.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2015,15, $q$A 4 kg body moving east at 5 m/s collides head-on with a 6 kg body moving west at 2 m/s, and the two bodies coalesce. Their common velocity after impact is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.8 m/s east"},{"label":"B","text":"2.6 m/s west"},{"label":"C","text":"3.8 m/s east"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 m/s west"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Total momentum before impact is (4 × 5) - (6 × 2) = 8 kg m/s east. The combined 10 kg therefore moves at 8/10 = 0.8 m/s east.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2015,16, $q$A man of mass 60 kg stands on a weighing scale inside a lift that is accelerating upwards at 2 m/s². Taking g = 10 m/s², the reading of the scale is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"480 N"},{"label":"B","text":"540 N"},{"label":"C","text":"600 N"},{"label":"D","text":"720 N"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The scale registers the normal reaction R = m(g + a) = 60 × 12 = 720 N. Option A is the reading for a lift accelerating downwards, the classic trap.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2015,17, $q$A crate slides down a plane inclined at 30° to the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between the crate and the plane is 0.3. Taking g = 10 m/s², sin 30° = 0.5 and cos 30° = 0.87, the acceleration of the crate down the plane is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.4 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"2.6 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"4.4 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 m/s²"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$a = g(sin 30° - 0.3 cos 30°) = 10 × (0.5 - 0.26) = 2.4 m/s². Friction acts up the plane, so its component must be subtracted before multiplying by g.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2015,18, $q$A car of mass 800 kg rounds a circular bend of radius 20 m at a steady 10 m/s. The centripetal force acting on the car is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"400 N"},{"label":"B","text":"800 N"},{"label":"C","text":"4000 N"},{"label":"D","text":"16000 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$F = mv²/r = 800 × 100/20 = 4000 N. Because the speed is squared, doubling v would quadruple the force.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2015,19, $q$In a pulley system of velocity ratio 6, an effort of 250 N steadily raises a load of 1000 N. The efficiency of the system is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40%"},{"label":"B","text":"56%"},{"label":"C","text":"67%"},{"label":"D","text":"80%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Mechanical advantage = load/effort = 1000/250 = 4, so efficiency = MA/VR = 4/6 = 0.67 or 67%. Efficiency can never reach 100% while friction and the weight of moving parts remain.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2015,20, $q$A spring of stiffness 200 N/m is stretched slowly by 0.20 m from its unstretched length. The work done in stretching the spring is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4 J"},{"label":"B","text":"8 J"},{"label":"C","text":"20 J"},{"label":"D","text":"40 J"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$W = 0.5 k x² = 0.5 × 200 × 0.04 = 4 J. The one-half appears because the average stretching force is only half the final force.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2015,21, $q$A pump raises 200 kg of water through a height of 15 m in 60 s. Taking g = 10 m/s², the useful power of the pump is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"200 W"},{"label":"B","text":"500 W"},{"label":"C","text":"3000 W"},{"label":"D","text":"5000 W"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Work done = mgh = 200 × 10 × 15 = 30000 J, so P = 30000/60 = 500 W. Dividing by 10 s instead of 60 s gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2015,22, $q$A capillary tube of internal radius 0.02 cm is dipped vertically in a liquid of surface tension 0.07 N/m and density 700 kg/m³. Taking g = 10 m/s², the height to which the liquid rises in the tube is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"8.0 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"10 cm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$h = 2T/(density × r × g) with r = 0.0002 m, so h = (2 × 0.07)/(0.0002 × 700 × 10) = 0.14/1.4 = 0.10 m = 10 cm. Halving the tube radius would double this height.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2015,23, $q$A metal block weighs 8.0 N in air but only 6.0 N when completely immersed in water. Taking g = 10 m/s² and the density of water as 1000 kg/m³, the density of the metal is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2500 kg/m³"},{"label":"B","text":"3000 kg/m³"},{"label":"C","text":"4000 kg/m³"},{"label":"D","text":"8000 kg/m³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Upthrust = 8.0 - 6.0 = 2.0 N, hence volume = 2.0/(1000 × 10) = 0.0002 m³. The mass is 8.0/10 = 0.8 kg, so density = 0.8/0.0002 = 4000 kg/m³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2015,24, $q$A pipe closed at one end has an actual length of 24.5 cm and an end correction of 0.5 cm. If the speed of sound in air is 340 m/s, the frequency of the fundamental note of the pipe is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"170 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"255 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"340 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"425 Hz"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The effective length is 24.5 + 0.5 = 25 cm = 0.25 m. For a closed pipe the fundamental wavelength is four times this, 1.0 m, so f = v/wavelength = 340 Hz. The end correction must be added to the length before the factor of four is applied.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2015,25, $q$Ripples are produced on a pond at the rate of 4 per second and are seen to travel 12 m in 6 s. The wavelength of the ripples is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 m"},{"label":"B","text":"0.5 m"},{"label":"C","text":"2 m"},{"label":"D","text":"8 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Wave speed = distance/time = 12/6 = 2 m/s and frequency = 4 Hz, so wavelength = speed/frequency = 2/4 = 0.5 m.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2015,26, $q$Two notes of the same pitch and loudness are nevertheless heard to differ in quality or timbre. This difference is due to$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the different speeds of the two notes in air"},{"label":"B","text":"the different frequencies of the two fundamentals"},{"label":"C","text":"the different numbers and strengths of overtones present"},{"label":"D","text":"the different amplitudes of vibration"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Quality is fixed by the harmonic content, that is how many overtones are present and how strong they are. Pitch already fixes frequency and loudness fixes amplitude, so neither explains a timbre difference.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2015,27, $q$A thin converging lens of focal length 20 cm forms a real image of an object placed 30 cm from the lens. Use the lens equation to find the position of the image and its linear magnification.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12 cm from the lens and 0.4"},{"label":"B","text":"20 cm from the lens and 0.67"},{"label":"C","text":"60 cm from the lens and 0.5"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm from the lens and 2.0"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The lens equation gives 1/v = 1/20 - 1/30 = 1/60, so the image forms 60 cm from the lens. The magnification is v/u = 60/30 = 2, and the image is real and inverted.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Apply the lens equation","kind":"math","content":"1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/20 - 1/30 = (3 - 2)/60 = 1/60","note":"u and f must be in the same units."},
{"title":"Image distance","kind":"math","content":"v = 60 cm, on the far side of the lens","note":"A positive v confirms a real image."},
{"title":"Magnification","kind":"math","content":"m = v/u = 60/30 = 2","note":"Linear magnification is the image distance divided by the object distance."},
{"title":"Character of the image","kind":"text","content":"Real, inverted and twice the object size.","note":"u between f and 2f always gives an enlarged real image."}]$st$, 3),
('physics','light-optics',2015,28, $q$A piece of blue cloth is viewed at night under pure red light. The cloth appears$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"blue"},{"label":"B","text":"black"},{"label":"C","text":"white"},{"label":"D","text":"purple"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Blue cloth reflects mainly blue light and absorbs red light strongly. With only red light available, almost nothing is reflected and the cloth appears black.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2015,29, $q$The refractive index of diamond is 2.0. The critical angle for light travelling from diamond into air is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"30°"},{"label":"B","text":"45°"},{"label":"C","text":"60°"},{"label":"D","text":"90°"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The critical angle obeys sin c = 1/n = 1/2, so c = 30°. This very small critical angle is why brilliant-cut diamonds trap light internally again and again.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2015,30, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 9 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm drives a network consisting of two 2 ohm resistors connected in parallel with each other. Find the current supplied by the battery and the terminal potential difference.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6 A and 6 V"},{"label":"B","text":"4.5 A and 6.75 V"},{"label":"C","text":"6 A and 9 V"},{"label":"D","text":"12 A and 6 V"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The parallel pair is 1 ohm, so the whole circuit is 1.5 ohm and the battery current is 6 A. The terminal p.d. is 9 - 6 × 0.5 = 6 V, equal to the p.d. across the pair. Option C forgets that the battery loses volts internally whenever current flows.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Combine the two branches","kind":"math","content":"For two equal resistors in parallel, R = (2 × 2)/(2 + 2) = 1 ohm","note":"Product over sum works for any pair."},
{"title":"Add the internal resistance","kind":"math","content":"Total circuit resistance = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 ohm","note":"The internal resistance is in series with the external network."},
{"title":"Battery current","kind":"math","content":"I = e.m.f./total resistance = 9/1.5 = 6 A","note":"The full current passes through the cell itself."},
{"title":"Terminal potential difference","kind":"math","content":"V = e.m.f. - I × r = 9 - 6 × 0.5 = 6 V","note":"The lost volts inside the cell are 3 V."}]$st$, 3),
('physics','electricity',2015,31, $q$A uniform wire has a resistance of 2 ohm. If the wire is stretched so that its length is doubled while its cross-sectional area is halved, its new resistance is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 ohm"},{"label":"B","text":"2 ohm"},{"label":"C","text":"4 ohm"},{"label":"D","text":"8 ohm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$R = resistivity × length/area, so doubling the length doubles R and halving the area doubles it again: 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 ohm. Forgetting the inverse dependence on area gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2015,32, $q$A 2 kW electric heater is used continuously for 5 hours. The electrical energy it consumes is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.4 kWh"},{"label":"B","text":"2.5 kWh"},{"label":"C","text":"10 kWh"},{"label":"D","text":"40 kWh"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Energy = power × time = 2 kW × 5 h = 10 kWh. One kilowatt-hour is the energy supplied at 1 kW for one hour, equal to 3.6 megajoules.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2015,33, $q$A charged particle moving at 2 million m/s passes through a region where there is a uniform magnetic field of flux density 0.5 T. The magnetic force on the particle is zero when its velocity is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"perpendicular to the field lines"},{"label":"B","text":"parallel or antiparallel to the field lines"},{"label":"C","text":"at 60° to the field lines"},{"label":"D","text":"at 45° to the field lines"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The magnetic force is qvB sin a, where a is the angle between velocity and field. Then sin 0° is zero, so motion along a field line, either way, feels no force; the force peaks at 90°, where the sine is 1.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2015,34, $q$A horizontal wire of length 0.3 m carries a current of 4 A and is placed at right angles to a uniform magnetic field of flux density 0.5 T. The force on the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.3 N"},{"label":"B","text":"0.6 N"},{"label":"C","text":"1.2 N"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$F = BIL sin 90° = 0.5 × 4 × 0.3 = 0.6 N. Option A comes from using sin 30°, and option D from taking the length as a full metre.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2015,35, $q$A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 8 days. If the initial mass of the pure isotope is 160 g, the mass remaining after 24 days is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 g"},{"label":"B","text":"20 g"},{"label":"C","text":"40 g"},{"label":"D","text":"80 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$24 days is three half-lives: 160 g decays to 80 g, then 40 g, then 20 g. Each half-life removes half of whatever mass remains, not half of the original.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2015,36, $q$Monochromatic light of photon energy 3.0 eV falls on a metal whose work function is 1.2 eV. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.2 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"1.8 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"4.2 eV"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each photoelectron leaves with hf - W, the photon energy minus the work function: 3.0 - 1.2 = 1.8 eV. Option C is what every electron receives from the light before 1.2 eV is spent escaping the surface.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2015,37, $q$A man walks 3 km due east and then 4 km due north. The magnitude of his resultant displacement from the starting point is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 km"},{"label":"B","text":"5 km"},{"label":"C","text":"7 km"},{"label":"D","text":"12 km"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The two legs are perpendicular, so the displacement is the square root of 9 + 16 = 5 km. Adding the distances gives 7 km, the total path length, which is not a displacement.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2015,38, $q$A point charge of 0.4 coulomb placed at a certain point in an electric field experiences a force of 4 N. The electric field intensity at that point is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 N/C"},{"label":"B","text":"3.6 N/C"},{"label":"C","text":"10 N/C"},{"label":"D","text":"16 N/C"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Field intensity = force per unit charge = 4/0.4 = 10 N/C. Dividing charge by force or multiplying them gives the other options.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2016,10, $q$A student times 20 complete oscillations of a simple pendulum and records 36 s. Taking g = 10 m/s² and pi squared as 10, the length of the pendulum is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.45 m"},{"label":"B","text":"0.81 m"},{"label":"C","text":"1.60 m"},{"label":"D","text":"1.80 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The period is T = 36/20 = 1.8 s, and since T = 2 pi times the square root of l/g, l = gT²/(4 × pi squared) = 10 × 3.24/40 = 0.81 m. Timing many oscillations in one timing reduces the percentage error in T.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2016,11, $q$Which of the following is a fundamental (base) quantity in the SI system?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Energy"},{"label":"C","text":"Electric current"},{"label":"D","text":"Power"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Electric current, with the ampere as its base unit, is one of the seven SI base quantities. Force, energy and power are all derived from mass, length, time and current.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2016,12, $q$A stone is dropped from the top of a building 45 m tall. Taking g = 10 m/s², the time it takes to reach the ground is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 s"},{"label":"B","text":"3.0 s"},{"label":"C","text":"4.5 s"},{"label":"D","text":"9.0 s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$From h = 0.5 g t², t = the square root of 2 × 45/10, which is the square root of 9, so t = 3.0 s. The impact speed would be 30 m/s.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2016,13, $q$A ball is kicked from level ground with a speed of 20 m/s at 30° above the horizontal. Taking g = 10 m/s², sin 30° = 0.5 and cos 30° = 0.87, find the time of flight and the horizontal range of the ball.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 s and 8.7 m"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 s and 17.4 m"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 s and 34.8 m"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 s and 69.6 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The vertical component 10 m/s gives a flight time of 2.0 s, and the horizontal component 17.4 m/s is constant throughout. The range is therefore 17.4 × 2.0 = 34.8 m. Option B stops after finding the range for only the upward half of the flight.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Resolve the velocity","kind":"math","content":"vy = 20 × 0.5 = 10 m/s and vx = 20 × 0.87 = 17.4 m/s","note":"The vertical component controls the time of flight."},
{"title":"Time of flight","kind":"math","content":"t = 2vy/g = 2 × 10/10 = 2.0 s","note":"Time up equals time down for level ground."},
{"title":"Horizontal range","kind":"math","content":"R = vx × t = 17.4 × 2.0 = 34.8 m","note":"The horizontal component stays constant in flight."},
{"title":"Check against pitfalls","kind":"text","content":"Adding the velocities or forgetting to double the rise time gives the smaller-range options.","note":"Range needs both components used correctly."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2016,14, $q$Two trains 100 km apart on the same track travel towards each other at 60 km/h and 40 km/h. The time that elapses before they meet is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 h"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 h"},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 h"},{"label":"D","text":"2.5 h"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Their closing speed is 60 + 40 = 100 km/h, so they meet after 100/100 = 1.0 h. Opposite directions mean the velocities add.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2016,15, $q$A conical pendulum consists of a bob on a string of length 2.0 m sweeping out a cone of semi-vertical angle 60° as the bob moves in a horizontal circle. Taking g = 10 m/s² and pi squared as 10, the period of revolution of the bob is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 s"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 s"},{"label":"C","text":"2.8 s"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The period is 2 pi times the square root of L cos 60° divided by g. Substituting L = 2.0 m gives 0.1 under the root, and since T² = 4 × pi × pi × 0.1 = 4, the period is 2.0 s. It is the cosine of the semi-vertical angle that enters, never the radius.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2016,16, $q$A machine gun of mass 5 kg fires a bullet of mass 20 g horizontally with a speed of 300 m/s. The recoil velocity of the gun is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.6 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.2 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"12 m/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Total momentum is zero before firing, so 5 × v = 0.020 × 300 = 6, giving v = 1.2 m/s backwards. The bullet mass must be converted from 20 g to 0.020 kg first.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2016,17, $q$A 5 kg block is tied to a 3 kg block and both rest on a smooth horizontal table. A horizontal force of 16 N applied to the 3 kg block pulls the pair along together. The tension in the string between the blocks is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 N"},{"label":"B","text":"6 N"},{"label":"C","text":"10 N"},{"label":"D","text":"16 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The pair accelerates together at a = 16/(5 + 3) = 2 m/s². The string hauls only the 5 kg block, so T = 5 × 2 = 10 N; using the 3 kg mass instead gives the misleading 6 N of option B.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2016,18, $q$A 2.0 kg mass attached to a spring of stiffness 50 N/m executes simple horizontal oscillations on a frictionless surface. The angular frequency of the oscillation is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 rad/s"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 rad/s"},{"label":"C","text":"10 rad/s"},{"label":"D","text":"25 rad/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The angular frequency omega is the square root of k/m = the square root of 50/2 = 5.0 rad/s. Its square, 25, is what appears under the root, which is the number in option D.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2016,19, $q$In a block-and-tackle of velocity ratio 5, an effort of 225 N raises a load of 900 N at a steady speed. The efficiency of the system is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50%"},{"label":"B","text":"60%"},{"label":"C","text":"70%"},{"label":"D","text":"80%"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Mechanical advantage = 900/225 = 4, and efficiency = MA/VR = 4/5 = 0.80 or 80%. The remaining one-fifth of the effort work is spent on friction and raising the lower pulleys.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2016,20, $q$A ball of mass 0.5 kg is thrown vertically upwards with a speed of 20 m/s. Taking g = 10 m/s² and neglecting air resistance, the maximum height reached is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 m"},{"label":"B","text":"20 m"},{"label":"C","text":"40 m"},{"label":"D","text":"80 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$At the top the kinetic energy has entirely become potential energy, so h = u²/(2g) = 400/20 = 20 m. The mass is not needed for the height.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2016,21, $q$A car of mass 1000 kg moving at 20 m/s is brought uniformly to rest in a distance of 50 m. The braking force is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1000 N"},{"label":"B","text":"2000 N"},{"label":"C","text":"4000 N"},{"label":"D","text":"20000 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The kinetic energy lost is 0.5 × 1000 × 20² = 200000 J. Force × distance = energy, so F = 200000/50 = 4000 N.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2016,22, $q$An air bubble of volume 2 cm³ is released at the bottom of a lake where the total pressure is 3 atmospheres. Taking its temperature to remain constant, the volume of the bubble just before it reaches the surface, where the pressure is 1 atmosphere, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"4 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"6 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"12 cm³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Boyle's law with absolute pressures gives P1V1 = P2V2, so V2 = 3 × 2 = 6 cm³. The doubling beyond that would only follow if the pressure ratio were taken as 6.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2016,23, $q$Calculate the total heat needed to convert 0.5 kg of ice at 0 °C completely to water at 20 °C. Take the specific latent heat of fusion of ice as 330 kJ/kg and the specific heat capacity of water as 4.2 kJ per kg per kelvin.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"165 kJ"},{"label":"B","text":"186 kJ"},{"label":"C","text":"207 kJ"},{"label":"D","text":"330 kJ"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Melting the ice absorbs 0.5 × 330 = 165 kJ, and warming the melt water through 20 K absorbs 0.5 × 4.2 × 20 = 42 kJ. The two heats add to 207 kJ; option A forgets the warming stage entirely.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Fuse the ice at 0 °C","kind":"math","content":"Q1 = m × L = 0.5 × 330 = 165 kJ","note":"Melting proceeds at constant temperature."},
{"title":"Warm the melt water","kind":"math","content":"Q2 = m × c × temperature rise = 0.5 × 4.2 × 20 = 42 kJ","note":"The temperature rises only after all the ice has gone."},
{"title":"Add the stages","kind":"math","content":"Q = Q1 + Q2 = 165 + 42 = 207 kJ","note":"Both heat quantities are supplied to the same sample."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2016,24, $q$A block of wood of density 600 kg/m³ floats in a liquid of density 900 kg/m³. The fraction of the block's volume that is submerged is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1/6"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"3/4"},{"label":"D","text":"2/3"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$For floating, the weight of displaced liquid equals the weight of the block, so the submerged fraction = density of block/density of liquid = 600/900 = 2/3. The fraction is always less than 1 for a floating body.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2016,25, $q$A string fixed at both ends has a vibrating length of 0.75 m and sounds its fundamental note at 220 Hz. The speed of transverse waves on the string is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"165 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"293 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"330 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"587 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$For the fundamental of a string fixed at both ends the wavelength is 2L = 1.5 m, so v = f × wavelength = 220 × 1.5 = 330 m/s. Taking L itself as the wavelength gives the tempting 165 m/s of option A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2016,26, $q$A man standing some distance from a tall cliff claps his hands and hears the echo 1.5 s later. Taking the speed of sound as 340 m/s, the distance of the man from the cliff is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"170 m"},{"label":"B","text":"255 m"},{"label":"C","text":"340 m"},{"label":"D","text":"510 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The sound covers the distance twice in 1.5 s, so d = 340 × 1.5/2 = 255 m. Option D forgets that the journey to the cliff and back is double.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2016,27, $q$Two tuning forks of frequencies 256 Hz and 260 Hz are sounded together. The number of beats heard per second is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"256"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Beat frequency = absolute difference of the two frequencies = 260 - 256 = 4 beats per second. Waxing one fork shifts the beat rate, which is how tuners work.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2016,28, $q$An object is placed 25 cm from a concave mirror of focal length 20 cm. Find the position of the image and its linear magnification.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12.5 cm in front and 0.5"},{"label":"B","text":"45 cm in front and 1.8"},{"label":"C","text":"100 cm in front and 4.0"},{"label":"D","text":"100 cm behind and 0.25"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/20 - 1/25 = (5 - 4)/100 = 1/100, so the image is 100 cm in front of the mirror. The magnification is v/u = 100/25 = 4, real and inverted. An object between f and 2f always images beyond 2f, magnified.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Mirror equation","kind":"math","content":"1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/20 - 1/25 = 1/100","note":"Real-is-positive convention: u = 25 cm, f = 20 cm."},
{"title":"Image distance","kind":"math","content":"v = 100 cm, in front of the mirror","note":"Positive v means a real image."},
{"title":"Magnification","kind":"math","content":"m = v/u = 100/25 = 4","note":"Same size ratio as a lens."},
{"title":"Image nature","kind":"text","content":"Real, inverted and four times the object size.","note":"u between f and 2f gives a magnified real image beyond 2f."}]$st$, 3),
('physics','light-optics',2016,29, $q$Monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm passes from air into glass of refractive index 1.5. Inside the glass the light travels with$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a wavelength of 400 nm"},{"label":"B","text":"a wavelength of 600 nm"},{"label":"C","text":"a wavelength of 900 nm"},{"label":"D","text":"a speed of 4.5 × 10 to the 8th m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$On entering a denser medium the wavelength divides by the refractive index: 600/1.5 = 400 nm, and the frequency stays constant. Light can never go faster in glass than in air, so option D is physically impossible.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2016,30, $q$In additive colour mixing, beams of red light and green light are made to overlap on a white screen. The spot where they overlap appears$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"yellow"},{"label":"B","text":"magenta"},{"label":"C","text":"cyan"},{"label":"D","text":"white"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Red plus green light stimulates red and green receptors together and is seen as yellow. Magenta needs red plus blue, and cyan needs green plus blue.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2016,31, $q$A transformer has 200 turns on its primary coil and 50 turns on its secondary. If 240 V alternating voltage is applied to the primary, the voltage across the secondary is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"15 V"},{"label":"B","text":"30 V"},{"label":"C","text":"60 V"},{"label":"D","text":"480 V"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Vs = Vp × Ns/Np = 240 × 50/200 = 60 V. With fewer secondary turns than primary turns the transformer steps the voltage down, so options above 240 V are impossible.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2016,32, $q$A 10 V battery is connected across a 3 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair made up of two 4 ohm resistors. The current drawn from the battery is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.4 A"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 A"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"2.5 A"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The parallel pair has resistance (4 × 4)/(4 + 4) = 2 ohm, so the total circuit resistance is 3 + 2 = 5 ohm. Hence I = 10/5 = 2.0 A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2016,33, $q$A cell of e.m.f. 1.5 V and internal resistance 0.5 ohm sends a current through a 2.5 ohm resistor. The potential difference across the terminals of the cell while current flows is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 V"},{"label":"B","text":"1.25 V"},{"label":"C","text":"1.50 V"},{"label":"D","text":"2.00 V"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$I = 1.5/(2.5 + 0.5) = 0.5 A, so the lost volts inside the cell are 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.25 V. The terminal p.d. is therefore 1.5 - 0.25 = 1.25 V, and it equals the e.m.f. only when no current flows.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2016,34, $q$The work done in moving a charge of 5.0 coulomb through a potential difference of 12 V is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.42 J"},{"label":"B","text":"2.4 J"},{"label":"C","text":"17 J"},{"label":"D","text":"60 J"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$W = QV = 5.0 × 12 = 60 J. Dividing one number by the other gives options A and B, and adding them gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2016,35, $q$The core of a practical electromagnet, such as that of an electric bell, is made of soft iron rather than steel mainly because soft iron$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"retains its magnetism permanently"},{"label":"B","text":"has high permeability and high retentivity"},{"label":"C","text":"has high permeability and practically no retentivity"},{"label":"D","text":"is a poor conductor of electricity"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Soft iron is strongly magnetised by the coil (high permeability) yet sheds nearly all its magnetism the instant the current stops (negligible retentivity). Steel would stay magnetised and defeat the switching action of the bell.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2016,36, $q$A narrow beam from a radioactive nuclide splits into three parts when passed between two oppositely charged plates: one bends towards the negative plate, one towards the positive plate, and one goes straight through undeflected. The undeflected, most penetrating component is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"alpha particles"},{"label":"B","text":"beta particles"},{"label":"C","text":"gamma rays"},{"label":"D","text":"either alpha or beta particles"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Gamma rays carry no charge, so no electric force acts on them and they pass straight through the field. They are also far more penetrating than the charged alpha and beta components, which bend in opposite directions because of their opposite charges.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2023,10, $q$Which of the following is the correct dimensional formula for power?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"MLT⁻²"},{"label":"B","text":"ML²T⁻²"},{"label":"C","text":"ML²T⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"ML⁻¹T⁻²"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Power = energy divided by time = ML²T⁻²/T = ML²T⁻³. Option B is energy or work, and option D is pressure.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2023,11, $q$The most suitable instrument for measuring accurately the internal diameter of the mouth of a beaker is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"micrometer screw gauge"},{"label":"B","text":"vernier calipers"},{"label":"C","text":"metre rule"},{"label":"D","text":"spherometer"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Vernier calipers carry inside jaws made exactly for internal diameters and read to 0.01 cm. A screw gauge grips small external dimensions only, and a rule is far too coarse.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2023,12, $q$A stone is thrown horizontally at 15 m/s from the top of a tower 20 m high. Taking g = 10 m/s², the speed with which it strikes the ground is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"15 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"20 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"25 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"35 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The stone takes 2.0 s to fall, arriving with a vertical velocity of 20 m/s while keeping the horizontal 15 m/s. Combining the perpendicular components by Pythagoras gives the square root of 625, which is 25 m/s. Option D simply adds the two velocities.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Find the time of fall","kind":"math","content":"t = sqrt(2h/g) = sqrt(2 × 20/10) = sqrt(4) = 2.0 s","note":"Only the vertical motion fixes the time."},
{"title":"Velocity components at impact","kind":"math","content":"vx = 15 m/s (unchanged) and vy = g × t = 10 × 2 = 20 m/s","note":"Gravity acts on vy alone."},
{"title":"Combine with Pythagoras","kind":"math","content":"v = sqrt(vx² + vy²) = sqrt(225 + 400) = sqrt(625) = 25 m/s","note":"Adding 15 + 20 directly is the classic error."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2023,13, $q$A body starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 4 m/s² for 5 s, after which it continues at the constant speed it has attained. The total distance covered in the first 10 s is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"100 m"},{"label":"B","text":"120 m"},{"label":"C","text":"150 m"},{"label":"D","text":"200 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In the first stage s = 0.5 × 4 × 25 = 50 m and the speed reaches 4 × 5 = 20 m/s. Cruising for the next 5 s adds 100 m, for a total of 150 m. Option A forgets the second stage altogether.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2023,14, $q$A ball is thrown vertically upwards with an initial speed of 40 m/s. Taking g = 10 m/s², the maximum height it reaches is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40 m"},{"label":"B","text":"80 m"},{"label":"C","text":"120 m"},{"label":"D","text":"160 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$At the highest point v = 0 and from v² = u² − 2gh, h = u²/(2g) = 1600/20 = 80 m. The whole flight lasts 8 s, of which 4 s is the rise.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2023,15, $q$A 2 kg sphere moving at 6 m/s collides head-on with a stationary 2 kg sphere in a perfectly elastic collision. The velocity of the second sphere immediately after impact is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"3 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"6 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"12 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Equal masses in a head-on perfectly elastic collision simply exchange velocities, so the incoming sphere stops and the second moves off at 6 m/s. Both momentum, 12 kg m/s, and kinetic energy, 36 J, are conserved this way.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2023,16, $q$A force of 20 N acts on a body of mass 2 kg for 0.5 s. The impulse given to the body is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 N s"},{"label":"B","text":"10 N s"},{"label":"C","text":"20 N s"},{"label":"D","text":"40 N s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Impulse = force × time = 20 × 0.5 = 10 N s, equal to the momentum gained. Starting from rest the body would reach 5 m/s, the value of option A disguised as a velocity.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2023,17, $q$A 0.5 kg mass attached to a spring of stiffness 20 N/m oscillates horizontally on a frictionless surface. Taking pi squared as 10, the period of the oscillation is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 s"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 s"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$For a spring-mass oscillator the period is 2 pi times the square root of m/k. With m/k = 0.025 and T² = 4 × pi × pi × 0.025 = 1.0, the period is 1.0 s exactly.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2023,18, $q$A 6 kg mass and a 4 kg mass hang on opposite sides of a frictionless pulley. Taking g = 10 m/s², the tension in the string while the masses move is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40 N"},{"label":"B","text":"44 N"},{"label":"C","text":"48 N"},{"label":"D","text":"60 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The net driving force is 20 N on a total mass of 10 kg, giving a = 2 m/s². For the rising 4 kg mass, T − 40 = 4 × 2, so T = 48 N.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2023,19, $q$In a hydraulic press the small piston has area 0.002 m² and the large piston has area 0.2 m². An effort of 50 N applied to the small piston raises a maximum load. The mechanical advantage of the press is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50"},{"label":"B","text":"100"},{"label":"C","text":"200"},{"label":"D","text":"400"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Pressure is transmitted equally, so the load is 50 × (0.2/0.002) = 50 × 100 = 5000 N. Mechanical advantage = load/effort = 100. Inverting the area ratio gives option A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2023,20, $q$A crate is pulled 10 m along a horizontal floor while a horizontal force of 100 N is applied to it. A constant frictional force of 40 N opposes the motion. The work done against friction is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"200 J"},{"label":"B","text":"400 J"},{"label":"C","text":"600 J"},{"label":"D","text":"1000 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Work against friction = frictional force × distance = 40 × 10 = 400 J. Option D is the work done by the applied force, and option C is the net work that goes into kinetic energy.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2023,21, $q$A fixed mass of gas held in a sealed container of constant volume has a pressure of 1.5 atmospheres at 27 °C (300 K). When it is heated to 127 °C (400 K) its new pressure is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 atm"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 atm"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 atm"},{"label":"D","text":"3.0 atm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At constant volume pressure is proportional to absolute temperature, so P2 = 1.5 × 400/300 = 2.0 atm. Treating the pressure as unchanged gives B, and arranging the ratio upside down gives A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2023,22, $q$An electric heater rated 100 W boils away 0.02 kg of a liquid already at its boiling point in 10 minutes of operation. The specific latent heat of vaporisation of the liquid is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60 kJ/kg"},{"label":"B","text":"600 kJ/kg"},{"label":"C","text":"1500 kJ/kg"},{"label":"D","text":"3000 kJ/kg"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Energy supplied = 100 × 600 = 60000 J, and dividing by the 0.02 kg evaporated gives L = 3 000 000 J/kg, that is 3000 kJ/kg. Option A quotes the total energy as if it were the latent heat, per kilogram.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2023,23, $q$A rectangular block weighs 2.0 N in air and 1.6 N when fully immersed in a liquid of density 800 kg/m³. Taking g = 10 m/s², the volume of the block is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"20 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"40 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"50 cm³"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Upthrust = 2.0 − 1.6 = 0.4 N = weight of displaced liquid = V × 800 × 10. Hence V = 0.4/8000 = 0.00005 m³ = 50 cm³. Using the density of water instead gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2023,24, $q$An open pipe of actual length 49.0 cm has an end correction of 0.5 cm at each of its two vibrating ends. Taking the speed of sound as 330 m/s, the frequency of the first overtone of the pipe is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"330 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"440 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"660 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"880 Hz"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The effective length is 49.0 + 2 × 0.5 = 50.0 cm = 0.50 m. The fundamental wavelength of an open pipe is twice this, 1.0 m, giving a fundamental of 330 Hz; the first overtone is the second harmonic, at 660 Hz. Option A is the fundamental, not the overtone asked for.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2023,25, $q$Twenty complete waves pass a fixed point in 4.0 s, and the wavelength of the waves is 0.5 m. The speed of the waves is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"5.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"10 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Frequency = 20/4.0 = 5 Hz, so speed = f × wavelength = 5 × 0.5 = 2.5 m/s. Option C is the frequency parading as a speed, with the unit changed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2023,26, $q$The loudness of a musical note is chiefly determined by the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"amplitude of vibration of the source"},{"label":"B","text":"frequency of the source"},{"label":"C","text":"wavelength of the note in air"},{"label":"D","text":"speed of the note in air"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A larger amplitude carries more energy per second past a given point, which the ear reports as louder. Frequency controls pitch instead, and speed and wavelength do not vary with how hard the source is struck.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2023,27, $q$Two thin converging lenses, of focal lengths 20 cm and 50 cm, are placed in contact with each other. The power of the combination is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 dioptres"},{"label":"B","text":"3 dioptres"},{"label":"C","text":"5 dioptres"},{"label":"D","text":"7 dioptres"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In metres the focal lengths are 0.20 m and 0.50 m, so their powers are 5.0 dioptres and 2.0 dioptres. Powers in contact simply add: 5.0 + 2.0 = 7 dioptres. Option B is their difference, a common slip.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2023,28, $q$A coin lies at the bottom of a beaker of water 12 cm deep. Taking the refractive index of water as 4/3, the apparent depth of the coin below the surface is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"9 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"12 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"16 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Real depth divided by apparent depth equals the refractive index, so apparent depth = 12 × 3/4 = 9 cm. Multiplying by 4/3 instead gives the impossible 16 cm of option D.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2023,29, $q$In a glass prism, the colour of light that travels fastest in the glass and is therefore deviated least is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"violet"},{"label":"B","text":"green"},{"label":"C","text":"yellow"},{"label":"D","text":"red"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The refractive index of glass is smallest for red light, and since speed = c/n, red also has the greatest speed in glass. Violet is slowed the most and deviated the most.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2023,30, $q$A 2 microfarad capacitor is charged to 100 V and then disconnected from the supply. It is reconnected in parallel with an uncharged 3 microfarad capacitor. The common potential difference across the pair is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20 V"},{"label":"B","text":"40 V"},{"label":"C","text":"50 V"},{"label":"D","text":"100 V"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The charge 2 × 100 = 200 microcoulomb is conserved and now sits on 2 + 3 = 5 microfarad, giving V = 200/5 = 40 V. Option D is what the p.d. would stay at if no charge shared at all.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Compute the stored charge","kind":"math","content":"Q = C × V = 2 × 100 = 200 microcoulomb","note":"The capacitor is disconnected, so the charge cannot leak away."},
{"title":"Total capacitance in parallel","kind":"math","content":"C = 2 + 3 = 5 microfarad","note":"In parallel, capacitances simply add."},
{"title":"Divide charge by capacitance","kind":"math","content":"V = Q/C = 200/5 = 40 V","note":"Charge flows until both plates reach a common potential."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','electricity',2023,31, $q$A 4 ohm resistor carries a current of 2 A for 10 s. The heat energy dissipated in the resistor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40 J"},{"label":"B","text":"80 J"},{"label":"C","text":"160 J"},{"label":"D","text":"320 J"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$W = I²Rt = 2² × 4 × 10 = 160 J. Dividing by two gives option B, and swapping the formula for I × R × t gives other wrong values.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2023,32, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 12 V and internal resistance 1 ohm is connected in series with a 2 ohm resistor and a 9 ohm resistor. Taking all connections to be ideal, find the current in the circuit and the terminal potential difference of the battery.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 A and 11.5 V"},{"label":"B","text":"0.5 A and 12 V"},{"label":"C","text":"1.0 A and 11 V"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 A and 12 V"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The external resistance is 2 + 9 = 11 ohm, so with the internal 1 ohm the total is 12 ohm and I = 12/12 = 1.0 A. The terminal p.d. is 12 − 1 × 1 = 11 V; option D wrongly reports the e.m.f.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Combine the series resistors","kind":"math","content":"External resistance = 2 + 9 = 11 ohm","note":"In series, resistances add."},
{"title":"Include the cell itself","kind":"math","content":"Total circuit resistance = 11 + 1 = 12 ohm","note":"Current must pass through the internal resistance too."},
{"title":"Current from the battery","kind":"math","content":"I = e.m.f./total = 12/12 = 1.0 A","note":"The same 1 A flows through every element."},
{"title":"Terminal potential difference","kind":"math","content":"V = e.m.f. − I × r = 12 − 1 = 11 V","note":"The lost volts inside the battery are I × r = 1 V."}]$st$, 3),
('physics','electricity',2023,33, $q$Two parallel metal plates 4 mm apart are connected to a 12 V battery. The electric field intensity between the plates is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"300 V/m"},{"label":"B","text":"3000 V/m"},{"label":"C","text":"30000 V/m"},{"label":"D","text":"3000000 V/m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$E = V/d = 12/0.004 = 3000 V/m. Neglecting to convert the 4 mm to metres gives option A, greater by exactly a factor of ten.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2023,34, $q$A straight wire of length 20 cm carrying a current of 3 A lies at right angles to a uniform magnetic field of flux density 0.5 T. The force on the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.15 N"},{"label":"B","text":"0.30 N"},{"label":"C","text":"0.60 N"},{"label":"D","text":"3.00 N"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$F = BIL sin 90° = 0.5 × 3 × 0.2 = 0.3 N. Halving any one of the three factors gives option A, and doubling the field would double the force to option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2023,35, $q$A transformer whose primary is connected to a steady (constant) direct voltage delivers no power to its secondary load. The reason is that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a steady current sets up an unchanging magnetic flux, so no e.m.f. is induced in the secondary"},{"label":"B","text":"the primary coil will burn out at once"},{"label":"C","text":"direct current cannot flow in a solenoid"},{"label":"D","text":"the core becomes permanently magnetised and blocks the current"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Transformer action depends on Faraday's law: only a changing flux induces an e.m.f. in the secondary. A constant direct current gives a constant flux, hence zero induced e.m.f., even though the primary current flows normally.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2023,36, $q$A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 5 years. If 80 g of the pure isotope is stored, the mass remaining after 15 years is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 g"},{"label":"B","text":"5 g"},{"label":"C","text":"10 g"},{"label":"D","text":"20 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Fifteen years is three half-lives: 80 g becomes 40 g, then 20 g, then 10 g. Choosing 20 g amounts to allowing only two half-lives.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2023,37, $q$Light whose photons each carry 6 eV of energy falls on a metal whose work function is 2 eV. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"4 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"6 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"8 eV"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each escaping electron spends the work function getting out and keeps the rest: 6 − 2 = 4 eV. The photon energy of 6 eV in option C is what is absorbed before the escape cost is deducted.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2023,38, $q$When a nucleus emits an alpha particle, the changes in its mass number A and atomic number Z are:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"A falls by 4 and Z falls by 2"},{"label":"B","text":"A falls by 2 and Z falls by 4"},{"label":"C","text":"A falls by 2 and Z falls by 2"},{"label":"D","text":"neither A nor Z changes"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$An alpha particle is a helium nucleus with 2 protons and 2 neutrons, so A drops by 4 and Z drops by 2. Charge and mass number are both conserved across the transmutation as a whole.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2024,10, $q$A micrometer screw gauge reads 4.28 mm for the diameter of a wire, but the gauge is known to have a negative zero error of 0.02 mm. The true diameter of the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4.24 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"4.26 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"4.28 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"4.30 mm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A negative zero error means the instrument reads low, so the correction is added: true = observed + 0.02 = 4.30 mm. Subtracting again would give 4.26 mm, option B, the classic slip.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2024,11, $q$The wavelength of green light in air is about 600 nm. Expressed in metres, this is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6 × 10⁻⁹ m"},{"label":"B","text":"6 × 10⁻⁸ m"},{"label":"C","text":"6 × 10⁻⁷ m"},{"label":"D","text":"6 × 10⁻⁵ m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$One nanometre is 10⁻⁹ m, so 600 nm = 600 × 10⁻⁹ = 6 × 10⁻⁷ m. Option A forgets the multiplying 600 and keeps only the nanometre factor.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2024,12, $q$A cyclist moving at 12 m/s applies the brakes uniformly and comes to rest in 6.0 s. The distance covered while braking is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"18 m"},{"label":"B","text":"36 m"},{"label":"C","text":"72 m"},{"label":"D","text":"144 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Average speed during uniform slowing is (12 + 0)/2 = 6 m/s, so distance = 6 m/s × 6.0 s = 36 m. Using the initial speed for the whole time gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2024,13, $q$A ball is projected from ground level with a speed of 20 m/s at 45° to the horizontal. Taking g = 10 m/s², the horizontal range of the ball is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20 m"},{"label":"B","text":"40 m"},{"label":"C","text":"56.6 m"},{"label":"D","text":"80 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Range = u² sin(2a)/g = 400 × sin 90°/10 = 40 m, since sin 90° = 1, with a the launch angle. Taking a as 45° but inserting sin 45° for sin 90° gives about 28.3 m, and doubling the answer gives option D.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2024,14, $q$A train 100 m long travelling at a steady 10 m/s crosses a platform 200 m long. The time taken for the train to pass completely through the platform is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 s"},{"label":"B","text":"20 s"},{"label":"C","text":"30 s"},{"label":"D","text":"60 s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The front of the train must cover the platform length plus its own 100 m before the rear clears: 300 m at 10 m/s. That is 30 s; using only the platform length gives 20 s, option B.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2024,15, $q$A conical pendulum has a string of length 1.25 m whose bob sweeps out a horizontal circle at a semi-vertical angle of 60°. Taking g = 10 m/s², sin 60° = 0.87, cos 60° = 0.5 and pi squared as 10, the period of revolution of the bob is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.6 s"},{"label":"B","text":"2.1 s"},{"label":"C","text":"3.1 s"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$T = 2 pi times the square root of L cos 60°/g. Substituting, L cos 60° = 0.625 m and the number under the root is 0.0625, whose root is 0.25, so T = 2 pi × 0.25 = 1.57 s, about 1.6 s. Using sin 60° instead of cos gives the 2.1 s of option B.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Take the vertical part of the string","kind":"math","content":"L cos 60° = 1.25 × 0.5 = 0.625 m","note":"The vertical component of the string controls the period."},
{"title":"Divide by gravity","kind":"math","content":"0.625/10 = 0.0625","note":"This is the quantity under the square root."},
{"title":"Root and scale","kind":"math","content":"Square root of 0.0625 = 0.25, so T = 2 × pi × 0.25 = 1.57 s","note":"With pi squared as 10: T² = 4 × 10 × 0.0625 = 2.5."},
{"title":"Check the angle error","kind":"text","content":"Using sin 60° would give 0.108 under the root and T of about 2.1 s.","note":"Cosine, never sine, is correct for the semi-vertical angle."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2024,16, $q$A 3 kg body moving at 4 m/s collides head-on with a 2 kg body moving at 1 m/s in the opposite direction, and they coalesce. Their common velocity is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"2.5 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Total momentum = (3 × 4) − (2 × 1) = 10 kg m/s in the direction of the heavier body. Divided by the 5 kg total mass this gives 2.0 m/s.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2024,17, $q$A box of mass 10 kg is dragged along a floor by a horizontal pull of 40 N. The coefficient of friction between box and floor is 0.2. Taking g = 10 m/s², the acceleration of the box is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"4.0 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 m/s²"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Friction = 0.2 × 10 × 10 = 20 N, so the resultant is 40 − 20 = 20 N and a = 20/10 = 2.0 m/s². Forgetting to subtract friction gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2024,18, $q$A crowbar has an effort arm of 1.5 m and a load arm of 0.3 m. An effort of 160 N just lifts a load of 700 N. The efficiency of the crowbar used as a machine is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"62.5%"},{"label":"B","text":"70%"},{"label":"C","text":"80%"},{"label":"D","text":"87.5%"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Velocity ratio = 1.5/0.3 = 5 and mechanical advantage = 700/160 = 4.375. Efficiency = MA/VR = 4.375/5 = 0.875, that is 87.5%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2024,19, $q$A spring of stiffness 100 N/m is compressed by 0.10 m and then released against a ball of mass 0.2 kg, which shoots away horizontally on a frictionless surface. Taking g = 10 m/s², the launch speed of the ball is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.2 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"7.5 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"10.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Stored elastic energy = 0.5 × 100 × 0.01 = 0.5 J, all delivered to the ball. Then v = the square root of 2 × 0.5/0.2 = the square root of 5, about 2.2 m/s. Option B forgets to take the square root of 5.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2024,20, $q$A body of mass 1.0 kg falls from rest through a height of 5 m onto a floor. Taking g = 10 m/s², the distance through which a constant frictional force of 20 N stops it on impact is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.25 m"},{"label":"B","text":"2.5 m"},{"label":"C","text":"5 m"},{"label":"D","text":"10 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$On reaching the floor the body carries kinetic energy mgh = 50 J. A frictional force of 20 N removes this over 50/20 = 2.5 m. Using the fall height directly gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2024,21, $q$A fixed mass of gas at constant pressure occupies 4.0 litres at 27 °C. If it is heated to 177 °C (that is, from 300 K to 450 K), its volume becomes$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.7 litres"},{"label":"B","text":"4.0 litres"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0 litres"},{"label":"D","text":"8.0 litres"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Charles' law needs kelvin: V2 = V1 × T2/T1 = 4.0 × 450/300 = 6.0 litres. Inverting the temperature ratio gives 2.7 litres, option A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2024,22, $q$0.4 kg of water at 80 °C is mixed with 0.2 kg of water at 20 °C in an insulated vessel, and no heat is lost to the vessel. The steady final temperature of the mixture is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"30 °C"},{"label":"B","text":"50 °C"},{"label":"C","text":"60 °C"},{"label":"D","text":"80 °C"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Heat lost = heat gained: 0.4 × c × (80 − T) = 0.2 × c × (T − 20), so 32 − 0.4T = 0.2T − 4 and T = 60 °C. A straight average of the two temperatures gives the misleading 50 °C of option B; the unequal masses must weight the average.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2024,23, $q$A copper block of mass 0.5 kg and specific heat capacity 400 J/kg·K is heated steadily from 20 °C to 70 °C. The heat it absorbs is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 kJ"},{"label":"B","text":"10 kJ"},{"label":"C","text":"20 kJ"},{"label":"D","text":"40 kJ"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Q = mcΔθ = 0.5 × 400 × 50 = 10000 J = 10 kJ. The temperature rise of 50 K is what matters; using 10 °C as the rise gives option A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2024,24, $q$Water rises in a capillary tube of internal radius 0.5 mm dipped in water of surface tension 0.07 N/m and density 1000 kg/m³. Taking g = 10 m/s², the capillary rise is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.7 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"1.4 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"2.8 cm"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$h = 2T/(density × r × g) with r = 0.0005 m, so h = 0.14/(0.0005 × 1000 × 10) = 0.14/5 = 0.028 m = 2.8 cm. Forgetting to convert the radius from millimetres blows the height up tenfold.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2024,25, $q$A sonometer wire of vibrating length 0.4 m carries transverse waves at 160 m/s. The fundamental frequency of the wire is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"200 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"400 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"600 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"800 Hz"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$For the fundamental mode the wire holds half a wavelength: wavelength = 2 × 0.4 = 0.8 m. Hence f = 160/0.8 = 200 Hz; the higher values listed are the successive harmonics.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2024,26, $q$An electric bell ringing inside a sealed glass jar grows fainter and finally inaudible as the air is pumped out of the jar, although the hammer keeps striking the bell. This observation shows that$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sound needs a material medium to travel through"},{"label":"B","text":"the hammer stops striking the bell in a vacuum"},{"label":"C","text":"sound travels faster in a vacuum than in air"},{"label":"D","text":"only high frequency sounds need air"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The bell keeps vibrating, yet with no air or other medium between bell and wall no sound reaches us. Light and other electromagnetic waves would cross the vacuum unchanged, which is precisely why sound differs from light here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2024,27, $q$The amplitude of a vibrating tuning fork is trebled (multiplied by three). The intensity of the note it emits is multiplied by$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"9"},{"label":"D","text":"27"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Intensity is proportional to the square of amplitude, so tripling the amplitude multiplies the intensity by 3² = 9. Option D cubes the factor, which has no basis in the wave equations.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2024,28, $q$An object stands 3.0 m in front of a convex driving mirror of radius of curvature 2.0 m. Use the mirror equation to find the position and linear magnification of its image.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.75 m in front of the mirror and 4.0"},{"label":"B","text":"0.75 m behind the mirror and 0.25"},{"label":"C","text":"0.40 m behind the mirror and 0.40"},{"label":"D","text":"3.0 m behind the mirror and 1.0"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$With the real-is-positive convention, f = −R/2 = −1.0 m for a convex mirror, and 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/1 − 1/3 = −4/3. Hence v = −0.75 m, a virtual image 0.75 m behind the mirror, with m = v/u = 0.75/3.0 = 0.25. Convex mirrors always shrink and can never form a real image, which kills options A and D at sight.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Fix the focal length and signs","kind":"math","content":"For a convex mirror f = −R/2 = −1.0 m, u = +3.0 m","note":"Real-is-positive: convex mirrors take negative f."},
{"title":"Solve the mirror equation","kind":"math","content":"1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1.0 − 0.33 = −1.33 per metre","note":"Rearranged: 1/v = 1/f − 1/u."},
{"title":"Image position","kind":"math","content":"v = −0.75 m, so the image is 0.75 m behind the mirror","note":"A negative v is a virtual image."},
{"title":"Magnification","kind":"math","content":"m = v/u = 0.75/3.0 = 0.25","note":"Virtual images are upright and diminished."}]$st$, 3),
('physics','light-optics',2024,29, $q$When a ray of monochromatic light passes from air into glass, the quantity that remains unchanged is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"its speed"},{"label":"B","text":"its wavelength"},{"label":"C","text":"its frequency"},{"label":"D","text":"its direction of travel"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Only the frequency is decided by the source itself; speed, wavelength and direction all change at the boundary. That is why refraction bends the ray without altering its colour.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2024,30, $q$An astronomical telescope in normal adjustment has an objective of focal length 80 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 5 cm. The distance between the two lenses is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"16 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"75 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"85 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"400 cm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In normal adjustment the tube length equals the sum of the focal lengths: 80 + 5 = 85 cm. Option A is the angular magnification, 80/5 = 16, not a length.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2024,31, $q$A 4 microfarad capacitor is charged to 200 V and disconnected from the supply. It is then joined in parallel with an uncharged 1 microfarad capacitor. The common potential difference across the pair is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"80 V"},{"label":"B","text":"100 V"},{"label":"C","text":"160 V"},{"label":"D","text":"200 V"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The conserved charge is 4 × 200 = 800 microcoulomb, now shared over 4 + 1 = 5 microfarad. V = 800/5 = 160 V; option D assumes no charge transfer takes place.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge before connection","kind":"math","content":"Q = C × V = 4 × 200 = 800 microcoulomb","note":"Once disconnected, the charge is trapped."},
{"title":"Combine the capacitances","kind":"math","content":"Parallel pair: C = 4 + 1 = 5 microfarad","note":"Parallel capacitances add directly."},
{"title":"Shared potential difference","kind":"math","content":"V = Q/C = 800/5 = 160 V","note":"Charge flows until both capacitors reach the same p.d."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','electricity',2024,32, $q$Two lamps, one rated 60 W and the other 100 W for the same 240 V mains, are connected in series across that mains. The lamp that glows brighter is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the 100 W lamp"},{"label":"B","text":"the 60 W lamp"},{"label":"C","text":"both lamps glow equally"},{"label":"D","text":"neither lamp produces light"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The 60 W lamp has the higher resistance, and in a series circuit they carry the same current, so P = I²R makes the 60 W lamp dissipate more power. In parallel the verdict would reverse, which is the classic trap.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2024,33, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 24 V and internal resistance 1 ohm drives an external load made up of a 5 ohm and a 6 ohm resistor in series. The rate at which heat is generated inside the battery is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 W"},{"label":"B","text":"4 W"},{"label":"C","text":"24 W"},{"label":"D","text":"48 W"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Total resistance = 5 + 6 + 1 = 12 ohm, so I = 24/12 = 2 A. The heating inside the battery is I²r = 2² × 1 = 4 W; option D is the power drawn from the whole circuit, not the internal loss.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2024,34, $q$Three resistors, of 2 ohm, 3 ohm and 6 ohm, are to be connected to a circuit so that the combination has an equivalent resistance of exactly 4 ohm. The correct arrangement is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"all three in series"},{"label":"B","text":"all three in parallel"},{"label":"C","text":"the 3 ohm and 6 ohm in parallel, in series with the 2 ohm"},{"label":"D","text":"the 2 ohm and 3 ohm in parallel, in series with the 6 ohm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The 3 ohm and 6 ohm in parallel give 2 ohm, and adding the series 2 ohm yields exactly 4 ohm. All-series gives 11 ohm and all-parallel gives 1 ohm, matching options A and B.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2024,35, $q$According to Lenz's law, the direction of an induced current in a circuit is always such that its magnetic effect$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"creates additional magnetic flux in the circuit"},{"label":"B","text":"opposes the change that produces it"},{"label":"C","text":"assists the change that produces it"},{"label":"D","text":"depends only on the resistance of the coil"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Lenz's law is energy conservation in disguise: the induced current must oppose the change, or free energy would appear from nothing. This is the minus sign in Faraday's law.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2024,36, $q$Light of photon energy 3.5 eV falls on a metal surface whose work function is 1.5 eV. The maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons emitted is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"3.5 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 eV"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each photoelectron keeps the photon energy less the escape cost: hf − W = 3.5 − 1.5 = 2.0 eV. Option C is the energy delivered by each photon and option D their sum, which has no physical meaning here.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('physics','measurement',2025,10, $q$A micrometer screw gauge of least count 0.01 mm is closed on a wire. Its main scale reads 2.5 mm and the thimble scale coincides at the 32nd division. The reading of the gauge is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.50 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"2.82 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"3.20 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"5.70 mm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Reading = main scale + 32 × 0.01 = 2.5 + 0.32 = 2.82 mm. Option D would follow if the least count were taken as 0.1 mm, ten times too large.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','measurement',2025,11, $q$Which of the following physical quantities has the same dimensions as work?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Momentum"},{"label":"C","text":"Power"},{"label":"D","text":"Moment of a force"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$A moment is force × distance, precisely the definition of work, so both have ML²T⁻². Power differs by one further division by time.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2025,12, $q$Two boats 60 m apart are approaching each other head-on with speeds of 4 m/s and 2 m/s. The time elapsed before they collide is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 s"},{"label":"B","text":"10 s"},{"label":"C","text":"15 s"},{"label":"D","text":"30 s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Closing speed = 4 + 2 = 6 m/s, so they meet after 60/6 = 10 s. Considering only one boat's motion over the gap gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2025,13, $q$A stone is thrown horizontally at 10 m/s from the top of a cliff 125 m high. Taking g = 10 m/s², the horizontal distance from the foot of the cliff at which the stone lands is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"25 m"},{"label":"B","text":"50 m"},{"label":"C","text":"100 m"},{"label":"D","text":"125 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The stone takes t = the square root of 2 × 125/10 = 5 s to fall. The horizontal range is u × t = 10 × 5 = 50 m. Option D confuses the fall height with the range.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','kinematics',2025,14, $q$A car travelling at 20 m/s brakes at a uniform deceleration of 2 m/s² until it stops. The distance covered during the braking is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"50 m"},{"label":"B","text":"100 m"},{"label":"C","text":"200 m"},{"label":"D","text":"400 m"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$From v² = u² − 2as with v = 0, s = 400/4 = 100 m. Option C omits the factor of two in the equation of motion.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2025,15, $q$A mass of 0.5 kg hangs from a spring of stiffness 200 N/m and executes simple harmonic motion with amplitude 5 cm. Taking g = 10 m/s², the maximum speed of the mass during the oscillation is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"0.5 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"1.0 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 m/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The angular frequency is the square root of k/m = the square root of 400 = 20 rad/s. Maximum speed = omega × A = 20 × 0.05 = 1.0 m/s, reached as the mass passes the equilibrium position. Option D quotes the angular frequency with a speed unit attached.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2025,16, $q$A 4 kg body moving at 3 m/s overtakes and coalesces with a 2 kg body at rest. Taking g = 10 m/s² where needed, the kinetic energy lost in the collision is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4 J"},{"label":"B","text":"6 J"},{"label":"C","text":"12 J"},{"label":"D","text":"18 J"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Momentum conservation gives a common velocity of 12/6 = 2 m/s. Kinetic energy falls from 0.5 × 4 × 9 = 18 J to 0.5 × 6 × 4 = 12 J, a loss of 6 J; options C and D are the final and initial kinetic energies.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','dynamics',2025,17, $q$A car of mass 1200 kg moves at 20 m/s round a level circular track of radius 30 m. The centripetal force required is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1600 N"},{"label":"B","text":"4000 N"},{"label":"C","text":"8000 N"},{"label":"D","text":"16000 N"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$F = mv²/r = 1200 × 400/30 = 16000 N. Dropping one factor of ten gives option C, and using the speed but not squaring it gives far smaller values.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2025,18, $q$In a pulley system of velocity ratio 4, an effort of 200 N just raises a load of 600 N at a steady speed. The efficiency of the system is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"62.5%"},{"label":"B","text":"70%"},{"label":"C","text":"75%"},{"label":"D","text":"80%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Mechanical advantage = 600/200 = 3, and efficiency = MA/VR = 3/4 = 0.75 or 75%. No real pulley system reaches 100% because friction and easing of moving parts always waste some effort.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2025,19, $q$A crane motor lifts a 500 kg load at a steady vertical speed of 2 m/s. Taking g = 10 m/s², the useful power output of the motor is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 kW"},{"label":"B","text":"10 kW"},{"label":"C","text":"25 kW"},{"label":"D","text":"50 kW"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The lifting force equals the weight, 5000 N, so P = force × speed = 5000 × 2 = 10000 W = 10 kW. At a steady speed there is no extra accelerating force to add.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','work-energy',2025,20, $q$A simple pendulum of length 1.25 m has its bob, of mass 0.2 kg, pulled aside until the string is horizontal and then released from rest. Taking g = 10 m/s², the tension in the string when the bob passes through the lowest point is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2 N"},{"label":"B","text":"4 N"},{"label":"C","text":"6 N"},{"label":"D","text":"12 N"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$At the low point the bob has speed v = the square root of 2gh = the square root of 25 = 5 m/s. The string must supply the weight 2 N plus the centripetal force mv²/L = 4 N, a total tension of 6 N. Subtracting instead of adding gives 2 N, option A.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Speed at the lowest point","kind":"math","content":"From energy: v = sqrt(2gh) = sqrt(2 × 10 × 1.25) = sqrt(25) = 5 m/s","note":"The bob falls a vertical distance equal to the string length."},
{"title":"Weight term","kind":"math","content":"mg = 0.2 × 10 = 2 N","note":"The weight acts downwards along the string."},
{"title":"Centripetal term","kind":"math","content":"mv²/L = 0.2 × 25/1.25 = 4 N","note":"At the lowest point this also acts along the string, towards the centre."},
{"title":"Add the two forces","kind":"math","content":"T = 2 + 4 = 6 N","note":"Adding, not subtracting, is correct at the bottom of the swing."}]$st$, 3),
('physics','fluids-heat',2025,21, $q$An air bubble of volume 1 cm³ is released at the bottom of a lake where the total pressure is 4 atmospheres. With its temperature unchanged, the volume of the bubble when it reaches the surface at 1 atmosphere is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"2 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"3 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"4 cm³"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Boyle's law on absolute pressures: P1V1 = P2V2, so V2 = 4 × 1 = 4 cm³. Option C uses the gauge pressure of 3 atmospheres instead of the absolute 4, a favourite trap.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Pin down the absolute pressure","kind":"text","content":"Absolute pressure at the bottom = 4 atmospheres, since the depth adds 3 atmospheres of water to the atmospheric 1.","note":"Boyle's law never uses gauge pressure."},
{"title":"Apply Boyle's law","kind":"math","content":"P1 × V1 = P2 × V2, so 4 × 1 = 1 × V2","note":"Temperature is constant throughout the rise."},
{"title":"Solve for the new volume","kind":"math","content":"V2 = 4 cm³ at the surface","note":"Quartering the pressure quarters the density and quadruples the volume."}]$st$, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2025,22, $q$An electric kettle rated 2 kW brings 2 kg of water from 25 °C to 100 °C. Taking the specific heat capacity of water as 4.2 kJ/kg·K, the heating time is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"105 s"},{"label":"B","text":"210 s"},{"label":"C","text":"315 s"},{"label":"D","text":"630 s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Heat required = 2 × 4.2 × 75 = 630 kJ. At 2 kJ/s the kettle needs 630/2 = 315 s, or 5.25 minutes; option D forgets the 2 kW rating and takes 1 kJ per second.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','fluids-heat',2025,23, $q$A steel rod of length 2.0 m and linear expansivity 0.000012 per kelvin is heated through a temperature rise of 100 K. The increase in its length is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.2 mm"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 mm"},{"label":"C","text":"2.4 mm"},{"label":"D","text":"4.8 mm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Expansion = expansivity × original length × temperature rise = 0.000012 × 2.0 × 100 = 0.0024 m = 2.4 mm. Option D doubles the answer by treating a fall of 100 K as two separate rises.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2025,24, $q$A stretched string is sounding its fundamental note. Holding the vibrating length fixed, the tension in the string is made four times larger. The new fundamental frequency is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"half the original"},{"label":"B","text":"unchanged"},{"label":"C","text":"twice the original"},{"label":"D","text":"four times the original"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Frequency varies as the square root of the tension, and the square root of 4 is 2, so the note rises one octave, that is to double the frequency. Mass per unit length and length are both unchanged.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2025,25, $q$A standing wave of wavelength 0.8 m is set up on a stretched string. The distance between two adjacent nodes of the pattern is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 m"},{"label":"B","text":"0.2 m"},{"label":"C","text":"0.4 m"},{"label":"D","text":"0.8 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Nodes occur at every half-wavelength, so adjacent nodes are 0.8/2 = 0.4 m apart. Taking the full wavelength as the node spacing gives option D.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','waves-sound',2025,26, $q$A ship's sonar sends an ultrasonic pulse straight down and listens for the echo from the sea bed; the echo returns 4.0 s after the pulse. Taking the speed of sound in sea water as 1500 m/s, the depth of the water at that point is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"750 m"},{"label":"B","text":"1500 m"},{"label":"C","text":"3000 m"},{"label":"D","text":"6000 m"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The pulse travels down and back, covering 1500 × 4 = 6000 m in the round trip. The depth is half of that, 3000 m; option D is the round-trip distance with no halving.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2025,27, $q$An object 4 cm tall stands 4 cm from a converging lens of focal length 5 cm, and is viewed as a magnifying glass. Use the lens equation to find the magnification of the image seen.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.2"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"5"},{"label":"D","text":"20"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Since the object lies inside the focal length, the lens forms a virtual image: 1/v = 1/5 − 1/4 = −1/20, so v = −20 cm on the object's side. Magnification = v/u = 20/4 = 5, upright. Taking the object height or the object distance as the magnification gives options D and B respectively.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Check where the object sits","kind":"text","content":"u = 4 cm is less than f = 5 cm, so the object is inside the focal length and the image must be virtual and upright.","note":"This is how a magnifying glass is used."},
{"title":"Apply the lens equation","kind":"math","content":"1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/5 − 1/4 = −1/20 per centimetre","note":"The negative result is exactly what a virtual image demands."},
{"title":"Image distance","kind":"math","content":"v = −20 cm, on the same side of the lens as the object","note":"Use the magnitude 20 cm in size ratios."},
{"title":"Magnification","kind":"math","content":"m = v/u = 20/4 = 5","note":"The image is five times as tall as the object."}]$st$, 3),
('physics','light-optics',2025,28, $q$An object moves directly towards a plane mirror with a speed of 2 m/s. The speed with which its image approaches the object is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"6 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"8 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"16 m/s"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The image also approaches the mirror at 2 m/s, closing the gap at 2 + 2 = 4 m/s. Adding mirror speed and image distance wrongly gives option B, and doubling the result once more gives option C.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','light-optics',2025,29, $q$In additive colour mixing, the colour of light that combines with blue light to give white light is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"cyan"},{"label":"B","text":"green"},{"label":"C","text":"magenta"},{"label":"D","text":"yellow"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Complementary pairs of light add to white: red with cyan, green with magenta, and blue with yellow. Pairing blue with cyan would only deepen the blue region of the spectrum, since cyan already contains the green component.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2025,30, $q$A battery of e.m.f. 12 V and internal resistance 1 ohm is connected to a network consisting of a 6 ohm resistor in parallel with a 3 ohm resistor. The current supplied by the battery is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.2 A"},{"label":"B","text":"2.4 A"},{"label":"C","text":"4.0 A"},{"label":"D","text":"6.0 A"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The parallel pair is (6 × 3)/(6 + 3) = 2 ohm, so the full circuit is 2 + 1 = 3 ohm. Hence I = 12/3 = 4.0 A; option D divides by the external 2 ohm alone and forgets the internal resistance.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2025,31, $q$The primary coil of a transformer has 2000 turns and operates from a 240 V alternating supply. For the transformer to deliver 12 V from its secondary, the number of turns on the secondary should be$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 turns"},{"label":"B","text":"25 turns"},{"label":"C","text":"50 turns"},{"label":"D","text":"100 turns"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Turns are proportional to voltage: Ns = 2000 × 12/240 = 100 turns. Option C, with 50 turns, would deliver only 6 V, half of what is required.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2025,32, $q$An electron, starting effectively from rest, is accelerated in a vacuum through a potential difference of 150 V. The kinetic energy it gains, expressed in electronvolts, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"15 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"150 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"1500 eV"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$One electronvolt is the energy gained by a charge of e coulomb falling through 1 V, so 150 V gives the electron 150 eV directly. The volt is not a unit of energy, which is why it must never be quoted in joules without converting first.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','electricity',2025,33, $q$Power lines carry a current of 10 A through a cable of total resistance 0.5 ohm. The rate at which heat is wasted in the cable is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5 W"},{"label":"B","text":"50 W"},{"label":"C","text":"500 W"},{"label":"D","text":"5000 W"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Power loss = I²R = 10² × 0.5 = 50 W. Because the current is squared, a tenfold current error produces a hundredfold power error, which is exactly the jumps between these options.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2025,34, $q$The coil of a simple generator consists of 100 turns, each of area 0.01 m², rotating in a uniform magnetic field of flux density 0.5 T. If the coil rotates with an angular velocity of 100 rad/s, the peak e.m.f. induced is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"25 V"},{"label":"B","text":"50 V"},{"label":"C","text":"100 V"},{"label":"D","text":"200 V"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Peak e.m.f. = NBA omega = 100 × 0.5 × 0.01 × 100 = 50 V. Taking the rotation rate as the frequency omega instead of the angular velocity halves the answer, giving option A.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','magnetism',2025,35, $q$When four rods of equal size -- wood, copper, aluminium and iron -- are brought one after the other close to a strong permanent magnet, the rod attracted to the magnet is the$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iron rod"},{"label":"B","text":"copper rod"},{"label":"C","text":"aluminium rod"},{"label":"D","text":"wooden rod"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Iron is ferromagnetic and is strongly attracted to a permanent magnet; copper and aluminium are paramagnetic at most and wood is non-magnetic. This is why iron filings, not copper filings, line up round a magnet's poles.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2025,36, $q$A radioactive source registers an activity of 40 counts per second on a detector. Its half-life is 2 hours. The activity registered 6 hours later is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 counts per second"},{"label":"B","text":"5 counts per second"},{"label":"C","text":"10 counts per second"},{"label":"D","text":"20 counts per second"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Six hours is three half-lives of 2 hours each: 40 becomes 20, then 10, then 5 counts per second. Option C allows only two half-lives to elapse.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('physics','modern-physics',2025,37, $q$Light of frequency 4 × 10 to the 14th Hz falls on a metal surface whose threshold frequency is 5 × 10 to the 14th Hz. What is observed at the surface?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"no photoelectrons are emitted, however intense the light becomes"},{"label":"B","text":"photoelectrons are emitted provided the light is made intense enough"},{"label":"C","text":"the rate of emission rises as the intensity is increased"},{"label":"D","text":"the work function of the metal is reduced until emission begins"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Emission demands photons each carrying hf of at least the work function hf0; since 4 × 10 to the 14th Hz lies below the threshold, no single photon can free an electron. Intensity only counts photons per second, so it can never rescue a frequency that is too low.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2023,10, $q$A charge of 965 C is passed through copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) solution using inert electrodes. What mass of copper deposits on the cathode? [Cu = 64; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.16 g"},{"label":"B","text":"0.32 g"},{"label":"C","text":"0.64 g"},{"label":"D","text":"6.4 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$965 C is 0.01 F, i.e. 0.01 mol of electrons. Since Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, the deposit is 0.005 mol of copper = 0.005 × 64 = 0.32 g; using one electron per ion doubles this wrongly.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge to electrons","kind":"math","content":"n(e⁻) = 965 ÷ 96500 = 0.01 mol","note":"one hundredth of a faraday"},{"title":"Half-equation ratio","kind":"text","content":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu at the cathode","note":"two electrons per copper ion"},{"title":"Scale and weigh","kind":"math","content":"n(Cu) = 0.01 ÷ 2 = 0.005 mol, m = 0.005 × 64 = 0.32 g","note":"dividing, not multiplying, by 2"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2023,11, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃ is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"but-1-ene"},{"label":"B","text":"but-2-ene"},{"label":"C","text":"but-1-yne"},{"label":"D","text":"butane"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The four-carbon chain carries one double bond, so counting from the end nearer the C=C gives position 1: but-1-ene. A double bond at position 2 would need the extra carbon on either side of the = group, while a triple bond is a different series altogether.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2023,12, $q$A solution turns methyl orange yellow and phenolphthalein pink. Its pH is most likely about:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"10"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Methyl orange is yellow above pH 4.4 and phenolphthalein is pink above pH 8.2, so both indicators together demand a clearly basic solution. A pH of 10 satisfies them both; pH 7 would leave phenolphthalein colourless.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2023,13, $q$The number of electrons in the outermost shell of a chlorine atom is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"17"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Chlorine's 2, 8, 7 configuration leaves seven electrons in the third shell, one short of the octet. The totals 17 and 18 count all electrons, not the outermost shell alone.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2023,14, $q$The bond between sodium and chlorine in NaCl results from the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sharing of an electron pair between the two atoms"},{"label":"B","text":"delocalisation of electrons among the cations"},{"label":"C","text":"transfer of an electron from sodium to chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"magnetic attraction between the atoms"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Sodium surrenders its lone outer electron to chlorine, creating Na⁺ and Cl⁻ held by electrostatic attraction. Sharing would describe covalent bonding, and delocalisation belongs to metals.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2023,15, $q$How many moles of NaOH are contained in 200 cm³ of a 0.25 M solution?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.025 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"0.050 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"0.100 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"0.200 mol"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Convert to moles by n = C × V: 0.25 × 0.2 = 0.05 mol. Treat 200 cm³ as 0.2 dm³ before multiplying, or the result will be out by a factor of 1000.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2023,16, $q$Muddy water left standing in a bucket clears from the bottom up; the settled clay is then removed from the clear liquid by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"decantation"},{"label":"B","text":"sublimation"},{"label":"C","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"crystallisation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Gravity sediments the coarse clay particles, after which gently pouring off the clear upper liquid decants them apart. Distillation and crystallisation suit dissolved or volatile components, not settled mud.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2023,17, $q$Which of the following changes will DECREASE the rate of reaction between marble chips and dilute HCl?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"grinding the chips to a powder"},{"label":"B","text":"warming the acid"},{"label":"C","text":"using larger chips of marble"},{"label":"D","text":"shaking the flask continuously"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Larger chips expose less total surface area, so fewer effective collisions occur each second. Grinding, warming and shaking all accelerate the reaction instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2023,18, $q$Which of these metals is stored in the laboratory under liquid paraffin?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"copper"},{"label":"C","text":"zinc"},{"label":"D","text":"aluminium"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Sodium reacts rapidly with both air and moisture, so it is submerged in kerosene to exclude them. The remaining metals form stable surface layers and need no such storage.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2023,19, $q$During the electrolysis of aqueous copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) with platinum electrodes, the product liberated at the anode is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"copper metal"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen gas"},{"label":"C","text":"sulphur"},{"label":"D","text":"oxygen gas"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$With inert electrodes, OH⁻ ions (or water) are oxidised at the anode: 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻. Copper deposits at the cathode instead, since reduction happens only where electrons arrive.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2023,20, $q$Ethanol is converted to ethene by dehydration using:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid at about 170 °C"},{"label":"B","text":"dilute sodium hydroxide solution"},{"label":"C","text":"concentrated trioxonitrate(V) acid"},{"label":"D","text":"acidified potassium tetraoxomanganate(VII) solution"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Hot concentrated H₂SO₄ removes the elements of water from ethanol, giving ethene. Oxidants such as KMnO₄ and HNO₃ destroy the alkanol differently, and NaOH is not a dehydrating agent.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2023,21, $q$A mixture of potassium trioxonitrate(V) and common salt, whose solubilities respond very differently to temperature, is best separated by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"fractional crystallisation on cooling a hot saturated solution"},{"label":"B","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"C","text":"paper chromatography"},{"label":"D","text":"sublimation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$KNO₃'s solubility climbs steeply with temperature while NaCl's barely changes, so cooling a hot saturated solution drops nitrate crystals first. Distillation and sublimation cannot separate dissolved salts, and chromatography is for tiny samples of mixtures such as dyes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2023,22, $q$From the equation CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂, how many moles of CaCO₃ react with 0.2 mol of HCl?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"0.2 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"0.4 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"2.0 mol"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Two moles of acid react with one mole of carbonate, so 0.2 mol of HCl consumes 0.2 ÷ 2 = 0.1 mol of CaCO₃. Dividing by the coefficient of the wrong species is the usual misstep.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2023,23, $q$20.0 cm³ of hydrochloric acid neutralises 25.0 cm³ of 0.10 M NaOH solution. The molarity of the acid is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.080 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.100 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.125 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.200 M"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(NaOH) = 0.025 × 0.10 = 0.0025 mol, and the 1:1 equation transfers all of it to HCl. Hence C(HCl) = 0.0025 ÷ 0.020 = 0.125 M; inverting the volume ratio gives the 0.080 M trap.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of base","kind":"math","content":"n(NaOH) = 0.10 × 0.025 = 0.0025 mol","note":"convert 25.0 cm³ to 0.025 dm³ first"},{"title":"Apply the 1:1 ratio","kind":"text","content":"HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O, so n(HCl) = n(NaOH)","note":"monobasic acid against monobasic base"},{"title":"Divide by the acid volume","kind":"math","content":"C(HCl) = 0.0025 ÷ 0.020 = 0.125 M","note":"the acid volume is 20.0 cm³ = 0.020 dm³"},{"title":"Trap check","kind":"text","content":"multiplying by 20 ÷ 25 rather than 25 ÷ 20 gives 0.080 M","note":"equality of moles is the anchor, not volumes"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2023,24, $q$Which of the following molecules shows appreciable hydrogen bonding between its own molecules?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methane"},{"label":"B","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"C","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"water"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Hydrogen bonds need an H already bonded to a strongly electronegative atom (N, O or F) plus a lone pair, conditions met in H₂O. CH₄ has no polar H, and CO₂ and O₂ carry no H at all.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2023,25, $q$For a given reversible reaction at fixed conditions, the equilibrium constant Kc changes only when the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"catalyst is added or removed"},{"label":"B","text":"concentrations of the reactants are altered"},{"label":"C","text":"temperature is changed"},{"label":"D","text":"total pressure on the gas mixture is altered"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Kc depends solely on temperature; every other listed change alters the amounts at equilibrium but not the constant itself. This is why catalysts, concentrations and pressures never appear in any Kc expression.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2023,26, $q$What mass of glucose, C₆H₁₂O₆, is needed to prepare 250 cm³ of a 0.1 M solution? [C = 12, H = 1, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.25 g"},{"label":"B","text":"4.5 g"},{"label":"C","text":"9.0 g"},{"label":"D","text":"18.0 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Moles required = 0.1 × 0.25 = 0.025 mol, and M(glucose) = 180 g mol⁻¹. The mass is 0.025 × 180 = 4.5 g; reading 250 as 1 dm³ gives the 18 g error.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2023,27, $q$Which of the following metals reacts with steam to give the oxide and hydrogen, yet does not react with cold water?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium"},{"label":"C","text":"iron"},{"label":"D","text":"copper"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Iron sits between the extremes: unreactive towards cold water but reduced by hot steam, 3Fe + 4H₂O → Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂. Sodium and calcium already attack cold water, and copper fails even with steam.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2023,28, $q$The sub-atomic particle discovered by J. J. Thomson in his cathode-ray experiments was the:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"proton"},{"label":"B","text":"electron"},{"label":"C","text":"neutron"},{"label":"D","text":"alpha particle"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Deflection of cathode rays in electric and magnetic fields revealed a light, negatively charged particle, the electron. Protons and neutrons were identified by later workers in other experiments.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2023,29, $q$Which of the following acids is dibasic?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"trioxonitrate(V) acid, HNO₃"},{"label":"B","text":"tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H₂SO₄"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrochloric acid, HCl"},{"label":"D","text":"ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Basicity counts the ionisable H atoms, and H₂SO₄ can donate two, forming HSO₄⁻ then SO₄²⁻. HNO₃, HCl and CH₃COOH are monobasic despite the trio of alkyl hydrogens in the latter.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2023,30, $q$The average oxidation number of carbon in the ethanedioate ion, C₂O₄²⁻, is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+3"},{"label":"B","text":"+4"},{"label":"C","text":"+2"},{"label":"D","text":"+6"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The four oxygens contribute 4 × (−2) = −8, so the two carbons together must total +6 for the −2 ion charge. Sharing that equally gives each carbon +3.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2023,31, $q$Which Period 3 element is the most electropositive?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium"},{"label":"C","text":"magnesium"},{"label":"D","text":"aluminium"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Electropositivity (metallic character) grows down a group and falls across a period, so sodium, at the left end of Period 3, parts with its electron most willingly. Chlorine, at the far right, is the least electropositive of all.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2023,32, $q$To separate a mixture of common salt and sand, the first operation after adding water and stirring is to:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"heat the mixture strongly"},{"label":"B","text":"filter the mixture"},{"label":"C","text":"distil the mixture immediately"},{"label":"D","text":"add silver trioxonitrate(V) solution"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Dissolving the salt leaves sand as the insoluble residue, which filtration traps on the paper; the salt solution passes through as filtrate. Heating or, worse, adding AgNO₃ would form new compounds instead of separating the two.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2023,33, $q$3.0 g of hydrogen gas is burnt in 32 g of oxygen gas. What mass of water is formed? [H₂O = 18; 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9 g"},{"label":"B","text":"18 g"},{"label":"C","text":"27 g"},{"label":"D","text":"36 g"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(H₂) = 1.5 mol and n(O₂) = 1.0 mol, but each mole of O₂ needs two of H₂, so hydrogen is the limiting reactant. The yield is 1.5 mol of water = 1.5 × 18 = 27 g, with oxygen left unreacted.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2023,34, $q$Which of the following is the most soluble in water?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"pentane"},{"label":"B","text":"hexane"},{"label":"C","text":"ethanol"},{"label":"D","text":"octane"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Ethanol's OH group hydrogen-bonds with water, so it mixes with it in all proportions. The alkanes are non-polar hydrocarbons whose solubility shrinks further as the chains lengthen.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2023,35, $q$A reaction takes 200 s to complete at 25 °C and the rate doubles for every 10 K rise in temperature. How long will the reaction take at 55 °C?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12.5 s"},{"label":"B","text":"50 s"},{"label":"C","text":"100 s"},{"label":"D","text":"25 s"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$From 25 to 55 °C there are three 10 K steps, so the rate doubles three times (×8). Time and rate are inversely related: 200 ÷ 8 = 25 s at the higher temperature.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Count the temperature steps","kind":"text","content":"55 − 25 = 30 K, i.e. three 10 K intervals","note":"work in kelvin-compatible intervals here"},{"title":"Build the rate factor","kind":"math","content":"rate multiplies by 2 × 2 × 2 = 8","note":"doubling per step compounds"},{"title":"Invert into time","kind":"math","content":"t = 200 ÷ 8 = 25 s at 55 °C","note":"faster reaction means shorter time"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2023,36, $q$When sodium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV), NaHCO₃, is heated strongly, the products are:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"Na₂CO₃, CO₂ and H₂O"},{"label":"B","text":"Na₂O and CO₂"},{"label":"C","text":"NaOH and CO₂"},{"label":"D","text":"Na₂O₂ and H₂"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$2NaHCO₃ → Na₂CO₃ + H₂O + CO₂ on heating: each pair of hydrogencarbonates loses one water and one carbon dioxide. Sodium carbonate itself survives the further heating, leaving the white anhydrous residue.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2023,37, $q$What is the percentage by mass of carbon in ethanol, C₂H₅OH? [C = 12, H = 1, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"13.0%"},{"label":"B","text":"26.1%"},{"label":"C","text":"52.2%"},{"label":"D","text":"69.5%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$M(ethanol) = 2(12) + 6(1) + 16 = 46 g mol⁻¹ and the two carbons weigh 24 g. So %C = 24 ÷ 46 × 100 = 52.2%; dividing by instead of multiplying the 46 runs to the 26.1% trap.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2023,38, $q$Which of the following is a giant covalent (macromolecular) solid?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"iodine"},{"label":"B","text":"diamond"},{"label":"C","text":"dry ice (solid CO₂)"},{"label":"D","text":"copper"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$In diamond every carbon tetrahedrally bonds four neighbours, extending covalent links throughout the crystal. Iodine and dry ice are molecular solids and copper is metallic.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2024,10, $q$13.0 g of zinc is added to 250 cm³ of 1.0 M hydrochloric acid. How many moles of hydrogen are liberated? [Zn = 65; Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.100 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"0.125 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"0.200 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"0.250 mol"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$There is 0.20 mol of zinc, which would need 0.40 mol of HCl, but only 0.25 mol of acid is present, so HCl is limiting. The equation then grants half the acid moles as hydrogen: 0.25 ÷ 2 = 0.125 mol of H₂.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Convert both reagents","kind":"math","content":"n(Zn) = 13.0 ÷ 65 = 0.20 mol; n(HCl) = 1.0 × 0.25 = 0.25 mol","note":"always convert both sides first"},{"title":"Test who runs out","kind":"text","content":"0.20 mol of Zn would need 0.40 mol of HCl, more than the 0.25 mol available, so the acid is limiting","note":"zinc is in excess"},{"title":"Scale hydrogen from the limiter","kind":"math","content":"n(H₂) = n(HCl) ÷ 2 = 0.125 mol","note":"2 : 1 ratio of HCl to H₂"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2024,11, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH₂OCH₃ is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1-methoxypropane"},{"label":"B","text":"butan-2-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"butanal"},{"label":"D","text":"2-methoxypropane"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The alkoxyalkane convention puts the smaller alkyl group as an alkoxy prefix on the longer chain, giving 1-methoxypropane. Reading the oxygen as sitting inside carbon 2 produces the false 2-methoxypropane, which would be a different structure.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2024,12, $q$The basicity of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, H₂SO₄, is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Basicity counts replaceable hydrogen atoms, and H₂SO₄ can part with two, forming HSO₄⁻ and then SO₄²⁻. The four oxygens in the name have nothing to do with the count.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2024,13, $q$Which of the following species is isoelectronic with argon?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"K"},{"label":"B","text":"Na"},{"label":"C","text":"K⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"Ca"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Argon holds 18 electrons, and K⁺ has lost its single outer one out of 19, matching at 18. Neutral K has 19, Na has 11, and neutral Ca has 20.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2024,14, $q$Which of the following pairs of elements will NOT form an ionic compound?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon and hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"sodium and chlorine"},{"label":"C","text":"magnesium and oxygen"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium and fluorine"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Carbon and hydrogen have almost equal electronegativities, so they share electrons covalently instead of transferring them. Each of the other pairings joins a strongly electropositive metal to a strongly electronegative non-metal, ideal for ionic bonding.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2024,15, $q$The reducing agent that acts directly on the iron(III) oxide inside the blast furnace is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"B","text":"hydrogen gas"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(II) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"methane"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$CO attacks the ore shaft by shaft as Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂, so carbon(II) oxide does the direct reducing. The coke first makes CO₂ and then CO, but CO is the agent at the oxide surface.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2024,16, $q$In the electrolysis of concentrated brine with inert electrodes, the gas liberated at the anode is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Concentrated brine carries so many Cl⁻ ions that they are discharged ahead of the water's OH⁻, giving Cl₂ at the anode. In very dilute sodium chloride solution the choice reverses and oxygen wins, a contrast worth memorising.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2024,17, $q$In paper chromatography, the stationary phase is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the moving solvent"},{"label":"B","text":"the dye mixture"},{"label":"C","text":"the glass beaker"},{"label":"D","text":"the chromatographic paper"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The paper stays put and adsorbs the dyes to differing degrees, while the solvent creeps up as the mobile phase and competes for them. Beakers and mixtures are props, not phases.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2024,18, $q$What volume of 0.2 M NaOH will neutralise 20 cm³ of 0.15 M HCl?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"15 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"20 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"30 cm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n(HCl) = 0.15 × 0.020 = 0.003 mol, matched 1:1 by NaOH. Dividing by 0.2 M gives V = 0.015 dm³ = 15 cm³; equal volumes would work only for equal concentrations.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2024,19, $q$Which of the following statements about catalysts is NOT correct?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a catalyst lowers the activation energy of the reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"a catalyst is not consumed in the reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"a catalyst changes the position of equilibrium"},{"label":"D","text":"a finely divided catalyst can be poisoned by impurities"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Catalysts shorten the time to equilibrium but cannot move its position or alter Kc. The other three statements are all standard truths of catalysis.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2024,20, $q$The monomer from which polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is manufactured is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"ethane"},{"label":"B","text":"ethyne"},{"label":"C","text":"chloroethene"},{"label":"D","text":"propane"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl, polymerises by opening its double bond into the repeating unit of PVC. Ethane, ethyne and propane have no chlorinated double bond to carry into the polymer chain.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2024,21, $q$A buffer solution resists changes in:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"colour"},{"label":"B","text":"volume"},{"label":"C","text":"temperature"},{"label":"D","text":"pH on dilution or small additions of acid or base"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The weak acid and its conjugate base in a buffer mop up any added H₃O⁺ or OH⁻, holding the pH nearly steady. It cannot stabilise colour, volume or temperature.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2024,22, $q$The gas chiefly responsible for the brown haze of photochemical smog in busy cities is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methane"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"carbon(IV) oxide"},{"label":"D","text":"nitrogen(IV) oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$NO₂ from vehicle exhausts absorbs sunlight and sets off the ozone and radical chemistry of smog, tinting the air brown. CO₂, O₂ and CH₄ are colourless bystanders in this process.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2024,23, $q$Element X has two isotopes of mass numbers 39 and 41 with relative atomic mass 39.8. The percentage abundance of the ³⁹X isotope is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60%"},{"label":"B","text":"40%"},{"label":"C","text":"50%"},{"label":"D","text":"80%"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Solving 39(100 − x) + 41x = 3980 gives x = 40% for the heavier isotope, so ³⁹X takes the remaining 60%. An average pulled close to 39 also hints that the lighter isotope must dominate.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2024,24, $q$What mass of sodium chloride is present in 2 dm³ of a 0.05 M solution? [Na = 23, Cl = 35.5]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.925 g"},{"label":"B","text":"5.85 g"},{"label":"C","text":"11.7 g"},{"label":"D","text":"58.5 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$n = 0.05 × 2 = 0.1 mol, and M(NaCl) = 58.5 g mol⁻¹, so mass = 0.1 × 58.5 = 5.85 g. Halving the molar mass or moles explains each wrong option.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2024,25, $q$The shape of the water molecule is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"linear"},{"label":"B","text":"trigonal planar"},{"label":"C","text":"V-shaped (bent)"},{"label":"D","text":"square planar"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Two O–H bonding pairs plus two lone pairs on oxygen repel into a bent arrangement of about 104.5°, the classic V shape. Linearity would require no lone pairs, as in CO₂.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2024,26, $q$Using the standard electrode potentials E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V, the standard cell potential of the zinc-copper (Daniell) cell is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+0.42 V"},{"label":"B","text":"+1.10 V"},{"label":"C","text":"−1.10 V"},{"label":"D","text":"+0.76 V"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The cathode is the more positive (copper) half-cell, so E°cell = E°(Cu) − E°(Zn) = 0.34 − (−0.76). Subtracting the negative potential yields +1.10 V, the sign that makes the cell spontaneous.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Rank the half-cells","kind":"text","content":"the more positive reduction potential, Cu²⁺/Cu at +0.34 V, is the cathode","note":"electrons flow from the more negative Zn half-cell"},{"title":"Apply the cell formula","kind":"text","content":"E°cell = E°(cathode) − E°(anode) = E°(Cu) − E°(Zn)","note":"right-hand minus left-hand convention"},{"title":"Compute","kind":"math","content":"E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V","note":"add the magnitudes; subtracting a negative is addition"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2024,27, $q$The most suitable indicator for the titration of ethanoic acid against sodium hydroxide solution is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methyl orange"},{"label":"B","text":"methyl red"},{"label":"C","text":"phenolphthalein"},{"label":"D","text":"litmus"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A weak acid against a strong base ends slightly alkaline (pH near 8.5-9), exactly in phenolphthalein's pink transition window. Methyl orange or methyl red would switch long before the equivalence point, understating the titre.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2024,28, $q$How many moles of copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) pentahydrate, CuSO₄·5H₂O, are present in 25.0 g of the crystals? [Cu = 64, S = 32, H = 1, O = 16]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.05 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"0.10 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"0.20 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"0.25 mol"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$M(CuSO₄·5H₂O) = 64 + 32 + 64 + 5(18) = 250 g mol⁻¹, so n = 25.0 ÷ 250 = 0.10 mol. Omitting the five waters of crystallisation would double the answer.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2024,29, $q$The functional group that characterises an ester is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"the hydroxyl group, OH"},{"label":"B","text":"the carboxyl group, COOH"},{"label":"C","text":"the ester linkage, COO"},{"label":"D","text":"the alkanal group, CHO"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$An ester (alkanoate) links an acyl carbon through an oxygen single bond to an alkyl group: the COO linkage. Alkanols carry OH, alkanoic acids COOH, and alkanals CHO.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2024,30, $q$Fine dust and smoke particles are removed from waste gases in factories by:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an electrostatic precipitator (Cottrell precipitator)"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration through paper"},{"label":"C","text":"fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"crystallisation"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Charged plates attract and collect the ionised smoke particles on their way up the stack. Paper filters cannot handle volumes of hot gas, and distillation or crystallisation are for liquids and solutions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2024,31, $q$For the gaseous equilibrium 2A(g) ⇌ B(g), increasing the total pressure on the system shifts the equilibrium:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"towards A, the side with more gas molecules"},{"label":"B","text":"towards B, the side with fewer gas molecules"},{"label":"C","text":"in no direction"},{"label":"D","text":"in a direction decided by the catalyst used"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Squeezing a gaseous equilibrium pushes it towards the side occupying least volume, here the single mole of B. A catalyst cannot dictate direction, and the shift vanishes only when both sides hold equal gas moles.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2024,32, $q$75 cm³ of a 0.2 M ethanoic acid solution is diluted with water to 150 cm³. The new concentration is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.05 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.1 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.4 M"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Moles of acid stay fixed at C₁V₁ = 0.2 × 75 = 15 mmol, so C₂ = 15 ÷ 150 = 0.1 M. Doubling the volume simply halves the concentration; doubling it again is the 0.05 M error.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Fix the moles present","kind":"math","content":"n = C₁V₁ = 0.2 × 0.075 = 0.015 mol","note":"dilution does not create or destroy solute"},{"title":"Apply C₁V₁ = C₂V₂","kind":"math","content":"C₂ = 0.2 × 75 ÷ 150 = 0.1 M","note":"works in any consistent volume units"},{"title":"Interpret the factor","kind":"text","content":"volume doubled, so concentration halves exactly","note":"never changes total moles"}]$st$, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2024,33, $q$As the alkane homologous series is descended from ethane towards decane, which physical property decreases?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"molecular mass"},{"label":"B","text":"boiling point"},{"label":"C","text":"volatility"},{"label":"D","text":"viscosity"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Longer chains attract one another (more dispersion force), so mass, boiling point and viscosity all rise while the ease of vapourising, the volatility, falls. Every other listed property trends upwards.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2024,34, $q$Calcium has the electronic configuration 2, 8, 8, 2. How many electrons occupy its M (third) shell?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The M shell is the third principal shell, holding the 8 electrons of the 3s² 3p⁶ set in calcium. Only the outermost pair of 2 sits in the N shell.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2024,35, $q$Metals are malleable because:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"their positive ions are held apart by strong repulsions"},{"label":"B","text":"rigid directional covalent bonds hold the atoms"},{"label":"C","text":"layers of positive ions can slide over one another within the sea of delocalised electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"free protons migrate between the atoms"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The non-directional metallic bond lets planes of cations glide without breaking the electron glue, deforming instead of shattering. Directional bonds, whether covalent networks or brittle ionic lattices, crack instead of sliding.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2024,36, $q$Which of the following changes is oxidation?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"gain of electrons"},{"label":"B","text":"loss of electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"gain of protons"},{"label":"D","text":"loss of neutrons"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Oxidation is the loss of electrons (OIL RIG), raising an atom's oxidation number. Gaining electrons is reduction, while protons and neutrons never migrate in ordinary redox changes.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2024,37, $q$Which of the following has the highest mass number?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"²⁰Ne"},{"label":"B","text":"⁴⁰Ar"},{"label":"C","text":"¹²⁷I"},{"label":"D","text":"²³⁸U"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The superscript is the mass number, and uranium-238's 238 tops the list. Iodine-127, argon-40 and neon-20 rank in decreasing order behind it.$q$,
 'single', null, 2)
;

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2025,10, $q$A steady current of 0.5 A is passed through dilute tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid with platinum electrodes for 965 s. What volume of hydrogen is evolved at the cathode, measured at STP? [molar volume = 22.4 dm³; F = 96500 C mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"28 cm³"},{"label":"B","text":"56 cm³"},{"label":"C","text":"112 cm³"},{"label":"D","text":"224 cm³"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Q = It = 0.5 × 965 = 482.5 C, giving 0.005 mol of electrons. From 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂ the gas is 0.0025 mol = 0.0025 × 22400 = 56 cm³. Dividing by 2 a second time would land on 28 cm³.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge from current and time","kind":"math","content":"Q = I × t = 0.5 × 965 = 482.5 C","note":"seconds of time, amperes of current"},{"title":"Electrons delivered","kind":"math","content":"n(e⁻) = 482.5 ÷ 96500 = 0.005 mol","note":"divide by the faraday"},{"title":"Half-equation halves the moles","kind":"text","content":"2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂: two electrons make one H₂ molecule","note":"hydrogen is diatomic"},{"title":"Convert to gas volume","kind":"math","content":"V = 0.0025 × 22400 = 56 cm³ at STP","note":"1 dm³ = 1000 cm³"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','organic',2025,11, $q$The IUPAC name of CH₃CH(CH₃)COCH₃ is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3-methylbutan-2-one"},{"label":"B","text":"2-methylbutan-3-one"},{"label":"C","text":"pentan-3-one"},{"label":"D","text":"3-methylbutanal"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The longest chain holding the carbonyl runs four carbons, and numbering from the nearer end puts the C=O on C-2 and the extra methyl on C-3. Naming it from the far end inflates the ketone locant to 3, breaking the lowest-locant rule.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2025,12, $q$Which of the following salts will form an aqueous solution that turns litmus red?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"sodium trioxocarbonate(IV), Na₂CO₃"},{"label":"B","text":"potassium trioxonitrate(V), KNO₃"},{"label":"C","text":"sodium chloride, NaCl"},{"label":"D","text":"aluminium tetraoxosulphate(VI), Al₂(SO₄)₃"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Small, highly charged Al³⁺ hydrolyses water and releases H₃O⁺, staining litmus red. Na₂CO₃ hydrolyses alkaline, while KNO₃ and NaCl, salts of fully strong acid-base pairs, stay neutral.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2025,13, $q$The atomic number of an element is equal to the number of:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"neutrons in its nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"protons plus neutrons in its nucleus"},{"label":"C","text":"nucleons in the atom"},{"label":"D","text":"protons in its nucleus"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The atomic number Z counts the protons, which in a neutral atom equals the electron count as well. Options A and B belong to neutron and mass-number ideas respectively.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2025,14, $q$Potassium reacts with water more violently than sodium because:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"potassium has more protons in its nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"the potassium valence electron is farther from the nucleus and is lost more easily"},{"label":"C","text":"potassium contains more neutrons"},{"label":"D","text":"potassium is a denser metal"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Down Group I each added shell pushes the outer electron further out, with extra shielding, so potassium sheds its 4s electron more readily than sodium sheds its 3s. Proton and neutron counts are irrelevant to the ease of electron loss.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2025,15, $q$What volume of CO₂ at STP is released when 50 g of calcium trioxocarbonate(IV) is completely decomposed by heat? [CaCO₃ = 100; molar volume at STP = 22.4 dm³]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.6 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"22.4 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"11.2 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"44.8 dm³"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$50 g of CaCO₃ is 0.5 mol, and CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ releases one mole of gas per mole of carbonate. Hence V = 0.5 × 22.4 = 11.2 dm³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','separation',2025,16, $q$Inside the fractionating column of a petroleum refinery, the temperature:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"rises steadily towards the top"},{"label":"B","text":"falls steadily towards the top"},{"label":"C","text":"is uniform from base to top"},{"label":"D","text":"is identical at every tray"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The still is hottest at the base and cools towards the top, so each fraction condenses where its boiling range matches the local temperature. Heavy bitumen stays low, light gases escape high, and C and D describe no real column.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2025,17, $q$Which of the following changes will decrease the rate of reaction between marble chips and dilute HCl?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"using a more concentrated acid"},{"label":"B","text":"using powdered marble"},{"label":"C","text":"keeping the reaction mixture in a refrigerator"},{"label":"D","text":"increasing the pressure on the solid"  } ]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Cooling lowers the average kinetic energy, so fewer collisions reach the activation energy. Concentrated acid, powdering and, to a lesser degree, pressure all push the rate up instead.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2025,18, $q$Which of the following is the strongest reducing agent in aqueous solution? [E°(Na⁺/Na) = −2.71 V, E°(Mg²⁺/Mg) = −2.37 V, E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V, E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"magnesium"},{"label":"B","text":"zinc"},{"label":"C","text":"copper"},{"label":"D","text":"sodium"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The most negative standard electrode potential marks the metal most eager to be oxidised, and Na at −2.71 V wins. Copper's positive potential makes it the weakest reducing agent of the four.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2025,19, $q$How many moles of oxygen are required for the complete combustion of 1 mol of ethyne, C₂H₂? [2C₂H₂ + 5O₂ → 4CO₂ + 2H₂O]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 mol"},{"label":"B","text":"5.0 mol"},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 mol"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0 mol"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The 2 : 5 equation ratio gives 5 ÷ 2 = 2.5 mol of O₂ per mole of ethyne. Reading the coefficient directly, or dividing in the wrong direction, produces the other figures.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2025,20, $q$Which of the following hydrocarbons decolourises bromine water most readily without a catalyst?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"methane"},{"label":"B","text":"ethene"},{"label":"C","text":"benzene"},{"label":"D","text":"ethane"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Ethene's exposed double bond adds bromine across itself at once, draining the red-brown colour. Benzene resists addition without a catalyst, and the alkanes are simply unreactive towards bromine water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2025,21, $q$25.0 cm³ of 0.2 M NaOH is exactly neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid solution. The molarity of the acid is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.250 M"},{"label":"B","text":"0.0625 M"},{"label":"C","text":"0.125 M"},{"label":"D","text":"0.500 M"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(NaOH) = 0.2 × 0.025 = 0.005 mol, and the dibasic acid holds only half that number of molecules: 0.0025 mol. Dividing by the acid's 0.020 dm³ gives 0.125 M; ignoring the basicity 2 gives the 0.250 M trap.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Moles of base","kind":"math","content":"n(NaOH) = 0.2 × 0.025 = 0.005 mol","note":"volume converted to dm³"},{"title":"H₂SO₄ is dibasic","kind":"text","content":"each acid molecule releases 2 H⁺, so n(acid) = 0.005 ÷ 2 = 0.0025 mol","note":"divide, never multiply, by the basicity"},{"title":"Concentration from moles and volume","kind":"math","content":"C(acid) = 0.0025 ÷ 0.020 = 0.125 M","note":"acid volume 20.0 cm³ = 0.020 dm³"}]$st$, 3),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2025,22, $q$In acidic medium the dichromate ion is reduced to chromium(III) ions: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + xe⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. The value of x is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"7"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each chromium falls from +6 to +3, releasing three electrons, and the ion caries two chromium atoms. Charge balance with the 14 H⁺ confirms the sum x = 6.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2025,23, $q$The number of sub-shells in the third energy level (n = 3) is:$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The third level carries the 3s, 3p and 3d sub-shells, one for each of the first three values of angular momentum. Five is the number of orbitals (not sub-shells) in the 3d set.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('chemistry','separation',2025,24, $q$In the fractional distillation of crude petroleum, the fraction that distils off between about 170 and 250 °C and is used as fuel for jet engines is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"diesel oil"},{"label":"B","text":"kerosene"},{"label":"C","text":"bitumen"},{"label":"D","text":"lubricating oil"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Fractions leave the column in order of increasing boiling point, and kerosene (paraffin), the fraction between petrol and diesel oils, is the aviation fuel. Bitumen and lubricating oil are heavy residues drawn off lower down.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','atomic-structure',2025,25, $q$A neutral atom of element X contains 19 electrons and 20 neutrons. The mass number of X is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"19"},{"label":"B","text":"20"},{"label":"C","text":"39"},{"label":"D","text":"40"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$A neutral atom has as many protons as electrons, so X has 19 protons. Mass number = protons + neutrons = 19 + 20 = 39, the nucleon number of this potassium isotope.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','periodicity-bonding',2025,26, $q$When an ammonia molecule combines with a proton to form the ammonium ion, NH₄⁺, the bond between nitrogen and the incoming proton is best described as$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"an ionic bond"},{"label":"B","text":"a hydrogen bond"},{"label":"C","text":"a covalent bond with one electron from each atom"},{"label":"D","text":"a dative covalent bond with both electrons from nitrogen"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The lone pair on nitrogen is donated to the proton, so the shared pair comes entirely from nitrogen. This is a dative (coordinate) covalent bond, after which the four N–H bonds in NH₄⁺ are indistinguishable.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','stoichiometry',2025,27, $q$What volume of hydrogen, measured at s.t.p., is liberated when 4.6 g of sodium reacts completely with excess water? [Na = 23.0; molar volume of gas at s.t.p. = 22.4 dm³ mol⁻¹]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2.24 dm³"},{"label":"B","text":"1.12 dm³"},{"label":"C","text":"4.48 dm³"},{"label":"D","text":"22.4 dm³"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$n(Na) = 4.6 ÷ 23.0 = 0.20 mol. From 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂, two moles of sodium give one mole of hydrogen, so n(H₂) = 0.10 mol and V = 0.10 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2025,28, $q$An aqueous solution of iron(III) chloride turns blue litmus red because the salt$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"does not hydrolyse in water"},{"label":"B","text":"is formed from a strong acid and a weak base"},{"label":"C","text":"is formed from a weak acid and a strong base"},{"label":"D","text":"is a neutral salt of a strong acid and a strong base"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$FeCl₃ comes from the strong acid HCl and the weak base Fe(OH)₃, so its Fe³⁺ ions hydrolyse in water to give H₃O⁺. Salts formed from a strong acid and a weak base therefore give acidic solutions.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','acids-bases',2025,29, $q$25.0 cm³ of a solution of tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid is completely neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.10 mol dm⁻³ NaOH solution. The concentration of the acid in mol dm⁻³ is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.02"},{"label":"B","text":"0.08"},{"label":"C","text":"0.04"},{"label":"D","text":"0.10"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$n(NaOH) = 0.10 × 0.020 = 2.0 × 10⁻³ mol. Since H₂SO₄ and NaOH react in the ratio 1 : 2, n(H₂SO₄) = 1.0 × 10⁻³ mol, and n ÷ V = 0.001 ÷ 0.025 = 0.04 mol dm⁻³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2025,30, $q$The oxidation number of chromium in the dichromate ion, Cr₂O₇²⁻, is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"+6"},{"label":"B","text":"+3"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+12"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The seven oxygen atoms contribute 7 × (−2) = −14, and for the ion 2x − 14 = −2, so x = +6. The value +7 belongs to manganese in the tetraoxomanganate(VII) ion, MnO₄⁻, and +12 is the sum for two chromium atoms.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2025,31, $q$Concentrated sodium chloride solution (brine) is electrolysed using carbon electrodes. The product liberated at the anode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"steam"},{"label":"D","text":"chlorine"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$In concentrated brine the Cl⁻ ions are discharged in preference to OH⁻ ions at the anode, so Cl₂ is liberated. Hydrogen is released at the cathode while NaOH accumulates in the solution.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','redox-electrochem',2025,32, $q$A current of 1.0 A is passed through a solution of copper(II) tetraoxosulphate(VI) for 965 s. [Faraday constant = 96 500 C mol⁻¹; Cu = 63.5] The mass of copper deposited at the cathode is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.16 g"},{"label":"B","text":"0.32 g"},{"label":"C","text":"0.64 g"},{"label":"D","text":"3.18 g"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Q = It = 1.0 × 965 = 965 C, so n(e⁻) = 965 ÷ 96 500 = 0.010 mol. From Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, n(Cu) = 0.010 ÷ 2 = 0.005 mol. Hence m = n × M = 0.005 × 63.5 = 0.3175 ≈ 0.32 g.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Charge passed","kind":"math","content":"Q = I × t = 1.0 × 965 = 965 C","note":"Multiply the current by the time in seconds."},{"title":"Moles of electrons","kind":"math","content":"n(e⁻) = Q ÷ F = 965 ÷ 96 500 = 0.010 mol","note":"One faraday is 96 500 C of electricity."},{"title":"Moles of copper","kind":"math","content":"Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so n(Cu) = 0.010 ÷ 2 = 0.005 mol","note":"Two electrons are needed to deposit one Cu²⁺ ion."},{"title":"Mass of copper","kind":"math","content":"m = n × M = 0.005 × 63.5 = 0.3175 g ≈ 0.32 g","note":"Round to two significant figures."}]$st$,
 3),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2025,33, $q$A gas X diffuses through a porous plug twice as fast as gas Y. If the relative molecular mass of Y is 64, the relative molecular mass of X is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"32"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"128"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$By Graham's law, rate of diffusion is inversely proportional to √M, so r(X) ÷ r(Y) = √(64 ÷ M(X)) = 2. Squaring gives 64 ÷ M(X) = 4, hence M(X) = 16, the value for methane, CH₄.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','rates-equilibrium',2025,34, $q$In the Haber process, N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) + heat. An increase in the total pressure on the system will$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"shift the position of equilibrium to the right"},{"label":"B","text":"shift the position of equilibrium to the left"},{"label":"C","text":"have no effect on the position of equilibrium"},{"label":"D","text":"decrease the yield of ammonia"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Four moles of gas are on the left but only two moles on the right. Increased pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules, so more ammonia is formed.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2025,35, $q$Aluminium, although a reactive metal, is widely used for cooking utensils because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"it is a good conductor of heat"},{"label":"B","text":"it has a low density"},{"label":"C","text":"it reacts very slowly with dilute acids"},{"label":"D","text":"it is protected by an unreactive layer of oxide"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Aluminium rapidly acquires a thin, adherent film of aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃, on its surface. This impermeable layer prevents further attack by air and by most food constituents, so the metal behaves as though it were unreactive.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','metals-nonmetals',2025,36, $q$Sodium carbonate solution is added to hard water containing dissolved calcium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV), Ca(HCO₃)₂. The calcium is removed from solution in the form of$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI)"},{"label":"B","text":"calcium trioxocarbonate(IV)"},{"label":"C","text":"calcium chloride"},{"label":"D","text":"calcium hydroxide"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Na₂CO₃ reacts with Ca(HCO₃)₂ to precipitate insoluble calcium trioxocarbonate(IV), CaCO₃, while NaHCO₃ remains in solution. Removing the Ca²⁺ ions as insoluble CaCO₃ softens the water.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('chemistry','organic',2025,37, $q$Polyester, made from a dicarboxylic acid and a diol, is a condensation polymer while polyethene is an addition polymer. Polyesters form by condensation because$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"a catalyst is always needed"},{"label":"B","text":"the monomers used are unsaturated"},{"label":"C","text":"small molecules such as water are eliminated as the monomers link"},{"label":"D","text":"the chains are held together by hydrogen bonds"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Each ester link forms between a carboxyl group and a hydroxyl group with the loss of one molecule of water. Monomers that join with elimination of small molecules give condensation polymers, whereas polyethene adds up alkene monomers with no atoms lost.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);
insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('mathematics','number-surds',2023,10, $q$Expand and simplify (√7 + 2)².$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"23 + 4√7"},{"label":"B","text":"11 + 4√7"},{"label":"C","text":"11 + 2√7"},{"label":"D","text":"9 + 4√7"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Use (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²: 7 + 4√7 + 4 = 11 + 4√7.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2023,11, $q$A worker's monthly salary was increased from ₦125 to ₦170. Find the percentage increase.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"32%"},{"label":"B","text":"38%"},{"label":"C","text":"40%"},{"label":"D","text":"36%"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The increase is ₦45 on ₦125, giving 45/125 × 100 = 36%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2023,12, $q$Evaluate the square root of 0.0009.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3 × 10⁻³"},{"label":"B","text":"9 × 10⁻³"},{"label":"C","text":"3 × 10⁻²"},{"label":"D","text":"6 × 10⁻²"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Write 0.0009 = 9 × 10⁻⁴ = (3 × 10⁻²)², so the square root is 0.03 = 3 × 10⁻².$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2023,13, $q$A packet of 8 identical pens costs ₦184. How much will 3 of the pens cost?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"₦56"},{"label":"B","text":"₦72"},{"label":"C","text":"₦69"},{"label":"D","text":"₦75"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$One pen costs 184/8 = ₦23, so 3 pens cost 3 × 23 = ₦69.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','indices-logs',2023,14, $q$Simplify 2^(x + 1) × 2^(x − 1).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2^(x + 2)"},{"label":"B","text":"2^(2x + 1)"},{"label":"C","text":"2^(2x)"},{"label":"D","text":"2^(x²)"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Add the indices when multiplying powers of the same base: (x + 1) + (x − 1) = 2x, giving 2^(2x).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','indices-logs',2023,15, $q$Solve the equation 3 log₁₀ x = log₁₀ 64.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"64"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Rewrite as log₁₀ x³ = log₁₀ 64, so x³ = 64 and x = 4. Check: log₁₀ 64 = 3 log₁₀ 4.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','indices-logs',2023,16, $q$Evaluate 2⁻³ + 2⁻².$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1/4"},{"label":"B","text":"3/8"},{"label":"C","text":"1/32"},{"label":"D","text":"3/16"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$2⁻³ = 1/8 and 2⁻² = 1/4, so the sum is 1/8 + 2/8 = 3/8.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2023,17, $q$In an arithmetic progression the 3rd term is 8 and the 7th term is 20. Find the 10th term.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"26"},{"label":"B","text":"32"},{"label":"C","text":"29"},{"label":"D","text":"35"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Subtracting gives 4d = 12, so d = 3 and a = 2. The 10th term is 2 + 9 × 3 = 29. Check: 2, 5, 8, ..., matches the 3rd term of 8.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2023,18, $q$The sum to infinity of a geometric progression is 40 and each term after the first is half the one before it. Find the first term.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20"},{"label":"B","text":"40"},{"label":"C","text":"80"},{"label":"D","text":"10"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The common ratio is ½, so S∞ = a/(1 − ½) = 2a = 40, giving a = 20.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Identify the ratio","kind":"text","content":"Each term is half of the previous one, so r = ½. Since |r| < 1 the series converges.","note":"Halving means the terms die away."},{"title":"Apply the sum to infinity","kind":"math","content":"S∞ = a/(1 − r) → 40 = a/(1/2) = 2a","note":"Multiply 40 by 1 − r."},{"title":"Read off the first term","kind":"math","content":"a = 20, so the series begins 20, 10, 5, ...","note":"20 + 10 + 5 + ... = 40."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2023,19, $q$In an arithmetic progression, the 8th term is twice the 4th term, and the 4th term is 4. Find the first term.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$a + 7d = 2(a + 3d) simplifies to a = d. Then the 4th term is a + 3d = 4a = 4, so a = 1. The progression begins 1, 2, 3, 4, ...$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2023,20, $q$Solve the equation 2x² − 5x − 3 = 0.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"x = 3 or −½"},{"label":"B","text":"x = −3 or ½"},{"label":"C","text":"x = 3 or ½"},{"label":"D","text":"x = −3 or −½"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Factorize: (2x + 1)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 3 or x = −1/2. The product of the roots is −3/2 and the sum is 5/2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2023,21, $q$If α and β are the roots of x² + 2x − 5 = 0, find α² + β².$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"9"},{"label":"C","text":"24"},{"label":"D","text":"14"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$α + β = −2 and αβ = −5, so α² + β² = (α + β)² − 2αβ = 4 + 10 = 14.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2023,22, $q$Express x² − 6x + 11 in the form (x − a)² + b, and hence find the value of b.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"9"},{"label":"C","text":"11"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Half the x coefficient is 3, and x² − 6x + 9 = (x − 3)². Completing the square: x² − 6x + 11 = (x − 3)² + 2, so b = 2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2023,23, $q$If cos θ = sin 25° for 0° < θ < 90°, find θ.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"25°"},{"label":"B","text":"65°"},{"label":"C","text":"55°"},{"label":"D","text":"115°"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Since cos θ = sin(90° − θ) = sin 25°, we get 90° − θ = 25°, so θ = 65°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2023,24, $q$Find the area of the triangle ABC in which side a = 6 cm, side b = 8 cm and the included angle C = 30°.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12 cm²"},{"label":"B","text":"24 cm²"},{"label":"C","text":"48 cm²"},{"label":"D","text":"6 cm²"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Use Area = ½ ab sin C = ½ × 6 × 8 × sin 30° = 24 × ½ = 12 cm². The sine of 30° is ½.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2023,25, $q$A cargo plane flies 40 km from airfield A on a bearing of 030°, then 30 km on a bearing of 120°. How far is the plane now from A?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"35 km"},{"label":"B","text":"60 km"},{"label":"C","text":"50 km"},{"label":"D","text":"70 km"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The bearings differ by 90°, so the two legs are perpendicular. Pythagoras gives √(40² + 30²) = 50 km.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Find the angle between the legs","kind":"text","content":"The bearings 030° and 120° differ by 90°, so the second leg turns through a right angle.","note":"120° − 030° = 90°."},{"title":"Apply Pythagoras","kind":"math","content":"distance² = 40² + 30² = 1600 + 900 = 2500","note":"The straight path back to A is the hypotenuse."},{"title":"Take the square root","kind":"math","content":"distance = √2500 = 50 km","note":"A 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 10."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2023,26, $q$Find the gradient of the normal to the curve y = x² at the point (1, 1).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"−2"},{"label":"B","text":"½"},{"label":"C","text":"−½"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The tangent gradient is dy/dx = 2x = 2 at x = 1. The normal gradient is the negative reciprocal: −1/2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2023,27, $q$Differentiate y = x² cos x with respect to x.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2x cos x − x² sin x"},{"label":"B","text":"2x cos x + x² sin x"},{"label":"C","text":"−2x sin x"},{"label":"D","text":"x² cos x − 2x sin x"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$By the product rule, dy/dx = 2x cos x + x²(−sin x) = 2x cos x − x² sin x.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2023,28, $q$Evaluate ∫₂⁴ (6x² − 4x + 3) dx.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"108"},{"label":"B","text":"94"},{"label":"C","text":"84"},{"label":"D","text":"104"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$An antiderivative is 2x³ − 2x² + 3x. At x = 4 it is 128 − 32 + 12 = 108, and at x = 2 it is 16 − 8 + 6 = 14. The difference is 94.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2023,29, $q$Given that y = 3x² and x changes at the rate of 0.5 units per second, find dy/dt when x = 4.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"6 units/s"},{"label":"B","text":"24 units/s"},{"label":"C","text":"12 units/s"},{"label":"D","text":"3 units/s"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$By the chain rule, dy/dt = 6x dx/dt = 6(4)(0.5) = 12 units per second.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2023,30, $q$Nine students scored 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13 and 15 marks. Find the lower quartile of the marks.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5.0"},{"label":"B","text":"5.5"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0"},{"label":"D","text":"11.0"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The lower quartile is the value at position (n + 1)/4 = 2.5, so average the 2nd and 3rd ordered values: (5 + 6)/2 = 5.5.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2023,31, $q$The mean of the six numbers 4, 6, 8, x, 14 and 16 is 10. Find the value of x.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10"},{"label":"B","text":"14"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The total must be 6 × 10 = 60, and 4 + 6 + 8 + 14 + 16 = 48. Hence x = 60 − 48 = 12.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2023,32, $q$Find the mean deviation of the data 5, 7, 9 and 11.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The mean is 8, giving deviations 3, 1, 1 and 3 with sum 8. The mean deviation is 8/4 = 2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2023,33, $q$A survey of the 120 students who belong to four interest clubs is shown on a pie chart: Music occupies 90°, Reading 90°, Art 60° and Sport 120°. How many students chose Sport?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"30"},{"label":"B","text":"40"},{"label":"C","text":"60"},{"label":"D","text":"20"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Sport is 120/360 = 1/3 of the circle, so 1/3 × 120 = 40 students chose Sport.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Convert the angle to a fraction","kind":"math","content":"Sport occupies 120° of the 360° circle, a fraction of 120/360 = 1/3","note":"A full turn represents all 120 students."},{"title":"Apply the fraction to the total","kind":"math","content":"Number for Sport = 1/3 × 120 = 40 students","note":"Each 90° sector corresponds to 30 students."},{"title":"Cross-check the chart","kind":"text","content":"Music and Reading use 90° each, giving 30 students; Art uses 60°, giving 20. The total is 30 + 30 + 20 + 40 = 120.","note":"All four sectors must use up all 360°."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2023,34, $q$A circle has a circumference of 44 cm. Find its area (take π = 22/7).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"154 cm²"},{"label":"B","text":"49 cm²"},{"label":"C","text":"176 cm²"},{"label":"D","text":"44 cm²"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$From 2πr = 44: r = 44 × 7/44 = 7 cm. Then area = 22/7 × 49 = 154 cm².$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2023,35, $q$Find the size of each interior angle of a regular dodecagon (12-sided polygon).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"144°"},{"label":"B","text":"150°"},{"label":"C","text":"156°"},{"label":"D","text":"162°"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Each exterior angle is 360/12 = 30°, so each interior angle is 180° − 30° = 150°. Check: 12 × 150 = 1800 = (12 × 2 − 4) × 90°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2023,36, $q$A chord of length 24 cm is drawn in a circle of radius 13 cm. How far is the chord from the centre of the circle?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"13 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"5 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"12 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"17 cm"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The perpendicular from the centre halves the chord, forming a right triangle with hypotenuse 13 and leg 12. The distance is √(169 − 144) = 5 cm.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2023,37, $q$Find the gradient of the straight line whose equation is 2x − 3y = 7.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2/3"},{"label":"B","text":"−3/2"},{"label":"C","text":"3/2"},{"label":"D","text":"−2/3"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Rearrange to y = (2x − 7)/3, written as y = (2/3)x − 7/3. The coefficient 2/3 is the gradient. Note the negative reciprocal is the perpendicular slope.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2023,38, $q$Express 0.0047 in standard form.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"47 × 10⁻⁴"},{"label":"B","text":"0.47 × 10⁻²"},{"label":"C","text":"4.7 × 10⁻⁴"},{"label":"D","text":"4.7 × 10⁻³"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Move the decimal point three places right to get 4.7, so 0.0047 = 4.7 × 10⁻³. The digit part must lie between 1 and 10.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

