 'D',
 $q$Add the indices: −2 + 5 = 3, so the product is 10³.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','indices-logs',2024,14, $q$Solve the equation 4^x = 64.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Write both sides as powers of 2: 2^(2x) = 2⁶, so 2x = 6 and x = 3. Check: 4³ = 64.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','indices-logs',2024,15, $q$Given that log 2 = 0.3010, evaluate log 8.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"0.9020"},{"label":"B","text":"0.9300"},{"label":"C","text":"0.9030"},{"label":"D","text":"0.9083"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Since 8 = 2³, log 8 = 3 × 0.3010 = 0.9030. The power law multiplies the log by 3.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2024,16, $q$In an arithmetic progression the first term is 5, the fifth term is 25 and the common difference is positive. Find the 13th term.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"60"},{"label":"B","text":"70"},{"label":"C","text":"75"},{"label":"D","text":"65"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$From a + 4d = 25 with a = 5: d = 5. The 13th term is 5 + 12 × 5 = 65.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2024,17, $q$The third term of a geometric progression is 27 and the fifth term is 243. Find the second term.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"9"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Dividing gives r² = 243/27 = 9, so r = 3. Then a = 27/9 = 3, and the second term is ar = 9. Check: 3, 9, 27, 81, 243.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2024,18, $q$A bank pays 12% per annum simple interest. How long will it take an investment of ₦2,500 to earn ₦600 interest?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1 year"},{"label":"B","text":"2 years"},{"label":"C","text":"3 years"},{"label":"D","text":"4 years"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Interest = P × R/100 × T, so 600 = 2500 × 12/100 × T = 300T. Hence T = 2 years. The interest per year is ₦300.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2024,19, $q$Solve the equation x² + 2x − 15 = 0.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"x = −3 or 5"},{"label":"B","text":"x = 3 or 5"},{"label":"C","text":"x = −3 or −5"},{"label":"D","text":"x = 3 or −5"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The pair with sum −2 and product −15 is −5 and 3, so (x − 3)(x + 5) = 0. Hence x = 3 or x = −5.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2024,20, $q$Find the equation whose roots are 3 and −2/3.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3x² + 7x − 6 = 0"},{"label":"B","text":"3x² − 7x + 6 = 0"},{"label":"C","text":"6x² − 7x − 3 = 0"},{"label":"D","text":"3x² − 7x − 6 = 0"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The sum of roots is 3 − 2/3 = 7/3 and the product is −2, so x² − (7/3)x − 2 = 0. Multiplying by 3: 3x² − 7x − 6 = 0. Check: (3x + 2)(x − 3) = 3x² − 7x − 6.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2024,21, $q$For what values of m does the equation x² + 6x + m = 0 have real and unequal roots?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"m > 9"},{"label":"B","text":"m < 9"},{"label":"C","text":"m = 9"},{"label":"D","text":"m ≥ 9"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Real and unequal roots require a positive discriminant: 36 − 4m > 0, so m < 9. At m = 9 the roots are equal.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2024,22, $q$Evaluate cos²60° − sin²60°.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"½"},{"label":"B","text":"√3/2"},{"label":"C","text":"−√3/2"},{"label":"D","text":"−½"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$cos 60° = ½ and sin 60° = √3/2, so the difference is ¼ − ¾ = −½. Equivalently cos 2(60°) = cos 120° = −½.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2024,23, $q$Two angles of a triangle are 40° and 60°. Find the third angle.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"70°"},{"label":"B","text":"90°"},{"label":"C","text":"80°"},{"label":"D","text":"100°"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The angles of a triangle sum to 180°, so the third angle is 180° − (40° + 60°) = 80°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2024,24, $q$Evaluate tan 30° × tan 60° + tan 45°.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"√3"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Since tan 30° × tan 60° = (1/√3)(√3) = 1 and tan 45° = 1, the value is 1 + 1 = 2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2024,25, $q$From the top of a tower 40 m high, the angle of depression of a car is 30°. How far is the car from the base of the tower (on level ground)?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"20√3 m"},{"label":"B","text":"40 m"},{"label":"C","text":"80 m"},{"label":"D","text":"40√3 m"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The angle of elevation from the car is 30°, so tan 30° = 40/d, giving d = 40/tan 30° = 40√3 m (about 69.3 m).$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2024,26, $q$If y = x³ − 6x² + 9x, find d²y/dx².$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"3x² − 12x + 9"},{"label":"B","text":"6x − 12"},{"label":"C","text":"6x"},{"label":"D","text":"12x − 6"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$First differentiate: dy/dx = 3x² − 12x + 9. Differentiating again gives d²y/dx² = 6x − 12.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2024,27, $q$The side of a square is increasing at the rate of 2 cm/s. Find the rate at which its area is increasing when the side is 5 cm.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"10 cm²/s"},{"label":"B","text":"20 cm²/s"},{"label":"C","text":"25 cm²/s"},{"label":"D","text":"50 cm²/s"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A = s², so dA/dt = 2s ds/dt = 2(5)(2) = 20 cm²/s.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2024,28, $q$Evaluate ∫₁⁵ (6x − 5) dx.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"48"},{"label":"B","text":"56"},{"label":"C","text":"52"},{"label":"D","text":"50"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$An antiderivative is 3x² − 5x. At x = 5 it is 75 − 25 = 50, and at x = 1 it is 3 − 5 = −2. The difference is 50 − (−2) = 52.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2024,29, $q$The curve y = x³ − 3x² + 1 has two turning points. Find the coordinates of the minimum point.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"(0, 1)"},{"label":"B","text":"(2, 3)"},{"label":"C","text":"(−2, −3)"},{"label":"D","text":"(2, −3)"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$dy/dx = 3x² − 6x = 3x(x − 2) = 0 at x = 0 and x = 2. At x = 2, y = 8 − 12 + 1 = −3, and since y″ = 6x − 6 > 0 there, the minimum point is (2, −3).$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Differentiate","kind":"math","content":"dy/dx = 3x² − 6x","note":"Differentiate the original function term by term."},{"title":"Set the derivative to zero","kind":"math","content":"3x(x − 2) = 0 gives x = 0 and x = 2","note":"Turning points occur where dy/dx = 0."},{"title":"Identify the minimum","kind":"math","content":"At x = 2: y = 8 − 12 + 1 = −3, and y″ = 6x − 6 = 6 > 0, so (2, −3) is the minimum","note":"The point (0, 1) is actually the maximum."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2024,30, $q$The marks of eight students are 4, 6, 9, 10, 12, 14, 15 and 18. Find the median mark.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"11"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"11.5"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$The data are in order, and with 8 values the median is the mean of the 4th and 5th: (10 + 12)/2 = 11.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2024,31, $q$Find the semi-interquartile range of the data 8, 12, 10, 16, 14, 6 and 20.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$In order: 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 20. The lower quartile is 8, the upper quartile 16, so QR = 16 − 8 = 8 and SIQR = 8/2 = 4.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2024,32, $q$A student's final score is a weighted mean of three components: 70 (weight 2), 60 (weight 3) and 80 (weight 5). Find the weighted mean score.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"68"},{"label":"B","text":"72"},{"label":"C","text":"70"},{"label":"D","text":"74"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The weighted total is 70 × 2 + 60 × 3 + 80 × 5 = 720. Dividing by the total weight 10 gives 72. The unweighted mean of 70 is the common error.$q$,
 'multi', $st$[{"title":"Weight each score","kind":"math","content":"70 × 2 + 60 × 3 + 80 × 5 = 140 + 180 + 400 = 720","note":"Multiply each score by its weight."},{"title":"Total the weights","kind":"math","content":"2 + 3 + 5 = 10","note":"The weights must sum to 10."},{"title":"Divide","kind":"math","content":"Weighted mean = 720/10 = 72","note":"The heaviest weight pulls the mean above 70."}]$st$, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2024,33, $q$The interior angles of a pentagon are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4 : 5 : 6. Find the largest angle.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"135°"},{"label":"B","text":"162°"},{"label":"C","text":"144°"},{"label":"D","text":"108°"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The interior angles sum to (5 − 2) × 180° = 540°. With 20 parts in total, one part is 27°, so the largest angle is 6 × 27° = 162°.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2024,34, $q$Find the gradient of the line joining the points A(1, 2) and B(3, 8).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"½"},{"label":"D","text":"6"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Gradient = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) = (8 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 6/2 = 3.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2024,35, $q$A sector of a circle of radius 14 cm has an angle of 45° at the centre. Find the perimeter of the sector (take π = 22/7).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"36 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"44 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"50 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"64 cm"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The arc length is (45/360) × 2 × 22/7 × 14 = (1/8) × 176 = 22 cm. The perimeter is this arc plus the two radii: 22 + 28 = 50 cm.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('mathematics','number-surds',2025,10, $q$Rationalize the denominator of 3/(√5 + √2).$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"√5 + √2"},{"label":"B","text":"√5 − √2"},{"label":"C","text":"(√5 − √2)/3"},{"label":"D","text":"(√5 + √2)/3"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$Multiply by (√5 − √2)/(√5 − √2): the result is 3(√5 − √2)/(5 − 2) = √5 − √2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2025,11, $q$The price of an article was reduced from ₦250 to ₦200. Find the percentage reduction.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"18%"},{"label":"B","text":"25%"},{"label":"C","text":"20%"},{"label":"D","text":"12%"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The reduction is ₦50 on ₦250, so 50/250 × 100 = 20%.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2025,12, $q$Simplify (√6 − √2)/√2.$q$,
