('mathematics','trigonometry',2025,25, $q$If 180° ≤ θ ≤ 270° and sin θ = −1/√2, find θ.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"135°"},{"label":"B","text":"240°"},{"label":"C","text":"315°"},{"label":"D","text":"225°"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The sine is negative in the third quadrant, where the basic angle is 45°, giving θ = 180° + 45° = 225°. Note sin 225° = −√2/2 = −1/√2.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2025,26, $q$Find dy/dx if y = sin 3x.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"−3 cos 3x"},{"label":"B","text":"cos 3x"},{"label":"C","text":"3 sin 3x"},{"label":"D","text":"3 cos 3x"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The derivative of sin u is cos u du/dx, so with u = 3x we get dy/dx = cos 3x × 3 = 3 cos 3x.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);

insert into public.questions (subject_slug, topic_slug, year, qnum, stem, options, answer, explanation, kind, steps, difficulty) values
('mathematics','indices-logs',2025,27, $q$Solve for x: log₂(8x) = 5.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"24"},{"label":"D","text":"32"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$log₂8 = 3, so the equation becomes 3 + log₂x = 5, giving log₂x = 2. Hence x = 2² = 4. The value 2 is the log, not x, and 24 comes from subtracting instead of dividing.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','progressions',2025,28, $q$The nth term of an arithmetic progression is 3n − 1. The sum of the first 15 terms is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"330"},{"label":"B","text":"345"},{"label":"C","text":"390"},{"label":"D","text":"660"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$The first term is 3(1) − 1 = 2 and the 15th term is 3(15) − 1 = 44. Sum = 15/2 × (2 + 44) = 15 × 23 = 345. The value 660 comes from multiplying 15 by the last term only.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','trigonometry',2025,29, $q$A boat leaves port P and sails 24 km due east to Q, then sails due north to R. If the bearing of R from P is 060°, the distance QR is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12 km"},{"label":"B","text":"24√3 km"},{"label":"C","text":"8√3 km"},{"label":"D","text":"24 km"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$The 24 km step is the eastward offset while QR is the northward offset, and the bearing gives tan 60° = 24 ÷ QR. Hence QR = 24 ÷ √3 = 8√3 km (≈ 13.9 km). The value 24√3 comes from multiplying instead of dividing.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2025,30, $q$In a grouped frequency distribution, the modal class interval is 40–49. The lower class boundary of the modal class is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"39.5"},{"label":"B","text":"40"},{"label":"C","text":"45"},{"label":"D","text":"49.5"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$A class boundary lies midway between neighbouring class limits, so between 39 and 40 it is (39 + 40)/2 = 39.5. Here 40 is the lower class limit and 45 is the class mark.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','number-surds',2025,31, $q$In a class of 40 students, 25 offer Chemistry and 20 offer Biology. If 8 students offer neither subject, how many offer both subjects?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"5"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"19"},{"label":"D","text":"13"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$Students offering at least one subject: 40 − 8 = 32. Therefore both = 25 + 20 − 32 = 13, and the 19 who offer exactly one subject give the common distractor.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','quadratics',2025,32, $q$A 2 × 2 matrix has k and 6 in its first row, and 2 and k in its second row. The positive value of k for which the matrix is singular is$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"√6"},{"label":"B","text":"2√3"},{"label":"C","text":"3√2"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}]$o$,
 'B',
 $q$A singular matrix has zero determinant: k × k − 6 × 2 = k² − 12 = 0. Thus k² = 12 and k = √12 = 2√3; 12 is k², not k.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','calculus',2025,33, $q$If y = 4x³ − x, find dy/dx and the value of dy/dx at x = 2.$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"12x² − x; 46"},{"label":"B","text":"12x² − 1; 95"},{"label":"C","text":"12x² − 1; 47"},{"label":"D","text":"12x²; 48"}]$o$,
 'C',
 $q$Differentiating term by term gives dy/dx = 12x² − 1. At x = 2, dy/dx = 12 × 4 − 1 = 47. The value 46 comes from keeping −x instead of −1, and 48 from dropping the −1.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','geometry',2025,34, $q$A sector of a circle of radius 14 cm subtends an angle of 135° at the centre. Find the area of the sector. [π = 22/7]$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"154 cm²"},{"label":"B","text":"77 cm²"},{"label":"C","text":"462 cm²"},{"label":"D","text":"231 cm²"}]$o$,
 'D',
 $q$The sector is 135/360 = 3/8 of the whole circle. Area = (3/8) × (22/7) × 14² = (3/8) × 616 = 231 cm²; 154, 77 and 462 correspond to the 1/4, 1/8 and 3/4 sectors.$q$,
 'single', null, 2),
('mathematics','statistics',2025,35, $q$A committee of 3 people is to be formed from 5 men and 4 women. In how many ways can the committee be selected if it must contain exactly 2 men?$q$,
 $o$[{"label":"A","text":"40"},{"label":"B","text":"84"},{"label":"C","text":"30"},{"label":"D","text":"10"}]$o$,
 'A',
 $q$Choose the 2 men in C(5,2) = 10 ways and the 1 woman in 4 ways, giving 10 × 4 = 40. The value 84 is C(9,3), the count of all committees with no restriction.$q$,
 'single', null, 2);
